const READ={"builder":[{"id":"00_supercram_lecture","track":"builder","title":"Super-Cram: The Numbers","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the last-hour listen. Every number here comes straight from the corpus. Breathe, listen, and let them land one more time."},{"t":"p","s":"## Licensing law"},{"t":"p","s":"The law is Article twenty four of the Occupational Code, Public Act two ninety nine of nineteen eighty, MCL 339.2401 through two four one two. The board has nine members: four residential builders, two M and A contractors, three public members. The licensure exemption is 600 dollars aggregate for labor, materials, and everything else, with an anti-evasion clause — you cannot split jobs. Prelicensure education is 60 hours, at least 6 hours in each of seven competency areas. The license term is 3 years. Continuing competency for builders first licensed on or after January first, two thousand nine is at least 21 hours per 3 year cycle, with at least 3 hours each calendar year in the first 6 years. Builders licensed before two thousand nine with no discipline need only 3 hours per cycle. Keep continuing competency records 5 years. You have 1 year after applying to finish everything, including the exam. A lapsed license under 3 years costs 185 dollars to relicense with no re-education; over 3 years means the full 60 hours again."},{"t":"p","s":"Homeowner complaints must be filed within 18 months. Notify the department of a change in control within 10 days. The administrative fine tops out at 10,000 dollars. Criminal fines for unlicensed building run five thousand to twenty five thousand dollars: up to 1 year in jail for a first offense, 2 years for a second, and it becomes a felony with up to 4 years if the work causes a death. An unlicensed builder cannot sue for payment and cannot record a lien."},{"t":"p","s":"Exam vitals: one hundred sixty questions — fifty business and law plus one hundred ten trade. Passing raw score one hundred sixteen, which is seventy two and a half percent, in two hundred forty minutes. Regulatory and statutory requirements is the biggest business and law category at thirteen of fifty. Application fee one ninety five, exam fee one seventeen, salesperson exam 85 dollars. The exam references the twenty fifteen Michigan Residential Code."},{"t":"p","s":"## Liens, insurance, and bonds"},{"t":"p","s":"Subcontractors and suppliers serve the Notice of Furnishing within 20 days of first furnishing. Laborers get 30 days from when unpaid wages were due. A homeowner has 10 days to return a requested Notice of Commencement. Record the claim of lien within 90 days after your last furnishing — warranty and punch-list work do not restart the clock. Serve a copy within 15 days of recording. File the foreclosure suit within 1 year of recording, in circuit court, or the lien dies. There are four lien waiver types: full and partial, each conditional or unconditional. A conditional waiver is effective only when payment actually clears. Residential lien rights require a written contract with the licensure statement and license number, at any dollar amount. The Homeowner Construction Lien Recovery Fund was abolished in twenty ten — any answer relying on it is wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"Public projects over 50,000 dollars require performance and payment bonds, each at least 25 percent of the contract amount. The performance bond protects the governmental unit; the payment bond protects subs, suppliers, and laborers. Bond claimants without privity give notice within 30 days after first furnishing and again within 90 days after last furnishing."},{"t":"p","s":"Workers comp is required with three or more employees at one time, or one employee working thirty five plus hours a week for thirteen or more weeks in the preceding fifty two. A general contractor is liable for comp claims of an uninsured sub's injured workers. Builders risk covers the structure and materials; CGL covers third-party injury and damage, never your own defective work."},{"t":"p","s":"## Contracts and consumer law"},{"t":"p","s":"HIFA triggers when the cash price exceeds 300 dollars on installment credit — exactly three hundred is excluded. HIFA rescission runs to five p m of the next business day. The Home Solicitation Sales Act cancellation runs to midnight of the third business day, and after cancellation the seller refunds within ten business days and has 20 days to pick up goods. HIFA type sizes: eight point body, ten point bold headings. Willful HIFA violation: 90 days and or 500 dollars."},{"t":"p","s":"Statutes of limitation: breach of contract, 6 years. Negligence, 3 years. The statute of repose for construction defects is 6 years from occupancy, use, or acceptance; the gross negligence exception allows 1 year after discovery with an absolute cap of 10 years. Retainage typically runs five to ten percent."},{"t":"p","s":"## Business management"},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan sales tax is 6 percent — contractors pay it on materials; installation labor is exempt. Markup is not margin: 25 percent markup is a 20 percent margin; the ten and ten formula is really 16.67 percent margin. To get a 20 percent margin, divide cost by point eight zero. FICA is 6.2 percent Social Security plus 1.45 percent Medicare — 7.65 percent each side. FUTA is 6 percent on the first 7000 dollars, netting to point six percent. Overtime is time and a half over 40 hours in a workweek. The ten ninety nine NEC threshold for twenty twenty six payments is 2000 dollars, up from six hundred. The LLC annual statement is due February fifteenth with a 25 dollar fee. New construction employers pay the construction industry average unemployment rate, not the standard 2.7 percent. Keep the MIOSHA three hundred log if you have eleven or more employees; post the three hundred A summary February first through April thirtieth."},{"t":"p","s":"## Safety"},{"t":"p","s":"Fall protection at 6 feet. Guardrail top rail at 42 inches plus or minus three, withstanding two hundred pounds. Scaffold guardrails trigger at 10 feet, and scaffolds carry four times the intended load. Ladders: four to one pitch, 3 feet above the landing, rungs ten to fourteen inches apart, 20 feet from power lines for a portable ladder — 10 feet is the equipment clearance up to fifty k V. Trenches: protective system at 5 feet, ladder at 4 feet within 25 feet of travel, spoil piles 2 feet back, engineer design at 20 feet. Soil slopes: Type A is three quarters to one at fifty three degrees, Type B one to one at forty five degrees, Type C one and a half to one at thirty four degrees — Type C can never be benched. Silica limit is fifty micrograms per cubic meter with an action level of twenty five. Construction noise limit: ninety d B A. Report a fatality within 8 hours; hospitalization, amputation, or eye loss within twenty four. Serious violation penalty: up to 7000 dollars; willful or repeat, up to seventy thousand. Call MISS DIG at eight one one three full working days before digging; the fine is up to 5000 dollars."},{"t":"p","s":"## Footings, foundations, concrete"},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan frost depth: 42 inches. Footings: 6 inches thick minimum, projecting at least 2 inches each side but never more than their own thickness. Anchor bolts: half inch diameter, 6 feet on center, 7 inches embedded, two per plate, within 12 inches of plate ends. Grade slopes away 6 inches in the first 10 feet. Foundation walls extend 4 inches above grade with veneer, 6 inches otherwise. Drain gravel goes 1 foot beyond the footing and 6 inches above it. Concrete strengths: twenty five hundred p s i for footings and interior work, three thousand for exposed vertical, thirty five hundred for garage slabs, porches, and steps. Air content five to seven percent. Slab on grade: three and a half inches thick on a 4 inch base with six mil poly. Rebar cover against earth: 3 inches, steel in the bottom third of the footing. Concrete hits about 70 percent strength at 7 days, design strength at twenty eight. Soil bearing: clay fifteen hundred, sand two thousand, sandy gravel three thousand, sedimentary rock four thousand pounds per square foot. Crawl space vents: one to one fifty; floor access eighteen by twenty four, wall access sixteen by twenty four. Septic tank minimum one thousand gallons, 50 feet to the well."},{"t":"p","s":"## Carpentry"},{"t":"p","s":"Joist bearing: one and a half inches on wood or metal, 3 inches on masonry or concrete. Bridging only for joists deeper than two by twelve. Joist cuts: notch depth one sixth, end notch one quarter, holes and notch length one third, with 2 inches of clearance — no notches in the middle third. Studs: notch 25 percent bearing, 40 percent nonbearing; bore 40 percent bearing, 60 percent nonbearing or doubled. A top plate cut over 50 percent gets a sixteen gauge tie, one and a half inches wide, eight ten-penny nails. Two by four bearing studs: 24 inches on center for roof and ceiling only, sixteen for one floor plus roof; two floors plus roof means two by six. Top plate joints offset 24 inches. Double the header and trimmers when a floor opening exceeds 4 feet. Ridge board 1 inch nominal; hips and valleys 2 inch nominal; below three in twelve the ridge is a designed beam. Collar ties in the upper third, one by four, 4 feet on center — rafter ties, not collar ties, resist thrust. Nailing: top plate to top plate sixteen penny at 16 inches; joists toenailed with three eight-penny nails; sheathing at 6 inches on edges, twelve in the field."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan stairs: maximum riser eight and a quarter inches, minimum tread 9 inches, variation three eighths of an inch, headroom 6 feet eight, width 36 inches, handrail thirty four to thirty eight inches on flights of four or more risers. Guards at 36 inches where you can fall more than 30 inches, rejecting a 4 inch sphere. Deck ledger: two by eight pressure treated with half inch hot-dipped galvanized lags or bolts, lateral hold-downs of fifteen hundred pounds at two locations, and deck footings at 42 inches."},{"t":"p","s":"## Masonry, roofing, siding"},{"t":"p","s":"Mortar strongest to weakest: M, S, N, O — twenty five hundred, eighteen hundred, seven fifty, three fifty p s i. Type M below grade, Type N for veneer. Veneer air space 1 inch to four and a half. Ties: 32 inches horizontal, twenty four vertical, 2.67 square feet each. Weeps at 33 inches, three sixteenths inch minimum, above the flashing. Lintels bear 4 inches each end. Firebox: 20 inches deep, 10 inch walls or eight with firebrick. Hearth 4 inches, extension two; extensions sixteen front and eight sides, or twenty and twelve at 6 square feet and up. Chimney: 3 feet above the roof penetration and 2 feet above anything within 10 feet; cricket required over 30 inches wide. Flue ratios: one twelfth round, one tenth square, one eighth for narrow rectangles. Chimney clearance 2 inches interior, 1 inch exterior."},{"t":"p","s":"Shingles: minimum slope two in twelve, double underlayment below four in twelve, four nails per strip. Ice barrier runs 24 inches inside the exterior wall line — not from the eave edge. Drip edge: quarter inch below sheathing, 2 inches onto the deck. Step flashing four by four. Open valley metal 24 inches wide. Tear off at two existing layers. Attic vent one to one fifty, dropping to one to three hundred with a qualifying condition; keep a 1 inch baffle airspace."},{"t":"p","s":"## Envelope and interior"},{"t":"p","s":"Insulation: ceiling R thirty eight in zone five A, R forty nine in six A and seven. Walls R twenty, or R thirteen plus five continuous. Window U factor point three two, skylight point five five. Air leakage four ACH fifty with a blower door test. Vapor retarder Class one or two on the interior side. Bath exhaust fifty c f m intermittent or twenty continuous; kitchen one hundred or twenty five. Egress windows: 5.7 square feet — five even at grade floor — 24 inches high, twenty wide, sill no more than 44 inches. Window wells 9 square feet with 36 inch projection. Safety glazing within a 24 inch arc of doors below 60 inches, and the four-part big-pane rule: 9 square feet, eighteen, thirty six, thirty six. Garage separation: half inch gypsum on walls, five eighths Type X on ceilings under habitable space; the house door is one and three eighths inches solid or twenty minute rated, self-closing. Attic access twenty two by thirty. Radon action level four picocuries per liter."},{"t":"p","s":"## Plans, math, permits"},{"t":"p","s":"Floor plans: quarter inch equals 1 foot. Window three zero five zero means 3 feet wide, 5 feet high. Board feet: thickness times width times length in feet, divided by twelve. Twenty seven cubic feet per cubic yard. Six point seven five bricks per square foot; one point one two five blocks. A square is 100 square feet, three bundles. A four by eight sheet is 32 square feet. Studs at sixteen on center: length times point seven five plus one. Earth swells about 25 percent loose. Written dimensions always govern over scaling. Permit exemptions: sheds 200 square feet, fences 7 feet, retaining walls 4 feet, decks 200 square feet under 30 inches. Permits issue in ten business days, expire after 180 days idle; certificate of occupancy in five business days. Sealed plans not needed under 3500 square feet. Plumbers: 6000 hours to journeyman, four thousand more to master. Electricians: eight thousand to journeyman, twelve thousand to master."},{"t":"p","s":"## The ten nastiest traps"},{"t":"p","s":"One. Frost depth is 42 inches — a Michigan amendment. The generic 12 inch footing answer is wrong here."},{"t":"p","s":"Two. Stairs: Michigan allows an eight and a quarter inch riser and 9 inch tread. The IRC's seven and three quarters and ten are the trap answers."},{"t":"p","s":"Three. Ceiling insulation in zone five A is R thirty eight by Michigan amendment — the base code's R forty nine is the trap for southern Michigan."},{"t":"p","s":"Four. Window U factor is point three two. Point three zero is the twenty twenty one code, which never took effect."},{"t":"p","s":"Five. The exam tests the twenty fifteen Michigan Residential Code — the twenty twenty one update was stayed and never adopted."},{"t":"p","s":"Six. Permit-exempt sheds are 200 square feet. One hundred twenty is the old-code trap."},{"t":"p","s":"Seven. Anchor bolts stay at 6 feet on center in Michigan — the 4 foot spacing is a seismic rule for other states."},{"t":"p","s":"Eight. MIOSHA's serious violation max is 7000 dollars by state statute — the sixteen thousand figure is federal."},{"t":"p","s":"Nine. The Lien Recovery Fund was abolished in twenty ten. Any answer leaning on it is dead wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"Ten. Wood shakes need only three in twelve under the MRC — four in twelve is the commercial code trap."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the whole deck. Walk in, find the Michigan number, and pass."}],"words":2429,"questions":0},{"id":"01_licensing-law_lecture","track":"builder","title":"Michigan Licensing Law and Exam Logistics","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"Before we start, let me tell you why this chapter is worth more than any other hour you will spend. On the business and law half of your exam there are fifty questions, and thirteen of them come out of this chapter alone. That is better than one in four."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is the good part. Code questions make you apply a rule to a situation. These questions just ask whether you know the law. No judgment, no interpretation. That makes this the most reliable block of points on the whole exam."},{"t":"p","s":"So I am not going to read you a list of rules. I am going to teach you eight ideas, one at a time, and after each one I will ask you a question and then be quiet while you answer it out loud. Answer out loud. The answer you can say is the answer you own."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"Who is in charge here, and under what law."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan licenses residential builders under Article twenty four of the Occupational Code. That is Public Act two ninety nine of nineteen eighty, and the sections run from MCL 339.2401 through two four one two. Hold onto that range. Almost everything in this chapter lives inside those twelve sections."},{"t":"p","s":"The agency that runs the program is LARA, the Department of Licensing and Regulatory Affairs, through its Bureau of Construction Codes. Sitting alongside LARA is a board, and the exam likes its makeup. It is the Residential Builders and Maintenance and Alteration Contractors Board. Nine members. Four licensed residential builders, two licensed M and A contractors, and three members of the public."},{"t":"p","s":"Nine, four, two, three. Notice the trades outnumber the public, but not by much. If a question offers you a board where the public members outnumber the builders, that is wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"Two framework facts that catch people out. Michigan does not license commercial builders at the state level. Article twenty four is residential only. And Michigan has no reciprocity with any other state. A builder licensed in Ohio for 30 years starts here from zero."},{"t":"q","s":"Question for you. Under what act and what section range does Michigan license residential builders, and how many people sit on the board. Say it out loud."},{"t":"a","s":"Article twenty four of the Occupational Code, Public Act two ninety nine of nineteen eighty, sections 2401 through two four one two. Nine board members, four builders, two M and A, three public. If that came out slowly, that is fine. It is the first time."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"What counts as a residential structure."},{"t":"p","s":"This one matters because the whole licensing scheme only applies to residential structures. If the building is not one, Article twenty four does not touch it."},{"t":"p","s":"Under the current statute a residential structure is one of two things. A detached one or two family dwelling. Or a townhouse of not more than three stories above grade plane with a separate means of egress."},{"t":"p","s":"Say that back. Detached one or two family. Or a townhouse, three stories or less, separate egress."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the trap, and it is a good one because it used to be true. This definition was rewritten in twenty twenty. Older study books talk about combination residential and commercial structures, and about counting units. If an answer choice tells you that any building of three units or less is a residential structure, that is the trap. Unit count alone is not the test. The test is the two shapes I just gave you."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. A four story townhouse with a separate entrance for each unit. Residential structure under Article twenty four, yes or no."},{"t":"a","s":"No. Three stories above grade plane is the ceiling. Four stories falls out of the definition, separate egress or not."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Who is a builder, who is an M and A contractor, and why a flipper gets caught."},{"t":"p","s":"A residential builder is anyone who, for compensation other than wages for personal labor only, undertakes or offers to undertake construction, repair, alteration, improvement, wrecking, or demolition of a residential structure."},{"t":"p","s":"Read that sentence again in your head and find the word offers. It is doing enormous work. Offering to undertake the job counts. You can violate the licensing law standing in a homeowner's kitchen, before you have driven a single nail, if you offer to do work you are not licensed to do."},{"t":"p","s":"The definition also sweeps in anyone dealing in prefabricated, precut, packaged, or shell housing, and anyone who erects a residential structure, except on their own property for their own use and occupancy."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the maintenance and alteration license, the M and A. It covers repair, alteration, additions, improvement, wrecking, building a garage, and laying concrete on residential property."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the relationship you need, because they will test it. A residential builder license covers everything an M and A license covers. But an M and A contractor cannot do new construction. Builder is the bigger license. Think of it as one circle inside another, with new construction sitting in the outer ring only."},{"t":"p","s":"And the M and A definition catches house flippers. If you buy, substantially rehab, and resell the same residential structure more than twice in one calendar year, you need a license. Two exits. If it is for your own use and occupancy, or if you hire all the work out to licensed contractors, you are clear."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. You rehab and sell three houses this calendar year, doing the work yourself. Licensed or not licensed."},{"t":"a","s":"You need the license. More than twice in one calendar year is the line, and three is more than twice. If you had subbed every bit of the work to licensed contractors, you would be outside it."},{"t":"p","s":"One more definition while we are here, and it is small. A salesperson is an employee or agent, other than a qualifying officer, of a licensed builder or contractor, who sells or solicits contracts for the licensee's services. Occasional and incidental sales do not count."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"When you need no license at all."},{"t":"p","s":"The exemptions live in section two four zero three. Government representatives. An owner building on their own property for their own use and occupancy. An owner of rental property doing maintenance and alteration on that rental property. Officers of a court. Subcontractors working solely under contract with a licensed builder. And licensed electrical, plumbing, and mechanical contractors, but only for their own trade work."},{"t":"p","s":"Then there is the famous one. If the aggregate price for labor, materials, and everything else on one undertaking is less than 600 dollars, no license is required."},{"t":"p","s":"Now watch, because two things hang off that number and the exam tests both."},{"t":"p","s":"The first is the anti evasion clause. You cannot slice a large job into a stack of little contracts under 600 dollars to dodge the act. If the work is part of a larger operation, the exemption is gone. Picture a full bathroom remodel written up as four separate 500 dollar contracts on the same day for the same homeowner. That is one undertaking wearing four hats, and the exemption does not apply."},{"t":"p","s":"The second is what 600 dollars is not. It is not a written contract trigger. It is purely a licensure exemption threshold. When you get to the contracts chapter you will meet a different dollar figure for when an agreement must be in writing. Do not let the exam swap them on you."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. 600 dollars. What does it decide, and what does it not decide."},{"t":"a","s":"It decides whether you need a license for that undertaking, measured on the aggregate price, and it cannot be dodged by splitting the job. It does not decide whether your contract has to be in writing."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"How you get the license in the first place."},{"t":"p","s":"Four requirements. Good moral character, and for a company that reaches every partner, officer, director, member, and shareholder. Proof of identity, meaning a copy of your driver license or state I D, which the department may use only to prove identity. The 60 hour prelicensure education. And the exam."},{"t":"p","s":"Now notice what is not on that list. Experience. The statute has no experience requirement. A 22 year old who has never framed a wall can hold the same license you do. People assume a trade license must require years in the field, and here it does not. Expect a question built on that assumption."},{"t":"p","s":"The 60 hour course has been required since June first, two thousand eight, and it has to include at least 6 hours in each of seven competency areas. Business management, estimating, and job costing. Design and building science. Contracts, liability, and risk management. Marketing and sales. Project management and scheduling. The current Michigan Residential Code. And MIOSHA construction safety standards."},{"t":"p","s":"Seven areas, 6 hours minimum in each, 60 hours total. Seven times six is forty two, so the remaining 18 hours float wherever the provider wants them. And a detail worth money, the certificate never expires, as long as the course was taken after June first, two thousand eight."},{"t":"p","s":"One clock to remember. Once LARA receives your application, you have 1 year to finish everything, including passing the exam. Miss the year and your fees are forfeited and the application is void."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Name the four requirements, and tell me the one thing people expect to be on the list that is not."},{"t":"a","s":"Good moral character, proof of identity, 60 hours of prelicensure education, and the exam. Experience is not required."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Keeping the license once you have it."},{"t":"p","s":"The license runs 3 years. You must maintain a place of business in Michigan, and every additional location needs its own branch office license. If you are a nonresident you file a consent to service of process, which simply means Michigan can serve you with legal papers here. And your contracts must carry your license information, both your individual license and any license you hold as a qualifying officer of a company."},{"t":"p","s":"At renewal you state two things to the department. That you have a current copy of the Michigan Residential Code. And that you meet your continuing competency requirements. Keep your continuing education documentation for at least 5 years."},{"t":"p","s":"The hours depend on when you were first licensed, and the split year is two thousand nine. Licensed on or after January first, two thousand nine, you owe 21 hours per 3 year cycle, and during your first 6 years at least three of those hours have to land in every calendar year, with at least 3 hours covering codes, safety, and changes in the law. Licensed before two thousand nine with a clean record, you owe just 3 hours per cycle, 1 hour of codes, 1 hour of safety, 1 hour of legal issues. And if you get disciplined, the department can order anywhere from three to twenty one hours in your next cycle."},{"t":"p","s":"If the license lapses there is a ladder, and the rung is 3 years. Expired less than 3 years, you file an application and pay 185 dollars and you are back. Lapsed more than 3 years, you start over. 60 hours of prelicensure again, application, fees, good moral character, and you retake the exam."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Your license expired 4 years ago. What do you have to do."},{"t":"a","s":"Start from the beginning. Past 3 years there is no shortcut back. 60 hours again, and the exam again."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"Companies and the qualifying officer."},{"t":"p","s":"When a corporation, a partnership, or an LLC does the building, the law treats it as a new legal person, and that person needs its own license. The company designates a qualifying officer out of its officers, partners, members, or managing agent. That person passes the exam and must also obtain and maintain their own individual license."},{"t":"p","s":"What is the qualifying officer for. Supervising and controlling the construction operations so the company complies with the law. That is the whole idea. The state wants one identifiable human being answerable for how the company builds. Everyone with control in the entity has to be at least eighteen."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the part that makes excellent exam questions, the suspension chains. If the qualifying officer's license is suspended, revoked, or denied, the company's license is suspended. If the qualifying officer leaves the company, the company license is suspended, although the department can allow a reasonable time to qualify a replacement. And a licensee on inactive status cannot serve as a qualifying officer."},{"t":"p","s":"The pattern is simple once you see it. The company's license hangs on the qualifying officer. Cut that person loose and the company drops."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Your qualifying officer quits on a Friday. What happens to the company license."},{"t":"a","s":"It is suspended. The department can give you a reasonable time to qualify a replacement, but the company does not simply carry on unaffected."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Discipline, and the workmanship complaint."},{"t":"p","s":"A homeowner has 18 months to file a complaint. 18 months from the latest of completion, occupancy, or purchase on a maintenance and alteration job, or from the certificate of occupancy or the closing on a project that needs an occupancy permit."},{"t":"p","s":"The list of conduct that gets you disciplined is long, so learn the greatest hits. Abandonment of a project without legal excuse. Diversion of funds, which means taking money for one project and spending it on another. Failure to account for money belonging to others. Willful departure from the plans and specifications without consent. Willful violation of building laws. Failing to notify the department within 10 days of a change in the control of the business. Failing to deliver the entire agreement to the purchaser. Aiding and abetting an unlicensed person, or lending out your license. Becoming insolvent or filing bankruptcy."},{"t":"p","s":"And one people never expect. Workmanship that does not meet the Michigan Residential Code is a discipline ground all by itself. No fraud, no theft, no complaint about your character. Bad work is enough."},{"t":"p","s":"Workmanship complaints have their own procedure, and the exam likes its deadlines. The homeowner sends the department a written complaint and sends you a copy at the same time. You are presumed innocent until the hearing examiner finds otherwise. Once a building inspection report verifies the complaint, the department must mail you the verified complaint by certified mail within 30 days. Miss that 30 days and the department loses the ability to fine you, though it can still pursue restitution or suspension."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is a tool you can actually use in your business. You may put an alternative dispute resolution clause in your contracts. If a complainant skips your contractual A D R procedure and sues you anyway, that failure is an affirmative defense."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Two numbers from this segment. How long does the homeowner have to complain, and how long does the department have to mail you a verified complaint."},{"t":"a","s":"18 months for the homeowner. 30 days for the department, by certified mail, once the inspection report verifies it."},{"t":"p","s":"Last idea. Penalties, in three buckets."},{"t":"p","s":"Bucket one is administrative, and this is what LARA can do to you directly. Limit, suspend, deny, or revoke your license. Censure. Probation. Restitution. And an administrative fine of up to 10,000 dollars."},{"t":"p","s":"Bucket two is criminal, and it is for unlicensed residential building. The fines run five thousand to twenty five thousand dollars. First offense is a misdemeanor with up to 1 year. Second offense, up to 2 years. And if the unlicensed work causes death or serious injury, it becomes a felony with up to 4 years."},{"t":"p","s":"Bucket three is civil. The attorney general or a prosecutor can sue, and the court shall assess a civil fine of five thousand to twenty five thousand dollars on top of any damages. Note the word shall. That fine is not optional."},{"t":"p","s":"Ten thousand for the agency. Five to twenty five thousand on the criminal and civil side. If you keep those two ranges straight you will get the penalty question."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the consequence that matters most in real life. An unlicensed builder cannot sue to collect payment. That is section two four one two. To bring the suit you must allege and prove you were licensed during the performance of the work. No license, no lawsuit. And no construction lien either. You can do the work, do it well, and have no legal way to get paid."},{"t":"p","s":"One trap to bury forever. There is no such statute as MCL 339.611. If you see it on an answer sheet, it is invented. The penalty sections are six zero one, six zero two, and two four one two."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. You did 20,000 dollars of work, you were unlicensed, the homeowner refuses to pay. What are your options in court."},{"t":"a","s":"You have none. No suit for payment, no lien. And you are exposed to the criminal and civil fines on top of eating the loss."},{"t":"p","s":"Now let us finish with exam logistics, because knowing the mechanics removes a surprising amount of test day anxiety."},{"t":"p","s":"PSI administers it. One hundred sixty questions. Fifty business and law, one hundred ten trade. You need one hundred sixteen correct, which is seventy two and a half percent. You get two hundred forty minutes and it is closed book."},{"t":"p","s":"Since November fourteenth, twenty nineteen it is one combined exam with one score. There are no separate section passes, so if you fail you retake the whole thing. Up to ten unscored experimental questions may be mixed in, which means a question that feels impossible may not even count."},{"t":"p","s":"The trade questions reference the twenty fifteen Michigan Residential Code, confirmed current in the PSI handbook updated August of twenty twenty six."},{"t":"p","s":"Fees. 195 dollars to apply to LARA. 117 dollars for the exam, and the same for a retest. The salesperson exam is a different animal, forty five questions, thirty two to pass, ninety minutes, 85 dollars, and no education requirement."},{"t":"p","s":"The practical rules. No walk ins. Cancel at least 2 days ahead or lose your fee. Arrive thirty minutes early, because if you show up after the start time you forfeit. Bring one valid signature bearing photo I D that matches the name you registered under. Your calculator must be silent, battery powered, non printing, and have no alphabet keys, and the measurement converting construction calculators are banned. Bring a plain one."},{"t":"p","s":"And how you find out. If you pass, you only see the word pass. If you fail, you get section scores. There is no post exam review, so you never see which questions you missed."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the rapid fire recap. I am going to say these fast. You are not learning them now, you are checking which ones you already have."},{"t":"p","s":"Article twenty four, Public Act two ninety nine of nineteen eighty. Nine board members, four builders, two M and A, three public. Residential structure, detached one or two family, or a townhouse of three stories or less with separate egress. 600 dollars, licensure exemption, aggregate price, anti splitting rule. 60 hours prelicensure, seven areas, 6 hours each, effective June first two thousand eight, certificate never expires. 1 year to finish licensure after applying. License term, 3 years. Continuing competency, 21 hours per cycle if licensed after two thousand nine, 3 hours per year in the first 6 years, and 3 hours per cycle if licensed before. Keep records 5 years. Twelve M and A crafts. Entity principals at least eighteen. Complaint window, 18 months. Change of control notice, 10 days. Verified complaint certified mail, 30 days. Administrative fine, ten thousand maximum. Criminal and civil fines, five to twenty five thousand. Jail, 1 year first offense, 2 years second, 4 years felony if death or serious injury. No license means no lawsuit and no lien. Exam, one sixty questions, fifty business and law, one ten trade, one sixteen to pass, two hundred forty minutes, closed book, combined since November twenty nineteen. Regulatory and statutory requirements, thirteen questions. Insurance, bonds and liens, eleven. Fees, one ninety five application, one seventeen exam, one eighty five relicensure, eighty five salesperson. And the code on the exam is the twenty fifteen Michigan Residential Code."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the chapter. Thirteen questions sitting right there. If any of the questions I asked you gave you trouble, that is your signal for where to drill, and the drill track for this chapter is the right place to do it. Go get those points."}],"words":3401,"questions":9},{"id":"02_liens-insurance-bonds_lecture","track":"builder","title":"Construction Liens, Insurance, and Bonds","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the heaviest category on the business and law half. Eleven of your fifty questions come from this one chapter. Nothing else comes close."},{"t":"p","s":"It is also the chapter people try to memorize as a pile of loose numbers, and that is why it does not stick. So we are going to do it differently. Almost all of this chapter is one story, the story of a job where somebody does not get paid, told in the order it actually happens. Once the story is in your head the deadlines have somewhere to live."},{"t":"p","s":"Then we will do insurance, which is one skill, given a loss, name the policy. And bonds, which is one idea, a bond protects somebody other than you."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"What a lien actually is, and the ceiling on it."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan's Construction Lien Act is MCL 570.1101 and following. It is Act four ninety seven of nineteen eighty. It gives contractors, subcontractors, suppliers, and laborers a legal claim against the real property they improved when they do not get paid. Not against the owner personally. Against the house."},{"t":"p","s":"Two ceilings sit on top of that claim, and the second one is the whole philosophy of the act."},{"t":"p","s":"First ceiling. Your own lien can never exceed your contract price minus what you have already been paid. Obvious enough."},{"t":"p","s":"Second ceiling. The total of everyone's liens against the owner can never exceed what the owner agreed to pay the general contractor, minus the payments the owner properly made through sworn statements and lien waivers."},{"t":"p","s":"That second one is called the no double payment principle, and it explains almost every piece of paper in this chapter. An owner who pays correctly, with the right paperwork in hand, cannot be made to pay twice for the same work. Every form we are about to walk through exists either to protect the owner from paying twice, or to protect you from not getting paid once."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the story. A job starts, and the paperwork happens in this order."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"The notice of commencement, and the residential versus commercial split."},{"t":"p","s":"On a residential project the owner prepares the notice of commencement, but only when somebody asks for it. A contractor, sub, supplier, or laborer sends a written request by certified mail, and the owner then has 10 days from the postmark to complete the form and return it. If the owner blows those 10 days, the owner can end up paying the expenses the requester runs up digging out the information themselves. If the owner does not live at the site, a copy has to be posted conspicuously at the site."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the trap, and it is one of the most reliable ones in the chapter. The residential notice of commencement is not recorded anywhere. It is provided on request, and posted. Commercial is the opposite. A commercial owner records the notice of commencement with the register of deeds before physical work begins."},{"t":"p","s":"Residential, on request and posted. Commercial, recorded before work starts. One more residential detail worth a point, the form must have a blank notice of furnishing attached to it, easily detachable, so the people downstream can use it."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. On a residential job, who prepares the notice of commencement, when, and where does it get recorded. Say it out loud."},{"t":"a","s":"The owner prepares it, only after a written request by certified mail, within 10 days of the postmark, and it is not recorded at all. If the answer choice has a homeowner recording anything with the register of deeds, that is the commercial rule wearing a residential hat."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"The notice of furnishing, which is how invisible people become visible."},{"t":"p","s":"Think about the owner's problem. The owner hired you. The owner has never met your drywall sub or the lumberyard. The notice of furnishing is how those people raise a hand and say, we are here, we expect to get paid."},{"t":"p","s":"A subcontractor or supplier must serve it within 20 days after first furnishing labor or material. 20 days from the first day on the job, not the last. That direction catches people, because every other lien deadline in this chapter runs from the last day."},{"t":"p","s":"It gets served on the designee named in the notice of commencement and on the general contractor, personally or by certified mail, and certified mail service is complete the moment it is mailed, not when it is received. If no designee is named, you serve the owner."},{"t":"p","s":"Who does not file one. The general contractor. Anyone in a direct contract with the owner is exempt, for the obvious reason that the owner already knows they exist."},{"t":"p","s":"Laborers have their own clock. A laborer's notice for unpaid wages is 30 days, and watch the trigger, it runs 30 days after the wages were due and not paid, not 30 days after starting work. For unpaid fringe benefits or withholdings, the deadline is the fifth day of the second month after the month they went unpaid."},{"t":"p","s":"Now a subtle one the exam does test. What happens if a sub serves the notice late. A late notice does not kill the lien. Everything furnished after the late notice is fully protected. Everything furnished before it is also protected, except to the extent the owner already paid the general contractor for that work while relying on a sworn statement or a lien waiver. So a late notice costs you only the money that already properly went out the door. The exception is the laborer. A laborer who misses the wage notice deadline loses the lien for those wages entirely."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Your tile sub started March first. What is the deadline for the notice of furnishing, and what does the sub lose by filing it 2 months late."},{"t":"a","s":"20 days after first furnishing, so 20 days from March first. Filing late does not destroy the lien, it only gives up the amounts the owner already properly paid the general contractor against a sworn statement or waiver."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"The sworn statement, which is the owner's shield."},{"t":"p","s":"A sworn statement is a notarized list of every sub and supplier you contracted with, plus any unpaid laborers, showing each one's contract price, the amount paid, and the amount still owing."},{"t":"p","s":"You must give one to the owner whenever payment is due, whenever you ask for payment, or whenever the owner demands it. A subcontractor gives one to the general contractor on those same payment triggers, and to the owner on demand."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is why it matters so much. The owner is allowed to rely on it. Relying on your sworn statement, the owner can withhold what is owed to the subs, or pay those subs directly. That is the mechanism that makes the no double payment principle work in practice."},{"t":"p","s":"Which is exactly why the penalty is severe. Giving a false sworn statement with intent to defraud is a crime, on top of civil fraud liability. And if you ever sue to foreclose a lien, your complaint has to show that you provided a sworn statement if one was requested or required."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"The claim of lien, and the 90 day wall."},{"t":"p","s":"You record the claim of lien within 90 days after your last furnishing of labor or material, with the register of deeds for the county where the property sits. Not with a court. The register of deeds."},{"t":"p","s":"Three things about those 90 days."},{"t":"p","s":"They run from your own last day of real contract work. Warranty visits, courtesy callbacks, and punch list touch ups do not restart the clock. Do not let a friendly return trip fool you into thinking you bought more time."},{"t":"p","s":"The deadline is absolute. No extensions, no good cause exception. This is one of the few places in Michigan law with no mercy in it at all."},{"t":"p","s":"And a sub or supplier has to attach proof that the notice of furnishing was served."},{"t":"p","s":"Then one more short clock. Within 15 days after recording, you serve a copy of the recorded claim of lien on the designee, or on the owner if there is no designee, personally or by certified mail."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Two numbers. How long to record the claim of lien, and how long after recording to serve it."},{"t":"a","s":"90 days from last furnishing to record. 15 days from recording to serve."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Turning a lien into money."},{"t":"p","s":"A recorded lien is not money. It is leverage. It clouds the title, so the house cannot be cleanly sold or refinanced. But leverage expires."},{"t":"p","s":"To turn it into money you file a foreclosure action, and you have exactly 1 year from the date the claim of lien was recorded. Miss the year and the lien simply expires, quietly, on its own."},{"t":"p","s":"The suit goes in circuit court for the county where the property is located. It is an equitable action, and when you file it you record a notice of lis pendens with the register of deeds, which is a public flag that litigation is pending on that property."},{"t":"p","s":"And there is a sweetener. The court may award reasonable attorney fees to a prevailing lien claimant. That cuts both ways, it can also award fees against you if your suit was vexatious."},{"t":"p","s":"So the full chain, and you should be able to say it in one breath. 20 days to announce yourself, 90 days to record, 15 days to serve, 1 year to sue."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"The residential overlay, which is where builders get wiped out."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan bolted an extra layer onto the lien act to protect homeowners, and it is brutal if you ignore it."},{"t":"p","s":"On a residential structure, a contractor has no lien at all unless the work was done under a written contract with the owner, and any amendments or change orders are in writing too. That written contract has to contain the statutory statement about licensing, and if a license is required for the work, your license number."},{"t":"p","s":"There is no dollar threshold on this. None. A handshake deal on a 35,000 dollar remodel means no lien. Not a reduced lien, no lien."},{"t":"p","s":"And if an unlicensed person records a lien on residential property anyway, the owner can sue to discharge it, and the unlicensed filer is liable for all the resulting damages, including actual costs and attorney fees. So the bad paperwork does not just cost you the lien, it can cost you money going the other direction."},{"t":"p","s":"The case that settled this is Stokes versus Millen Roofing. An unlicensed residential contractor cannot enforce the contract, cannot recover the value of the work under quantum meruit, which is the fallback claim for the fair value of work done, and has no lien. Nothing. If you sue on work that requires a license, you must plead and prove you were licensed."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Verbal agreement, 40,000 dollar kitchen, you are fully licensed, work is perfect, homeowner refuses to pay. Do you have a lien."},{"t":"a","s":"No. Residential liens require a written contract with the licensing statement and your license number, at any dollar amount. Being licensed is necessary but it is not sufficient. Get it in writing."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"The four lien waivers."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan recognizes exactly four statutory waivers, and the exam will make you tell them apart. It is a simple grid, two questions."},{"t":"p","s":"Question one, how much does it release. A full waiver releases all lien rights. A partial waiver releases only a stated amount."},{"t":"p","s":"Question two, when does it take effect. An unconditional waiver is effective the moment it is signed. A conditional waiver is effective only upon actual payment of the amount shown."},{"t":"p","s":"Full or partial, conditional or unconditional. Four boxes."},{"t":"p","s":"The practical rule falls right out of it. Sign conditional waivers before the check clears. Sign unconditional only after the money is actually yours. An unconditional waiver handed over against a promise is a signature giving up your rights for nothing."},{"t":"p","s":"Three more waiver rules that get tested."},{"t":"p","s":"Advance waivers are void. Nobody can require you, as part of the contract, to waive your lien rights before the work is performed. That clause is against public policy and invalid, so a contract that contains one does not strip your rights."},{"t":"p","s":"Accepting a promissory note does not by itself waive your lien. Taking an I O U is not the same as giving up the claim."},{"t":"p","s":"And on residential jobs, if a homeowner receives a waiver from somebody other than the claimant who signed it, say the general hands over a stack of sub waivers, the homeowner cannot rely on it without verifying with the actual claimant that it is authentic, in writing, by phone, or in person."},{"t":"p","s":"The forms themselves carry the practical warning, do not sign blank or incomplete waiver forms, and keep a copy of everything you sign."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. You are handed a check on Friday for a progress payment. Which waiver do you sign, and when."},{"t":"a","s":"Conditional and partial. Conditional because it only takes effect when the payment actually happens, partial because it releases only that stated amount. Unconditional after the funds clear, if at all."},{"t":"p","s":"Now a trap that is pure memory, so I am just going to hand it to you."},{"t":"p","s":"The Homeowner Construction Lien Recovery Fund is gone. Abolished. Repealed by Public Act one forty seven of twenty ten, effective August twenty third, twenty ten. Builders used to pay into it, and homeowners and subs could recover out of it. It does not exist."},{"t":"p","s":"If an answer choice describes the recovery fund as a current remedy, or as a current license fee, it is wrong. The only true present tense statement about that fund is that it was abolished in twenty ten."},{"t":"p","s":"Now we switch subjects. Insurance."},{"t":"p","s":"There is really only one skill being tested here. Given a loss, name the policy. Three policies, three lanes, and they do not overlap."},{"t":"p","s":"Builders risk is first party property insurance, which means it pays you for damage to your own thing. It covers the structure while it is under construction, plus materials on site, in transit, or in storage, against perils like fire, wind, and theft. Half built house burns down, that is builders risk. Lumber package stolen off the slab, builders risk. Either the owner or the contractor can buy it, the contract should say which, and it ends at completion."},{"t":"p","s":"What it does not do. It does not cover injuries. It does not cover other people's property. And it does not cover your tools. Tools and equipment ride on an inland marine policy, sometimes called an equipment floater. If a question is about a stolen compressor or a saw that walked off, the answer is inland marine, not builders risk."},{"t":"p","s":"Commercial general liability, CGL, is third party coverage. Other people. Bodily injury and property damage to other people, arising out of your operations or your completed operations. A visitor trips over your ladder. Your crew cracks the neighbor's windshield. A connection you soldered lets go a year later and floods the finished kitchen. All CGL."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is the exclusion that matters more than anything else in your real business. CGL never pays to redo your own defective work. It is called the your work exclusion. General liability is not a warranty on your craftsmanship. It pays for the damage your bad work did to other things, not for going back and doing your work again."},{"t":"p","s":"Workers compensation covers your employees, on a no fault basis, and it is generally the employee's exclusive remedy against you. That phrase means the hurt employee takes the comp benefits instead of suing you."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan's thresholds have two prongs and the exam wants both. Coverage is required if you regularly employ three or more employees at one time. Or, and this is the prong people forget, if you employ fewer than three but at least one employee has worked thirty five or more hours per week for thirteen or more weeks during the preceding fifty two weeks. So one steady full time helper triggers mandatory coverage all by himself. Public employers are always covered."},{"t":"p","s":"Then the rule that should change how you run your subs, the statutory principal rule. If a general contractor hires a sub that carries no comp insurance, and that sub's worker gets hurt, the general contractor pays the comp benefits as if it had employed that worker directly. That is MCL 418.171."},{"t":"p","s":"That single rule is why you collect a certificate of insurance from every sub before they set foot on the site, and why you watch the expiration dates on those certificates. An additional insured endorsement goes one step further, extending the sub's liability policy to protect you as well."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Your framing crew drops a bundle of sheathing through the neighbor's garage roof, and one of your guys breaks his wrist doing it. Which policies answer, one for each."},{"t":"a","s":"The neighbor's garage is CGL, other people's property. The broken wrist is workers compensation, your employee. And if the answer choice offers builders risk for either of those, it is wrong, builders risk is for the structure you are building."},{"t":"p","s":"Last topic. Bonds."},{"t":"p","s":"Start with the one sentence that carries most of the bond questions. A bond is not insurance for the contractor. It protects somebody else."},{"t":"p","s":"A surety bond has three parties. The principal, that is you, the contractor whose performance is being guaranteed. The obligee, the party the bond protects, an owner or a government unit. And the surety, the company standing behind the promise."},{"t":"p","s":"The surety expects to lose nothing. If the surety pays out on a claim, you have to pay the surety back. Insurance spreads your risk. A bond guarantees your promise to somebody else, with you still on the hook."},{"t":"p","s":"Four types to know."},{"t":"p","s":"A license or permit bond guarantees you will comply with a law or ordinance. Think municipal permit bonds for right of way or sidewalk work. And note this, the state residential builder license itself does not require a bond."},{"t":"p","s":"A bid bond guarantees that if you win the job, you will actually sign the contract and post the required bonds."},{"t":"p","s":"A performance bond guarantees the job gets completed per the plans and specs. On public work it protects the governmental unit, solely."},{"t":"p","s":"A payment bond guarantees that the subs, suppliers, and laborers get paid, and it protects only those claimants."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan public work has hard numbers. On any public contract exceeding 50,000 dollars, the contractor must furnish both a performance bond and a payment bond before the contract is awarded. Each bond is set by the governmental unit but cannot be less than 25 percent of the contract amount."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, why does the payment bond exist at all. Because you cannot record a construction lien against public property. No lien on the courthouse. No lien on the school. The entire lien machinery we just spent twenty minutes on does not apply. So the unpaid sub on public work has one remedy, a claim on the payment bond, and the bond is standing in for the lien."},{"t":"p","s":"That claim has its own clock. A claimant with no direct contract with the principal contractor must serve written notice on the principal contractor within 30 days after first furnishing. And must give written notice of the claim to both the principal contractor and the governmental unit within 90 days after last furnishing. All by certified mail. Suit on the bond is allowed once you have gone unpaid for 90 days after your last furnishing."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Who does a performance bond protect, and could you lien a public school if you went unpaid."},{"t":"a","s":"The performance bond protects the obligee, on public work the governmental unit, never you. And no, you can never lien public property. The payment bond is the substitute."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the rapid fire recap. Say these out loud with me. If one of them is a blank, that is tonight's drilling."},{"t":"p","s":"Construction Lien Act, MCL 570.1101 and following. Residential notice of commencement, returned within 10 days of the request, posted if the owner lives elsewhere, never recorded. Commercial notice of commencement, recorded before work begins. Notice of furnishing for subs and suppliers, 20 days from first furnishing, served on the designee and the general contractor. Laborer wage notice, 30 days from wages due but unpaid. Claim of lien, recorded with the register of deeds within 90 days of last furnishing, absolute deadline, warranty work does not count. Serve the recorded lien within 15 days. Foreclosure, circuit court, within 1 year of recording, with a lis pendens, attorney fees possible for the prevailing claimant. Residential lien requires a written contract with the license number, at any dollar amount. Four waivers, full and partial, conditional and unconditional. Conditional means effective only on actual payment. Advance waivers void. Recovery fund abolished, twenty ten. Workers comp, three employees at one time, or one employee at 35 hours for thirteen weeks. Uninsured sub, the general pays under MCL 418.171. Builders risk covers the building, CGL covers other people, comp covers your crew, inland marine covers your tools. Public work over 50,000 dollars, performance bond and payment bond, each at least 25 percent. Performance protects the government, payment protects the subs. Payment bond notices, 30 days from first furnishing, 90 days from last. No liens on public property, ever."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the whole domain. Eleven questions, and every number they can ask you was in there. The five you have to own cold are twenty, thirty, ninety, fifteen, and 1 year. Run the drill track until those roll off without thinking, and this becomes the easiest eleven points on the exam."}],"words":3608,"questions":7},{"id":"03_contracts-hifa_lecture","track":"builder","title":"Contracts, HIFA, Risk and Warranty","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This chapter is about seven questions out of your fifty business and law questions, and it is the most trap heavy territory on the whole exam. Not because the law is hard. Because Michigan has four different statutes here that each do a completely different job, and almost every wrong answer on this part of the test is built by taking one statute's rule and hanging it on a different statute's name."},{"t":"p","s":"So the goal of this chapter is not memorizing. It is separation. If you can keep these four laws in four separate boxes, the traps stop working on you."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"The map. Four laws, four jobs."},{"t":"p","s":"Law one is MCL 570.1114, inside the Construction Lien Act. Its job is your lien. It says a contractor has no construction lien on a residential structure unless the work was done under a written contract, and any amendments or additions to that contract are also in writing. The contract must contain a statement that residential builders, electricians, plumbers, and mechanical contractors are required to be licensed, and if your work requires a license, it must say you are licensed and include your license number. There is no dollar threshold. A 500 dollar handyman job and a 200,000 dollar addition are treated exactly alike. And a verbal change order means the added scope carries no lien protection."},{"t":"p","s":"Law two is MCL 339.2412, in the Occupational Code. Its job is the courthouse door. An unlicensed builder cannot sue to collect compensation for residential work. You can do the work. You just cannot use the courts to get paid for it."},{"t":"p","s":"Law three is MCL 339.2403. Its job is licensure, and this is where the famous 600 dollar number lives. Work where labor and materials combined come to under 600 dollars is exempt from the licensure requirement."},{"t":"p","s":"Hear that clearly, because it is the single most swapped fact in this chapter. 600 dollars is a licensure exemption and nothing else. It is not a written contract trigger. If an answer choice tells you that contracts over 600 dollars must be in writing under HIFA, that answer is wrong twice, wrong about the number's job and wrong about which statute it lives in."},{"t":"p","s":"Law four is HIFA, the Home Improvement Finance Act, Act three thirty two of nineteen sixty five, MCL 445.1101 and following. Its job is financing. Not contracts generally. Financing."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Which of those four laws decides whether your contract has to be in writing to protect your lien, and which one owns the 600 dollar number. Say it out loud."},{"t":"a","s":"570 point 1114, the lien act, owns the written contract rule, at any dollar amount. 339 point 2403 owns the 600 dollars, and it is only about needing a license."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"When HIFA actually applies, which is narrower than people think."},{"t":"p","s":"HIFA applies only when the homeowner is paying on credit through you. An installment contract or a charge agreement with the contractor. And only when the cash price is more than 300 dollars. Exactly three hundred does not count, it has to exceed three hundred."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the exclusions, because they are the easier way to spot a HIFA question. New home construction is completely excluded. Commercial property is excluded. A loan the homeowner gets directly from their own bank is excluded, because then you are not the one extending the credit. And free standing appliances, a stove or a refrigerator, are excluded."},{"t":"p","s":"So the picture is this. Existing home, improvement work, and you are carrying the paper. That is HIFA. Cash, or the homeowner's own bank loan, and HIFA never enters the room."},{"t":"p","s":"And one trap to bury. There is no section 445 point eleven oh eight. It does not exist. Any answer built on it is invented."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. You are financing a 12,000 dollar roof for a homeowner on an installment contract, on a house they have lived in for 10 years. HIFA, yes or no. Then change one fact, the homeowner takes a loan from their credit union and pays you in full. HIFA now."},{"t":"a","s":"Yes to the first, you are extending the credit on an existing home above 300 dollars. No to the second, their own bank loan is excluded."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"What HIFA makes you put on the paper."},{"t":"p","s":"When HIFA applies, it is fussy, and the exam tests the fussiness."},{"t":"p","s":"The contract must be dated and in writing, with the printed portion in at least eight point type. The words home improvement installment contract must appear in at least ten point bold, either at the top or directly above the buyer's signature. Eight point body, ten point bold heading."},{"t":"p","s":"There is a required notice to the buyer in bold type carrying four messages. Do not sign before you read it. You are entitled to a completely filled in copy. You may pay off early and get a partial refund of the finance charge. And you may rescind the contract."},{"t":"p","s":"The money has to be itemized, not lumped. Cash price. Down payment. Unpaid cash balance. Insurance premiums if any. Official fees. Principal amount financed. The finance charge stated in dollars. The time balance, the number and amount of the installments, and the time sale price."},{"t":"p","s":"And if one installment is a balloon, meaning substantially bigger than the others, the contract needs a bold legend saying this contract is not payable in installments of equal amounts, with that amount and its due date spelled out."},{"t":"p","s":"Then the rule that is worth a question all by itself. You must hand the buyer a completed copy of the contract at the time it is signed. Until that completed copy is delivered, the buyer is not obligated to pay you anything. That is section twelve oh four. Not a technicality, a suspension of their duty to pay."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"The two rescission clocks, which is the biggest trap in the chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Slow down here, because these two get swapped constantly."},{"t":"p","s":"Under HIFA, the buyer may rescind until five P M on the business day following the date of the contract. One business day, ending at five in the afternoon."},{"t":"p","s":"That is not the 3 day right you have heard about your whole career. The 3 day right lives in a different statute entirely, the Home Solicitation Sales Act, and it runs to midnight of the third business day."},{"t":"p","s":"Say both, and attach each one to its statute. HIFA, five P M, next business day, because it is about financing. Home solicitation, midnight, third business day, because it is about being sold to in your own kitchen."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is the part that separates the people who understand it from the people who memorized it. If a question describes a door to door sale with contractor financing, the buyer gets both rights. They are different statutes doing different jobs, so they stack."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Storm chaser signs a homeowner at the kitchen table on Monday, with financing through the contractor. What cancellation rights does that homeowner have."},{"t":"a","s":"Both. The HIFA right until five P M on the next business day, and the home solicitation right until midnight of the third business day. Whichever is more generous is not the point, the buyer holds both."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"What HIFA forbids you from putting in the contract."},{"t":"p","s":"Section twelve oh six bans a list of provisions outright."},{"t":"p","s":"You cannot make the buyer waive claims or defenses, either against you or against a finance company that buys the paper from you. You cannot give the holder power to accelerate the balance arbitrarily when the buyer is not in default. You cannot make the buyer waive the right to sue over illegal collection tactics. You cannot authorize breaking into the property or breaching the peace to repossess."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is the one builders get wrong, because it contradicts what is true everywhere else. You cannot put a liquidated damages clause in a HIFA contract that pays you for the buyer's cancellation or rescission."},{"t":"p","s":"Understand the contrast, because the exam will test the contrast. In an ordinary construction contract a reasonable liquidated damages clause is enforceable, per day delay damages for example, as long as it is a genuine pre estimate of the harm and not a penalty. In a HIFA installment contract, liquidated damages for cancellation are flatly prohibited. The offending clause is void, though the rest of the contract survives."},{"t":"p","s":"On top of all that, section fourteen oh nine says no act, agreement, or statement of the buyer is a valid waiver of any protection under the act. The buyer cannot sign away HIFA rights even if they want to. So there is no clever clause that gets you out of this."},{"t":"p","s":"Willfully violating HIFA is a misdemeanor. Up to 90 days in jail, a fine up to 500 dollars, or both, for a first offense. Repeat offenses go up to a year. And paying somebody a reward or a referral fee to procure a home improvement finance contract is prohibited too."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"The Home Solicitation Sales Act, and the emergency waiver that matters in your real business."},{"t":"p","s":"The Home Solicitation Sales Act is Act two twenty seven of nineteen seventy one, MCL 445.111 through one seventeen. It applies to seller solicited sales at the buyer's home. The classic picture is the storm chaser at the kitchen table."},{"t":"p","s":"The buyer can cancel until midnight of the third business day after signing. And note how cancellation counts. A mailed cancellation is effective when it is dropped in the mailbox, properly addressed with postage paid. Not when you receive it. So a cancellation can be effective days before you know it happened."},{"t":"p","s":"The contract must carry the cancellation statement right next to the signature line, plus a notice of cancellation form in duplicate. And you cannot send a courier to collect the buyer's payment at the house before the 3 days run out."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the emergency exception, and this one you should learn for work, not just for the exam. To cut off the buyer's cancellation right in an emergency, three things must all be true."},{"t":"p","s":"One, the buyer asked for the goods or services without delay because of an emergency. Two, you in good faith made a substantial beginning of performance before any cancellation notice. Three, and this is the one to memorize, the buyer gave you a separate, dated, signed personal statement, in the buyer's own handwriting, describing the emergency and expressly waiving the 3 day right."},{"t":"p","s":"Handwriting. Not typed. Not a checkbox on your form. Their own hand, their own words. Michigan requires it and the federal cooling off rule requires it too."},{"t":"p","s":"That means on a burst pipe or a tree through the roof, you get that handwritten waiver before you set a single fan, or you are working at your own risk with a customer who can walk for three business days."},{"t":"p","s":"If the buyer does cancel in time, you return every payment, any trade in, and any signed note within ten business days of receiving the notice. Business days, not calendar days. The buyer makes any delivered goods available at the house, and if you do not pick them up within 20 days, the buyer keeps them for free."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Water loss at nine at night, homeowner wants you starting immediately. What do you need from them, in what form, and by when."},{"t":"a","s":"A separate, dated, signed statement in their own handwriting describing the emergency and waiving the 3 day right, before you begin, along with their request for immediate service and your good faith substantial beginning of performance."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"Contract fundamentals, because PSI tests the national material too."},{"t":"p","s":"A valid contract needs five elements. Offer, acceptance, consideration, capacity, and legality. Consideration means each side gives something of value. If a party lacks capacity, a minor for instance, the contract is voidable by that party. An illegal contract is simply void from the start."},{"t":"p","s":"Then know your contract types by one question, who eats the cost risk."},{"t":"p","s":"Fixed price, also called lump sum. You carry the risk. Overrun the budget and you eat it, beat the budget and you keep the savings."},{"t":"p","s":"Cost plus. The owner carries the risk, paying your actual costs plus a fee."},{"t":"p","s":"Time and materials. The owner again, paying hourly labor and materials, and it is used where scope is genuinely uncertain."},{"t":"p","s":"Unit price. Shared. You carry the unit cost risk, the owner carries the quantity risk."},{"t":"p","s":"Change orders get written and signed by both parties, describing the scope change and the price or time adjustment. And in Michigan that is not merely good practice. Remember law one from the top of this chapter, written amendments are a condition of your lien rights."},{"t":"p","s":"Two completions to keep apart. Substantial completion means the owner can use the work for its intended purpose. It triggers release of retainage, which is typically five to ten percent held back from progress payments, and it starts the warranty clock. Final completion means every punch list item is finished."},{"t":"p","s":"On breach, a material breach excuses the other party's performance. Remedies include compensatory damages, rescission, quantum meruit for the value of work performed, and rarely specific performance, meaning a court order to actually do the job. And the injured party always has a duty to mitigate, which means you cannot sit back and run up damages you could reasonably have avoided."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"The clocks, and the difference between a limitation and a repose."},{"t":"p","s":"Breach of contract in Michigan is 6 years, under MCL 600.5807, subsection nine."},{"t":"p","s":"Negligence causing injury to a person or property is 3 years, under MCL 600.5805, subsection two."},{"t":"p","s":"Then the big one, the statute of repose, MCL 600.5839. For claims arising from a defective or unsafe improvement to real property, against a contractor, architect, or engineer, the action must be brought within 6 years from occupancy, use, or acceptance of the completed improvement."},{"t":"p","s":"Read that again. From occupancy. Not from discovery. A defect found in year seven is barred even if nobody on earth could have discovered it earlier. That is the entire point of a repose, it protects builders from claims that surface forever."},{"t":"p","s":"There is one exception, gross negligence. Then the claimant gets 1 year after the defect is discovered or should have been discovered, but never more than 10 years after occupancy. So the 10 year number is only the ceiling on the gross negligence exception. It is not the general rule, and an answer choice that offers 10 years as the ordinary period is wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the one line version of the distinction. A limitation runs from the breach or the injury. A repose runs from a fixed event and kills the claim regardless of discovery."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. A homeowner discovers a hidden structural defect 7 years after moving in. Ordinary negligence by the builder. What is the outcome."},{"t":"a","s":"Barred. 6 years from occupancy under the statute of repose, and discovery does not extend it. Only gross negligence opens the extra year, capped at 10 years from occupancy."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Warranties, in three layers."},{"t":"p","s":"Express warranties are the written promises in your contract, and they are enforceable exactly as written, no more and no less."},{"t":"p","s":"The implied warranty of habitability says a new home sold by a builder vendor must be fit for human habitation. Michigan recognizes it, but Michigan courts may enforce a disclaimer of it, so it is not untouchable."},{"t":"p","s":"The implied warranty of workmanship says the work must meet the standard of a reasonably skilled tradesperson, and it exists whether or not your contract mentions it. You cannot get out of it by silence."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the classic scenario they build a question from. Bad flashing leaks 3 years in, and your 1 year express warranty expired long ago. What is the homeowner's best theory. The implied warranty of workmanship. Still alive, and still inside the 6 year windows."},{"t":"p","s":"Last, the insurance and liability round out."},{"t":"p","s":"Commercial general liability covers third party bodily injury, third party property damage, and personal and advertising injury. Never your own work product, and never your own property."},{"t":"p","s":"Builders risk covers the structure while it is being built, plus materials on site and in transit, and it ends at occupancy."},{"t":"p","s":"Errors and omissions covers design mistakes, which CGL excludes. If a question involves a bad detail on a drawing rather than bad installation, that is E and O."},{"t":"p","s":"Umbrella coverage adds limits and may broaden coverage. Excess only adds limits. That is the whole difference and it is a fair exam question."},{"t":"p","s":"A certificate of insurance is a snapshot. It proves coverage existed on the day it was issued and nothing more, which is why you re collect them. An additional insured endorsement puts the general contractor on the sub's policy. A waiver of subrogation stops an insurer from chasing the named party after it pays a claim."},{"t":"p","s":"And the liability theories. Negligence needs four things, duty, breach, causation, and damages. Strict liability applies to defective products with no fault required. Vicarious liability means the general contractor can answer for a sub's actions on the site."},{"t":"p","s":"Rapid fire recap. Say these out loud with me."},{"t":"p","s":"HIFA trigger, cash price over 300 dollars, on installment credit, existing homes only. HIFA rescission, five P M, next business day. HIFA type sizes, eight point body, ten point bold headings. HIFA copy rule, no completed copy, no duty to pay. HIFA penalty, misdemeanor, 90 days, 500 dollars. Liquidated damages for cancellation in a HIFA contract, prohibited and void. Home solicitation cancellation, midnight of the third business day. Emergency waiver, separate, dated, signed, in the buyer's own handwriting. Refund after cancellation, ten business days. Goods pickup, 20 days or the buyer keeps them. Licensure exemption, under 600 dollars, labor plus materials. Unlicensed builder, cannot sue to collect. Lien rights, written contract, license number, written change orders, any dollar amount. Contract statute of limitations, 6 years. Negligence, 3 years. Statute of repose, 6 years from occupancy, use, or acceptance. Gross negligence, one extra year after discovery, capped at ten. Retainage, five to ten percent, released at substantial completion. Five contract elements, offer, acceptance, consideration, capacity, legality. Four negligence elements, duty, breach, causation, damages."},{"t":"p","s":"If those come out cold, you own all seven contract questions. And if only one thing from this chapter survives the week, make it this, five P M next business day is HIFA financing, midnight of the third business day is the kitchen table sale. Keep driving."}],"words":3080,"questions":5},{"id":"04_business-management_lecture","track":"builder","title":"Business Management, Estimating, Finance, Personnel and Workers Comp","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the biggest block on the business and law half of your exam. Out of fifty business and law questions, roughly nineteen come from this one chapter. Six on project management and administration, six on accounting and finance, four on types of business organization, two on personnel, and one on estimating."},{"t":"p","s":"Nineteen out of fifty. More than a third of that section lives here. If you learn nothing else properly, learn this chapter properly."},{"t":"p","s":"It is also the chapter with actual arithmetic in it, and I am going to slow all the way down on the one calculation I can nearly promise you will see, which is markup versus margin. Everything else here is comparison. Two things that look alike, and knowing which is which."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"The five business structures, compared on two axes."},{"t":"p","s":"Only two questions matter for each structure. Who can they come after if things go wrong, and how is it taxed."},{"t":"p","s":"A sole proprietorship needs no state filing at all. It is the simplest thing there is, and the price of that simplicity is unlimited personal liability. The house, the truck, the savings account, all exposed. All profit lands on your personal return and every dollar gets hit with self employment tax."},{"t":"p","s":"A general partnership is the same idea with two or more people, and it is worse than most people realize. Each partner has unlimited joint and several liability. That phrase means a creditor can collect the entire partnership debt from any one partner, including a debt the other partner ran up without you. One bad partner can take your house."},{"t":"p","s":"The LLC fixes the liability problem. You file Articles of Organization with LARA, and as long as you maintain the veil, your personal assets are protected. Maintaining the veil means separate bank accounts, no commingling of money, adequate capitalization, and contracts signed in the company name. Taxes pass through to you by default."},{"t":"p","s":"Then the S corporation, and here is the trap the exam builds on it. S corp status is not a Michigan filing. It is a federal election you make with the IRS on form twenty five fifty three. You do not go to LARA to become an S corp. It gives you pass through taxation, but an owner who works in the business must take a reasonable W two salary."},{"t":"p","s":"And the C corporation, which is the double taxation entity. The corporation pays tax on its profits, then the shareholders pay tax again on the dividends. Small builders almost never want it."},{"t":"p","s":"One housekeeping number that gets tested. The LLC annual statement is due to LARA by February fifteenth every year, and the fee is 25 dollars. Miss it for about 2 years and the company falls to not in good standing status. But note the distinction, that status is restorable. The company does not automatically dissolve."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Which structure is created by a federal election rather than a Michigan filing, and what date does an LLC owe its annual statement."},{"t":"a","s":"The S corporation, by election with the IRS on form twenty five fifty three. And February fifteenth, 25 dollars."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"Cash versus accrual, and the two financial statements."},{"t":"p","s":"Cash basis records income when the money actually arrives and expenses when they are actually paid. Accrual records income when it is earned, meaning when you bill it, and expenses when they are incurred."},{"t":"p","s":"For real job costing you need accrual, because it matches the revenue to the cost that produced it. Cash basis will tell you that you had a great March because a February job finally paid."},{"t":"p","s":"For long term contracts, know the two revenue methods by name. Percentage of completion recognizes revenue as the work progresses. Completed contract recognizes it all at the end."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the two statements, and the difference is time."},{"t":"p","s":"The balance sheet is a snapshot at a single point in time. It is built on the accounting equation, assets equal liabilities plus owner's equity. Always, in every question, no exceptions."},{"t":"p","s":"The income statement, the profit and loss, covers a period of time. Revenue minus direct job costs gives gross profit. Gross profit minus overhead gives net profit."},{"t":"p","s":"So if a question asks where the year's profit shows up, the answer is the income statement, not the balance sheet. Snapshot versus period. That is the whole distinction."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Markup versus margin, which is the calculation on this exam."},{"t":"p","s":"Stay with me here, because this is the one people get wrong in business as well as on the test."},{"t":"p","s":"Markup is a percentage of your cost. Margin is a percentage of the selling price. They are never the same number, and margin is always the smaller of the two."},{"t":"p","s":"Work it with me. A job costs 100,000 dollars. You add a 25 percent markup, so you sell it for one hundred twenty five thousand. Your profit is 25,000 dollars."},{"t":"p","s":"Now ask the margin question. Twenty five thousand of profit out of the one hundred twenty five thousand you sold it for. That is 20 percent."},{"t":"p","s":"Same job, same money, two different numbers. A 25 percent markup is a 20 percent margin."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the famous ten and ten. 10 percent overhead plus 10 percent profit is a 20 percent markup, and it produces a margin of only 16.67 percent. That gap is where contractors quietly go broke, thinking they made twenty and actually making sixteen and a half."},{"t":"p","s":"Two formulas to carry. Margin equals markup divided by one plus markup. And to hit a target margin, divide your cost by one minus the margin."},{"t":"p","s":"Watch the second one, because the trap is built right into it. To earn a 20 percent margin on an 80,000 dollar job, you divide eighty thousand by point eight, which prices it at one hundred thousand. If you instead multiply by one point two you get ninety six thousand, and you just fell into the trap. Divide by one minus the margin. Do not multiply."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Job costs sixty thousand. You want a 25 percent margin. What is the price."},{"t":"a","s":"Sixty thousand divided by point seven five, which is 80,000 dollars. If you multiplied by one point two five you got seventy five thousand, which is a 25 percent markup and only a 20 percent margin."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Two more money formulas, then cash flow."},{"t":"p","s":"Overhead rate is total annual overhead divided by total annual direct costs. One hundred twenty six thousand of overhead against eight hundred forty thousand of direct cost is 15 percent."},{"t":"p","s":"Break even revenue is fixed overhead divided by gross margin percentage. One hundred fifty thousand of overhead at a 30 percent margin means you need 500,000 dollars of revenue just to get to zero."},{"t":"p","s":"Now cash flow, and this is the part that kills real companies. Contractors pay for labor and materials before the owner pays them. That timing gap is the business."},{"t":"p","s":"Accounts receivable is money owed to you. Accounts payable is money you owe. A company with big receivables and no cash is profitable on paper and dying in real life. That sentence is the answer to more than one exam question and to a lot of contractor bankruptcies."},{"t":"p","s":"Underbilling means you have done more work than you have billed, so you are financing the owner's project out of your own pocket. Overbilling is the reverse. Bill promptly and keep your billings matched to the work in place."},{"t":"p","s":"Billing runs off a schedule of values, which is the contract price broken into line items. The industry forms are A I A G seven zero two, the payment application summary, and G seven zero three, the line by line continuation sheet. Seven zero two summarizes, seven zero three itemizes."},{"t":"p","s":"Retainage is the five to ten percent the owner withholds from each draw until substantial or final completion. Note this for Michigan, there is no statutory cap on retainage for private residential work."},{"t":"p","s":"And a lien safe draw schedule ties each draw to a completed milestone, with each check exchanged for a conditional lien waiver. Conditional, because it only takes effect when the payment actually clears. Never sign an unconditional waiver before the money is in your bank, because an unconditional waiver is effective immediately whether you got paid or not."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Estimating math, which is free money on this exam."},{"t":"p","s":"Board feet. Nominal thickness times nominal width times length in feet, divided by twelve. So a two by six that is 10 feet long is two times six times ten over twelve, which is ten board feet."},{"t":"p","s":"Concrete. Length times width times thickness, all in feet, gives you cubic feet. Divide by twenty seven for cubic yards, because a cubic yard is three by three by three."},{"t":"p","s":"Work one. A 4 inch slab, 24 feet by 36 feet. 4 inches is one third of a foot. Twenty four times thirty six times one third is two hundred eighty eight cubic feet. Divide by twenty seven and you get about ten point seven yards. Then you order twelve, because of waste."},{"t":"p","s":"A roofing square is 100 square feet. That one is just memory."},{"t":"p","s":"Waste factors. Framing lumber ten to fifteen percent. Drywall ten to twelve. Shingles ten to fifteen. Concrete five to ten. And engineered trusses essentially zero, because they are built to order."},{"t":"p","s":"Contingency is a risk reserve, five to ten percent on new construction and ten to twenty percent on remodels. And here is the tested part, contingency goes into the estimate before overhead and profit, because it is not profit. It is money set aside for what you cannot see yet."},{"t":"p","s":"Contract types by risk, same idea as the contracts chapter. Lump sum puts the cost risk on you and needs complete plans. Cost plus puts the risk on the owner and fits unknown scope, ideally with a not to exceed cap. Time and materials is hourly plus materials for small or undefined work. Unit price fits work where quantities are uncertain, like excavation."},{"t":"p","s":"And an allowance is a plug number for something not yet selected, a tile budget before anyone picked tile. The difference gets settled by written change order. In Michigan, every change order is in writing, and you know why by now, your lien rights depend on it."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. 4 inch slab, twenty by 30 feet. How many cubic yards, before waste."},{"t":"a","s":"Twenty times thirty times one third is two hundred cubic feet. Divided by twenty seven is about seven point four yards."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Project management, and the critical path."},{"t":"p","s":"The critical path is the longest chain of dependent tasks through the job, and it sets the minimum project duration. Tasks on the critical path have zero float."},{"t":"p","s":"Float, sometimes called slack, is how long a task can slip without moving the completion date. So a task with float can run late and hurt nobody. A task on the critical path runs late and the whole job moves."},{"t":"p","s":"That is why the critical path is the longest chain and not the shortest. It is the chain you cannot compress by working around it."},{"t":"p","s":"A Gantt chart is the standard bar chart picture of a schedule, but note its limitation, it does not inherently show dependencies. Network diagrams do."},{"t":"p","s":"Substantial completion means the owner can use the work for its intended purpose. It triggers the punch list, the warranty clock, and release of most retainage. Final completion means everything is done, punch list included."},{"t":"p","s":"And the three bonds, one line each. The bid bond guarantees you honor your bid. The performance bond guarantees you finish the work. The payment bond guarantees your subs and suppliers get paid. When the exam asks which bond protects subcontractors, it is the payment bond, every time."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"Employee or independent contractor, and the payroll numbers."},{"t":"p","s":"Whether somebody is your employee is decided by the IRS common law test, which looks at behavioral control, financial control, and the relationship of the parties, built on the twenty factors of revenue ruling eighty seven forty one. Michigan's workers comp statute adopted that exact twenty factor test for anything on or after January first, twenty thirteen."},{"t":"p","s":"And one shortcut worth remembering. If you withhold federal income tax for somebody, that alone is prima facie evidence they are your employee. You have already treated them like one."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the payroll numbers, and they come in pairs."},{"t":"p","s":"FICA is 6.2 percent social security plus 1.45 percent medicare, which is 7.65 percent. The employer pays that, and the employee pays the same. Seven point six five each side."},{"t":"p","s":"Federal unemployment, F U T A, is 6 percent of the first 7000 dollars of wages, but with the state credit it nets down to point six percent."},{"t":"p","s":"Withheld taxes are trust fund taxes. That name matters, because owners are personally liable if they do not get remitted. The corporate veil does not protect you there."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan unemployment through the UIA. The statutory default taxable wage base is 9500 dollars, but it drops to 9000 dollars for qualified employers, meaning those with all reports filed and no unpaid balance, while the trust fund stays healthy. Nine thousand is the base that actually applies in twenty twenty five and twenty twenty six. Know both numbers, because the exam can ask for either."},{"t":"p","s":"New employers pay 2.7 percent, except construction. A new construction employer pays the average rate for the construction industry, which is several times higher. That exception is exactly the kind of thing this exam loves, so expect to see two point seven offered as a wrong answer for a builder."},{"t":"p","s":"One genuine change for twenty twenty six. The ten ninety nine reporting threshold rose from 600 dollars to 2000 dollars for payments made this year. Older study materials still say six hundred, so trust this over an old book."},{"t":"p","s":"Overtime under the Fair Labor Standards Act is one and a half times the regular rate for hours over forty in a workweek. And note the negative, there is no federal daily overtime. 12 hours on Monday is not overtime by itself."},{"t":"p","s":"MIOSHA recordkeeping. Employers with eleven or more employees keep the form three hundred injury log, post the annual summary from February first to April thirtieth, and keep the records 5 years. Report a fatality within 8 hours. Report an in patient hospitalization, an amputation, or the loss of an eye within 24 hours."},{"t":"p","s":"8 hours for a death, twenty four for the other three."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Give me the FICA rate on each side, and the two MIOSHA reporting clocks."},{"t":"a","s":"7.65 percent from the employer and seven point six five from the employee. Fatality within 8 hours, and hospitalization, amputation, or loss of an eye within 24 hours."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan workers compensation."},{"t":"p","s":"The law is the Workers Disability Compensation Act, and here is a detail people miss, it is administered by the Workers Disability Compensation Agency under the Department of Labor and Economic Opportunity. Not LARA. Different agency from the one that licenses you."},{"t":"p","s":"It is a no fault system and it is the exclusive remedy. The injured worker gets benefits, medical, wage loss, vocational rehabilitation, and death benefits, but cannot sue you."},{"t":"p","s":"Coverage is required if you hit either of two triggers, and either one alone is enough."},{"t":"p","s":"Trigger one, you regularly employ three or more employees at one time, part timers included."},{"t":"p","s":"Trigger two, even with fewer than three, if just one employee has worked thirty five or more hours a week for thirteen weeks or longer during the preceding fifty two weeks."},{"t":"p","s":"A sole proprietor with no employees is exempt but may opt in."},{"t":"p","s":"Owners can be excluded by filing form W C three thirty seven. That covers a corporate officer who owns at least 10 percent of a corporation with ten or fewer stockholders, an LLC member manager who owns at least 10 percent of an LLC with ten or fewer members, or a partner. 10 percent ownership, ten or fewer owners."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the general contractor trap, and you met its cousin in the lien chapter. If your sub has no workers comp and no valid exclusion on file, and his worker gets hurt on your site, the claim lands on you. So you collect the comp certificate or the exclusion form before anybody swings a hammer."},{"t":"p","s":"Penalties for going bare are serious. It is a misdemeanor, up to 1000 dollars and up to 6 months, and every single day is a separate offense. On top of that the state can get a court injunction shutting you down, plus a civil fine of up to 1000 dollars per day, looking back 3 years."},{"t":"p","s":"And the worst part is not the fine. An uninsured employer loses the exclusive remedy shield, which means the injured worker can sue you in civil court, with no cap."},{"t":"p","s":"One last insurance term. The experience modification rate, the E M R. One point zero is average. Below one means a good safety record and cheaper premiums, above one means the opposite."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. You have two employees. One of them works 40 hours a week and has for the last 6 months. Do you need workers comp coverage."},{"t":"a","s":"Yes. You fail trigger one with only two employees, but trigger two catches you, one employee at thirty five or more hours a week for thirteen weeks or more in the preceding fifty two. Either trigger alone requires coverage."},{"t":"p","s":"One more number before the recap, because it shows up and it is easy. Michigan sales tax is 6 percent. The contractor pays it on materials. Labor is exempt, and you never charge sales tax on the contract price."},{"t":"p","s":"Rapid fire recap. Say these with me."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan sales tax, 6 percent, contractor pays on materials, labor exempt, never charged on the contract price. LLC annual statement, February fifteenth, 25 dollars. Markup to margin, 20 percent markup is sixteen point seven margin, twenty five markup is twenty margin, fifty markup is thirty three point three margin. Board feet, thickness times width times length over twelve. Concrete, cubic feet divided by twenty seven. Roofing square, 100 square feet. Contingency, five to ten new, ten to twenty remodel, and it goes in before profit. Retainage, five to ten percent. Workers comp triggers, three employees at one time, or one employee at 35 hours for thirteen weeks in the last fifty two. Exclusion, form W C three thirty seven, ten or fewer owners, 10 percent or more ownership. Penalty, a thousand dollars, 6 months, each day separate, plus a thousand a day civil. Independent contractor test, IRS twenty factors, adopted in Michigan since January first twenty thirteen. FICA, seven point six five each side. F U T A, point six percent net on the first seven thousand. UIA wage base, nine thousand five hundred statutory default, nine thousand for qualified employers right now. New construction employers pay the industry average, not two point seven. Ten ninety nine threshold, 2000 dollars starting this year. Overtime, time and a half over forty per week, no federal daily overtime. MIOSHA, three hundred log at eleven employees, fatality in 8 hours, hospitalization, amputation, or eye in twenty four. Payment bond pays the subs. Conditional waiver until the check clears. Assets equal liabilities plus equity."},{"t":"p","s":"That is nineteen questions worth of material. The two things to drill tonight are the markup and margin arithmetic, because it is the only real math in the section, and the workers comp triggers, because they show up in two different chapters. Run the deck until the numbers come without thinking."}],"words":3254,"questions":5},{"id":"05_miosha-safety_lecture","track":"builder","title":"MIOSHA Construction Safety","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"MIOSHA safety is worth about ten of the one hundred ten trade questions on your exam, and it is the easiest ten on the test. Here is why. Safety questions are number questions. Heights, depths, hours, dollars. There is almost no judgment in them."},{"t":"p","s":"But there is a catch, and it is the reason people lose these easy points. Several of these numbers come in pairs that look interchangeable and are not. Six and ten. Four and five. Twenty and ten. Eight and twenty four. The exam writes its wrong answers by handing you the other number in the pair."},{"t":"p","s":"So the way I am going to teach this is by the pairs. Learn them as pairs and the traps disappear."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"Who MIOSHA is, and the two agency traps."},{"t":"p","s":"MIOSHA is the Michigan Occupational Safety and Health Administration, created by the MIOSHA Act, Act one fifty four of nineteen seventy four. Michigan runs its own state plan, which by federal law has to be at least as effective as federal OSHA."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the discriminator the exam likes. MIOSHA covers public sector employees, meaning state, county, and city workers. Federal OSHA does not. So if a question asks who MIOSHA protects that federal OSHA does not, the answer is public employees."},{"t":"p","s":"And the second trap, who runs it. The Department of Labor and Economic Opportunity, LEO. Not LARA. It moved from LARA to LEO by executive order in twenty nineteen. On construction sites the enforcement arm is the Construction Safety and Health Division."},{"t":"p","s":"You have now met LEO twice, because it also runs workers compensation. LARA licenses you. LEO regulates how you work and what happens when somebody gets hurt. If an answer choice says LARA administers MIOSHA, that is the trap."},{"t":"p","s":"Then the rule that catches everything the specific standards miss, the General Duty Clause. Every employer must furnish a workplace free from recognized hazards that are causing or likely to cause death or serious physical harm. That is MCL 408.1011."},{"t":"p","s":"The key to using it is knowing when it applies. It applies when no specific standard covers the hazard. It is the catch all, which is why an unusual hazard with no rule of its own still gets you cited."},{"t":"p","s":"And under the construction general rules, every construction employer must have an accident prevention program. Not optional, and not waived because you run a small crew."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Who administers MIOSHA, and who does MIOSHA cover that federal OSHA does not."},{"t":"a","s":"The Department of Labor and Economic Opportunity, LEO, not LARA. And public sector employees."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"The first pair, six and ten. This is the most tested trap in the domain."},{"t":"p","s":"Fall protection is Part forty five, and the general trigger is 6 feet. If a work area is 6 feet or more above a lower level, workers need fall protection."},{"t":"p","s":"Three conventional systems. Guardrails. Safety nets. Or a personal fall arrest system, meaning harness, lanyard, and anchorage."},{"t":"p","s":"The guardrail numbers. A top rail sits at 42 inches, plus or minus three, and it has to take a two hundred pound force. In a personal fall arrest system, maximum free fall is 6 feet, and the rig cannot let the worker hit a lower level. The anchorage must hold five thousand pounds per worker."},{"t":"p","s":"And on a residential roof, where conventional systems are truly infeasible at a particular spot, the fallback is a written, site specific fall protection plan. Written. Not a decision you make in your head that morning."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the other half of the pair. Scaffolds are Part twelve, and a guardrail on the open side of a scaffold platform is required at 10 feet. Not six."},{"t":"p","s":"So read the question for the word scaffold. If it says scaffold, the answer is ten. If it says leading edge, floor opening, or just general fall protection, the answer is six."},{"t":"p","s":"Six for general fall protection. Ten for a scaffold guardrail. Say it back to yourself right now, because this single pair is probably worth a point on your exam."},{"t":"p","s":"While we are on scaffolds, the rest of Part twelve. A scaffold must support four times its maximum intended load. Planking is scaffold grade two by ten. Access must be a ladder, stair, or ramp, and climbing the cross braces is always a violation, no exceptions. Suspension type scaffolds, like a boatswain's chair or a ladder jack, require a personal fall arrest system, and a two point suspension scaffold requires both a harness system and a guardrail."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. A worker is on a scaffold platform 8 feet up with no guardrail. Violation, yes or no. Then the same worker at 8 feet on a leading edge with no protection."},{"t":"a","s":"On the scaffold, no violation for the missing guardrail, because the scaffold guardrail trigger is 10 feet. On the leading edge, yes, a violation, because general fall protection triggers at six."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Ladders, and the second pair, twenty and ten."},{"t":"p","s":"Ladders are Part eleven, and there are four things to know."},{"t":"p","s":"The angle is four to one. For every 4 feet of height, the base sits 1 foot out from the wall."},{"t":"p","s":"The extension is 3 feet. Side rails must extend at least 3 feet above the upper landing, so you have something to hold while you step off."},{"t":"p","s":"Put those together on a real house. A ladder to a 16 foot eave needs to reach about 19 feet, and the base sits about 4 feet out. Sixteen divided by four is 4 feet of base, and sixteen plus three is the nineteen."},{"t":"p","s":"Third, secure it. Safety feet, or tie it, block it, or otherwise fix it in place."},{"t":"p","s":"Fourth, never stand on the top 3 feet."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the pair. A portable ladder near power transmission or distribution lines needs 20 feet of clearance. Twenty. That is different from the 10 foot clearance for equipment near lines up to fifty kilovolts."},{"t":"p","s":"Ladders twenty, equipment ten. It feels backwards, since the equipment is the bigger thing, and that is exactly why they test it."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Excavations, and the third pair, four and five."},{"t":"p","s":"Excavation is Part nine, and this is the densest set of numbers in the chapter, so slow down with me."},{"t":"p","s":"A trench or excavation more than 5 feet deep requires a protective system. Sloping, shoring, or a trench shield. Below 5 feet you still need protection if the ground shows signs of hazardous movement."},{"t":"p","s":"Egress is a different rule with a different number. At 4 feet deep, 48 inches, workers need a ladder. That ladder must extend 3 feet above the top of the trench and must be within 25 feet of lateral travel of every worker in the trench."},{"t":"p","s":"So say it as a pair. 4 feet means a ladder. 5 feet means a protective system. The exam will offer you five for the ladder and four for the shoring, and both are wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"Two more excavation numbers. Spoil piles and equipment stay at least 2 feet back from the edge. And a competent person, meaning somebody who can spot hazards and has the authority to fix them, must inspect the excavation on an ongoing basis and again after every rainstorm."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Trench is four and a half feet deep. What do you need, and what do you not need."},{"t":"a","s":"You need a ladder for egress, because that triggers at 4 feet, extending 3 feet above the top and within 25 feet of every worker. You do not automatically need a protective system, because that triggers over 5 feet, unless the ground is showing signs of hazardous movement."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Soil types, weakest to flattest."},{"t":"p","s":"The exam tests the federal three letter system, and there is one idea underneath all of it. The weaker the soil, the flatter you have to lay the slope back."},{"t":"p","s":"Type A is the most stable. Clay, silty clay. Maximum slope three quarters horizontal to one vertical, which is fifty three degrees, the steepest allowed."},{"t":"p","s":"Type B is the middle. Silt, angular gravel, and any previously disturbed soil. Slope one to one, forty five degrees."},{"t":"p","s":"Type C is the weakest. Sand, gravel, anything submerged or seeping water. Slope one and a half horizontal to one vertical, thirty four degrees, the flattest."},{"t":"p","s":"Two hooks to carry out of this. The weaker the soil, the flatter the slope, so the degree number goes down as the letter goes up. And previously disturbed soil can never be Type A. That second one matters on real jobs, because the ground around an existing utility has been dug before, so you are at Type B at best no matter how good the clay looks."},{"t":"p","s":"And before you dig at all, Michigan law says contact MISS DIG, eight one one, at least three full working days before you excavate."},{"t":"p","s":"Know the paint colors, they are easy points. Red is electric. Yellow is gas or oil. Orange is telecom. Blue is water. Green is sewer. White marks your proposed dig. Pink is survey. Purple is reclaimed water."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Electrical, and the health limits."},{"t":"p","s":"Electrical is Part seventeen. Every one hundred twenty volt, fifteen or twenty amp receptacle that is not part of the building's permanent wiring needs GFCI protection."},{"t":"p","s":"There is one alternative, and know it by name. An assured equipment grounding conductor program, which is a documented testing program covering every cord set, every temporary receptacle, and every plug connected tool. So the rule is GFCI or the assured grounding program, one or the other, never neither."},{"t":"p","s":"And lockout tagout. Before servicing equipment, de energize it, lock it, and tag it."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the health numbers, which come with their own bait."},{"t":"p","s":"First aid. A person with a valid first aid certificate must be present at the worksite."},{"t":"p","s":"Silica. The permissible exposure limit is fifty micrograms per cubic meter over 8 hours, and the action level is twenty five. And note the order of controls, wet cutting and vacuum collection come before respirators. Engineering controls first, protective equipment last."},{"t":"p","s":"Noise. In construction the limit is ninety decibels over 8 hours. Now the bait, the eighty five decibel action level with hearing tests is a general industry rule, not a construction rule. They will offer you eighty five. In construction it is ninety."},{"t":"p","s":"Hazard communication is the right to know. Labels, safety data sheets, and training. A safety data sheet has to be on site and accessible for every hazardous chemical, no matter who brought it onto the job."},{"t":"p","s":"Confined spaces. Big enough to enter, limited entry and exit, not designed for occupancy. And you test the atmosphere before anybody goes in."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"What happens when an inspector arrives, because you are the employer now."},{"t":"p","s":"A MIOSHA inspector who shows credentials may enter your site without delay at reasonable times. There is no advance notice, and tipping off an employer is itself an offense. You can insist on a warrant, but understand what you are buying, they will get one."},{"t":"p","s":"The inspection runs in a fixed order. Opening conference, walkaround, closing conference, citations. Both you and an employee representative may walk along."},{"t":"p","s":"Your employees have rights you should know. They can file confidential complaints. They can refuse imminently dangerous work, after asking you to fix it first. And they are protected from retaliation for doing either."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Penalties, and the last pair, seven and seventy."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan sets its own penalty numbers, and this is where a national study book will fail you."},{"t":"p","s":"Serious and other than serious violations run up to 7000 dollars each. Willful or repeat violations run up to seventy thousand, with a 5000 dollar minimum on a willful. And a willful violation that kills a worker is a felony."},{"t":"p","s":"If an answer choice shows the federal 16,000 dollar figure, it is a trap. Michigan is seven and seventy."},{"t":"p","s":"Then reporting, which is the pair you already met in the business chapter, so this is your second pass at it."},{"t":"p","s":"A fatality must be reported to MIOSHA within 8 hours of learning of it. An in patient hospitalization, an amputation, or the loss of an eye must be reported within 24 hours."},{"t":"p","s":"Eight for a death, twenty four for the other three, and never forty eight. Forty eight is not a MIOSHA number at all."},{"t":"p","s":"Recordkeeping uses three forms. The three hundred log. The three hundred A annual summary, posted from February first through April thirtieth. And the three oh one incident report."},{"t":"p","s":"Employers with ten or fewer employees are exempt from the routine logs. But nobody, at any size, is ever exempt from the 8 hour and 24 hour reports. Small shop, dead worker, you still call within 8 hours."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Two of your guys go to the hospital overnight after a fall, and you have six employees total. What do you have to do, and by when."},{"t":"a","s":"Report the in patient hospitalization within 24 hours. You are exempt from keeping the routine three hundred log at six employees, but the reporting duty applies to every employer regardless of size."},{"t":"p","s":"One neighbor topic rides along with safety, and you have seen it twice now, so this is just reinforcement. Michigan workers compensation is required with one or more employees working thirty five or more hours a week for thirteen or more weeks, or three or more employees at any one time. And if your subcontractor has no coverage and his worker gets hurt on your job, the general contractor can be on the hook. Verify every sub's certificate."},{"t":"p","s":"Rapid fire recap. Say these with me, and listen for the pairs."},{"t":"p","s":"Fall protection, 6 feet. Scaffold guardrail, 10 feet. Guardrail height, 42 inches, plus or minus three. Maximum free fall, 6 feet. Anchorage, five thousand pounds."},{"t":"p","s":"Ladders, four to one angle. 3 feet above the landing. Ladder near power lines, 20 feet. Equipment near power lines, 10 feet."},{"t":"p","s":"Trenches, protective system at 5 feet. Ladder at 4 feet, sticking up 3 feet, within 25 feet of travel. Spoil pile, 2 feet back. Competent person, ongoing, and after every rain."},{"t":"p","s":"Soil, Type A three quarters to one at fifty three degrees. Type B one to one at forty five degrees. Type C one and a half to one at thirty four degrees. Disturbed soil is never Type A."},{"t":"p","s":"MISS DIG, three full working days."},{"t":"p","s":"Scaffolds, four times the load. Two by ten planking. Never climb the cross braces."},{"t":"p","s":"Electrical, GFCI on every temporary one twenty volt receptacle, or an assured grounding program."},{"t":"p","s":"Silica, fifty limit, twenty five action. Noise, ninety in construction."},{"t":"p","s":"Reporting, fatality 8 hours. Hospitalization, amputation, eye, 24 hours. Summary posted February first to April thirtieth. Ten or fewer employees, exempt from logs, never from reports."},{"t":"p","s":"Penalties, seven thousand serious. Seventy thousand willful or repeat, five thousand minimum."},{"t":"p","s":"And the agency, LEO runs MIOSHA, MIOSHA covers public employees, and the General Duty Clause fills every gap."},{"t":"p","s":"Ten questions, all numbers. If you only drill the four pairs, six and ten, four and five, twenty and ten, and eight and twenty four, you will still take most of these points home."}],"words":2510,"questions":4},{"id":"06_sitework-concrete-foundations_lecture","track":"builder","title":"Sitework, Concrete, and Foundations","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the biggest block on your trade exam. Excavation and sitework, site engineering, concrete, and footings and foundations together are thirty two of the one hundred ten trade questions. Almost one in three."},{"t":"p","s":"Everything here comes from the twenty fifteen Michigan Residential Code, chapter four, plus the MISS DIG law and the MIOSHA excavation rules. The exam is closed book, so these numbers have to be in your head, not in a table you know how to find."},{"t":"p","s":"We are going to walk it in the order a real job happens. Engineer the site. Dig. Form and pour. Then keep water out of the basement. That order is doing work for you, because the numbers stop being a list and start being a sequence you have lived."},{"t":"p","s":"And I will tell you now what the single most important number in this chapter is, so you are listening for it. 42 inches. We will get there."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"Reading the site, and two words people mix up."},{"t":"p","s":"Before anyone digs you read the plot plan. It shows property lines, setbacks, easements, the building footprint, utilities, well and septic, and contour lines."},{"t":"p","s":"Two definitions the exam loves, and they are easy to keep straight if you attach each one to a direction."},{"t":"p","s":"A benchmark is a permanent point of known elevation. That is vertical control, your starting point for every height measurement on the job."},{"t":"p","s":"A monument is a permanent marker of a property corner or boundary. That is horizontal control, and disturbing one is illegal."},{"t":"p","s":"Benchmark, height. Monument, boundary."},{"t":"p","s":"Contour lines connect points of equal elevation. Close together means steep, far apart means flat, and contour lines never cross."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the leveling math, because they will make you do it, and it is only two sentences of arithmetic."},{"t":"p","s":"Height of instrument equals the benchmark elevation plus the backsight reading. Then any point's elevation equals the height of instrument minus the foresight reading at that point."},{"t":"p","s":"Work one with me. Benchmark is one hundred point zero. You shoot it and the backsight reads four and a half feet. So the height of instrument is one hundred four and a half. Now you shoot the garage corner and the foresight reads six and a quarter. One hundred four and a half minus six and a quarter is ninety eight and a quarter. That corner sits at ninety eight and a quarter."},{"t":"p","s":"And carry this logic check, because it catches sign errors. A higher rod reading means lower ground. If the rod reads big, you are standing in a hole."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"Wells and septic, for rural sites."},{"t":"p","s":"A percolation test measures how fast water drains through the soil, in minutes per inch, and it sizes the septic drain field. Too slow, think dense clay, slower than about sixty minutes per inch, and it fails."},{"t":"p","s":"The numbers, and I will tell you which are state code and which are local practice, because that distinction is itself testable."},{"t":"p","s":"A one thousand gallon tank is the minimum regardless of flow, and that one is in the state E G L E criteria. Twelve fifty for four bedrooms is common local practice."},{"t":"p","s":"The state well code minimum is 50 feet from a single family well to both the septic tank and the drain field. Many local health departments push the drain field out to seventy five or one hundred feet. Tank 10 feet from the foundation and drain field 20 feet are local practice numbers."},{"t":"p","s":"The well casing runs at least 25 feet below grade and sticks up 12 inches above grade. That is state well code, rule R three twenty five point sixteen thirty two."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"MISS DIG, and the trap about what is actually the crime."},{"t":"p","s":"Before a single bucket of dirt moves in Michigan you call MISS DIG. Dial eight one one at least three full working days before excavating."},{"t":"p","s":"Working days. Not calendar days, and not 72 hours. That is Public Act one seventy four of twenty thirteen."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the part the exam tests. Failing to call gets you a civil fine of up to 5000 dollars. But knowingly damaging or hiding utility marks is the misdemeanor. So if a question asks which conduct is criminal, it is not the failure to call, it is destroying or concealing the marks."},{"t":"p","s":"And one more limit that matters on real jobs. MISS DIG only marks member utility lines. The homeowner's private lines, the feed to the detached garage, the line to the well, are on you. Hitting the homeowner's private line is your problem, and the ticket says you called."},{"t":"p","s":"Know the colors. Red is electric. Yellow is gas or oil. Orange is telecom. Blue is water. Green is sewer. White is your own proposed dig. Pink is survey. Purple is reclaimed water."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. You call MISS DIG on Monday morning. When can you dig, and what is the criminal offense in this whole area of law."},{"t":"a","s":"Three full working days later, so not before Thursday, and weekends and holidays do not count. And the misdemeanor is knowingly damaging or hiding the marks, not failing to call."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Trench numbers, which are four numbers in one ladder."},{"t":"p","s":"At 4 feet deep you need a ladder or ramp within 25 feet of lateral travel."},{"t":"p","s":"At 5 feet deep you need a protective system. Sloping, benching, shoring, or a trench box. Unless you are in stable rock."},{"t":"p","s":"At 20 feet deep the protective system must be designed by a registered professional engineer."},{"t":"p","s":"And the spoil pile and equipment stay at least 2 feet back from the edge."},{"t":"p","s":"Four, five, twenty, two. Ladder, protection, engineer, setback."},{"t":"p","s":"Then the contrast that catches everyone. 6 feet is the fall protection trigger. 5 feet is the trench trigger. Different rules, different numbers, and the exam will put them in the same question to see if you blend them."},{"t":"p","s":"Soil types again, and you met these in the safety chapter, so this is your second pass."},{"t":"p","s":"Stable rock can stand vertical. Type A is stiff clay, one and a half tons per square foot or more, sloped three quarters to one, fifty three degrees. Type B is the medium soils, one to one, forty five degrees. Type C is sand, gravel, or anything submerged or seeping, one and a half to one, thirty four degrees."},{"t":"p","s":"The weaker the soil, the flatter the slope."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is the Type C trap that is specific to this chapter. You can never bench Type C soil. Benching is for A and B only. Type C gets sloped or boxed, never stepped."},{"t":"p","s":"Every excavation needs a competent person, somebody who can identify hazards and has the authority to fix them, inspecting daily and after every rain."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Two Michigan permits people confuse."},{"t":"p","s":"A soil erosion and sedimentation control permit, Part ninety one of the natural resources act, is triggered by disturbing one acre or more, or by any earth change within 500 feet of a lake or stream."},{"t":"p","s":"Wetlands are a completely different program, Part three oh three."},{"t":"p","s":"So if the question says wetland, the answer is not Part ninety one. One acre or 500 feet of water is Part ninety one. Wetland is Part three oh three."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"What the footing sits on."},{"t":"p","s":"Footings sit on undisturbed natural soil or engineered fill. Never on organic muck, never on loose fill, and never on frozen ground."},{"t":"p","s":"Presumptive bearing values, worst to best, and hear the ladder. Clay and silt, fifteen hundred pounds per square foot. Sand, two thousand. Sandy gravel, three thousand. Sedimentary rock, four thousand. Crystalline bedrock, twelve thousand."},{"t":"p","s":"Compaction gets verified against a Proctor test. The lab finds the soil's maximum dry density, and the field spec is usually ninety to ninety five percent of it."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"42 inches, and the footing itself."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the number I promised you. All exterior footings and foundation systems extend 42 inches below actual grade. That is Michigan frost depth."},{"t":"p","s":"And it is a Michigan amendment, which is exactly why it is worth a question. The generic IRC answer of 12 inches below grade is a trap on this exam. If you have studied from a national book, that book is wrong for Michigan. Forty two."},{"t":"p","s":"There are exemptions. Freestanding accessory buildings up to 600 square feet of light frame construction, or 400 square feet of heavier construction, with eaves 10 feet or less. And freestanding decks not attached to the house."},{"t":"p","s":"Now footing dimensions."},{"t":"p","s":"Minimum thickness is 6 inches. The footing projects at least 2 inches beyond each face of the wall, and here is the rule that goes with it, that projection can never exceed the footing's own thickness. So a 6 inch footing can project up to 6 inches, and no more."},{"t":"p","s":"Width comes from the code tables based on soil strength and the number of stories. 12 inches is the everyday light frame answer on decent soil, and it widens as the soil weakens."},{"t":"p","s":"The top of a footing must be level. The bottom can slope up to one in ten, which is 10 percent. Steeper than that and you step the footing."},{"t":"p","s":"And building next to a slope steeper than one vertical in three horizontal, which is 33 percent, triggers special setback rules."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Michigan frost depth for exterior footings, and what is the limit on how far a footing projects past the wall."},{"t":"a","s":"42 inches below actual grade. And the projection is at least 2 inches but never more than the thickness of the footing itself."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Anchor bolts, which is one string you should be able to say in one breath."},{"t":"p","s":"Half inch diameter minimum. 6 feet on center maximum. 7 inches embedded into the concrete or grouted block. At least two bolts per plate section. One within 12 inches of each end. Sitting in the middle third of the plate width. Nut and washer tightened."},{"t":"p","s":"Say that again on your own after this. Half inch, 6 feet, 7 inches, two per plate, 12 inches from each end."},{"t":"p","s":"Now disarm the trap. You may have read that buildings over two stories need 4 foot spacing. That is a seismic rule, for seismic design categories C and D. Michigan is category A or B. So 6 feet on center governs here no matter how many stories."},{"t":"p","s":"Same reason you do not need the big three by three plate washers. That is earthquake country equipment, not Michigan equipment."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Foundation walls and backfill."},{"t":"p","s":"Wall thickness comes from tables driven by three things, wall height, unbalanced backfill height, and soil class."},{"t":"p","s":"The wall must stick up above finished grade at least 4 inches where masonry veneer is used, and 6 inches everywhere else. Four with veneer, six without."},{"t":"p","s":"And the backfill rule, which is a real world rule as much as an exam rule. Never backfill until the wall has cured and is braced or anchored to the floor framing above. Then backfill in six to twelve inch compacted lifts of clean material. A green wall with a dozer pushing against it is how basement walls crack before the house is even framed."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"Keeping the basement dry, which is three separate systems."},{"t":"p","s":"The exam tests whether you can tell these three apart, so name them as you learn them."},{"t":"p","s":"System one is surface grading. The ground falls 6 inches in the first 10 feet away from the foundation. Impervious surfaces need 2 percent."},{"t":"p","s":"System two is foundation drains. Drain tile or perforated pipe at or below the top of the footing, discharging by gravity or pump to an approved outlet."},{"t":"p","s":"The gravel geometry around that pipe gets tested, so hear it precisely. The gravel extends one full foot beyond the outside edge of the footing, and 6 inches above the top of the footing, wrapped in filter fabric. The pipe sits on 2 inches of washed gravel with 6 inches over it."},{"t":"p","s":"And there is an exemption. No drain is needed on well drained gravelly Group One soils."},{"t":"p","s":"System three is the wall coating, and this is the discrimination question. Dampproofing is the default. It runs from the top of the footing to finished grade, a bituminous coating, and masonry walls get parged first with three eighths of an inch of portland cement."},{"t":"p","s":"Waterproofing, meaning an actual membrane, is required only where a high water table or severe soil water conditions are known."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the one line that answers every question about the pair. Dampproofing stops soil moisture. Waterproofing stops water under pressure."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Name the three systems that keep a basement dry, and tell me which wall coating is the default."},{"t":"a","s":"Surface grading at 6 inches in 10 feet, foundation drains at or below the top of the footing, and the wall coating. Dampproofing is the default. Waterproofing only where a high water table or severe soil water conditions are known."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"Crawl spaces, which is four numbers."},{"t":"p","s":"Vent area is one square foot of vent per 150 square feet of floor. That drops to one per fifteen hundred if you have a ground vapor retarder, and you need a vent within 3 feet of each corner."},{"t":"p","s":"Access sizes. Through the floor, eighteen by 24 inches. Through a perimeter wall, sixteen by twenty four."},{"t":"p","s":"Hook for that pair. Floor is bigger, eighteen. Wall is sixteen."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea twelve"},{"t":"p","s":"Concrete strength, and why Michigan is different."},{"t":"p","s":"All of Michigan is severe weathering. That single fact drives the strength table, so learn three numbers."},{"t":"p","s":"Twenty five hundred p s i for footings, interior slabs, and foundation walls not exposed to weather."},{"t":"p","s":"Three thousand for vertical concrete exposed to weather."},{"t":"p","s":"Thirty five hundred for garage floor slabs, porches, carport slabs, and steps. Anything that is going to see deicing salt off a truck or a boot."},{"t":"p","s":"And every piece of exterior concrete gets air entrainment, five to seven percent. Those microscopic bubbles give freezing water somewhere to expand into, which is the whole reason concrete survives a Michigan winter."},{"t":"p","s":"Mix basics. Water cement ratio runs around point four five to point five zero. And know the consequence of the site request you get on every pour, adding water on site raises the slump but kills strength."},{"t":"p","s":"A slump test measures workability. Four to six inches is normal residential, and footings are often two to four."},{"t":"p","s":"Admixtures, four types by job. Accelerators for cold weather, but never calcium chloride around embedded or prestressed steel, because chlorides corrode it. Retarders for hot weather and long hauls. Water reducers when you want workability without adding water. Air entrainers for freeze and thaw."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea thirteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Where the steel goes, which is a classic discrimination question."},{"t":"p","s":"Footing rebar goes in the bottom third, because that is the tension zone, with 3 inches of cover where concrete is cast against earth."},{"t":"p","s":"Welded wire mesh in a slab goes in the upper third, on chairs. Mesh lying in the dirt does nothing at all. It is not a little less effective, it is useless."},{"t":"p","s":"Bottom third for footing rebar, upper third for slab mesh."},{"t":"p","s":"Bar sizes are eighths of an inch. Number three is three eighths. Number four is a half inch. Number five is five eighths."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea fourteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Finishing, in order, because the order is the answer."},{"t":"p","s":"Screed. Bull float. Then stop."},{"t":"p","s":"That stop is the tested part. You let the bleed water evaporate, and you never finish bleed water back into the surface. Working that water back in is what gives you a dusting, scaling slab."},{"t":"p","s":"Then edge, cut your joints, float, and trowel. Trowel interior only, because a steel trowel makes exterior concrete dangerously slick. Outside gets a broom finish for traction."},{"t":"p","s":"Three joints, three jobs. Control joints get cut at least a quarter of the slab depth, spaced roughly eight to twelve feet on a 4 inch slab. Isolation joints separate the slab from walls and columns. Construction joints end a day's pour."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea fifteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Curing and cold weather."},{"t":"p","s":"Concrete reaches about 70 percent of design strength at 7 days, and full design strength at 28 days. Keep it moist and around fifty degrees."},{"t":"p","s":"In cold weather, never pour on frozen ground. And protect fresh concrete from freezing until it reaches five hundred p s i, which is roughly the first forty eight to seventy two hours."},{"t":"p","s":"Interior slabs are three and a half inches thick minimum, over 4 inches of granular base, over six mil poly."},{"t":"p","s":"And the volume math, same as the estimating chapter. Length times width times thickness, all in feet, divided by twenty seven gives cubic yards. Then order 10 percent extra."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Garage floor slab in Michigan. What strength, what air content, and what finish."},{"t":"a","s":"Thirty five hundred p s i because it sees deicing salt, five to seven percent air entrainment because it is exterior exposure, and a broom finish for traction, never a steel trowel."},{"t":"p","s":"Rapid fire recap. Say these with me."},{"t":"p","s":"42 inches, Michigan frost depth. Six in ten, grade away from the house. 6 inches footing thickness, 2 inches footing projection, never more than the thickness. Half inch, 6 feet, 7 inches, two per plate, twelve from the end, anchor bolts. Four with veneer, six otherwise, wall above grade. 1 foot beyond and 6 inches above, drain gravel. Twenty five hundred, three thousand, thirty five hundred, footings, exposed walls, garage slabs. Five to seven percent air. Four to six slump. 7 days 70 percent, 28 days full strength. Fifty degrees and five hundred p s i for cold weather. Three and a half inch slab, 4 inch base, six mil poly. One fifty crawl venting, eighteen by twenty four floor access, sixteen by twenty four wall access. Fifteen hundred, two thousand, three thousand, four thousand, twelve thousand, clay, sand, gravel, sedimentary rock, bedrock. Eight one one, three working days, 5000 dollar fine. 4 feet ladder, 5 feet protection, 2 feet spoil, 20 feet engineer. Fifty three, forty five, thirty four degrees for A, B, and C, and you never bench Type C. One acre or 500 feet of water, erosion permit. 50 feet tank to well. One thousand gallons for three bedrooms."},{"t":"p","s":"Thirty two questions live in this chapter. If you own 42 inches, the anchor bolt string, the three concrete strengths, and the trench ladder of four, five, twenty, you have most of it. Go drill those four things."}],"words":3062,"questions":4},{"id":"07_carpentry-framing_lecture","track":"builder","title":"Carpentry and Structural Framing","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the single biggest trade category on your exam. Sixteen of the one hundred ten trade questions come from carpentry and framing, and nothing else comes close."},{"t":"p","s":"Almost every one of them is a number. A fraction of a joist depth. A percentage of a stud width. A nail count. An inch measurement. Numbers can be drilled, so that is what we are going to do, walking the house from the floor up to the roof, then out to the stairs and the deck."},{"t":"p","s":"One thing before we start, and it shapes the whole chapter. The exam tests the twenty fifteen Michigan Residential Code. Not the twenty twenty one IRC. The newer code was stayed by a court order in July of twenty twenty five and never took effect."},{"t":"p","s":"That matters because Michigan amended some values away from the national defaults, and the exam builds its wrong answers out of the national number. Every time I flag a Michigan amendment, that is your cue to burn it in twice, because the wrong answer will be the one you have read in every national book."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"Where joists land, and where they need help."},{"t":"p","s":"Every joist, beam, or girder needs bearing at its ends. One and a half inches minimum on wood or metal. 3 inches minimum on masonry or concrete."},{"t":"p","s":"One and a half on wood, three on concrete. Say it back."},{"t":"p","s":"And a detail with a reason attached. If a wood girder pockets into a concrete or block wall, it gets that 3 inches of bearing plus a half inch air space around the untreated wood, so it can dry instead of rot."},{"t":"p","s":"Joist ends also need lateral restraint, meaning something that keeps them from rolling over. Full depth solid blocking of at least 2 inch nominal, or attachment to a full depth header, band joist, rim joist, or an adjoining stud."},{"t":"p","s":"Now mid span, and here is a Michigan exam classic. Bridging, which means solid blocking, diagonal bridging, or a one by three strip nailed across the bottoms, is only required for joists that exceed a nominal two by twelve. When it is required, the intervals cannot exceed 8 feet."},{"t":"p","s":"Hear the trap inside that. A two by ten does not need bridging. A two by twelve does not need bridging. Only joists deeper than a two by twelve. And the wrong answer on your exam will say two by ten, because that is what a lot of people were taught in the field."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"Cutting, drilling, and notching, which is one pattern of fractions."},{"t":"p","s":"This shows up constantly, so learn it as a pattern rather than four separate rules."},{"t":"p","s":"Notches in the top or bottom of a joist. Maximum depth one sixth of the joist depth. The notch cannot be longer than one third of the depth. And no notches at all in the middle third of the span, because that is where the bending is worst."},{"t":"p","s":"At the very end of the joist, over a bearing, the notch can be deeper, up to one fourth of the depth."},{"t":"p","s":"Holes have their own rule. Maximum diameter one third of the depth, and every hole stays at least 2 inches away from the top edge, the bottom edge, any other hole, and any notch."},{"t":"p","s":"So the pattern is, one sixth for notches in the span, one fourth for notches at the ends, one third for holes and for notch length, and 2 inches of clearance from everything."},{"t":"p","s":"Run it on a real board. A two by ten is actually nine and a quarter inches deep. So a notch is about an inch and a half. An end notch is about two and five sixteenths. A hole is about three and one sixteenth."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the exception that is worth an easy point. Engineered lumber is a different world. You do not cut, notch, or drill an I joist, an L V L, a glulam, or a truss. Not at all, unless the manufacturer's literature allows it or an engineer signs off."},{"t":"p","s":"Those pre stamped knockouts in an I joist web are the manufacturer's permission, which is why they exist. And a truss that got field cut needs a registered design professional to fix it."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Two by ten joist, nine and a quarter inches deep. How deep can you notch it mid span, and where can you not notch it at all."},{"t":"a","s":"One sixth of the depth, so about an inch and a half. And never in the middle third of the span."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Floor openings and cantilevers."},{"t":"p","s":"For an opening like a stairwell, the rule steps up twice."},{"t":"p","s":"If the header joist span is 4 feet or less, a single header the same size as the joists is fine, with single trimmers, as long as the header lands within 3 feet of the trimmer bearing."},{"t":"p","s":"Once the header span goes over 4 feet, you double the header and double the trimmers."},{"t":"p","s":"Over 6 feet, you hang it on approved joist hangers."},{"t":"p","s":"4 feet, double. 6 feet, hangers. Two steps, two numbers."},{"t":"p","s":"And if you have double joists under a parallel bearing partition that get spread apart for piping, you block between them, full depth, 2 inch nominal lumber, at no more than 4 feet on center."},{"t":"p","s":"Cantilevers, and here is another twenty twenty one trap. The twenty fifteen code says a prescriptive floor cantilever cannot exceed the nominal depth of the joist. So a two by ten hangs out about 10 inches. Beyond that you are into the cantilever tables."},{"t":"p","s":"You may have heard the L over four rule, cantilever equals a quarter of the back span. That is a deck rule out of the twenty twenty one IRC, and it is not in the tested code. If the question says per the code, the answer is the depth rule and the tables."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Walls, studs, and the four percentages."},{"t":"p","s":"Stud sizes first, and it works as a ladder of what is stacked on top."},{"t":"p","s":"A two by four bearing wall carrying just a roof and ceiling can go 24 inches on center, up to 10 feet tall."},{"t":"p","s":"Add one floor above and those two by fours drop to 16 inches on center."},{"t":"p","s":"Two floors plus a roof, and two by fours are out entirely. You need two by sixes at sixteen."},{"t":"p","s":"Nonbearing two by fours can run twenty four on center up to 14 feet. And utility grade studs are the cheap seats, sixteen on center maximum, roof and ceiling only, 8 feet maximum in exterior walls."},{"t":"p","s":"Top plates are doubled, overlapped at corners and intersections, with end joints offset at least 24 inches."},{"t":"p","s":"There is a single top plate exception, and it comes with its own hardware. A three by 6 inch, twenty gauge galvanized splice plate with six eight penny nails on each side. Hold onto twenty gauge, because a different plate is coming in a minute and the exam puts them side by side."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the four percentages for studs, and this is the wire they want you to cross."},{"t":"p","s":"Notching. 25 percent of the stud width in a bearing or exterior wall. 40 percent in a nonbearing partition."},{"t":"p","s":"Boring. 40 percent in a single bearing stud. 60 percent in a nonbearing stud, or in a bearing stud if you double the studs, and you can only bore two successive doubled studs that way."},{"t":"p","s":"Every bored hole stays five eighths of an inch from the edge of the stud, and never in the same cross section as a notch."},{"t":"p","s":"So notch is twenty five and forty. Bore is forty and sixty. Notice that 40 percent appears on both lists, notching a nonbearing stud and boring a bearing stud. That overlap is the trap."},{"t":"p","s":"And the top plate repair, the one with the other gauge. When somebody cuts or notches more than 50 percent of a bearing wall top plate, usually the plumber running a vent, the fix is a galvanized metal tie, sixteen gauge, an inch and a half wide, nailed with eight ten penny nails on each side, extending at least 6 inches past the opening."},{"t":"p","s":"Sixteen gauge repair tie. Twenty gauge single plate splice. Two plates, two gauges, two jobs."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Bearing wall stud. What is the maximum notch, and what is the maximum bored hole."},{"t":"a","s":"Notch 25 percent of the stud width. Bore 40 percent, or 60 percent if you double the studs."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Headers and wall bracing."},{"t":"p","s":"Headers over doors and windows come out of the header tables, sized by the header's lumber, the snow load, and the building width. The same table tells you how many jack studs go under each end."},{"t":"p","s":"Jack studs, also called trimmers, are cut to header height and carry the load. King studs run full height beside them. Jack carries, king ties it together."},{"t":"p","s":"Wall bracing. Every exterior wall line is a braced wall line. Along it, a braced wall panel has to begin within 10 feet of each end of the line, with no more than 20 feet between adjacent panels. Ten from the ends, twenty between."},{"t":"p","s":"Know the method letters, because they will use the abbreviations. L I B is a one by four let in brace. WSP is wood structural panel, minimum three eighths inch. G B is gypsum board. C S dash WSP is continuous sheathing."},{"t":"p","s":"And remember what governs here. In Michigan, wind governs bracing, not earthquakes, because we are seismic design category A or B. That is the same reason the anchor bolt seismic spacing did not apply back in the foundation chapter."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"The roof, and the tie question that is almost guaranteed."},{"t":"p","s":"Start with the ridge. The ridge board is at least 1 inch nominal thick, and at least as deep as the cut end of the rafter. Valley and hip rafters are beefier, 2 inch nominal minimum, same depth rule."},{"t":"p","s":"And when the roof pitch drops below three in twelve, a ridge board is not enough. The ridge, hips, and valleys have to be designed as beams. Low slope means the geometry stops helping you, so the member has to carry it."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the tie question. Rafter ties versus collar ties. If you learn one thing from this section, learn this."},{"t":"p","s":"Rafter ties, usually your ceiling joists, sit down low at the plate. Their job is to resist the outward thrust that wants to push your walls apart. Minimum two by four when installed as dedicated ties."},{"t":"p","s":"Collar ties sit up high, in the upper third of the attic, minimum one by four, no more than 4 feet on center. Their job is to resist wind uplift at the ridge."},{"t":"p","s":"Low ties stop spreading. High ties stop uplift. Say that one twice, because the words sound alike and the jobs are opposite."},{"t":"p","s":"And the consequence. If your ceiling joists run perpendicular to the rafters, or are not connected at the plate, you add rafter ties or you engineer a ridge beam. Something has to take that thrust."},{"t":"p","s":"A few more roof numbers. Ceiling joists lap at least 3 inches over bearing partitions, or butt and get toenailed."},{"t":"p","s":"Purlins, which shorten rafter spans, must be at least the size of the rafters they support, braced down to bearing walls with two by fours at forty five degrees or steeper, braces 4 feet on center maximum, and no brace longer than 8 feet unbraced."},{"t":"p","s":"And rafter notching follows the exact same fractions as joists. One sixth, one fourth at the ends, one third for holes. Same pattern, second time you have heard it."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Which tie stops the walls from spreading, where does it sit, and what is the minimum size."},{"t":"a","s":"The rafter tie. Down low at the plate, minimum two by four. Collar ties are the high ones in the upper third, minimum one by four, and they handle uplift."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"The fastening schedule, table R six oh two point three."},{"t":"p","s":"Nobody memorizes the whole table. Learn the greatest hits, because that is what gets asked."},{"t":"p","s":"Joist to sill or girder, three eight penny toenails. Ceiling joist to plate, the same, three eight penny toenails."},{"t":"p","s":"Top plate to top plate, sixteen penny commons at 16 inches on center. Double top plate splice, eight sixteen penny nails on each side of the joint."},{"t":"p","s":"Stud to plate, two sixteen penny end nails. Collar tie to rafter, three ten penny face nails. Band joist to joist, three sixteen penny end nails."},{"t":"p","s":"Built up girders, twenty penny nails at 32 inches on center top and bottom, staggered, with two more at every end and splice."},{"t":"p","s":"And structural sheathing, 6 inches on center at the panel edges, twelve in the field. Six and twelve. Edges get twice the nails because that is where panels move."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Stairs, which is Michigan's signature amendment."},{"t":"p","s":"Lock this down, because it is one of the most likely questions on your whole exam."},{"t":"p","s":"Minimum tread depth, 9 inches. Maximum riser height, eight and a quarter inches."},{"t":"p","s":"The IRC says 10 inch treads and seven and three quarter risers. In Michigan those are the trap answers."},{"t":"p","s":"And notice the direction of each rule, because it explains something that confuses people. Nine is a minimum and eight and a quarter is a maximum. So a stair built to the stricter national numbers still passes here. Michigan is more permissive, not less."},{"t":"p","s":"Risers and treads cannot vary more than three eighths of an inch within a flight. That small number is a real safety rule, because your foot learns the first step and expects every other one to match."},{"t":"p","s":"Winders. 10 inches of tread at the walk line, which is 12 inches from the narrow side, and 6 inches minimum anywhere."},{"t":"p","s":"Headroom, 6 feet 8 inches."},{"t":"p","s":"Width, 36 inches clear. Thirty one and a half with one handrail. Twenty seven with two."},{"t":"p","s":"Handrails kick in at four or more risers, set thirty four to thirty eight inches above the nosings, and a round rail grips at an inch and a quarter to 2 inches."},{"t":"p","s":"Guards are required when the walking surface is more than 30 inches up. At least 36 inches high, no opening that passes a 4 inch sphere, and built to take a two hundred pound load."},{"t":"p","s":"Thirty triggers the guard, thirty six is its height, 4 inch sphere, two hundred pounds."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Michigan stairs. Minimum tread, maximum riser, and what triggers a guard."},{"t":"a","s":"9 inch tread minimum, eight and a quarter inch riser maximum. Ten and seven and three quarters are the national trap. And a guard is required over 30 inches of drop, at 36 inches high."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Decks."},{"t":"p","s":"The ledger is where decks kill people, so the code is specific. Minimum two by eight, pressure treated, number two grade. Bolted or lag screwed with half inch hot dipped galvanized or stainless fasteners."},{"t":"p","s":"Never electroplated. The treated lumber chemistry eats them."},{"t":"p","s":"Spacing comes off the table based on joist span, staggered in two rows, and it gets flashed where it meets the house."},{"t":"p","s":"Where hold downs provide the lateral connection, it is two devices minimum, fifteen hundred pounds each."},{"t":"p","s":"Deck footings go to Michigan frost depth. 42 inches, same as the house, and you already own that number from the foundation chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"And one last twenty twenty one trap. The deck post height table, four by four up to 8 feet, six by six up to 14 feet, is not in the tested code. That is the twenty twenty one IRC again."},{"t":"p","s":"Rapid fire recap. Say these with me."},{"t":"p","s":"Bearing, one and a half on wood, three on masonry. Bridging, only past a two by twelve, at 8 foot intervals. Joist notch one sixth, end notch one fourth, hole one third, 2 inch clearances, no notches in the middle third. Opening headers, double past 4 feet, hangers past six. Stud notch twenty five bearing, forty nonbearing. Stud bore forty bearing, sixty nonbearing or doubled. Five eighths edge distance. Plate cut past 50 percent, sixteen gauge tie, inch and a half wide, eight ten penny each side, 6 inches past the cut. Plate joints offset twenty four. Two by four studs, twenty four on center roof only, sixteen with a floor above, two by six for two floors. Ridge board 1 inch, hips and valleys 2 inch, designed as beams below three in twelve. Collar ties upper third, one by four, 4 feet. Ceiling joist lap 3 inches. Purlin braces two by four, forty five degrees, 4 feet on center, 8 feet maximum. Top plates sixteen penny at sixteen. Sheathing six and twelve. Stairs, 9 inch tread, eight and a quarter riser, three eighths variation, six eight headroom, thirty six wide, handrail at four risers, thirty four to thirty eight. Guards, 30 inches triggers, thirty six high, 4 inch sphere, two hundred pounds. Deck, two by eight treated ledger, half inch galvanized bolts, fifteen hundred pound hold downs, 42 inch footings."},{"t":"p","s":"Sixteen questions live in those numbers. The three I would drill first are the nine and eight and a quarter stairs, because that is Michigan's own amendment, the stud percentages, because of the 40 percent overlap, and rafter ties versus collar ties, because the words are so easy to swap. Run it again tomorrow and they are yours."}],"words":2883,"questions":4},{"id":"08_masonry-roofing-siding_lecture","track":"builder","title":"Masonry, Roofing, Siding and Exterior Finishes","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This chapter is worth about twenty two questions on your trade exam. Nine on masonry, seven on roofing, three on siding, and a few more from the finishes category. It all comes from the twenty fifteen Michigan Residential Code and standard trade practice."},{"t":"p","s":"This chapter has more trap pairs in it than any other, numbers that sit one apart or rules that look interchangeable. Thirty two and thirty three. 2 inches and 1 inch. Sixteen and eight versus twenty and twelve. So every time I give you a pair, stop and say both halves out loud with the thing each one belongs to. That habit is what protects the points."},{"t":"p","s":"We will go in build order. Mortar, then veneer, then the fireplace and chimney, then the roof, then siding and finishes."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"The four mortars, ranked, and why strongest is the wrong instinct."},{"t":"p","s":"There are four mortar types, and you rank them strongest to weakest as M, S, N, O. The trick is the word masonwork. Take every other letter and you get M, S, N, O."},{"t":"p","s":"Type M is twenty five hundred p s i, and it goes below grade. Foundations, retaining walls, anything touching earth."},{"t":"p","s":"Type S is eighteen hundred p s i, the structural workhorse at or above grade."},{"t":"p","s":"Type N is seven hundred fifty p s i, general purpose. And here is the one the exam is really asking about, Type N is the standard choice for brick veneer above grade."},{"t":"p","s":"Type O is three hundred fifty p s i, for interior non load bearing work and for repointing old soft masonry only."},{"t":"p","s":"Now hear the reasoning, because it is the whole question. Do not reach for Type M just because it is strongest. Strongest is not what is being asked. Application is. Mortar that is harder than the brick around it will damage the brick instead of protecting it, which is exactly why old soft masonry gets Type O."},{"t":"p","s":"And keep grout separate from mortar in your head. They are different products with different jobs. Grout is fluid, at least two thousand p s i, and it gets poured into the block cells around the rebar. Mortar is stiff, goes between the units in bed and head joints, and those joints are typically three eighths of an inch."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. What mortar for brick veneer above grade, and what mortar below grade."},{"t":"a","s":"Type N for veneer above grade. Type M below grade. If you answered Type M for the veneer because it is strongest, that is exactly the trap."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"Masonry weather rules."},{"t":"p","s":"Cold weather masonry kicks in below forty degrees."},{"t":"p","s":"Between twenty and forty degrees, you heat the mixing water and sand so the mortar sits between forty and one hundred twenty degrees when you use it, and you protect the finished work for 24 hours."},{"t":"p","s":"Below twenty degrees, no laying at all without a heated enclosure."},{"t":"p","s":"And burn this one in, because it is an absolute. Antifreeze in mortar is never allowed. Never, at any temperature, for any reason."},{"t":"p","s":"Hot weather rules trigger above one hundred degrees, or above ninety degrees with wind over eight miles an hour. Then you shade the materials, dampen absorptive brick, and fog spray the work."},{"t":"p","s":"Mixed mortar has a board life of about two and a half hours, and you can retemper it once inside that window. Once."},{"t":"p","s":"One vocabulary item that shows up. Efflorescence is that white powdery salt stain on masonry. It means water is moving through the wall, dissolving salts, and leaving them behind when it evaporates. So the answer to an efflorescence question is never a cleaner. You fix the water, meaning flashing and weeps. The stain is a symptom."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Brick veneer, where the numbers get dense."},{"t":"p","s":"The air space between the veneer and the sheathing is 1 inch minimum, four and a half inches maximum."},{"t":"p","s":"The veneer hangs on wall ties, and each tie can support no more than 2.67 square feet of wall. Spacing is 32 inches on center horizontally and 24 inches vertically."},{"t":"p","s":"Hear the pattern so you do not flip them. The bigger number, thirty two, runs horizontally along the course. Corrugated ties are twenty two gauge, seven eighths of an inch wide."},{"t":"p","s":"Weep holes go in the first course, immediately above the flashing, at a maximum of 33 inches on center, and at least three sixteenths of an inch in diameter."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the nasty pair. Ties are thirty two. Weeps are thirty three. Weeps get the extra inch. There is no logic to help you there, so just say it twice, ties thirty two, weeps thirty three."},{"t":"p","s":"Lintels over veneer openings. A three by three by quarter inch steel angle spans up to 6 feet, with 4 inches of bearing at each end."},{"t":"p","s":"And the principle underneath all of it, veneer carries only its own weight. Never floor loads, never roof loads. It is a rain jacket made of brick."},{"t":"p","s":"Behind every siding and veneer is the water resistive barrier, number fifteen felt or an approved housewrap, lapped 2 inches horizontally and 6 inches at vertical joints, always upper over lower, shingle fashion."},{"t":"p","s":"Two and six. That pair shows up on exams, and shingle fashion is the reason for both, water always runs over a lap, never into one."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Which joint goes in which wall."},{"t":"p","s":"This one is easy if you learn the movement instead of the joint."},{"t":"p","s":"Clay brick expands over its life as it absorbs moisture. So brick walls get expansion joints, with compressible filler for the brick to push into."},{"t":"p","s":"Concrete block shrinks as it cures. So block walls get control joints, which give the shrinkage a planned place to crack."},{"t":"p","s":"Brick grows, block shrinks. Opposite movements, opposite joints."},{"t":"p","s":"Reinforcement basics. Number four rebar in the grouted cells for typical residential work, and nine gauge ladder wire in the bed joints every 16 inches, which is every other course of 8 inch block."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"The fireplace, which is a cluster of numbers worth real points."},{"t":"p","s":"The firebox. Walls are 10 inches of solid masonry, or 8 inches total when lined with 2 inches of firebrick. Firebox depth is 20 inches minimum."},{"t":"p","s":"The hearth. The hearth slab is 4 inches thick. The hearth extension out in front is 2 inches thick, and it can drop to three eighths of an inch if the firebox opening is raised at least 8 inches above it."},{"t":"p","s":"The extension size depends on the size of the opening, and this is a pair."},{"t":"p","s":"Opening under 6 square feet, the extension runs 16 inches in front and 8 inches to each side."},{"t":"p","s":"Opening 6 square feet or bigger, 20 inches in front and twelve to each side."},{"t":"p","s":"Sixteen and eight, or twenty and twelve. The bigger fire throws sparks farther."},{"t":"p","s":"Clearance to combustibles around the fireplace is 2 inches at the front faces and sides, and 4 inches at the back."},{"t":"p","s":"The damper sits at least 8 inches above the top of the opening."},{"t":"p","s":"And the whole assembly sits on a footing at least 12 inches thick, extending 6 inches beyond the face on all sides, and going down below Michigan's 42 inch frost depth. There is that number again."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Fireplace opening is 7 square feet. How far does the hearth extension run in front and to the sides."},{"t":"a","s":"20 inches in front and twelve to each side, because it is 6 square feet or larger. Sixteen and eight is the smaller opening."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"The chimney, and the three two ten rule."},{"t":"p","s":"Chimney height is the famous three two ten rule, and you need all of it, not part of it."},{"t":"p","s":"At least 3 feet above the highest point where the chimney passes through the roof. And at least 2 feet higher than any part of the building within 10 feet horizontally."},{"t":"p","s":"Both conditions, always. Not whichever is easier."},{"t":"p","s":"The crown on top slopes at least ten degrees, is 2 inches thick at the edge, and overhangs 2 inches."},{"t":"p","s":"Flue sizing is a ratio of the fireplace opening area, and there are three ratios."},{"t":"p","s":"A round flue needs one twelfth of the opening area. A square or rectangular flue under a two to one shape needs one tenth. And a skinny rectangle at two to one or more needs one eighth."},{"t":"p","s":"Work one. A thirty six by 30 inch opening is one thousand eighty square inches. Divided by twelve, a round flue needs ninety square inches."},{"t":"p","s":"Notice the direction, the skinnier and less efficient the flue shape, the bigger the fraction of the opening it needs."},{"t":"p","s":"Chimney clearance to combustibles. 2 inches for a chimney inside the house or in an exterior wall. 1 inch for a chimney built entirely outside the walls."},{"t":"p","s":"Read that split carefully, because the wrong answer is designed to sound better. The split is interior versus exterior. It is not below the roof versus above the roof, no matter how tempting that choice looks."},{"t":"p","s":"And the last chimney item, a cricket. That is the little diverter roof on the uphill side, and it is required when the chimney is more than 30 inches wide parallel to the ridge and does not intersect the ridge."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Chimney passes through the roof, and there is a gable wall 8 feet away that stands 2 feet higher than the chimney top. Are you compliant."},{"t":"a","s":"No. 3 feet above the penetration is only half the rule. You also need 2 feet above any part of the building within 10 feet horizontally, and that wall is inside 10 feet."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"Asphalt shingles and the ice barrier."},{"t":"p","s":"Asphalt shingles need a slope of at least two in twelve, and there is a middle band with its own rule."},{"t":"p","s":"From two in twelve up to four in twelve, you must run double underlayment, a 19 inch starter and 36 inch sheets lapped 19 inches. Four in twelve and steeper takes a single layer."},{"t":"p","s":"Fasteners are twelve gauge roofing nails with three eighths inch heads, driven at least three quarters of an inch into the sheathing. The code minimum is four nails per strip shingle."},{"t":"p","s":"Note that carefully. Six nails is a manufacturer or high wind spec, not the code floor. If the question asks for the code minimum, it is four."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the ice barrier, which is the most Michigan roofing rule there is."},{"t":"p","s":"The ice barrier membrane runs from the lowest edge of the roof to at least 24 inches inside the exterior wall line."},{"t":"p","s":"Inside the wall line. Not 24 inches up from the eave. That distinction is the whole question, and it has a real consequence, a big overhang means a lot more membrane, because the measurement starts from the wall, not the edge."},{"t":"p","s":"On roofs eight in twelve or steeper, it is at least 36 inches measured along the slope. And the only exemption is a detached accessory building with no conditioned space."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Flashing, and the sequencing trap."},{"t":"p","s":"Drip edge extends a quarter inch below the sheathing, 2 inches back onto the deck, and laps 2 inches piece to piece."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the sequencing trap, and it is one of the best questions on the exam because it sounds inconsistent until you understand it."},{"t":"p","s":"At the eaves, underlayment goes over the drip edge. At the rakes, drip edge goes over the underlayment."},{"t":"p","s":"Why. At the eave, water running down the underlayment has to get out on top of the metal. At the rake, wind driven water has to be kept from getting under the edge. Different threat, different order."},{"t":"p","s":"Step flashing where a roof meets a sidewall is four by four minimum. And at the bottom of that run you need a kickout flashing to throw the water into the gutter instead of behind the siding. In Michigan that kickout is the difference between a dry wall and a rotten one, and it is the most commonly missing piece of flashing in the field."},{"t":"p","s":"Open valley metal is 24 inches wide."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Low slope minimums, and reroofing."},{"t":"p","s":"Quick and clean, these are just memory."},{"t":"p","s":"Standing seam metal, a quarter in twelve. Lapped metal with sealant, half in twelve. Without sealant, three in twelve. Built up roofing and single ply membranes like E P D M, a quarter in twelve. Mineral surfaced roll roofing, one in twelve. Wood shingles and wood shakes both need three in twelve, and shakes get an 18 inch wide number thirty felt interlayment between courses."},{"t":"p","s":"Reroofing. Tear off is mandatory in three situations. When the deck is water soaked. When the old roof is shake, slate, or tile. Or when there are already two layers of anything up there."},{"t":"p","s":"Two layers is the ceiling. A third layer is never allowed."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"Attic ventilation, and the Michigan wrinkle."},{"t":"p","s":"The base rule is one square foot of net free vent area for every 150 square feet of attic."},{"t":"p","s":"You can cut that to one in three hundred by either of two paths, and either path alone is enough."},{"t":"p","s":"Path one, a class one or two vapor retarder on the warm side of the ceiling, but only in climate zones six, seven, and eight."},{"t":"p","s":"Path two, put forty to fifty percent of the vent area high, within 3 feet of the ridge, with the rest down at the eaves."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the Michigan wrinkle, and this is the part a national book will get wrong for you. Southern lower Michigan is climate zone five A. So down there the vapor retarder path does not qualify, and you need the balanced high and low vents to earn the one in three hundred."},{"t":"p","s":"And keep 1 inch of clear air space between the insulation and the sheathing, using baffles. Insulation stuffed tight to the deck blocks the very airflow you just calculated."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Attic in southern lower Michigan. How do you qualify for the one in three hundred ratio."},{"t":"a","s":"The balanced vent path only. Forty to fifty percent of the vent area within 3 feet of the ridge and the rest at the eaves, because zone five A does not qualify for the vapor retarder path."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"Siding."},{"t":"p","s":"Fasteners generally penetrate an inch and a quarter into sheathing and framing combined."},{"t":"p","s":"Vinyl siding lives or dies on movement. A 12 foot panel can move half an inch with temperature. So you center the nails in the slots, leave a thirty second of an inch under the head, and a quarter inch gap at the trim. Nail vinyl tight and it buckles, and that is a callback, not a warranty item."},{"t":"p","s":"Installation runs bottom up. Barrier, flashing, level starter strip, corners, J channel, then panels locking course over course."},{"t":"p","s":"Fiber cement laps an inch and a quarter minimum, and note that this one is a code number, not a manufacturer suggestion. Every field cut edge gets primed. The manufacturer clearances are 6 inches to grade and 2 inches to roofing."},{"t":"p","s":"Do not cross those wires. An inch and a quarter is the lap. 6 inches is the grade clearance."},{"t":"p","s":"Stucco takes three coats over lath, two coats over masonry, with each coat kept moist for 48 hours. The weep screed at the bottom sits 4 inches above earth or 2 inches above paving."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea twelve"},{"t":"p","s":"Interior finishes, briefly, because a few questions come from here."},{"t":"p","s":"Drywall levels, one through five. Level one is tape embedded, for plenums. Level two is tape plus one coat, for garages and behind tile. Level three is two coats, for heavy texture. Level four is three coats, the standard painted wall. Level five adds a skim coat over the whole surface, and it is required for gloss or semi gloss paint and for critical lighting."},{"t":"p","s":"Level five is the answer whenever the question mentions shiny paint or a wall that gets raking light."},{"t":"p","s":"Tile. Thinset, the cement mortar, works everywhere including showers. Mastic is for dry area walls only, never wet areas and never floors. Shower tile goes over cement board with a waterproof membrane, and green board is not a wet area substrate."},{"t":"p","s":"Hardwood flooring runs perpendicular to the joists, blind nailed through the tongue, with a three eighths inch expansion gap at the walls."},{"t":"p","s":"Rapid fire recap. Breathe, and say these with me."},{"t":"p","s":"Mortar, M twenty five hundred, S eighteen hundred, N seven fifty, O three fifty, and veneer gets N. Grout two thousand. Air space 1 inch minimum. Ties, 2.67 square feet, thirty two horizontal, twenty four vertical, twenty two gauge. Weeps, thirty three on center, three sixteenths diameter, above the flashing. Lintel, three by three by a quarter, 6 foot span, 4 inch bearing. Felt laps, two horizontal, six vertical. Firebox, 10 inches solid or eight with firebrick, twenty deep. Hearth four, extension two, or three eighths when raised eight. Extensions, sixteen and eight, or twenty and twelve at 6 square feet. Fireplace clearance, two at the front and sides, four at the back. Damper eight above the opening. Footing twelve thick, six past the face. Chimney, 3 feet, 2 feet, 10 feet. Crown, ten degrees, two thick, two overhang. Flues, one twelfth round, one tenth square, one eighth skinny rectangle. Chimney clearance, two interior, one exterior. Cricket at 30 inches. Shingles, two twelve minimum, double felt below four twelve, four nails, three quarter inch penetration. Ice barrier 24 inches inside the wall line, thirty six along the slope on steep roofs. Drip edge, quarter inch down, two onto the deck. Step flashing four by four. Valley metal twenty four. Two roof layers maximum. Vents, one fifty, or one three hundred with a vapor retarder in zone six or with forty to fifty percent high. Baffle space 1 inch. Siding nails penetrate an inch and a quarter. Fiber cement laps an inch and a quarter, 6 inches to grade. Stucco, three coats on lath, two on masonry, 48 hour cure, screed four above earth and two above paving. Level five for gloss. Thinset in showers, never mastic."},{"t":"p","s":"Twenty two questions in this chapter. The pairs are what decide it, so tonight drill ties thirty two against weeps thirty three, sixteen and eight against twenty and twelve, and interior 2 inches against exterior 1 inch on the chimney. Those three pairs are where the points leak."}],"words":3044,"questions":4},{"id":"09_envelope-interior_lecture","track":"builder","title":"Envelope and Interior: Insulation, Ventilation, Windows and Doors, Finishes, and Specialties","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This chapter is about fifteen questions on your trade exam. Roughly five on insulation, two on ventilation, five on windows, doors, and skylights, and the rest split between interior finishes and specialty items."},{"t":"p","s":"It all comes from the twenty fifteen Michigan Residential Code, not the twenty twenty one code. And this chapter more than any other is where Michigan amended the national numbers. That is not a footnote, it is the exam's whole strategy here. The wrong answer will usually be the correct national number."},{"t":"p","s":"So listen for the phrase Michigan amendment, and when you hear it, say the Michigan number twice and the national number once, labeled as the trap."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"The three Michigan climate zones."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan has three, and you need to know which one a city is in."},{"t":"p","s":"Zone five A is the southern Lower Peninsula. Detroit, Grand Rapids, Lansing. That is where you live and work, so that is the zone most of your questions will describe."},{"t":"p","s":"Zone six A is the northern Lower Peninsula and the eastern Upper Peninsula."},{"t":"p","s":"Zone seven is the western U P, the coldest part of the state."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the single most important Michigan amendment in this chapter. In zone five A, the ceiling insulation requirement is R thirty eight."},{"t":"p","s":"The national energy code says R forty nine for zone five. Michigan amended it down to thirty eight."},{"t":"p","s":"So if the question says southern Michigan, or names a southern city, the answer is R thirty eight, and R forty nine is the trap. Up in zones six A and seven, northern Michigan and the U P, the ceiling really is R forty nine."},{"t":"p","s":"South thirty eight, north forty nine. Say it back."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"The rest of the insulation table."},{"t":"p","s":"Walls are easier, because they are the same statewide. R twenty in the cavity, or R thirteen cavity plus R five continuous insulated sheathing on the outside."},{"t":"p","s":"Floors over unconditioned space are R thirty, except zone seven where they rise to R thirty eight."},{"t":"p","s":"Basement walls. In zone five A you need R ten continuous or R thirteen cavity. In zones six A and seven it is R fifteen continuous or R nineteen cavity."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is a reading skill that is worth a point by itself. When you see two numbers with a slash, like ten slash thirteen, the first number is always continuous insulation, meaning rigid foam on the face, and the second is cavity insulation in a framed wall. Either one complies. It is not a sum, it is a choice."},{"t":"p","s":"Slab edges get R ten in every zone. What changes is the depth. 2 feet down in zone five A, 4 feet down in six A and seven."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Windows, and two numbers that are not the national ones."},{"t":"p","s":"The maximum window U factor in Michigan is zero point three two, in every zone."},{"t":"p","s":"Not zero point three zero. That is the twenty twenty one national number and it is not in force here."},{"t":"p","s":"Skylights get zero point five five."},{"t":"p","s":"And keep the direction straight, because U factor runs backward from R value. Lower is better, because U factor is one divided by R. A big R is good, a small U is good."},{"t":"p","s":"One more giveaway, and it is an easy point when you spot it. There is no solar heat gain requirement in Michigan. S H G C limits are for hot southern climates. If a Michigan question offers you a solar heat gain number, it is a trap, because the table says not required."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Air leakage, another Michigan amendment."},{"t":"p","s":"The maximum is four air changes per hour at fifty pascals, verified with a mandatory blower door test."},{"t":"p","s":"The model code says three. Michigan says four. Four is your answer."},{"t":"p","s":"Hold onto that number, because it comes back in a few minutes and connects to something else."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Vapor retarders, which installers get wrong in real life."},{"t":"p","s":"In climate zones five, six, and seven, which is all of Michigan, you need a Class one or Class two vapor retarder on the interior side of frame walls."},{"t":"p","s":"Interior means the warm in winter side. That is the principle, and if you hold it, you can reason out the rest."},{"t":"p","s":"The three classes, by permeability. Class one is poly sheeting or foil facing, a tenth of a perm or less. Class two is kraft faced batts, up to one perm. Class three is just latex paint, up to ten perms."},{"t":"p","s":"Class three is only allowed when the wall can dry, and there are two ways to earn it. Either vented cladding over the sheathing, or enough continuous insulated sheathing to keep the wall warm, which is R five over a two by four wall in zone five, and R seven and a half over a two by six."},{"t":"p","s":"And now the rule that is worth saying out loud twice. Never put poly on the outside of a Michigan wall."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is why, because the reason makes it stick. You already have a required vapor retarder on the inside. Add poly on the outside and you have built a double vapor barrier. Moisture gets into that wall, and then it cannot dry in either direction. That is rot and mold, and on the exam it is a wrong answer every single time."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Where does the vapor retarder go in a Michigan wall, and what happens if you also wrap the outside in poly."},{"t":"a","s":"On the interior side, the warm in winter side. Poly on the outside creates a double vapor barrier that traps moisture with no drying path, which is rot."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Insulation odds and ends."},{"t":"p","s":"R values per inch, roughly. Fiberglass batts about R three point two per inch. Blown cellulose about three point five. Extruded polystyrene about five. Closed cell spray foam six or better."},{"t":"p","s":"And one performance fact that is really a job site fact. Compressing a batt into a smaller cavity lowers its R value. An R nineteen squeezed into a two by four wall performs like R thirteen. You do not get nineteen just because the label says nineteen."},{"t":"p","s":"At the eaves, insulation baffles must hold a 1 inch minimum air space between the insulation and the roof sheathing, so ventilation air can actually move."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"Attic ventilation, and the arithmetic."},{"t":"p","s":"The base rule is one to one fifty. Net free vent area equal to one one hundred fiftieth of the attic floor area."},{"t":"p","s":"You can cut that in half, to one three hundredth, if either of two conditions is met."},{"t":"p","s":"Condition one, a Class one or two vapor retarder on the warm side of the ceiling. But that only counts in climate zones six and seven, not in zone five A."},{"t":"p","s":"Condition two, forty to fifty percent of the vent area placed high in the attic, within 3 feet of the ridge measured vertically, with the rest down at the eaves."},{"t":"p","s":"Either condition alone is enough."},{"t":"p","s":"Quick math so the ratio is not abstract. A fifteen hundred square foot attic at one to three hundred needs 5 square feet of net free area. At one to one fifty it needs ten."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Mechanical ventilation, and two pairs of numbers."},{"t":"p","s":"The exam swaps these constantly, so learn them as a pair with a reason."},{"t":"p","s":"Bathrooms, fifty CFM intermittent, or twenty continuous."},{"t":"p","s":"Kitchens, one hundred CFM intermittent, or twenty five continuous."},{"t":"p","s":"Bigger room load, bigger number. The kitchen gets a hundred because you are boiling water and searing food in it."},{"t":"p","s":"And an absolute rule. Every exhaust fan discharges outdoors. Never into the attic, never into a soffit, never into a crawl space. Dumping moist air into an attic breeds mold and feeds ice dams, which in Michigan means you built the problem you will be back to fix."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the connection question they like, and this is where that four comes back."},{"t":"p","s":"Whole house mechanical ventilation is required whenever the blower door test comes in under five air changes per hour. Michigan requires every new house to test at four or tighter."},{"t":"p","s":"So follow the logic. Four is tighter than five. Which means every new Michigan home needs whole house mechanical ventilation. Not most. Every one."},{"t":"p","s":"A typical rate for a house between fifteen hundred and three thousand square feet with two or three bedrooms is sixty CFM continuous."},{"t":"p","s":"One specialty item rides along here. Radon. The EPA action level is four picocuries per liter. The standard new construction answer is a passive sub slab depressurization system, meaning a 4 inch gravel layer under the slab, a poly soil gas retarder, and a three to four inch vent pipe running from under the slab straight up through the roof, with power roughed in for a future fan."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Why does every new Michigan house need whole house mechanical ventilation."},{"t":"a","s":"Because whole house ventilation is triggered under five air changes per hour, and Michigan's own air leakage limit requires every new house to test at four or tighter. The tightness rule guarantees the ventilation rule."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Egress windows, which is the heart of the window questions."},{"t":"p","s":"Emergency escape and rescue openings are required in every sleeping room, in basements with habitable space, and in habitable attics."},{"t":"p","s":"Four numbers, and they are the ones to burn in."},{"t":"p","s":"5.7 square feet of net clear opening. Five point zero if it is a grade floor opening."},{"t":"p","s":"Minimum clear height 24 inches. Minimum clear width 20 inches. Maximum sill height 44 inches above the floor."},{"t":"p","s":"And it has to open from inside without keys, tools, or special knowledge."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the classic trick, and it is a genuinely good question. A window at exactly 20 inches wide and 24 inches high is only 3.33 square feet."},{"t":"p","s":"Do that multiplication in your head. Twenty times twenty four is four hundred eighty square inches, and there are one hundred forty four square inches in a square foot, so that is three point three three."},{"t":"p","s":"So meeting both minimum dimensions does not meet the area. One dimension has to be bigger than its minimum. That is the point of the question."},{"t":"p","s":"If the egress window is below grade, the window well needs 9 square feet of horizontal area with 36 inches in each direction. And if the well is deeper than 44 inches, it needs a permanent ladder or steps."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"Safety glazing, section R three oh eight point four."},{"t":"p","s":"Tempered glass is required in hazardous locations, and the list is worth learning as locations rather than as numbers."},{"t":"p","s":"All glass in doors."},{"t":"p","s":"Glass within a 24 inch arc of either side of a closed door, when the bottom edge is under 60 inches above the floor."},{"t":"p","s":"Big panes that meet all four of these together. Over 9 square feet. Bottom edge under 18 inches from the floor. Top edge over 36 inches. And a walking surface within 36 inches. All four, not any one."},{"t":"p","s":"Glass in guards and railings, always, no exceptions."},{"t":"p","s":"Wet locations. Any glass with a bottom edge less than 60 inches above a tub or shower drain and within 60 inches horizontally of the water. Sixty, not seventy two."},{"t":"p","s":"And stairways. Glass within 36 inches horizontally of a walking surface and under 60 inches above it, plus glass within 60 inches of the bottom tread."},{"t":"p","s":"Tempered panes carry a permanent etched label. No label, no pass. That is how an inspector calls it, and it is how the exam calls it."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"Three door and window rules."},{"t":"p","s":"Window fall protection. If an operable window's sill is less than 24 inches above the floor inside, and the drop outside is more than 72 inches, then the opening cannot pass a 4 inch sphere, or it needs an approved opening control device."},{"t":"p","s":"Twenty four inside, seventy two outside. A low sill over a long drop."},{"t":"p","s":"Every house needs at least one side hinged exterior egress door with 32 inches of clear width and 78 inches of clear height."},{"t":"p","s":"And the door from an attached garage into the house. One and three eighths inch solid wood, one and three eighths solid or honeycomb core steel, or a twenty minute fire rated door. Self closing. And it may never open into a bedroom."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea twelve"},{"t":"p","s":"Drywall, and the garage separation that is nearly guaranteed."},{"t":"p","s":"Fastener spacing without adhesive. Ceilings take nails every 7 inches or screws every twelve. Walls take nails every eight or screws every sixteen."},{"t":"p","s":"Ceilings are always tighter, and the reason is gravity. That is your check if you cannot remember which is which."},{"t":"p","s":"Screws have to bite five eighths of an inch into the wood."},{"t":"p","s":"Half inch board is standard on walls and on ceilings framed 16 inches on center. At 24 inches on center, ceilings step up to five eighths, or half inch sag resistant board."},{"t":"p","s":"Type X is the fire rated core. Type C is the upgraded fire rated core that outperforms X."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the garage separation, which shows up on this exam somewhere almost every time."},{"t":"p","s":"Walls between the garage and the house get half inch gypsum on the garage side. Every wall, always."},{"t":"p","s":"The ceiling of a garage with living space above it gets five eighths Type X."},{"t":"p","s":"Structure supporting that floor gets half inch."},{"t":"p","s":"The trap runs in both directions, so watch it. Type X on the walls is wrong. And plain half inch on a ceiling under a bedroom is wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"And one more thing they test. This is a separation, not a rated assembly. If an answer choice says 1 hour fire wall, that is the trap. The code calls for separation materials, and it never requires an hour rating for a house garage."},{"t":"p","s":"Ducts passing through the separation must be twenty six gauge steel with no openings into the garage."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Garage with a bedroom above it. What goes on the garage side walls, and what goes on the garage ceiling."},{"t":"a","s":"Half inch gypsum on the walls. Five eighths Type X on the ceiling because there is living space above. And it is a separation, not a 1 hour rated wall."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea thirteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Finish levels and wet areas, which is your second pass on both."},{"t":"p","s":"Drywall finish levels, zero through five. Level one, tape embedded, for plenums. Level two, tape plus one coat, for garages and behind tile. Level three, two coats, for heavy texture. Level four, three coats, standard for flat paint. Level five adds a skim coat over the whole surface, for gloss, semi gloss, and critical lighting."},{"t":"p","s":"Wet areas. Tub and shower walls need a nonabsorbent finish to 6 feet above the floor."},{"t":"p","s":"Tile in wet areas goes over cement board, fiber cement, or glass mat gypsum. Never paper faced green board. And it is set with thinset, the cement mortar. Mastic is for dry walls only, because it softens in moisture and fails."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea fourteen"},{"t":"p","s":"The specialties, four quick items."},{"t":"p","s":"Attic access is required when the attic is over 30 square feet with 30 inches or more of height. The rough opening is twenty two by 30 inches, located in a hallway or other accessible spot, with 30 inches of headroom above the hatch."},{"t":"p","s":"Memory hook, thirty, thirty, thirty, through a twenty two by thirty opening."},{"t":"p","s":"Smoke alarms. In every bedroom, outside every sleeping area, and on every story including the basement. All interconnected, hardwired, with battery backup."},{"t":"p","s":"Carbon monoxide alarms. The trigger is a fuel burning appliance or an attached garage. The location is outside each sleeping area. And here is the discriminator, under the twenty fifteen code they do not have to interconnect. Interconnection is a smoke alarm rule."},{"t":"p","s":"Pool barriers. 48 inches high, no opening that passes a 4 inch sphere, and a self closing self latching gate with the latch at 54 inches or on the pool side."},{"t":"p","s":"Rapid fire recap. Say these with me."},{"t":"p","s":"Ceiling insulation, R thirty eight south, R forty nine north. Walls, R twenty, or thirteen plus five. Basement, ten thirteen south, fifteen nineteen north. Slab, R ten, 2 feet south, 4 feet north. Window U factor, point three two. Skylight, point five five. Air leakage, four ACH fifty with a blower door test. Vapor retarder, Class one or two, interior side. Attic vents, one to one fifty, or one to three hundred. Baffle air space, 1 inch. Bath fan, fifty and twenty. Kitchen fan, one hundred and twenty five. Whole house trigger, under five ACH fifty. Egress, 5.7 square feet, five point zero at grade, twenty four high, twenty wide, forty four sill. Window well, 9 square feet, 36 inches, ladder past forty four deep. Tempered glass, 24 inch door arc under sixty, 9 square feet with eighteen and thirty six and thirty six, sixty above the drain, thirty six and sixty at stairs. Fall protection, sill under twenty four inside, drop over seventy two outside, 4 inch sphere. Egress door, thirty two by seventy eight clear. Garage door to house, one and three eighths, self closing, never into a bedroom. Garage gypsum, half inch walls, five eighths Type X ceiling. Drywall fasteners, seven and twelve on ceilings, eight and sixteen on walls. Screws, five eighths bite. Tub walls, nonabsorbent to 6 feet. Attic access, twenty two by thirty, 30 square feet, 30 inches headroom. Radon, four picocuries per liter."},{"t":"p","s":"Fifteen questions here. The three I would drill hardest are R thirty eight for southern Michigan, because the national R forty nine is sitting right there as bait, the egress numbers including why twenty by twenty four fails, and the garage separation in both directions. Those three carry the chapter."}],"words":2935,"questions":3},{"id":"10_plans-math-trades_lecture","track":"builder","title":"Plans, Estimating Math, Trade Boundaries and Permits","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the second biggest block of the trade exam. Plans, specifications, and estimating are fourteen of the one hundred ten trade questions. On top of that, this chapter covers trade boundaries, meaning what you may not touch without a plumbing, electrical, or mechanical license, and the permit and inspection process, which shows up on the business and law side too."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the good news about this chapter. Almost every question in the estimating half is a formula with numbers plugged into it. There is no interpretation and no judgment. If you can run the formula, you get the point, and I am going to run each one out loud with you rather than just naming it."},{"t":"p","s":"Everything keys to the twenty fifteen Michigan Residential Code. The twenty twenty one update was stayed by a court in July of twenty twenty five and never took effect, so if an answer choice smells like the newer national code, be suspicious of it."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"Reading the drawings."},{"t":"p","s":"Floor plans and elevations are drawn at one quarter inch equals 1 foot. That is the single most tested scale fact on this exam."},{"t":"p","s":"And it gives you a shortcut worth knowing. On a quarter scale plan, multiply the plan inches by four and you get real feet. 6 inches on the paper is 24 feet of real wall."},{"t":"p","s":"Site plans are different. They use an engineer's scale, usually 1 inch equals 20 feet or 1 inch equals 30 feet. And details, like a stair connection or a flashing condition, get drawn much bigger, anywhere from three quarters of an inch up to 3 inches equals 1 foot."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the drawing types, and the way to keep them straight is to ask what each one shows you."},{"t":"p","s":"An elevation is a flat picture of the outside face of the house. Finishes, window and door openings, the grade line, the roof slope."},{"t":"p","s":"A section is a vertical cut through the building, and it is where you find what is inside the wall or floor. Sheathing, studs, insulation, floor to floor heights."},{"t":"p","s":"So if the question asks where you would find the layers of a wall assembly, the answer is the wall section. Elevation shows the face, section shows the guts."},{"t":"p","s":"A detail is a blow up of one small condition, referenced by a bubble. If you see the number three over the letters A five in a circle, that means detail three, drawn on sheet A five."},{"t":"p","s":"And sheet prefixes matter. A is architectural. S is structural. M is mechanical. E is electrical. P is plumbing. C is civil."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"What wins when the documents disagree."},{"t":"p","s":"Two rules, and the exam likes both."},{"t":"p","s":"First, a written dimension always beats a scaled measurement. Never scale a drawing when the number is printed on it. Paper stretches, prints shrink, and the writer meant the number."},{"t":"p","s":"Second, when the drawings and the written specifications disagree, the specifications generally govern. But note what the best exam answer usually is, notify the architect and get clarification before you proceed. On a test that offers you both, the professional answer wins."},{"t":"p","s":"And remember the division of labor between the two documents. Specs control quality. Drawings control quantity and location."},{"t":"p","s":"Schedules are the tables keyed to marks on the plan. A window called out as three zero five zero is 3 feet 0 inches wide by 5 feet 0 inches high. Width first, then height. A door marked two eight six eight is 2 feet 8 inches by 6 feet 8 inches."},{"t":"p","s":"For specifications, know the classic sixteen division C S I MasterFormat. The ones that get tested are division three concrete, division four masonry, division six wood and plastics, division seven thermal and moisture protection, division eight doors and windows, division nine finishes, division fifteen mechanical, and division sixteen electrical."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"The estimating formulas, run out loud."},{"t":"p","s":"Board feet first. Board feet equals thickness in inches, times width in inches, times length in feet, divided by twelve."},{"t":"p","s":"Run it. A two by six that is 10 feet long. Two times six is twelve, times ten is one hundred twenty, divided by twelve is ten board feet."},{"t":"p","s":"And lumber is priced per thousand board feet, so the pricing question has one extra step. Eight hundred board feet at 600 dollars per thousand is point eight times six hundred, which is 480 dollars."},{"t":"p","s":"Concrete. Twenty seven cubic feet make one cubic yard. Length times width times thickness, all in feet, divided by twenty seven. Convert inches to feet first, so 4 inches is a third of a foot."},{"t":"p","s":"Run it. A twenty by thirty slab at 4 inches. Twenty times thirty is six hundred, times one third is two hundred cubic feet. Divided by twenty seven is seven point four yards. Add five to ten percent waste and round up, so you order eight."},{"t":"p","s":"And that is the general rule on any ordering question. Round up. Never order short. The exam is not testing whether you can shave a yard."},{"t":"p","s":"Masonry has two magic numbers. Standard modular brick runs six point seven five bricks per square foot of wall. Standard eight by eight by sixteen block runs one point one two five blocks per square foot."},{"t":"p","s":"Run both. A thousand square foot wall with a hundred square feet of openings is nine hundred net. Nine hundred times six point seven five is six thousand seventy five bricks."},{"t":"p","s":"And a 40 foot wall, 8 feet tall, is 320 square feet. Times one point one two five is three hundred sixty blocks."},{"t":"p","s":"Roofing is counted in squares, and a square is 100 square feet. A twenty four hundred square foot roof is twenty four squares. Standard three tab shingles run three bundles per square, so that is seventy two bundles, plus roughly 10 percent waste."},{"t":"p","s":"Drywall and sheathing come in four by eight sheets, which is 32 square feet each. Total your wall and ceiling area, divide by thirty two, round up, add waste."},{"t":"p","s":"Framing counts. Studs at 16 inches on center, take the wall length in feet, multiply by three quarters, add one, then add extras for corners and openings. The fast field rule is one stud per lineal foot of wall, which builds in the extras."},{"t":"p","s":"Joists work the same way. A 24 foot run at sixteen on center is twenty four times point seven five, which is eighteen, plus one, so nineteen joists."},{"t":"p","s":"Plates are three times the wall length, because you have one bottom plate and a double top."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. How many board feet in a two by eight that is 12 feet long, and how many joists at sixteen on center across a 32 foot run."},{"t":"a","s":"Two times eight times twelve is one hundred ninety two, divided by twelve is sixteen board feet. And thirty two times point seven five is twenty four, plus one, so twenty five joists."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Excavation, where the trap is in the direction of the math."},{"t":"p","s":"Soil swells when you dig it and shrinks when you compact it, so the same dirt has three different volumes depending on where it is."},{"t":"p","s":"Bank yards are in the ground. Loose yards are in the truck. Compacted yards are in the fill."},{"t":"p","s":"Common earth swells about 25 percent. So one hundred bank yards becomes one hundred twenty five loose yards to haul."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the trap, and it is the same shape as the markup and margin trap from the business chapter. If you need one hundred compacted yards and shrinkage is 10 percent, you divide by point nine, which gives about one hundred eleven bank yards."},{"t":"p","s":"You divide by one minus the shrinkage. You do not just add 10 percent. Adding 10 percent gives one hundred ten, and it is wrong for the same reason multiplying was wrong on margin."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Grade and slope."},{"t":"p","s":"Percent grade is rise over run, times one hundred. 2 feet of fall in 100 feet is 2 percent."},{"t":"p","s":"Two conversions worth memorizing. A quarter inch per foot is about 2 percent. An eighth inch per foot is about 1 percent, and that is your garage floor slope."},{"t":"p","s":"And the code number you already know from the foundation chapter, the ground must fall 6 inches in the first 10 feet away from the foundation, which is 5 percent, or 2 percent minimum on pavement."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"The stair problem, which is the money question."},{"t":"p","s":"This is the calculation most likely to appear, and Michigan differs from the national code, so run it my way."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan allows a maximum riser of eight and one quarter inches and a minimum tread of 9 inches. The national IRC numbers, seven and three quarters and 10 inches, are the trap answers."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the method, four steps."},{"t":"p","s":"Step one. Total rise divided by the maximum riser, then round up. That gives your riser count."},{"t":"p","s":"Step two. Total rise divided by that riser count gives the actual riser height."},{"t":"p","s":"Step three. Treads are always one fewer than risers."},{"t":"p","s":"Step four. Treads times the minimum tread depth gives the minimum total run."},{"t":"p","s":"Now run it. Floor to floor rise is 108 inches. Divide by eight point two five and you get thirteen point zero nine. Round up to fourteen risers."},{"t":"p","s":"The actual riser is one hundred eight divided by fourteen, which is about 7.7 inches. Treads are thirteen, and at 9 inches each the minimum total run is 117 inches."},{"t":"p","s":"Now understand why you round up, because that is the real question hiding inside the arithmetic. If you rounded down to thirteen risers, each one would be one hundred eight divided by thirteen, which is 8.31 inches. That is over the eight and a quarter maximum, so it is illegal. Rounding up is what keeps you under the limit."},{"t":"p","s":"And one more stair number you already have, risers and treads cannot vary more than three eighths of an inch within a flight."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Total rise is 99 inches. How many risers, and how tall is each one."},{"t":"a","s":"Ninety nine divided by eight point two five is exactly twelve, so twelve risers at eight and a quarter inches, right at the maximum. Eleven treads. If the number had come out at twelve point one, you would round up to thirteen."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"Trade boundaries, and what your license does not let you do."},{"t":"p","s":"Your residential builder license lets you build the house and lets you coordinate the job. It never lets you do plumbing, electrical, or mechanical work. Each of those trades has its own license, its own permit, and its own inspections."},{"t":"p","s":"All three are now licensed under one law, the Skilled Trades Regulation Act, Act four zero seven of twenty sixteen, which replaced the old separate trade acts."},{"t":"p","s":"Plumbing runs apprentice, then journeyman at 6000 hours over 3 years, then master with four thousand more hours over 2 years."},{"t":"p","s":"Electrical runs journeyman at 8000 hours over 4 years, then master at 12,000 hours over 6 years, including 2 years as a journeyman."},{"t":"p","s":"And in both trades, the contracting business itself needs a separate contractor license, and it must employ a master. Hear that distinction, because it is tested. A master license alone does not make you a contractor."},{"t":"p","s":"Now mechanical, which is the odd one and a favorite trap. Under the same act, Michigan licenses the mechanical contractor as a business entity only. There is no individual journeyman or master mechanical license in Michigan. The company designates a qualifying officer and carries classification endorsements for the kinds of work it does."},{"t":"p","s":"So if an answer choice mentions a Michigan master mechanical license, it is wrong. There is no such thing."},{"t":"p","s":"And anyone handling refrigerant also needs federal EPA section six zero eight certification."},{"t":"p","s":"The homeowner exemption. A homeowner may do their own trade work on their own primary residence, under their own permit. Rentals are excluded. And a contractor may never work under a homeowner's permit. That is illegal, and it is a common real world scheme that the exam expects you to reject."},{"t":"p","s":"Your job as the builder is to hire licensed subs, verify their licenses on the LARA website before they start, make sure they pull their own permits, and sequence their inspections."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Does Michigan issue a master mechanical license to an individual, and can your plumbing sub work under the homeowner's permit."},{"t":"a","s":"No to both. Mechanical is licensed to the business entity only, with a qualifying officer. And a contractor may never work under a homeowner's permit."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Permits, and the exemption list."},{"t":"p","s":"The governing law is Act two thirty of nineteen seventy two, the Stille DeRossett Hale Single State Construction Code Act."},{"t":"p","s":"No regulated work starts before a permit is issued. Ever."},{"t":"p","s":"But some work is exempt, under section R one zero five point two, and this list is pure memorization, so here it is."},{"t":"p","s":"A one story detached accessory structure, like a shed, up to 200 square feet. A fence up to 7 feet high. A retaining wall up to 4 feet, measured from the bottom of the footing, that holds back no surcharge. Sidewalks and driveways not more than 30 inches above grade. Painting, papering, tiling, carpeting, cabinets, and countertops. Above ground prefab pools less than 24 inches deep. Window awnings projecting 54 inches or less. And decks of 200 square feet or less that sit no more than 30 inches above grade and do not serve the exit door."},{"t":"p","s":"Watch the shed trap. 200 square feet is the current number. One twenty is the old code, and it is sitting in a lot of study material."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the timelines."},{"t":"p","s":"The building department has ten business days to issue or deny a permit, and fifteen for an unusually complicated building or structure."},{"t":"p","s":"An issued permit dies if work does not start within 180 days, or if work stops for 180 days."},{"t":"p","s":"The certificate of occupancy must be issued within five business days of written application, once every permit is finaled, every inspection passed, and every fee paid."},{"t":"p","s":"And sealed architect plans are not required for one and two family dwellings under 3500 square feet."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Inspections, and the one rule that is never negotiable."},{"t":"p","s":"The sequence runs footing and foundation before the pour. Waterproofing before backfill. Rough framing. Then rough electrical, plumbing, and mechanical before anything is covered. Then insulation. Then drywall. Then the four finals. Then the certificate of occupancy."},{"t":"p","s":"Notice the pattern, every inspection happens at the last moment the work is still visible. That is the logic of the whole sequence, and it gives you the iron rule."},{"t":"p","s":"Never cover uninspected work."},{"t":"p","s":"If you do, the inspector can issue a stop work order and make you tear it open. And after a stop work order, the only work allowed is what makes the site safe."},{"t":"p","s":"Penalties. A knowing violation is a misdemeanor, up to 500 dollars and up to 90 days. Each knowing violation is a separate offense. And each day counts as a separate offense specifically when you keep working through a stop construction order."},{"t":"p","s":"Unlicensed residential work draws a separate civil fine of one hundred to five hundred dollars."},{"t":"p","s":"Rapid fire recap. Say these with me."},{"t":"p","s":"Quarter inch equals 1 foot for floor plans. 1 inch equals twenty or thirty feet for site plans. Window three zero five zero is 3 feet by 5 feet, width first. Board feet is thickness times width times length over twelve. Twenty seven cubic feet in a yard, five to ten percent concrete waste. Six point seven five bricks per square foot. One point one two five blocks per square foot. A roofing square is 100 square feet, three bundles per square. A four by eight sheet is 32 square feet. Studs at sixteen on center, length times point seven five plus one. Common earth swells 25 percent, and you divide by one minus the shrinkage. Grade is rise over run times one hundred, and 6 inches in 10 feet away from the house. Michigan riser eight and a quarter maximum, tread nine minimum, variance three eighths, treads equal risers minus one, and always round the riser count up. Plumber hours, six thousand journeyman, four thousand more for master. Electrician hours, eight thousand journeyman, twelve thousand master. No individual mechanical license, entity only, with a qualifying officer. Permit exemptions, shed 200 square feet, fence 7 feet, retaining wall 4 feet, deck 200 square feet under 30 inches, awning 54 inches. Permit in ten business days, dead after one hundred eighty idle days, certificate of occupancy in five business days, sealed plans only at 3500 square feet and up. And never cover uninspected work."},{"t":"p","s":"Fourteen questions here, and the two that will decide them are the stair calculation, because it has four steps and a rounding rule, and the excavation shrinkage, because it is the same divide, do not multiply trap you met in the business chapter. Drill those two until they are boring."}],"words":2829,"questions":3}],"law":[{"id":"00_supercram_lecture","track":"law","title":"Super-Cram: Michigan Insurance Law, The Numbers","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the last-hour listen. Every number comes straight from the verified law docs."},{"t":"p","s":"## Michigan legal system"},{"t":"p","s":"District court has exclusive civil jurisdiction up to 25,000 dollars, MCL 600.8301. Circuit court is the general trial court, and picks the umpire when appraisers disagree; the Court of Claims only hears claims against the state. The Court of Appeals hears an appeal as of right from a final circuit judgment; the Supreme Court takes a case by leave. A published Court of Appeals opinion binds every lower court, court rule seven point two one five; unpublished, like Tredenick, twenty twenty one, is persuasive only."},{"t":"p","s":"Four instruments, descending force: statute, administrative rule, DIFS bulletin, pending bill. Michigan has no matching rule for siding or shingles; rule 500 point twenty two oh two bans discretionary clauses. DIFS investigates and disciplines licensees; a complaint is leverage and a record, not an order to pay. The ordinary contract statute of limitations is 6 years, MCL 600.5807, but a fire policy runs its own 1 year clock, tolled from notice until formal denial."},{"t":"p","s":"## Policy interpretation"},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan enforces a policy exactly as written. Rory versus Continental, 473 Mich 457, two thousand five, holds a court cannot refuse an unambiguous term as unreasonable. Wilkie, 469 Mich 41, two thousand three, killed reasonable expectations outright. Klapp, 468 Mich 459, two thousand three, says a policy is ambiguous when its provisions are capable of conflicting interpretations; construing it against the insurer is a last resort, after extrinsic evidence fails."},{"t":"p","s":"Heniser, 449 Mich 155, nineteen ninety five, puts the burden on the insured to show the loss falls inside the policy; Hunt versus Drielick, 496 Mich 366, two thousand fourteen, then shifts the burden to the insurer to prove an exclusion, not Heniser for both. Kirschner, 459 Mich 587, nineteen ninety nine, says waiver and estoppel usually cannot broaden coverage never sold. Sunshine Motors, 209 Mich App 58, nineteen ninety five, enforces an anti concurrent causation clause written into an exclusion; Vanguard, 438 Mich 463, nineteen ninety one, is the Supreme Court declining to adopt concurrent causation."},{"t":"p","s":"Gavrilides, February first, twenty twenty two, requires something tangible and measurable in, on, or to the premises, and separately enforced the virus exclusion. Every fire policy must pay at least actual cash value, MCL 500.2833, subsection one, subdivision a. Salesin, 229 Mich App 346, nineteen ninety eight, puts overhead and profit in an actual cash value payment when a homeowner is reasonably likely to need a general contractor, hired or not. Bulletin twenty twenty four dash twenty six dash INS requires a standalone, optional, premium reduced endorsement before depreciating labor, taxes, fees, or overhead and profit, personal lines issued or renewed on or after July first, twenty twenty five."},{"t":"p","s":"## Standard fire policy"},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2833 requires nineteen mandatory provisions, a through s. Subsection a: actual cash value floor. Subsections h and i: the insured cancels anytime, an insurer cancelling mails notice 10 days ahead, minimum earned premium is pro rata or 25 dollars, whichever is greater. Subsection j: if the insured fails to render proof of loss, the mortgagee, on notice, has 60 days to render its own. Subsection m, appraisal: 20 days to name your appraiser, fifteen more to agree an umpire, any two of three sign to set the amount. Subsection p: payable within 30 days of proof of loss amount. Subsection q: suit within 1 year after the loss, or the policy's period if longer, tolled from notice to formal denial. Subsection s: effective twelve oh one a m."},{"t":"p","s":"Two escrow statutes look alike and are not. MCL 500.2845 covers counties under four hundred twenty five thousand, capping the 25 percent withholding at 12,000 dollars from two thousand fifteen, adjusted every January first. MCL 500.2227 covers larger counties, or a fifty thousand plus city, adds vandalism, wind, hail, and its base jumped to twenty four thousand on July first, twenty twenty four, adjusted every July first; Bulletin 2026 dash sixteen dash INS sets it at twenty five thousand ninety for the year beginning July first, 2026."},{"t":"p","s":"## Bad faith and unfair trade practices"},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2006: pay on time or pay 12 percent interest. Subsection three gives the insurer 30 days after receiving a claim to specify what a satisfactory proof of loss needs; a partial claim is timely if paid within 60 days of that partial proof. Subsection four starts 12 percent simple interest 60 days after satisfactory proof of loss, for the insured or anyone directly entitled to benefits; the reasonably in dispute defense lives only in the second sentence, for third party tort claimants."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2026 lists fourteen unfair practices DIFS can punish, only as a course of conduct showing a persistent tendency, never an isolated incident, and no private lawsuit lies under it. The fines sit two sections later, in twenty thirty eight: up to one thousand per violation, ten thousand aggregate, or five thousand per violation and fifty thousand aggregate for knowing violations in a 6 month period. Twenty forty adds twenty thousand per violation for defying a cease and desist order. Young, 139 Mich App 600, nineteen eighty four, confirms the insured can privately sue to recover the 2006 interest penalty, just not a tort claim built on either section."},{"t":"p","s":"Kewin, 409 Mich 401, nineteen eighty, killed first party bad faith as its own tort, leaving only contract benefits. Commercial Union, 426 Mich 127, nineteen eighty six, is where bad faith survives: a liability insurer's duty to settle within limits, arbitrary, reckless, indifferent, or intentional disregard. Griswold, 276 Mich App 551, two thousand seven, and Nickola, 500 Mich 115, two thousand seventeen, confirm the 12 percent belongs to a first party insured regardless of dispute. Smith versus Globe Life, 460 Mich 446, nineteen ninety nine, let that claim proceed then; today MCL 445.904, subsection three, bars it for conduct on or after March twenty eighth, two thousand one. Senate Bill two forty five is still pending, not law."},{"t":"p","s":"## Appraisal and suit limits"},{"t":"p","s":"Appraisal decides how much is owed, never whether it is owed, Kwaiser, 190 Mich App 482, nineteen ninety one. The 20 day clock runs from receipt of the written demand, the 15 day clock from the appraisers' failure to agree, neither from the date of loss. Cantina, 349 Mich App 682, twenty twenty four, held that paying part of a claim without formally denying is an admission opening the whole thing to appraisal. Maksym, February twenty fourth, 2026, narrowed that: paying items you agree are covered while expressly denying others is not a partial payment, denied items stay out of appraisal until coverage is resolved."},{"t":"p","s":"White, 293 Mich App 419, twenty eleven, allows a contingency fee appraiser if the carrier is told; a party appraiser need only be independent, not the umpire's tougher impartial standard. Dupree, 497 Mich 1, twenty fourteen, confirms an award reopens only for bad faith, fraud, misconduct, or manifest mistake; replacement cost still needs proof repairs were completed. Tolling from notice to formal denial comes from Tom Thomas, 396 Mich 588, nineteen seventy six. Smitham, 297 Mich App 537, twenty twelve, says a formal denial must be explicit and unequivocal, a policy cannot condition tolling on it, void under MCL 500.2860, and a partial payment alone does not restart the clock."},{"t":"p","s":"## Adjuster and public adjuster licensing"},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.1222 requires a license to adjust, advertise, or hold yourself out as an adjuster; subsection two lists exactly five exemptions, an attorney, a marine average adjuster, an insurer's employee, a producer with granted claim authority, and a narrow supervised data entry role, no contractor exemption. MCL 500.1224, subsection four, is a categorical bar: no adjuster license to anyone employed by, owning stock in, an officer or director of, or connected in any manner with a fire repair contractor."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.1226, subsection three, caps a public adjuster's fee at 10 percent of what the insurer pays in settlement. Subsection four requires a DIFS approved contract form before services begin; a contract signed within 48 hours of the loss is voidable for 10 days. Summary suspension, if the licensee asks for a hearing, gets one not later than 20 days, a decision within thirty more. Under twelve forty four, fines run to one thousand per violation, or five thousand if knowing, hard capped at fifty thousand per order; defying a cease and desist order runs to twenty thousand per violation, capped at one hundred thousand."},{"t":"p","s":"Tredenick, unpublished, twenty twenty one, denied a public adjuster on an 8 percent contract both the fee and a fallback quantum meruit payment, for using a contract form DIFS never approved, missing the mandatory 48 hour voidability clause. DIFS revoked Guldshtadt's license April first, 2026, and fined him 50,000 dollars, for contracts charging 35 percent, roughly triple the cap, and a connection to a fire repair contractor."},{"t":"p","s":"## Claims clocks and DIFS"},{"t":"p","s":"The Michigan claim calendar, in order: 30 days from receipt of the claim for the insurer to specify what a satisfactory proof of loss needs. 30 days from proof of the amount of loss for payment to be due. 60 days from satisfactory proof of loss before 12 percent interest starts. 10 days' mailed notice to cancel a fire policy mid term. 1 year, tolled from notice to formal denial, to sue."},{"t":"p","s":"The Essential Insurance Act adds a longer layer: termination for anything but nonpayment needs 30 days' written notice, or twenty within the policy's first 55 days, matching the insurer's filed rules. A carrier can nonrenew on claim history: three paid claims in the preceding 3 years. The statute still prints three thousand excluding weather related claims and four thousand including them, but Bulletin twenty twenty four dash oh two dash INS certified new minimums as of January first, two thousand twenty four: five thousand exclusive of weather related claims, six thousand six hundred including them. Fannie Mae's initial release is the greater of forty thousand or thirty three percent of proceeds, only 25 percent if delinquent thirty one plus days, capped at ten thousand."},{"t":"p","s":"## Contractors, liens, and assignment of benefits"},{"t":"p","s":"An unlicensed builder cannot sue for payment or record a lien, MCL 339.2412. Epps, 498 Mich 518, twenty fifteen, held a contract with an unlicensed builder voidable, not void, and only for an innocent homeowner; the statute is a shield, not a sword to claw back money paid. Home lien rights need a written contract with the license number, no dollar floor, MCL 570.1114. A subcontractor or supplier serves notice of furnishing within 20 days, a laborer thirty, a direct contractor neither. Record the lien within 90 days of last furnishing, foreclose within 1 year."},{"t":"p","s":"Post loss assignment of an already accrued claim needs no carrier consent, from Roger Williams versus Carrington in eighteen eighty, restated in Shah, 324 Mich App 182, twenty eighteen. No published Michigan property case gives an assignee contractor the 12 percent as its own right; at best it is enforced derivatively. Small claims tops out at 7000 dollars, neither side gets a lawyer unless removed first. The home improvement financing act gives one business day, to five p m, for installment contracts over 300 dollars; the solicitation act gives three business days, to midnight, for anything solicited at the door."},{"t":"p","s":"## Essential Insurance Act and market"},{"t":"p","s":"Chapter twenty one, the Essential Insurance Act, added nineteen seventy nine. MCL 500.2117 is the must offer, must renew rule: an insurer cannot refuse or limit home insurance except on ten listed grounds, physical condition tied to a real code standard, claim history at renewal, vacancy over 60 days, a changed adjacent hazard, never traffic patterns. Every underwriting rule must be written, filed, and applied uniformly."},{"t":"p","s":"Termination covers cancellation and nonrenewal under one definition, on the thirty and twenty day notice rules above. Redlining is banned outright, MCL 500.2108. Chapter twenty nine creates the FAIR plan: every insurer must belong and serve every qualified applicant, insurance to value floors of 80 percent of replacement cost on a replacement cost policy, 100 percent of market value on a repair cost policy. The pool's fraud disqualifier needs a denied claim over 2000 dollars that also exceeds 15 percent of coverage in force under a repair cost policy, or 10 percent under a replacement cost policy, a test the voluntary market does not carry."},{"t":"p","s":"## Bar exam and legal foundations"},{"t":"p","s":"Rule one, subsection C requires sixty semester hours or ninety quarter hours before starting law school, not a finished bachelor's degree. Rule two, subsection B treats an American Bar Association approved law degree as automatically reputable and qualified; anything else needs a discretionary board waiver. The bar exam is the Uniform Bar Exam: multistate bar exam 50 percent, essay 30 percent, two performance test items 20 percent, on a four hundred point scale. Michigan's passing score is two hundred sixty eight, valid for 3 years once achieved; its professional responsibility score is eighty five, with no Michigan time limit on when that exam was taken. A score transfers in from another jurisdiction only if earned within the 3 years immediately preceding. Michigan moves to the next generation exam starting July twenty twenty eight; February twenty twenty eight is the last legacy exam."},{"t":"p","s":"No standalone insurance law subject exists, it lives inside contracts, torts, and evidence. Michigan recites five contract elements, not the national four: competent parties, proper subject matter, legal consideration, mutuality of agreement, mutuality of obligation, Hess, 265 Mich App 582, two thousand five. Parol evidence cannot contradict a clear written contract. A condition precedent is a fact the parties intend must happen before a right to performance exists, Harbor Park Market, 277 Mich App 126, two thousand seven; a missed proof of loss deadline bars recovery regardless of prejudice, absent waiver or estoppel. Kewin ties expectation damages to Hadley versus Baxendale: recovery caps at policy value, no emotional distress. Titan versus Hyten, 491 Mich 547, twenty twelve, lists six fraud elements, no duty to investigate. Kandil Elsayed, 512 Mich 95, twenty twenty three, restates negligence as duty, breach, causation, harm, moving open and obvious into breach and comparative fault. Tort statute of limitations 3 years, contract six."},{"t":"p","s":"## The traps"},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.1221 does not exist; the adjuster prohibition is 1222. Klapp is 468 Mich 459, not four sixteen. Kwaiser is 190 Mich App 482, not page four. Kewin's famous phrase, wholly separate and independent, appears nowhere in the opinion; the real holding declines to make bad faith breach an independent and separately actionable tort."},{"t":"p","s":"Cantina never meant any partial payment opens a whole claim to appraisal, Maksym narrowed that hard. Tom Thomas never held a 1 year suit limit unreasonable, it rested only on tolling, and Rory overruled only its reasonableness dicta, not the tolling holding. Guldshtadt's license ended revoked, not merely suspended, and DIFS charged him under subsection three of 1224 plus subsection two of twelve forty two, not the categorical subsection four bar. And the statute's three thousand and four thousand nonrenewal dollars are superseded by the bulletin certified five thousand and six thousand six hundred."},{"t":"p","s":"Say the right Michigan number, know which case said it, and walk in ready."}],"words":2520,"questions":0},{"id":"01_michigan-legal-system_lecture","track":"law","title":"Michigan Legal System and Method","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the first chapter of the Michigan insurance law track, and I want to be honest with you about what it is. It is not a statute and it is not a case. It is the map. Every other chapter in this track sits on top of it, and if the map is solid, everything after it gets easier. If it is not, you will spend the later chapters memorizing things that never quite connect."},{"t":"p","s":"So here is what the map has on it. Which court hears an insurance case. Which document beats which when two sources disagree. How to read a citation so you can go find the real text yourself instead of taking somebody's word for it. What the Department of Insurance and Financial Services really is, and what it is not. And the clocks and boundaries, statutes of limitations and unauthorized practice of law, that sit underneath every claim fight you will study later."},{"t":"p","s":"I am going to teach you eleven ideas, one at a time. After most of them I will ask you a question and then go quiet while you answer it out loud. Answer out loud. The answer you can say is the answer you own."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"Which trial court, decided by the dollar amount."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan splits its trial level jurisdiction mostly two ways: by how much money is in controversy, and by who the defendant is. Start with the money."},{"t":"p","s":"The district court has exclusive jurisdiction over civil actions where the amount in controversy does not exceed 25,000 dollars. That comes straight from MCL 600.8301. So a small water loss dispute, a few 1000 dollars of contents, a fight over a deductible, that often starts and ends in district court."},{"t":"p","s":"Cross the 25,000 dollar line and you are in circuit court, Michigan's court of general jurisdiction. MCL 600.605 says circuit courts have original jurisdiction to hear and determine all civil claims and remedies, except where exclusive jurisdiction is given by the constitution or a statute to some other court."},{"t":"p","s":"Hear what that exception is doing. Circuit court is the default. It gets everything unless something specifically takes it away. That is what general jurisdiction means."},{"t":"p","s":"So most first party property insurance lawsuits, a homeowner suing a carrier over a denied or underpaid fire, water, or wind claim above 25,000 dollars, get filed in circuit court."},{"t":"p","s":"And circuit court shows up inside the appraisal process itself, which is worth holding onto now because you will meet it again. If the two appraisers cannot agree on a neutral umpire within 15 days, either side can ask a judge of the circuit court, in the county where the loss happened or where the property sits, to pick the umpire. That is written directly into the standard fire policy statute."},{"t":"p","s":"Two numbers so far, and they do different jobs. 25,000 dollars is the district court ceiling. 15 days is the umpire clock. Do not let them blur together."},{"t":"q","s":"Question for you. A homeowner is fighting over 18,000 dollars of contents. Which trial court, and what is the number that decides it. Say it out loud."},{"t":"a","s":"District court, because eighteen thousand does not exceed 25,000 dollars, and the section is MCL 600.8301."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"The Court of Claims, and the trap inside it."},{"t":"p","s":"This is the court that trips people up, so let me name the trap before I explain the court. The word claims is doing something dishonest to your ear. You work in insurance claims. This court has nothing to do with insurance claims."},{"t":"p","s":"The Court of Claims is a statewide court, housed at the Court of Appeals, and it has exclusive jurisdiction over money, equitable, or declaratory claims against the State of Michigan or its departments and officers."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the whole key phrase: against the state."},{"t":"p","s":"So think about who you actually sue in this work. State Farm. Auto Owners. A third party administrator. Every one of those is a private company. Suing a private insurance company is not a claim against the state, so it belongs in circuit court. The Court of Claims only comes in if you are suing a state agency itself."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. You are suing an insurance carrier for 200,000 dollars on a denied fire claim. Does the word claim put you in the Court of Claims."},{"t":"a","s":"No. Circuit court. The Court of Claims is about who the defendant is, the State of Michigan, not about the word claim."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"The two courts above, and how you get to each one."},{"t":"p","s":"Above circuit court sits the Court of Appeals, the intermediate appellate court. The thing to know is the phrase jurisdiction as of right, which means no permission needed. The Court of Appeals has jurisdiction as of right over final judgments and final orders from the circuit court, the Court of Claims, and the probate court."},{"t":"p","s":"Some other orders are not as of right. An appeal from a guilty plea, or certain interlocutory orders, meaning orders in the middle of a case rather than at the end, are only reviewable if the Court of Appeals grants leave."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is why you should care about that court specifically. Nearly every published Michigan insurance case you will study in the chapters ahead, the ones setting the rules on policy interpretation, bad faith, and appraisal, came out of the Court of Appeals."},{"t":"p","s":"At the very top sits the Michigan Supreme Court, the court of last resort. It generally takes cases by leave, meaning it chooses which appeals to hear, and once it rules, that ruling binds every lower court in the state. Nickola versus MIC General, decided by the Supreme Court in May of twenty seventeen, is exactly that kind of case."},{"t":"p","s":"So the pair to hold is as of right versus by leave. Court of Appeals, as of right from a final judgment. Supreme Court, by leave, its choice."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Let me walk one case up the ladder so it stops being abstract."},{"t":"p","s":"A homeowner's 80,000 dollar fire claim gets denied."},{"t":"p","s":"Eighty thousand is above the 25,000 dollar district court ceiling, so it starts in circuit court."},{"t":"p","s":"The insurer wins summary disposition, which we will get to, and the homeowner appeals. That goes to the Court of Appeals as a matter of right, because what is being appealed is a circuit court final judgment."},{"t":"p","s":"And then it stops, unless the Supreme Court agrees to grant leave."},{"t":"p","s":"Notice what that example is really teaching. The dollar amount picked the starting court. The finality of the judgment made the first appeal automatic. And nothing after that is automatic at all."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Published versus unpublished, and what unpublished actually means."},{"t":"p","s":"This is governed by Michigan Court Rule seven point two one five, subsection C. Here is the rule almost word for word. An unpublished opinion is not precedentially binding under the rule of stare decisis. Unpublished opinions should not be cited for propositions of law where published authority already exists, and a party who does cite one must explain why and how it is relevant."},{"t":"p","s":"Compare that to a published opinion, which has precedential effect under stare decisis, and that effect does not go away just because somebody asks the Supreme Court to review it."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the plain English, because this is where people go wrong in both directions. Unpublished does not mean useless, and it does not mean forbidden. It means not binding. A trial court can find an unpublished opinion persuasive and follow its reasoning, and it is equally free to reach a different conclusion."},{"t":"p","s":"Tredenick, a twenty twenty one Court of Appeals decision out of the adjuster licensing world, docket number three four nine eight eight seven, is exactly this kind of opinion. Unpublished. Persuasive only. Never treated as settled law. You will meet it again in the licensing chapter, and when you do, that is the label it carries."},{"t":"p","s":"One more piece. Publication is not automatic. The court rule lists eight standards, things like establishing a new rule of law, resolving a first impression question about a statute, or reversing existing law. If none of them apply, the opinion is deemed unpublished by default."},{"t":"p","s":"And when you want the actual text of any opinion, published or not, or the current Michigan Court Rules themselves, go to courts dot michigan dot gov."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Somebody hands you an unpublished Court of Appeals opinion that says exactly what you want it to say. What is it worth in court."},{"t":"a","s":"It is persuasive, not binding. The judge may follow it or may not, and you should not lean on it where published authority already covers the point. If you do cite it, you have to explain why and how it is relevant."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"The instrument hierarchy, which is the single most useful idea in this chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"When two sources disagree, you need to know which one wins. In Michigan insurance work you will hit four types constantly, and they rank."},{"t":"p","s":"At the top, a statute. An MCL section, passed by the legislature and signed into law. That is primary, binding law. It changes only if the legislature amends or repeals it."},{"t":"p","s":"Below that, an administrative rule. One of the Michigan Administrative Code's R numbers, adopted by an agency like DIFS under delegated authority. It is binding, but it is subordinate to the statute that authorized it, and it can be struck down if the agency exceeded that authority."},{"t":"p","s":"Below that, a DIFS bulletin. Interpretive guidance, explaining how the department reads an existing statute or rule. It is not independent law. The department can reissue it or withdraw it at any time, with no legislative process at all. Bulletin twenty twenty four dash twenty six dash INS, the one limiting labor depreciation on actual cash value payments, is a bulletin. Not a statute. It has a very real practical effect on what carriers do, and it is still a bulletin."},{"t":"p","s":"And at the very bottom, a pending bill, which has zero legal force until it passes both chambers and is signed. Senate Bill two forty five, proposing a first party bad faith cause of action, is the live example. It was reported favorably out of committee and referred to the Committee of the Whole. It has not passed either chamber. It is not law. Treat it as pending until that changes, and be suspicious of anybody who talks about it as though it already happened."},{"t":"p","s":"Now a trap inside this idea, and it is a good one because the number looks so promising. Rule 500 point two two zero two sounds like it should be a matching rule, the kind of thing you would want when a carrier will only pay for half a roof of siding. It is not. It is actually titled discretionary clauses prohibited. Michigan has no matching statute and no matching administrative rule at all."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. A carrier's adjuster tells you a DIFS bulletin requires them to do something, and the statute plainly says otherwise. Which one controls, and why."},{"t":"a","s":"The statute. A bulletin is interpretive guidance about how the department reads the law, it is not independent law, and the department can withdraw it tomorrow. Statute beats rule, rule beats bulletin, and a pending bill is worth nothing at all."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"Reading a citation, so you can go look it up yourself."},{"t":"p","s":"Take MCL 500.2833, subsection one, subdivision m."},{"t":"p","s":"500 is the chapter number, and chapter 500 is the Insurance Code of nineteen fifty six, formally Act two eighteen of nineteen fifty six. 2833 is the section. The one in parentheses is subsection one. The m is subdivision m. Read out loud, that is act two eighteen of nineteen fifty six, section 2833, subsection one, subdivision m."},{"t":"p","s":"The same logic works for chapter 600, which is the Revised Judicature Act of nineteen sixty one, Act two thirty six of nineteen sixty one. That is the one governing Michigan's courts, jurisdiction, and civil procedure, including statutes of limitations and unauthorized practice of law. So when you see a section starting with 600, you are in the courts and procedure book. 500, you are in the insurance book. That one habit will orient you instantly."},{"t":"p","s":"Court rules follow a similar pattern. MCR 2.116, subsection C, subsection ten. Chapter two is civil procedure. Subrule C lists the grounds for summary disposition. Sub subrule ten is the specific ground about no genuine issue of material fact."},{"t":"p","s":"Case citations work differently. Instead of a chapter and section you get a volume and a page in an official reporter. 409 Mich 401 means volume 409 of the Michigan Reports, page 401. And here is the tell worth memorizing: Mich with no App always signals a Supreme Court opinion. That one is Kewin versus Massachusetts Mutual Life Insurance, decided in nineteen eighty."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the pair. 276 Mich App 551. Mich App means the Michigan Appeals Reports, which is Court of Appeals opinions. That one is Griswold Properties versus Lexington Insurance, decided in two thousand seven."},{"t":"p","s":"Mich, Supreme Court. Mich App, Court of Appeals. That is the whole trick, and it is two letters."},{"t":"p","s":"And an unpublished opinion never gets a reporter volume or page at all, which is why you cite it by docket number and decision date instead. That is why Tredenick came to you as docket three four nine eight eight seven rather than a volume and page."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. You see 276 Mich App 551, and you see 409 Mich 401. Which court decided each one, and how do you know."},{"t":"a","s":"276 Mich App 551 is the Court of Appeals, because of the App. 409 Mich 401 is the Supreme Court, because Mich with no App means Supreme Court."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"What DIFS can do, and what it cannot."},{"t":"p","s":"People badly misunderstand this one, and the misunderstanding costs homeowners real time."},{"t":"p","s":"Start with what the department says about itself. DIFS provides consumer information, investigates consumer complaints, and works to assist consumers in resolving those complaints whenever possible. Listen to that phrasing. Assist in resolving whenever possible. And the department encourages you to try resolving a dispute with the company directly first."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the teeth, because it does have some. When you file a complaint, DIFS investigates. If it finds that a licensed adjuster, solicitor, or insurance counselor violated the Insurance Code's licensing chapter, the director can order real consequences after a hearing. A cease and desist order. A civil fine. A refund of overcharges. Restitution to the insured for losses caused by that violation. And suspension or revocation of the license."},{"t":"p","s":"That is a serious list. Now here is the boundary, and this is the part to hold."},{"t":"p","s":"DIFS cannot act as your personal attorney. It cannot make a binding factual determination about who is right in a disputed coverage question. And it does not order a private insurer to simply pay a claim the way a court judgment can."},{"t":"p","s":"Even the restitution power is narrower than it sounds. It only reaches a licensee who was found, after a DIFS hearing, to have violated the licensing chapter. That is not DIFS stepping in as a judge to award general breach of contract or bad faith damages in a routine coverage fight."},{"t":"p","s":"So here is the practical sorting rule. If what you want is money out of an insurer, the tool is a lawsuit in circuit court, or appraisal where it applies. DIFS is the right tool for a licensing violation or a pattern of unfair practices. It is the wrong tool if what you want is a payment ordered."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Homeowner says the carrier underpaid her fire claim by 40,000 dollars and she wants DIFS to make them pay it. What do you tell her."},{"t":"a","s":"DIFS will investigate, and it may act against a licensee who violated the licensing chapter, but it will not decide the disputed coverage question for her and it will not order the carrier to pay the claim. For the money, the tool is circuit court, or appraisal if it applies."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"The shape of a lawsuit, start to finish."},{"t":"p","s":"You do not need to litigate one. You need to recognize where a case is standing when somebody tells you about it."},{"t":"p","s":"It starts with a complaint, where the person suing states the facts, the legal claims, and what they want. The other side files an answer, admitting or denying each allegation and raising defenses, maybe a statute of limitations defense, maybe an exclusion argument."},{"t":"p","s":"Then discovery. Both sides exchange documents like the policy and the claim file. They send written interrogatories under rule two point three zero nine. They take depositions under rule two point three zero six, which is sworn out of court testimony."},{"t":"p","s":"After discovery, either side can move for summary disposition, asking the judge to decide some or all of the case without a trial. The most common ground in insurance fights is the one I gave you earlier, MCR 2.116, subsection C, subsection ten. Except as to the amount of damages, there is no genuine issue as to any material fact, and the moving party is entitled to judgment as a matter of law."},{"t":"p","s":"In plain words: if the facts that matter are not really in dispute, and the law clearly points one way, the judge can end the case without a jury."},{"t":"p","s":"And if the case survives that, it goes to trial. But a jury is not automatic, and this is the detail people lose. Under Michigan Court Rule two point 508, a party must file a written jury demand, as a separate document, within 28 days after the answer, and pay the jury fee. Miss that window and the issues are tried by the judge alone, which is a bench trial."},{"t":"p","s":"Hear all three parts of that requirement. Written. Separate document. 28 days after the answer. Plus the fee."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"The two clocks, and why the short one is not as short as it looks."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan's default statute of limitations for an ordinary breach of contract claim is 6 years. That is MCL 600.5807, subsection nine, the catch all for contract actions not described elsewhere in that section."},{"t":"p","s":"A fire insurance policy does not get that 6 year window, because the standard fire policy statute writes its own, much shorter clock directly into the policy. MCL 500.2833, subsection one, subdivision q, requires suit within 1 year after the loss, or the time period specified in the policy, whichever is longer."},{"t":"p","s":"Stop on that phrase, whichever is longer. It makes 1 year a floor, not a ceiling. A policy cannot shorten it below 1 year. A longer contractual period would control."},{"t":"p","s":"6 years for ordinary contract. 1 year floor for the fire policy. That is the pair, and the exam of real life will hand you the wrong one."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the tolling rule built into that same subdivision, because it changes the math completely. The clock pauses entirely from the moment the insured notifies the insurer until the insurer formally denies liability. Tolled just means paused. Not running at all."},{"t":"p","s":"Let me do the arithmetic with you."},{"t":"p","s":"A pipe bursts in a basement on March first. The homeowner reports it that same day. The insurer investigates for 4 months and does not formally deny the claim until July first."},{"t":"p","s":"Her 1 year clock did not run during those 4 months. It was tolled from notice until formal denial. So her effective filing window runs close to a full year from July first, not from the March first date of loss."},{"t":"p","s":"If you had counted from the date of loss, you would have told her she was nearly 4 months worse off than she actually is. That is the practical value of knowing one sentence of one subdivision."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Loss on January tenth, reported January tenth, formally denied on June tenth. Roughly when does her window close, and what is the rule that gets you there."},{"t":"a","s":"Roughly June tenth of the following year. The 1 year clock under MCL 500.2833, subsection one, subdivision q, was tolled from notice until formal denial, so those 5 months did not count against her."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"Unauthorized practice of law, and two statutes that are not interchangeable."},{"t":"p","s":"Do not confuse these two, because they solve completely different problems, and the confusion is the kind that gets a contractor in trouble."},{"t":"p","s":"The first is the general rule, MCL 600.916. It says a person shall not practice law, shall not lead others to believe they are authorized to practice law, and shall not call themselves an attorney, unless actually licensed to practice law in Michigan. The penalty is contempt of the supreme court and of the circuit court of the county where the violation happened, punishable as provided by law. The statute carves out one narrow exception: an attorney licensed in another state, temporarily in Michigan, handling one specific matter, does not violate it."},{"t":"p","s":"The second lives deep inside the insurance code. MCL 500.1222, subsection two, subdivision a, exempts a person admitted to the practice of law in Michigan from needing a separate adjuster's license. That subsection lists exactly five exemptions. The attorney. A marine average adjuster. An insurer's own employee adjusting under the insurer's policy. A licensed producer with claim authority. And a supervised data entry clerk."},{"t":"p","s":"Now hear the difference, because it is the whole idea. 600 point 916 stops a non lawyer from practicing law or pretending to be a lawyer. 500 point 1222, subsection two, subdivision a, only tells you that a lawyer who is already licensed does not also need a separate adjuster license."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is the trap that matters to you personally. A contractor cannot borrow that exemption by working alongside an attorney, or by being related to one. It belongs to the licensed attorney personally, and to no one else."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. A contractor says he can handle claims for homeowners without an adjuster license because his brother is a lawyer. Which statute is he misreading, and what is wrong with it."},{"t":"a","s":"He is misreading MCL 500.1222, subsection two, subdivision a. That exemption belongs to the licensed attorney personally. It is not transferable, not to a relative and not to a coworker."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the rapid fire recap. I am going to say these fast. You are not learning them now, you are checking which ones you already have."},{"t":"p","s":"District court, 25,000 dollars or less, MCL 600.8301. Circuit court above that, general jurisdiction, MCL 600.605. Circuit court picks the umpire if the appraisers cannot agree in 15 days. Court of Claims, only against the State of Michigan. Court of Appeals, as of right from final judgments of circuit, Court of Claims, and probate. Supreme Court, by leave, binds everyone, Nickola versus MIC General, May twenty seventeen. Michigan Court Rule seven point two one five, subsection C, unpublished is not binding, persuasive only, eight publication standards, unpublished by default if none apply. Tredenick, twenty twenty one, docket three four nine eight eight seven, unpublished. Hierarchy, statute, then administrative rule, then DIFS bulletin, then pending bill with no force. Rule 500 point two two zero two is discretionary clauses prohibited, not matching, and Michigan has no matching rule. Bulletin twenty twenty four dash twenty six dash INS, labor depreciation, is a bulletin. Senate Bill two forty five, pending, not law. Chapter 500 is the Insurance Code of nineteen fifty six, Act two eighteen. Chapter 600 is the Revised Judicature Act of nineteen sixty one, Act two thirty six. MCR 2.116, subsection C, subsection ten, summary disposition, no genuine issue of material fact. Interrogatories two point three zero nine, depositions two point three zero six. Jury demand, MCR 2.508, written, separate document, 28 days after the answer, plus the fee. Mich means Supreme Court, Mich App means Court of Appeals. 409 Mich 401 is Kewin, nineteen eighty. 276 Mich App 551 is Griswold Properties versus Lexington, two thousand seven. DIFS can order cease and desist, civil fine, refund of overcharges, restitution, suspension or revocation, and cannot be your attorney, cannot decide a disputed coverage question, cannot order a claim paid. Contract statute of limitations, 6 years, MCL 600.5807, subsection nine. Fire policy suit, 1 year floor, MCL 500.2833, subsection one, subdivision q, whichever is longer, tolled from notice to formal denial. Unauthorized practice of law, MCL 600.916, contempt, one exception for an out of state attorney on one matter. Adjuster license exemption, MCL 500.1222, subsection two, subdivision a, five exemptions, attorney, marine average adjuster, insurer's employee, producer with claim authority, supervised data entry clerk."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the whole map. Courts by dollar amount and by defendant. Precedent that depends on publication. An instrument hierarchy running from statute down to a bill that is worth nothing. Citations that tell you exactly where to look. A regulator that investigates but is not your lawyer and cannot order payment. The stages of a lawsuit. And two very different clocks, 6 years and 1 year, with a tolling rule that quietly gives months back."},{"t":"p","s":"Carry this chapter into every one that follows. If any of the questions I asked gave you trouble, that is your signal for where to drill."}],"words":4186,"questions":8},{"id":"02_policy-interpretation_lecture","track":"law","title":"Policy Interpretation","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the chapter that decides every other chapter. Before you can argue a deadline, a bad faith claim, or an appraisal fight, you have to know how a Michigan court actually reads the words in the contract. Everything downstream assumes you already know this."},{"t":"p","s":"And there is a particular reason this chapter is worth slowing down for. Most of what you will hear on a job site about what a policy means is wrong, confidently. The homeowner has a theory, the adjuster has a theory, and the contractor has a theory, and Michigan law agrees with almost none of them. What I am going to give you is the actual order of operations a court uses."},{"t":"p","s":"Eleven ideas, in the order Michigan courts apply them. After most of them I will ask you a question and then go quiet. Answer out loud."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"Rory, and the sentence that ends most arguments."},{"t":"p","s":"The single most important rule in Michigan insurance law comes from Rory versus Continental Insurance Company, Michigan Supreme Court, July twenty eighth, two thousand five. The dispute itself was small, a 1 year deadline to sue under an auto policy, but the holding reaches every policy in the state."},{"t":"p","s":"Unambiguous contracts, including insurance policies, are enforced as written unless a provision violates a law or public policy."},{"t":"p","s":"Then the court went further, and this second half is the part that does the work. Judicial determinations of reasonableness are an invalid basis to refuse to enforce an unambiguous contract provision."},{"t":"p","s":"Sit with what that means. A judge cannot look at a clear policy term, decide it seems unfair, and rewrite it. Before Rory, lower courts had been using exactly that door, striking policy language they considered unreasonable. Rory shut it."},{"t":"p","s":"So if you remember one sentence from this chapter, make it this one. Unambiguous language wins, and fairness is not a legal argument."},{"t":"p","s":"That is a hard thing to say to a homeowner standing in a burned kitchen. It is still the law."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"Wilkie, and a doctrine Michigan threw out."},{"t":"p","s":"This one matters because other states do have it, and if you read national material you will absorb it by accident."},{"t":"p","s":"In some states an insured can argue that even though the policy says one thing, a reasonable policyholder expected something broader, and the court should honor that expectation instead of the words on the page. That is the reasonable expectations doctrine."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan does not have it. Wilkie versus Auto Owners Insurance Company, Michigan Supreme Court, July sixteenth, two thousand three, held flatly that the rule of reasonable expectations has no application in Michigan, and overruled the prior cases that had recognized it."},{"t":"p","s":"So when a homeowner says, I always assumed my policy covered this, hear that for what it is. Sympathetic, sometimes genuinely sad, and not a legal theory in Michigan. It does not move an unambiguous exclusion an inch."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Homeowner says the policy is unfair and that nobody would have expected this exclusion. Name the two cases that dispose of both halves of that, and what each one says."},{"t":"a","s":"Rory, two thousand five, unambiguous provisions are enforced as written and a judge cannot refuse on reasonableness grounds. Wilkie, two thousand three, the reasonable expectations doctrine has no application in Michigan."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Klapp, ambiguity, and the order of operations everybody gets wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"If reasonable expectations does not work, what does an insured do when the policy language genuinely conflicts with itself? That is Klapp versus United Insurance Group Agency, Michigan Supreme Court, June eighteenth, two thousand three."},{"t":"p","s":"Memorize this citation, because it gets mangled constantly. Four hundred sixty eight Michigan four hundred fifty nine. Not four sixteen. Four fifty nine. Hear that pair, because the wrong number is the one people repeat."},{"t":"p","s":"Klapp defines ambiguity this way. A contract is ambiguous when its provisions are capable of conflicting interpretations. If two clauses in the same policy irreconcilably conflict, the policy is ambiguous."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the part almost everybody gets wrong, including people who cite this case for a living. Finding an ambiguity does not mean the insured wins."},{"t":"p","s":"Klapp sets an order of operations. First the factfinder looks at extrinsic evidence, meaning things outside the four corners of the policy. The application. Correspondence with the agent. Industry custom. Anything showing what the parties actually intended."},{"t":"p","s":"And if that evidence answers the question, that answer controls, even if it favors the insurance company."},{"t":"p","s":"Only after all of that fails does contra proferentem apply, the rule that ambiguous language is construed against whoever drafted it, which is almost always the insurer. Klapp calls contra proferentem a tie breaker, used only after conventional interpretation, including extrinsic evidence, has been tried and found wanting."},{"t":"p","s":"So the trap answer is: I found an ambiguity, therefore the insured wins. The real answer is: I found an ambiguity, therefore we go look at extrinsic evidence, and only if that comes up empty does the insured get the benefit of the tie breaker."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. You find two clauses in a policy that irreconcilably conflict. What happens next, and what does not happen next."},{"t":"a","s":"What happens next is extrinsic evidence, the application, agent correspondence, industry custom, to find what the parties actually intended, and that controls even if it helps the carrier. What does not happen is the insured automatically winning. Contra proferentem is a last resort tie breaker."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Who proves what. A two step relay race."},{"t":"p","s":"Step one belongs to the insured. Heniser versus Frankenmuth Mutual Insurance Company, Michigan Supreme Court, July sixth, nineteen ninety five, holds that it is the insured's burden to establish that the claim falls within the terms of the policy."},{"t":"p","s":"Step two comes only after step one is satisfied: whether that coverage is negated by an exclusion."},{"t":"p","s":"Now read Heniser carefully, because this is where a lot of study material goes wrong. Heniser gives you the order, and the insured's half of the burden, and nothing more. It never says the insurer carries the burden on the exclusion."},{"t":"p","s":"For that half you cite a different case. Hunt versus Drielick, four hundred ninety six Michigan three hundred sixty six, two thousand fourteen. Hunt restates Heniser's test and then quotes an older case, Fresard, for the rule that the insurer should bear the burden of proving an absence of coverage."},{"t":"p","s":"Hunt also adds the counterweights, and they cut both ways. Exclusionary clauses are strictly construed in favor of the insured. But clear and specific exclusions must be enforced."},{"t":"p","s":"So the pair is: Heniser for step one, Hunt for step two. Two cases, two halves. If somebody cites Heniser for the insurer's burden, they have overstated it."},{"t":"p","s":"And the practical consequence is worth saying plainly. If the carrier cannot prove the exclusion fits the facts, the insured's initial showing of coverage stands and the claim is owed."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Which case gives you the insured's burden, which gives you the insurer's, and what is the mistake people make."},{"t":"a","s":"Heniser, nineteen ninety five, the insured proves the claim falls within the policy. Hunt versus Drielick, two thousand fourteen, the insurer bears the burden of proving an absence of coverage. The mistake is citing Heniser for both halves. It only does the first."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"An honest gap, and why I am telling you about it."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is a question that comes up in almost every real dispute. Suppose the insurer proves an exclusion applies, but that exclusion has its own exception, something like an ensuing loss clause. Whose burden is the exception?"},{"t":"p","s":"Logically it should be the insured's, since the insured is the one who benefits from it. But no controlling Michigan case squarely holding that was opened this session."},{"t":"p","s":"So know the logic, and do not quote a Michigan case name for it. If you attach a case name to a proposition that case does not hold, you will get caught, and everything else you said gets doubted with it. Being able to say I know the reasoning and I do not have Michigan authority for it is a stronger position than a confident wrong cite."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Kirschner, and the word usually."},{"t":"p","s":"This is where insureds try to argue their way into coverage that was never sold to them. Kirschner versus Process Design Associates, Michigan Supreme Court, May eleventh, nineteen ninety nine, is the controlling case."},{"t":"p","s":"The key word is usually, and you should notice it every single time you see this case cited."},{"t":"p","s":"Kirschner holds that the application of waiver and estoppel is limited, and usually the doctrines will not be applied to broaden coverage to protect the insured against risks that were not included in the policy or that were expressly excluded."},{"t":"p","s":"Why not? The court's own reason is the memorable part. An insurance company should not be required to pay for a loss for which it has charged no premium."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the whole logic in one line. You cannot get coverage for free just because someone at the insurance company said something careless on the phone."},{"t":"p","s":"But usually is not never, and the exam of real life will hand you the exception. Kirschner points to two situations where courts have let waiver or estoppel work. Where the insurance company misrepresented the terms of the policy. And where the insurer defended the insured in a liability case without reserving the right to later deny coverage."},{"t":"p","s":"Even then it takes a showing that the inequity to the insured outweighs the inequity to the insurer. And note this, because it tells you how narrow the opening is: Kirschner applied neither exception on its own facts."},{"t":"p","s":"Outside those two patterns, a verbal assurance from an adjuster almost never survives. Which is exactly why you get coverage commitments in writing. If an adjuster says do not worry, we will cover the detached garage, and there is no endorsement for it, that promise is not enough on its own."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Adjuster tells your homeowner on the phone that the detached garage is covered. It is not in the policy. What are the two Kirschner openings, and does a phone call fit either."},{"t":"a","s":"The two openings are the insurer misrepresenting the terms of the policy, and the insurer defending a liability case without a reservation of rights. A casual phone assurance is neither, and you would still have to show the inequity to the insured outweighs the inequity to the insurer."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"Salesin, overhead and profit, and the trap on both sides."},{"t":"p","s":"This is where restoration contractors and public adjusters spend a lot of energy, so let me give you both the win and the limit."},{"t":"p","s":"The case is Salesin versus State Farm Fire and Casualty Company, Michigan Court of Appeals, April twenty first, nineteen ninety eight, published."},{"t":"p","s":"Salesin repaired his own home instead of hiring a general contractor. State Farm argued that since he never actually paid a contractor's overhead and profit, he should not be paid for it."},{"t":"p","s":"The court rejected that. Not actually incurring the cost is, in its words, not dispositive. It adopted a test from a Pennsylvania case, Gilderman: repair or replacement costs include any cost the insured is reasonably likely to incur, and in some instances that includes a general contractor and his 20 percent overhead and profit."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the trap, and it runs the other direction from what contractors want. Salesin does not create an automatic rule that overhead and profit is always owed. The court said so directly. Gilderman does not support the position that overhead and profit should always and automatically be included in an actual cash value payment."},{"t":"p","s":"So what is the actual test? Whether a general contractor was reasonably likely to be needed for that scope. Not whether one was hired."},{"t":"p","s":"Picture the two ends of it. A single trade repair, say just replacing carpet, is a weak case. Nobody is reasonably likely to need a general contractor to coordinate one flooring crew. Now a multi trade rebuild, roofing, drywall, flooring, and paint in sequence, is exactly the situation Gilderman and Salesin describe, where a homeowner is reasonably likely to need someone coordinating trades. There, overhead and profit belongs in the payment regardless of who swings the hammer."},{"t":"p","s":"Match the argument to the scope of work, not to a blanket rule. A blanket rule is how you lose an argument you should have won."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Homeowner does the work himself on a four trade rebuild and the carrier strips overhead and profit because he hired nobody. What is your answer, and what is the answer you should not give."},{"t":"a","s":"The answer is Salesin: not incurring the cost is not dispositive, and the test is whether a general contractor was reasonably likely to be needed, which on a four trade sequence it is. The answer you should not give is that overhead and profit is always owed, because Salesin expressly says it is not automatic."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"When a covered peril and an excluded peril combine."},{"t":"p","s":"There are three competing legal tools fighting over this outcome, and you should be able to name all three before you pick one."},{"t":"p","s":"Concurrent causation says a covered peril's contribution alone triggers coverage. Efficient proximate cause says the dominant cause that started the chain controls the whole loss. And an anti concurrent causation clause, an A C C clause, is language the insurer writes into the exclusion to shut both doctrines off, by saying the exclusion applies regardless of any other cause, whether it acted before, during, or after the excluded peril."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan's controlling authority is Sunshine Motors Incorporated versus New Hampshire Insurance Company, Michigan Court of Appeals, February twenty second, nineteen ninety five, published."},{"t":"p","s":"Here are the facts, because they are easy to picture. A car dealership flooded when heavy rain combined with a piece of wood partially blocking the local drainage system. The insured argued the blocked drain was a separate, non excluded cause."},{"t":"p","s":"The court disagreed, and the reason is entirely about the policy language. The exclusion carried exactly the anti concurrent causation wording I just described: coverage excluded regardless of any other cause or event contributing concurrently or in any sequence to the loss. So whether the blocked drain was a direct or an indirect cause, the principal factor or merely a contributing factor, made no difference. The policy expressly excluded coverage either way."},{"t":"p","s":"Now let me be honest about the limits, because overselling this is how people lose credibility. Sunshine Motors is a Court of Appeals decision and it is carrier favorable."},{"t":"p","s":"But the Supreme Court has spoken on the underlying doctrine and went the same direction. In Vanguard Insurance Company versus Clarke, four hundred thirty eight Michigan four hundred sixty three, nineteen ninety one, a Court of Appeals panel used what the Supreme Court called the minority rule of concurrent causation to defeat an auto related exclusion in a homeowner's liability policy. The Supreme Court reversed, saying it discerned no compelling legal or policy basis for that doctrine to nullify an unambiguous exclusion."},{"t":"p","s":"Vanguard is a liability case, not a first party property case, so do not oversell it either. But between the two, no Michigan case adopting a broad, pro coverage concurrent causation doctrine, the way Florida's Sebo decision did, was found this session."},{"t":"p","s":"One argument does survive, and it is the practical one. You can try to segregate damage caused solely by the covered peril. That rests on the exclusion's own caused by wording, not on any Michigan case found this run, so do not claim a Michigan court has blessed it."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Direct physical loss, and a case that is widely misquoted."},{"t":"p","s":"You will see this phrase in almost every business income and civil authority form: direct physical loss of or damage to property."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan's leading statement on it came out of the COVID nineteen wave. Gavrilides Management Company versus Michigan Insurance Company, Michigan Court of Appeals docket number three five four four one eight, published February first, twenty twenty two."},{"t":"p","s":"Two Lansing area restaurants were closed by the governor's pandemic orders and sought business income coverage, arguing the closure itself was a direct physical loss."},{"t":"p","s":"The court disagreed, and here is its actual language, because a wrong paraphrase of this case circulates widely. The word physical necessarily requires the loss or damage to have some manner of tangible and measurable presence or effect in, on, or to the premises. The restaurants, it said, were unambiguously closed by impersonal operation of a general law, not because anything about or inside the premises had physically changed."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the part people get backwards, and it is the reason to be careful with this case. Gavrilides did not stop at the coverage trigger. The plaintiffs asked to amend to allege the virus was physically present, so the court said it was necessary to address that alternative basis for denying coverage, and in a separate section held that the defendant properly denied the claim pursuant to the virus exclusion."},{"t":"p","s":"Two holdings, not one. If you cite Gavrilides for only the first, somebody who has read it will correct you."},{"t":"p","s":"And the lesson generalizes far beyond COVID, which is why it belongs in a property chapter at all. Direct physical loss requires something tangible and measurable in, on, or to the premises. A legal restriction on how the property may be used, without more, does not meet that bar in Michigan."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"Actual cash value, the statutory floor, and the depreciation fight."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan Compiled Laws section five hundred point twenty eight thirty three, subsection one, paragraph a, sets a statutory floor. Every fire insurance policy in Michigan must provide, at a minimum, coverage for the actual cash value of the property at the time of the loss."},{"t":"p","s":"Now notice what it does not do. It sets a floor and never defines how actual cash value is calculated. That gap is the whole fight."},{"t":"p","s":"The Department of Insurance and Financial Services, DIFS, addressed that gap in Bulletin twenty twenty four dash twenty six dash INS, issued November eighth, twenty twenty four."},{"t":"p","s":"The bulletin describes actual cash value as ordinarily meaning the loss of value due to wear and tear, deterioration, and obsolescence to physical or tangible items."},{"t":"p","s":"Then it makes the key move. Nontangible items, defined as labor, taxes, fees, and overhead and profit, are not physical objects, and do not deteriorate, age, or decline in value the way a roof or a carpet does."},{"t":"p","s":"Hear the logic there, because it is genuinely good. You can depreciate a 20 year old roof because the shingles wore out. You cannot depreciate the labor of installing it, because labor does not sit on a roof getting older."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the trap, and it is a trap in the direction of overclaiming. For personal lines homeowners or dwelling policies issued or renewed effective July first, twenty twenty five or after, an insurer that wants to depreciate a nontangible item in an actual cash value payment can only do so through a standalone endorsement identifying those items, not by burying it in the policy form's definitions. That endorsement has to be optional, and because it reduces coverage, it has to be offered with a commensurate premium reduction."},{"t":"p","s":"What the bulletin does not do is ban depreciating labor, taxes, fees, or overhead and profit. It conditions the practice on transparency. Those are very different things, and the difference is the whole argument."},{"t":"p","s":"And remember the instrument hierarchy from chapter one. A bulletin is guidance from the DIFS director, not a statute. Proof of that: DIFS superseded an earlier bulletin, twenty twenty four dash eighteen dash INS, through that same administrative process, with no legislative vote required. What one bulletin gives, another can take."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Carrier depreciates labor on a claim under a policy renewed in September twenty twenty five, with nothing but a definition buried in the form. What is wrong, and what is the thing you must not say."},{"t":"a","s":"It needed a standalone, optional endorsement identifying those nontangible items, offered with a commensurate premium reduction, not a buried definition. What you must not say is that depreciating labor is banned. The bulletin conditions it on transparency, and it is a bulletin, not a statute."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"Named peril versus open peril."},{"t":"p","s":"This last one is industry background rather than Michigan law, but it decides which side is even arguing."},{"t":"p","s":"A named peril policy lists the perils it covers, and anything left off the list is not covered. An open peril policy, sometimes called special form or all risk, covers everything except what is specifically excluded."},{"t":"p","s":"You already saw that grant quoted inside Gavrilides: covered causes of loss means risks of direct physical loss, unless excluded or limited."},{"t":"p","s":"And the common H O three form splits the difference, which is why homeowners get confused about their own policy. Dwelling and other structures are open peril. Personal property is typically named peril unless the homeowner upgrades."},{"t":"p","s":"So on the same policy, the building is covered unless the carrier finds an exclusion, and the contents are covered only if the cause is on the list. That single split explains a large share of the arguments you will have over a contents claim."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the rapid fire recap. I am going to say these fast. You are not learning them now, you are checking which ones you already have."},{"t":"p","s":"Rory, two thousand five: unambiguous contracts enforced as written, no judicial reasonableness override. Wilkie, two thousand three: no reasonable expectations doctrine in Michigan. Klapp, four sixty eight Michigan four fifty nine, two thousand three: contra proferentem is a last resort tie breaker, after extrinsic evidence. Heniser, nineteen ninety five: insured proves coverage first. Hunt versus Drielick, two thousand fourteen: insurer proves the absence of coverage. Kirschner, nineteen ninety nine: waiver and estoppel usually will not broaden coverage. Salesin, nineteen ninety eight: self performance is not dispositive on overhead and profit, but there is no automatic rule. Sunshine Motors, nineteen ninety five: anti concurrent causation clauses are enforced where they actually appear in the exclusion at issue. Vanguard, nineteen ninety one: the Supreme Court will not let concurrent causation nullify an unambiguous exclusion. Gavrilides, twenty twenty two: direct physical loss needs something tangible and measurable in, on, or to the premises, and that same opinion separately enforced the virus exclusion. Bulletin twenty twenty four dash twenty six dash INS: a standalone, optional, premium reduced endorsement before an insurer depreciates labor, taxes, fees, or overhead and profit, on policies issued or renewed on or after July first, twenty twenty five."},{"t":"p","s":"Learn this chapter cold, because every other chapter in this track assumes you already know how Michigan reads a policy. If any of the questions I asked gave you trouble, that is where to drill."}],"words":3787,"questions":6},{"id":"03_standard-fire-policy-2833_lecture","track":"law","title":"The Standard Fire Policy, MCL 500.2833","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the backbone statute of Michigan property insurance, and I want to tell you up front why it repays the effort more than almost anything else in this track."},{"t":"p","s":"Every fire policy sold in this state, and every homeowners policy built on top of a fire chassis, has to contain a specific list of provisions. That list lives in one section: MCL 500.2833. So when you are standing in an argument about a claim, you are almost always arguing about a lettered subdivision of one statute. Know it cold and you can point to the exact letter of the exact subsection that controls."},{"t":"p","s":"The hard part of this chapter is that it is a list of letters, a through s, and a pile of numbers. A list is the easiest thing in the world to nod along to and the hardest thing to keep. So I am going to teach it in groups that belong together, and I am going to teach the numbers in pairs, because the wrong answer is almost always the other number in the pair."},{"t":"p","s":"Thirteen ideas. After most of them, a question and then silence. Answer out loud."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"Where this statute came from, and one honest gap."},{"t":"p","s":"Section 2833 sits in Chapter twenty eight of the Insurance Code of nineteen fifty six, the chapter called Fire Insurance Contracts, which runs from section two eight zero four to section two eight six six."},{"t":"p","s":"The section itself was added in nineteen ninety, by Public Act three oh five, with immediate effect on December fourteenth of that year."},{"t":"p","s":"It replaced an older approach, and the history matters because it still has legal force. Until the end of nineteen ninety one, Michigan had a separate section, 500 point two eight three two, that spelled out an actual standard fire policy form, word for word. The same nineteen ninety act repealed it, effective January first, nineteen ninety two. Today the legislature's website shows only a one line compiler's note saying it, quote, pertained to form of standard policy, end quote."},{"t":"p","s":"So here is an honest gap, and I would rather hand you the gap than a confident guess. I cannot tell you word for word what that old form said, because its text was not verified against a primary source."},{"t":"p","s":"What is certain is this. The modern statute, in subsection two, still leans on that repealed section as a floor. Every fire policy must contain, at minimum, the coverage that was in the standard fire policy under former section two eight three two. The old form is gone as a document, but its substance survives as an incorporated minimum."},{"t":"p","s":"Hold the pair: 2833 is the live statute, two eight three two is the repealed form that still sets the floor underneath it."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"Letters a and b. What must be covered at all."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection one is the list, lettered a through s, of provisions every fire policy issued or delivered in Michigan must contain. This is the state's non negotiable checklist, no matter which company writes the policy."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter a is the actual cash value floor. The policy has to cover, at minimum, the actual cash value of the property at the time of the loss. A carrier can offer more, replacement cost coverage for example, but it can never offer less than actual cash value as the baseline. Floor, not ceiling. That phrase is going to come back three more times in this chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter b is the core peril, plus a rule people forget exists. Minimum coverage is direct loss by fire and lightning. And on top of that, pro rata coverage for 5 days for property that gets moved to another location to save it from a covered peril."},{"t":"p","s":"Picture what that is actually for. Furniture carried out of a burning house, sitting in the yard, and then it rains on it for 2 days. That 5 day removal provision is what protects it. It is a small rule that shows up on real jobs constantly."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. What is the minimum a Michigan fire policy must pay on the valuation side, and how long does removed property stay covered."},{"t":"a","s":"Actual cash value at the time of the loss, under letter a, and 5 days of pro rata coverage for removed property, under letter b."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Letters c through g. The disclosure block."},{"t":"p","s":"These five hang together, so learn them as a block rather than five separate facts. They are all about the policy telling you where coverage stops."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter c lets the policy be void for misrepresentation, fraud, or concealment."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter d requires the policy to state what property is not covered."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter e requires it to state what perils are not covered."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter f requires a statement of what conditions suspend or restrict coverage."},{"t":"p","s":"And letter g requires a provision for waiving or changing a policy term."},{"t":"p","s":"Notice the theme. The statute is not deciding what gets excluded. It is requiring that the exclusions be written down where you can find them. Property, perils, conditions, and how terms change. That is the block."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Letters h and i. Cancellation, taught as a pair."},{"t":"p","s":"These two are mirror images, and the exam of real life will hand you one and see if you apply the other one's rule."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter h is the insured's right to cancel. The insured can cancel at any time. The insurer's minimum earned premium is not less than the pro rata premium for the expired time, or 25 dollars, whichever is greater."},{"t":"p","s":"Do the arithmetic on that, because that is where it becomes real. Somebody cancels early in the term and the pro rata number works out to eight or nine dollars. The insurer still keeps twenty five, because twenty five is greater. Whichever is greater always favors the insurer here."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter i is the mirror image, the insurer's right to cancel. The insurer can cancel at any time too, but it has to mail written notice to each named insured, at their last known address, at least 10 days before cancellation takes effect, postage fully prepaid."},{"t":"p","s":"The same 25 dollar or pro rata minimum earned premium applies. And if there is excess premium the insurer is not tendering up front, the cancellation notice itself has to say that the excess will be refunded on demand."},{"t":"p","s":"So the pair is: either side may cancel at any time, the same 25 dollar floor applies to both, and only the insurer owes the 10 day mailed notice."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Insurer cancels. How much notice, delivered how, and what happens to leftover premium it is not sending along with the notice."},{"t":"a","s":"At least 10 days, mailed written notice to each named insured at the last known address, postage fully prepaid. If it is not tendering the excess premium, the notice has to state that the excess will be refunded on demand."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Letter j. The mortgagee clause."},{"t":"p","s":"This one matters because almost every damaged house has a mortgage, and this is where a lot of real money gets stuck."},{"t":"p","s":"If loss is payable to a mortgagee who is not named as an insured, the insurer can cancel that mortgagee's interest by giving the mortgagee at least 10 days written notice."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the piece people forget, and it is the one that saves claims. If the insured never gets around to filing proof of loss, the mortgagee itself, once notified, has 60 days to render its own proof of loss."},{"t":"p","s":"Hear the pair inside a single letter: 10 days to cancel the mortgagee's interest, 60 days for the mortgagee to file its own proof of loss. Ten and sixty, both in letter j, doing completely different jobs."},{"t":"p","s":"Two more mechanics. If the insurer pays the mortgagee while denying the underlying claim against the homeowner, the insurer steps into the mortgagee's shoes for recovery, which is subrogation, up to the amount it paid, without cutting off the mortgagee's own right to sue. Alternatively the insurer can pay off the mortgage debt entirely and take an assignment of the debt and the mortgage. Either side, or both together, can sue to recover their respective shares."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Letters k and l. Two short ones."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter k is the pro rata other insurance rule. If a second policy also covers the same peril, the first insurer's liability is capped at its pro rata share, not the whole loss."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter l simply requires the policy to state its notification requirements when a loss occurs."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"Letter m. Appraisal, and the numbers to carve in."},{"t":"p","s":"This is probably the single most tested provision in the whole section, so slow down here."},{"t":"p","s":"If the insured and the insurer cannot agree on the actual cash value or the amount of the loss, either party can make a written demand for appraisal."},{"t":"p","s":"Once that demand goes out, each side has 20 days to pick its own competent, independent appraiser and name that appraiser to the other side."},{"t":"p","s":"The two appraisers then have to agree on an umpire. If they cannot agree within 15 days, either side can ask a circuit court judge, in the county where the loss happened or where the property sits, to pick the umpire for them."},{"t":"p","s":"The appraisers set the amount item by item. If they agree, that is the number. If they do not, they kick their disagreement to the umpire, and a written agreement signed by any two of the three, meaning the two appraisers, or one appraiser plus the umpire, sets the final amount of loss."},{"t":"p","s":"On cost: each party pays for its own appraiser, and the umpire's fee and the other appraisal expenses split fifty fifty."},{"t":"p","s":"Say the numbers back with me. 20 days to name an appraiser. 15 days to agree on an umpire. Any two of three sign off. Twenty and fifteen, in that order, and the order is the thing people reverse."},{"t":"p","s":"Now memorize the limit of what appraisal actually does, because this is where the real fights happen. The statute's own words limit it to the amount of the loss or the actual cash value. Whether something is covered at all is a coverage question."},{"t":"p","s":"The Court of Appeals held in Maksym versus Auto Owners, a published twenty twenty six decision, that an insured is not entitled to appraisal on items whose coverage is still in dispute. That dispute goes to court, not to the appraisal panel."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Carrier agrees the water damage is covered but says your number is too high, and separately says the hardwood floors are not covered at all. Which part goes to appraisal, which part does not, and what are the two clocks."},{"t":"a","s":"The amount dispute goes to appraisal. The coverage dispute over the floors does not, under Maksym. 20 days to name your appraiser, 15 days for the appraisers to agree on an umpire before either side can ask a circuit court judge to pick one."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Letters n and o. The insurer's options, and no walking away."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter n gives the insurer options besides paying cash. It can repair, replace, rebuild, or simply take the damaged property."},{"t":"p","s":"That is a real right, not a formality. When a carrier sends a letter saying we are sending our own crew, that is letter n. It is rarely used, and it is genuinely theirs."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter o shuts the door on abandonment. There is no right for the insured to just hand over the wreckage and expect a full payout as though it were a total loss without going through the claim process."},{"t":"p","s":"So the pair: the insurer may elect to take the property, and the insured may not force it on them."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Letter p. The payment clock, and its exception."},{"t":"p","s":"The loss is payable within 30 days after the insurer receives proof of the amount of loss."},{"t":"p","s":"But notice the opening words of the letter, because they matter more than people think: except as otherwise provided in section two eight four five."},{"t":"p","s":"Section two eight four five is Michigan's municipal escrow withholding statute, which we will get to in a moment. Where it applies, part of the payment can be legitimately delayed and redirected under its own separate rules."},{"t":"p","s":"So here is the practical consequence. A payment that runs past 30 days because of a two eight four five escrow is not a violation of the 30 day rule. If you accuse a carrier of blowing the clock when an escrow is in play, you will be wrong, and you will have spent your credibility."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"Letters q, r, and s. The suit clock and two closers."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter q is the suit limitation clause, and it has a built in trap."},{"t":"p","s":"An action under the policy must be commenced within 1 year after the loss, or within whatever longer period the policy itself specifies."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the twist. That clock is tolled, meaning paused, from the moment the insured notifies the insurer of the loss until the insurer formally denies liability."},{"t":"p","s":"So an insured does not always have exactly 1 year from the date of loss to sue. Work the example. The insured notifies the carrier promptly, and the carrier takes a year and a half to formally deny the claim. The suit clock has been sitting paused that entire time. It only starts ticking again once that formal denial happens."},{"t":"p","s":"If you had counted a flat year from the date of loss, you would have told that homeowner she was out of time when she was not."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter r requires the standard subrogation provision: the insurer steps into the insured's shoes to recover from any other party responsible for the loss."},{"t":"p","s":"And letter s sets the effective time for every fire policy under this section: twelve zero one a m, standard time, at the location of the property."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection two, as I said at the start, ties the whole thing back to that repealed former section two eight three two, requiring the minimum coverage of that old standard fire policy as a floor, on top of everything in subsection one."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Loss on May first, reported May first, formal denial 14 months later. Is the homeowner out of time, and why."},{"t":"a","s":"No. The 1 year clock under letter q was tolled from notice until formal denial, so it was not running during those 14 months. It starts at the formal denial."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"The two escrow statutes, and why they get confused."},{"t":"p","s":"These two sections, 500 point two eight four five and 500 point two two two seven, are constantly mixed up, so let me separate them by the one thing that actually divides them: the size of the community."},{"t":"p","s":"Both let a municipality require an insurer to withhold 25 percent of a fire loss settlement, as security to make sure a damaged structure actually gets fixed or torn down instead of left standing as a hazard. 25 percent is common ground."},{"t":"p","s":"Section two eight four five covers the smaller municipalities. Cities, villages, and townships in counties under four hundred twenty five thousand people, as long as that particular city, village, or township does not itself have fifty thousand or more residents. Its residential cap has been 12,000 dollars since two thousand fifteen, adjusted every January first by the consumer price index."},{"t":"p","s":"Section two two two seven is the mirror statute for the bigger jurisdictions. Counties of four hundred twenty five thousand or more, plus any city, village, or township of fifty thousand or more even inside a smaller county. And two two two seven also covers a wider range of perils. Not just fire and explosion, but vandalism, malicious mischief, wind, hail, riot, and civil commotion."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the critical twenty twenty four update, and this is where most stale material goes wrong. Public Act eighty two of twenty twenty four amended section two two two seven, and only two two two seven, raising its residential cap from 12,000 dollars to 24,000 dollars, effective July first, twenty twenty four, and switching its adjustment date to every July first instead of January first."},{"t":"p","s":"Section two eight four five was not touched. It is still on the old 12,000 dollar, January first schedule."},{"t":"p","s":"So the two statutes now run on different numbers and different calendars. Twelve thousand on January for the small municipalities, twenty four thousand on July for the big ones."},{"t":"p","s":"Which brings us to the current bulletin. DIFS Bulletin twenty twenty six dash sixteen dash INS, issued and entered June second, twenty twenty six, and signed by Director Anita G Fox, sets the section two two two seven maximum withholding amount at 25,090 dollars, effective July first, twenty twenty six, through June thirtieth, twenty twenty seven. This bulletin supersedes the prior year's bulletin. And for context, the year before that, July first twenty twenty five through June thirtieth twenty twenty six, the figure was 24,096 dollars."},{"t":"p","s":"25,090 dollars, and it belongs to section two two two seven, not two eight four five. If you attach that number to the wrong statute you have made exactly the mistake this idea exists to prevent."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. A fire in a township of eight thousand people, in a county of two hundred thousand. Which escrow statute, which cap, and which adjustment date."},{"t":"a","s":"Section two eight four five, because the county is under four hundred twenty five thousand and the township is under fifty thousand. 12,000 dollar residential cap, adjusted every January first. The 25,090 dollar figure belongs to two two two seven and does not apply here."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea twelve"},{"t":"p","s":"The escrow rhythm both statutes share."},{"t":"p","s":"Once you know which statute applies, the sequence is the same, and it is a clean chain of numbers."},{"t":"p","s":"The municipality gets 15 days after the withholding notice to say it wants the money escrowed, or the funds default to the insured."},{"t":"p","s":"Once escrowed, the insured has 10 days to object."},{"t":"p","s":"If a mortgage is in default, the treasurer releases funds to the first mortgagee within 10 days of a written request."},{"t":"p","s":"If nobody shows reasonable proof of repair or demolition within 120 days, the municipality can use the retained money itself to secure, repair, or demolish the structure, returning any leftover to the insured."},{"t":"p","s":"And an insured who wants to sue a municipality to get withheld proceeds back has 3 years from the original withholding notice."},{"t":"p","s":"There is also an opt out, and it is the most useful thing in this idea for somebody in the trades. File a qualifying repair contract with the insurer within 15 days of the final settlement, consent to paying the contractor directly, and have the insurer notify the municipality, and there is no withholding at all."},{"t":"p","s":"Fifteen, ten, ten, one hundred twenty, 3 years. And fifteen again for the opt out."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea thirteen"},{"t":"p","s":"How this maps onto a real policy and a real job."},{"t":"p","s":"A modern H O three policy is built on the same fire coverage base this statute governs, and its Section One Conditions roughly track two eight three three's subdivisions."},{"t":"p","s":"A condition called Appraisal mirrors letter m's 20 day and 15 day mechanics. Our Option mirrors letter n's repair, replace, rebuild, or take language. The Mortgage Clause mirrors letter j. And Suit Against Us mirrors letter q."},{"t":"p","s":"One careful point on that last one. Some policies give 2 years to sue, and in Michigan that longer period controls, because letter q says 1 year or the policy's period, whichever is longer. The statute is a floor, not a ceiling. A policy may lengthen the suit window. It can never shorten it below 1 year, and it can never drop the tolling."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the job site version, which is where this stops being academic."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter j's mortgagee mechanics matter because loss checks are so often made out jointly to the homeowner and the bank, and a slow homeowner can trigger that separate 60 day mortgagee clock."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter m's appraisal right belongs to the insured, not the contractor. And here is the line to be careful about. A contractor whose detailed estimate supports the homeowner's own appraisal demand is doing legitimate work. A contractor who personally demands appraisal or negotiates the umpire for a fee is edging into unlicensed public adjusting under a different statute, 500 point one two two two. Same job site, two very different sides of a licensing line."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter n's insurer option clause is why a we are sending our own crew letter from a carrier is a right the statute actually gives them, even if it is rarely used."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter p's 30 day payment clock is the number a contractor plans draws and cash flow against."},{"t":"p","s":"And letter q's 1 year, tolled suit clock matters because a contractor's timeline and lien strategy have to be planned against the homeowner's outer legal deadline."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the rapid fire recap. Fast, as a self check."},{"t":"p","s":"25 dollars or pro rata, whichever is greater, for either party's cancellation. 10 days mailed notice for an insurer cancellation, and 10 days notice to cancel a mortgagee's interest, with 60 days for a mortgagee to render its own proof of loss. 20 days to name an appraiser, 15 days to agree on an umpire, any two of three sign the amount. 30 days to pay after proof of amount of loss, subject to section two eight four five. 1 year to sue, or the policy's longer period, tolled from notice to formal denial. Twelve zero one a m as the effective time. 25 percent withholding under both two eight four five and two two two seven, but two eight four five's cap is 12,000 dollars on a January schedule, while two two two seven's cap is 24,000 dollars on a July schedule since Public Act eighty two of twenty twenty four, now 25,090 dollars for July twenty twenty six through June twenty twenty seven under Bulletin twenty twenty six dash sixteen dash INS."},{"t":"p","s":"Lock these numbers in. If any of the questions I asked gave you trouble, that is exactly where to drill."}],"words":3676,"questions":5},{"id":"04_bad-faith-utpa_lecture","track":"law","title":"Bad Faith and Unfair Trade Practices","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"If you take one thing out of this chapter, take this. In Michigan the words bad faith get used loosely, and the law splits the idea into three separate tools. Mixing them up is the most common mistake in this entire area, and it is the mistake that makes a contractor or a homeowner sound like they do not know what they are talking about in front of an adjuster."},{"t":"p","s":"So we fix the framework first, and then everything else drops into one of three buckets."},{"t":"p","s":"Twelve ideas. After most of them, a question and then silence. Answer out loud."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"The three tools. Learn this before anything else."},{"t":"p","s":"Tool one is the penalty interest statute, MCL 500.2006. That is a private right. An insured can collect 12 percent interest on a late paid claim, with no bad faith lawsuit required. It is a mechanical calculation."},{"t":"p","s":"Tool two is the unfair trade practices list, MCL 500.2026. Fourteen things an insurer is not supposed to do. The catch is that an insured cannot sue under it. It is enforced by the Department of Insurance and Financial Services, DIFS, administratively."},{"t":"p","s":"Tool three is common law bad faith, a tort suit. Michigan does not recognize that for a first party insured suing their own carrier. It survives only when a liability insurer refuses to settle within policy limits and an excess judgment lands on its own insured."},{"t":"p","s":"Say those back in one breath. 2006, private, 12 percent. 2026, no private suit, DIFS enforces. Common law bad faith, third party only."},{"t":"p","s":"Every single thing in the rest of this chapter sorts into one of those three. When something confuses you later, come back and ask which bucket it is in."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"Section 2006, subsections one and two."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection one says the insurer must pay benefits on a timely basis to its insured, to a person directly entitled to benefits, or to a third party tort claimant, or in the alternative pay 12 percent interest on the late claim. Failing to do either one is an unfair trade practice, unless the claim is reasonably in dispute."},{"t":"p","s":"Now notice where that phrase reasonably in dispute sits, because its location is the whole ballgame later. It is in subsection one. And in subsection four it protects only a narrow category of claimant. Hold that thought, because in about five minutes it is going to decide a real argument for you."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection two is a small housekeeping rule. If the insurer loses in court and pays the judgment plus the required interest, that alone is not an additional unfair trade practice. Paying what you were ordered to pay does not stack another violation on top."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection three, the clock everybody in claims work should have memorized."},{"t":"p","s":"The insurer must specify in writing exactly what documents constitute a satisfactory proof of loss within 30 days after receiving the claim, unless the claim settles inside those 30 days."},{"t":"p","s":"Then, once a satisfactory proof of loss is supplied, the insurer has 60 days from receiving it to pay the supported amount, or the payment is untimely."},{"t":"p","s":"Thirty and sixty. Two different clocks, doing two different jobs. 30 days to tell you what it needs. 60 days to pay once you have given it."},{"t":"p","s":"Let me walk the dates so it sticks. A fire claim is reported May first. The insurer has until roughly May thirty first to send the letter spelling out what it needs. Say the homeowner supplies everything on June fifteenth. The insurer then has until roughly August fourteenth to pay the supported amount."},{"t":"p","s":"Miss that, and subsection four is triggered."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Claim reported March first, proof of loss materials letter due when, and if the homeowner delivers everything April tenth, when is payment late."},{"t":"a","s":"The letter is due by roughly March thirty first, 30 days after the claim. Payment is late after roughly June ninth, 60 days after the satisfactory proof of loss came in on April tenth."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection four, sentence one. This is the sentence that pays."},{"t":"p","s":"The first sentence of subsection four says that if benefits are not paid on a timely basis, the benefits paid bear simple interest, from a date 60 days after satisfactory proof of loss was received, at the rate of 12 percent per annum, if the claimant is the insured or a person directly entitled to benefits under the insured's insurance contract."},{"t":"p","s":"Now listen to what is missing from that sentence. No qualifier. No mention of whether the claim was disputed."},{"t":"p","s":"If you are the insured, or someone directly entitled to benefits under your own policy, and you get paid late, you get 12 percent. That is it."},{"t":"p","s":"This is the single most useful fact in the chapter for real claims work, and it is the one carriers most often talk past."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection four, sentence two, and why it is not yours."},{"t":"p","s":"The second sentence is where the reasonably in dispute language lives, and it applies only to third party tort claimants."},{"t":"p","s":"For one of them to get the same 12 percent, three things must all be true. The insurer's liability must not be reasonably in dispute. The insurer must have refused payment in bad faith. And a court of law must have determined that bad faith."},{"t":"p","s":"That is a much higher bar, and it belongs only to people who are not the policyholder but have a liability claim against someone the insurer covers."},{"t":"p","s":"So here is the trap, and it is the one adjusters run on homeowners constantly. An adjuster says, we do not owe you 12 percent because the claim is reasonably in dispute. If the person he is talking to is the insured, that defense is not in their sentence. It lives in the third party sentence. Different claimant, different rule."},{"t":"p","s":"Four other mechanics round out subsection four. Interest is paid on top of, and at the same time as, the loss payment. If the loss exceeds the policy limit, interest runs on the limit. If the insurer offers to pay, the claimant turns it down, and never recovers more than that offer, no interest is owed. And interest under this section is offset against any separate judgment interest the insurer owes."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Your homeowner is the named insured, paid 90 days after a clean proof of loss, and the carrier says the claim was reasonably in dispute so no interest. What is your answer."},{"t":"a","s":"The reasonably in dispute limit is in the second sentence of subsection four, which covers third party tort claimants. She is the insured, so the first sentence governs, and she gets 12 percent simple interest running from 60 days after satisfactory proof of loss."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Section 2026, the list, and the threshold that guards it."},{"t":"p","s":"The statute's own preamble sets the threshold, and it is worth memorizing close to verbatim. Unfair methods of competition and unfair or deceptive acts or practices in the business of insurance, other than isolated incidents, are a course of conduct indicating a persistent tendency to engage in that type of conduct."},{"t":"p","s":"Read that closely. DIFS is looking for a pattern, not a single bad claim file. One rough denial letter, standing alone, generally will not trigger an enforcement action, though it can still be evidence somewhere else."},{"t":"p","s":"The list runs fourteen items, lettered a through n. The ones that come up constantly in property claims are these."},{"t":"p","s":"Misrepresenting pertinent facts or policy provisions relating to coverage. Failing to acknowledge or act reasonably and promptly on claim communications. Failing to adopt reasonable standards for prompt investigation. Refusing to pay claims without a reasonable investigation based on the available information. Failing to affirm or deny coverage within a reasonable time after proof of loss is completed. Failing to attempt in good faith to settle promptly and fairly once liability has become reasonably clear. Compelling insureds to sue by offering substantially less than what is owed. Making a payment without stating what coverage it was paid under. Delaying investigation or payment by demanding duplicate paperwork purely to duplicate a verification already done. Failing to promptly settle one portion of a claim, where liability is clear, to pressure the insured on a different, disputed portion. And failing to give a reasonable, fact based, policy based explanation for a denial or a compromise offer."},{"t":"p","s":"That second to last one deserves a second of your attention, because you will see it. Holding up the part of the claim nobody disputes, in order to get leverage on the part somebody does dispute, is on the list by name."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection two also requires insurers to keep a complete written record of every complaint since their last DIFS exam, with the number, the type, the outcome, and the time it took. Failing to keep that record is itself a violation."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"How DIFS actually enforces a list nobody can sue under."},{"t":"p","s":"A pipeline runs through five sections, and knowing it tells you what a complaint really sets in motion."},{"t":"p","s":"Section two zero two eight lets the commissioner investigate on probable cause."},{"t":"p","s":"Section two zero two nine requires written notice of a hearing. But before that notice goes out, bureau staff must give the insurer a chance to confer informally, and the matter can be resolved summarily by agreement."},{"t":"p","s":"Section two zero three zero governs the hearing. The insurer may be represented by counsel and show cause why a cease and desist order should not issue. And here is the detail that surprises people: the burden of proof sits on the agency, not the insurer. The insurer may also request an independent hearing officer instead of the commissioner, and gets one dismissal of that officer within 20 days."},{"t":"p","s":"Section two zero three eight is where the money comes in. On a finding of violation after a hearing, the director issues written findings and a cease and desist order, and may impose a monetary penalty."},{"t":"p","s":"Here are the penalty pairs, and they are easy to swap by mistake. For an ordinary violation, 1000 dollars per violation, capped at ten thousand total. For a knowing violation, 5000 dollars per violation, capped at fifty thousand for all violations in any 6 month period. One and ten for ordinary. Five and fifty for knowing."},{"t":"p","s":"The director may also suspend or revoke the license for knowing, persistent violations, and order a refund of overcharges."},{"t":"p","s":"Section two zero four zero covers defying a cease and desist order that is already in effect. That runs up to 20,000 dollars per violation, on top of suspension or revocation. And the order can never punish conduct predating it."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Knowing violations, what is the per violation amount and the cap, and over what window."},{"t":"a","s":"5000 dollars per violation, capped at fifty thousand for all violations in any 6 month period. The one thousand and ten thousand pair is the ordinary violation."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Young, and what it did not say."},{"t":"p","s":"Young versus Michigan Mutual Insurance Company, nineteen eighty four. An insured tried three theories after a denied claim. A tort claim on section 2006. A tort claim on section 2026. And a freestanding bad faith tort."},{"t":"p","s":"The court rejected all three. There is no implied private cause of action in tort for violation of section 2006. And under section 2026, isolated incidents do not constitute unfair trade practices, because one insurance client's dealings with one company are, by their nature, an isolated incident. The insured does not have an independent cause of action under that section."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the nuance people miss, and it matters because it is the difference between having a remedy and not."},{"t":"p","s":"Young did not say an insured can never collect the section 2006 interest penalty. The same opinion notes that Michigan case law already lets a private party directly recover it. What is barred is dressing the claim up as an independent tort. The ordinary statutory interest claim is untouched."},{"t":"p","s":"So Young closes the tort door and leaves the interest door open. If somebody tells you Young means you cannot get the 12 percent, they have read it too fast."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Kewin, and getting the quote right."},{"t":"p","s":"Kewin versus Massachusetts Mutual Life Insurance Company, nineteen eighty, is the foundational no first party bad faith tort case. An insured sued for mental distress and exemplary damages after his disability insurer denied a claim."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the court's actual language, because a common paraphrase gets it wrong. The court said, we decline to follow the California court and to declare the mere bad faith breach of an insurance indemnity contract an independent and separately actionable tort."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the real quote. You will often hear people describe Kewin's rule using the phrase wholly separate and independent duty, but that specific four word phrase is nowhere in the actual opinion. The underlying rule is the same either way, no independent tort without a duty that exists apart from the contract itself. But if you are asked to quote Kewin verbatim, use the actual words, an independent and separately actionable tort, not the borrowed phrase."},{"t":"p","s":"Watch the outcome too, because the numbers tell you how thoroughly the door closed. Kewin's insured kept only his contract money, 16,500 dollars on the disability policy plus 798 dollars and forty cents on the life policy. The mental distress award stayed reversed and the 50,000 dollar exemplary damages award was set aside."},{"t":"p","s":"Justice Williams wrote separately and would have allowed emotional distress contract damages. That opinion did not command the court, so do not quote it as the rule."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"The one place bad faith really is a tort in Michigan."},{"t":"p","s":"Commercial Union Insurance Company versus Liberty Mutual Insurance Company, nineteen eighty six. This is about a liability insurer's duty to its own insured when there is a chance to settle within policy limits and an excess judgment could follow a refusal."},{"t":"p","s":"The court defined bad faith, for jury instruction purposes, as arbitrary, reckless, indifferent, or intentional disregard of the interests of the person owed a duty."},{"t":"p","s":"Two halves of that definition matter. Bad faith can exist without actual dishonesty or fraud, if the insurer is motivated by protecting its own interests at the insured's expense. And on the other side, honest mistakes, good faith denials, and simple negligence are not enough."},{"t":"p","s":"The court listed twelve non exclusive factors a jury may weigh, none decisive alone. Things like failing to keep the insured informed of settlement offers, failing to accept a reasonable within limits offer, ignoring the advice of an adjuster or attorney, and failing to investigate properly before refusing a settlement."},{"t":"p","s":"And it added a caution worth carrying into every file you ever review. Judge the insurer by what it knew at the time, not with twenty twenty hindsight."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"Griswold and Nickola, which lock the 12 percent down."},{"t":"p","s":"Back to the first party 12 percent rule, because two cases settle it."},{"t":"p","s":"Griswold Properties versus Lexington Insurance Company, two thousand seven, a special conflict panel, held that a first party insured is entitled to 12 percent penalty interest if a claim is not timely paid, irrespective of whether the claim is reasonably in dispute."},{"t":"p","s":"Then the Michigan Supreme Court in Nickola versus MIC General Insurance Company, 500 Michigan one fifteen, two thousand seventeen, settled it in an underinsured motorist claim. An insured claiming under their own policy cannot be treated as a third party tort claimant under subsection four, so the reasonably in dispute exemption does not reach that claim. The court called this entirely consistent with Griswold and overruled an earlier Court of Appeals case that had gone the other way."},{"t":"p","s":"So if idea five felt like an argument, this is the authority behind it. Griswold at the Court of Appeals level, Nickola at the Supreme Court."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea twelve"},{"t":"p","s":"Smith versus Globe, and a correction you will need."},{"t":"p","s":"Smith versus Globe Life Insurance Company, nineteen ninety nine, is often cited as proof that the Michigan Consumer Protection Act does not reach insurance at all. The actual opinion says almost the opposite on its own facts, which is exactly the kind of error that gets repeated because nobody opens the case."},{"t":"p","s":"Smith actually held two things."},{"t":"p","s":"First, and this half is still good law, the general exemption does cover the sale of insurance, because the test asks whether the general transaction is specifically authorized, not whether the specific misconduct is."},{"t":"p","s":"Second, under the statute as it then read, the court still let the insured's consumer protection claim over a credit life policy proceed, because the exemption then in place had its own built in exception for certain private actions. The claim survived summary disposition and was remanded."},{"t":"p","s":"The legislature closed that door shortly afterward, effective March twenty eighth, two thousand one. The current version, section four forty five point nine zero four, subsection three, says the Consumer Protection Act does not apply to, or create a cause of action for, conduct made unlawful by chapter twenty of the insurance code, for conduct occurring on or after March twenty eighth, two thousand one."},{"t":"p","s":"So today that route really is closed for chapter twenty claims. But get the reason right. Smith is the case that prompted the fix, not the case that states the current rule."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea thirteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Two things that are not law, and one contrast."},{"t":"p","s":"Do not teach these as law, and do not let anyone sell them to you as law."},{"t":"p","s":"Senate Bill two forty five, from the twenty twenty five session, would add a new chapter thirty B titled the right to a civil cause of action against insurers for certain conduct. It would let a person damaged by a violation of section 2006, 2026, or two zero two seven sue the insurer directly, with punitive and emotional distress damages if the violation was in bad faith, and a rebuttable presumption of bad faith from a 2026 violation."},{"t":"p","s":"In other words, it would undo Kewin and Young by statute. That is a big deal, and it has not happened. As of the most recent check, it has only been reported favorably out of committee and referred to the Committee of the Whole in June of 2026. It has not passed either chamber. It is a bill, not a law."},{"t":"p","s":"And DIFS Bulletin two thousand twenty five, dash, twenty five, dash, INS, issued October twenty fourth of twenty twenty five, reminds insurers about improper claims administration practices, quoting the same reasonable investigation and prompt settlement subsections we covered. It is written around automobile claims, not property, so treat it as guidance, not a property rule."},{"t":"p","s":"The contrast worth knowing: Florida has a real statutory first party bad faith law. Michigan has none. Only the 12 percent hammer and ordinary breach damages."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the rapid fire recap. Fast, as a self check."},{"t":"p","s":"30 days to specify proof of loss materials. 60 days after proof of loss to pay before interest starts. 12 percent simple interest for the insured, no reasonably in dispute defense available to them. Fourteen enumerated unfair practices. 10,000 dollars maximum aggregate penalty for ordinary violations, fifty thousand for knowing violations in a 6 month window, twenty thousand per violation for defying a cease and desist order. No first party bad faith tort. No private lawsuit under the unfair practices list. And Florida, for contrast, has a real statutory first party bad faith law. Michigan has none, only the 12 percent hammer and ordinary breach damages."},{"t":"p","s":"Know those cold and this chapter is yours. If any of the questions I asked gave you trouble, that is where to drill."}],"words":3267,"questions":3},{"id":"05_appraisal-and-suit-limits_lecture","track":"law","title":"Appraisal and Suit Limits","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the part of Michigan insurance law that resolves fights over money. The machine that turns a disagreement into a number, and the clock that forces everybody to move."},{"t":"p","s":"Two ideas run through all of it, and if you hold these two, the rest is detail."},{"t":"p","s":"First, appraisal decides how much is owed, never whether it is owed. Second, the 1 year suit clock is less scary than it sounds, because it stops running while the carrier is still working the claim."},{"t":"p","s":"Twelve ideas. After most of them I will ask you a question and go quiet. Answer out loud."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"The appraisal machine, step by step."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2833, subsection one m, is the appraisal clause every Michigan fire insurance policy must contain."},{"t":"p","s":"If the insured and insurer cannot agree on the actual cash value or the amount of the loss, either side can send a written demand that the amount be set by appraisal."},{"t":"p","s":"Once that demand is received, each party has 20 days to pick a competent, independent appraiser and tell the other side who it is."},{"t":"p","s":"The two appraisers then try to agree on a competent, impartial umpire."},{"t":"p","s":"Now stop on those two different words, because the difference is going to matter in about ten minutes. Party appraisers only have to be independent, meaning not controlled by the other side. The umpire has to be impartial, neutral to both. Independent and impartial are not the same standard, and the gap between them is where a real rule lives."},{"t":"p","s":"If the two appraisers cannot agree on an umpire within 15 days, either side can ask a judge of the circuit court, in the county where the loss happened or where the property sits, to pick one."},{"t":"p","s":"The appraisers then set the amount of the loss and the actual cash value item by item, not as one lump sum. That is exactly why a good line item estimate with photographs is the right document to bring into an appraisal. The process is built to receive line items, so bring line items."},{"t":"p","s":"If they cannot agree within what the statute calls a reasonable time, they hand their differences to the umpire. And be honest about that phrase: reasonable time is never defined anywhere. No number of days, no case that sets one."},{"t":"p","s":"Whatever any two of the three sign in writing is the binding amount of the loss."},{"t":"p","s":"On money, each side pays for its own appraiser, and the umpire's fee and other appraisal expenses split fifty fifty. There is no loser pays rule here."},{"t":"q","s":"20 days, 15 days, any two of three. Say it back."},{"t":"a","s":"20 days to name your appraiser. 15 days for the appraisers to agree on an umpire before either side can go to the circuit court. Any two of the three signatures bind."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"Amount, never coverage. The rule that decides which track you are on."},{"t":"p","s":"This is the single most important idea in the chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"The leading case is Auto Owners Insurance Company versus Kwaiser, from nineteen ninety one, a published Court of Appeals opinion. It describes appraisal as a simple and inexpensive method for the prompt adjustment and settlement of claims, essentially a stand in for a lawsuit about dollar amounts."},{"t":"p","s":"But Kwaiser also draws the line. If the parties cannot agree on coverage itself, a court decides that first, in a declaratory action, before any appraiser touches the file."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is a precision point worth having, because people attach it to the wrong case. The flip side, that once an insurer admits a loss is covered a court is statutorily required to send the parties into appraisal, is not actually a Kwaiser line. That sentence comes from a federal case called The D Boys, and the Michigan Court of Appeals adopted it word for word in the published Cantina opinion, which is what makes it binding here."},{"t":"p","s":"The Michigan Supreme Court said the same thing in Dupree versus Auto Owners Insurance Company in twenty fourteen: while matters of coverage are generally determined by the courts, the method of determining the loss is reserved for the appraisers."},{"t":"p","s":"So the sorting question in every file is one sentence. Are we arguing about whether it is covered, or about how much it is worth. Coverage goes to a judge. Amount goes to appraisers."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Cantina, where continuing to work the claim became an admission."},{"t":"p","s":"So how do you tell, in a real file, which kind of fight you have? Two recent cases answer that, and you need both, because the second one narrows the first."},{"t":"p","s":"The first is Cantina Enterprises Two Incorporated versus Property Owners Insurance Company, a published two thousand twenty four decision."},{"t":"p","s":"A restaurant kitchen caught fire. The carrier never disputed that the fire was covered. It argued about whether certain items counted as the tenant's improvements and betterments, and it kept asking for more paperwork. But at the same time, it started paying, including 30,000 dollars toward the disputed category."},{"t":"p","s":"The Court of Appeals sided with the insured. By paying on the claim and never formally denying liability, the carrier admitted the fire was covered. So the only question left, how much of the disputed betterments were owed, was an amount of loss question for the appraisers."},{"t":"p","s":"Cantina has a second lesson too, and it is a useful one to have in your pocket. The carrier waived the policy's 60 day proof of loss deadline by twice extending its own document requests and continuing to accept paperwork past that deadline. A carrier that keeps asking for documents after its own deadline has passed has trouble enforcing that deadline later."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"Maksym, which tightened Cantina hard."},{"t":"p","s":"For years, policyholder lawyers read Cantina broadly, as if any partial payment on any claim automatically opened the whole file to appraisal."},{"t":"p","s":"Then, in February of twenty twenty six, the Court of Appeals published Maksym versus Auto Owners Insurance Company and drew the line much tighter."},{"t":"p","s":"Here are the facts. A pipe broke in a second floor bathroom and damaged the kitchen below. The carrier's first letter confirmed coverage for the water damage from the leaking pipe. But in that same letter it expressly denied coverage for the plumbing repairs themselves and for additional living expenses."},{"t":"p","s":"Over the following months the carrier paid about 10,000 dollars for the specific kitchen items it accepted, while repeatedly saying it was still denying the plumbing claims."},{"t":"p","s":"The insureds demanded appraisal on their entire 135,000 dollar claim, arguing Cantina entitled them to it."},{"t":"p","s":"The Court of Appeals said no, and read Cantina's holding as far narrower than the insureds argued."},{"t":"p","s":"The key difference is worth saying slowly. The carrier in Cantina never formally denied liability, it just wanted more proof. The carrier in Maksym paid for the specific items it determined were covered while expressly disputing liability for the others."},{"t":"p","s":"That is not a partial payment on the disputed claims. That is payment on the accepted claims, plus an actual denial on the rest."},{"t":"p","s":"So the rule is: paying the parts you agree on while expressly denying the rest is not an admission on the denied parts. Those need a coverage decision first, whether by negotiation, a carve out, or a lawsuit."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Carrier pays 8000 dollars on the items it accepts and its letter says it is denying the rest outright. Can you drag the whole claim into appraisal on the strength of that payment."},{"t":"a","s":"No. That is Maksym, not Cantina. Payment on accepted items alongside an express denial of the others is not an admission on the denied items, and those go to a coverage decision first."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"What a formal denial actually has to say."},{"t":"p","s":"That raises the next question, and it matters twice over, because a formal denial both takes an item out of appraisal and restarts the 1 year suit clock."},{"t":"p","s":"The controlling language comes from Smitham versus State Farm Fire and Casualty Company, from two thousand twelve, quoted directly in Maksym. A formal denial must be explicit, and must unequivocally impress upon the insured the need to go to court."},{"t":"p","s":"Hear how high that bar is. Not a hint. Not discouragement. It has to make clear that the courthouse is the next stop."},{"t":"p","s":"Maksym itself shows both sides of the line inside one document. The carrier's first letter clearly denied the plumbing claim. But that same letter, on the second floor bathroom and other kitchen items, only asked for more documentation and warned that coverage may be jeopardized. The Court of Appeals said that language was not explicit or unequivocal, and sent the case back down."},{"t":"p","s":"So a single letter can formally deny some items while leaving others open. Read every carrier letter line by line, item by item. Do not read the first denial sentence and treat the whole letter as a denial."},{"t":"p","s":"Smitham adds a partial payment rule that is worth remembering when you are chasing supplements. The insured's awareness of how much the carrier paid is not itself a formal denial. To restart the clock, the carrier has to say in no uncertain terms that it is denying all liability in excess of what it has already paid."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. A letter says we need more documentation and coverage may be jeopardized. Formal denial, yes or no, and what is the test."},{"t":"a","s":"No. Under Smitham a formal denial must be explicit and must unequivocally impress upon the insured the need to go to court. Asking for documents and warning that coverage may be jeopardized is neither."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"The suit clock, and the pause built into it."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2833, subsection one q, says an action under the policy must be commenced within 1 year after the loss, or within the time period specified in the policy, whichever is longer."},{"t":"p","s":"So it is not always exactly a year. If the policy itself grants more time, the longer period wins. Floor, not ceiling, again."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is the part that should make you breathe easier. The statute tolls, meaning it pauses, the running of that clock from the time the insured notifies the insurer of the loss until the insurer formally denies liability."},{"t":"p","s":"Now connect that to the last idea. The tolling runs until formal denial. And you just learned how hard a formal denial is to make. So a carrier that keeps asking for documents instead of denying is not running out your clock. It is holding its own clock still."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"Tom Thomas, where the tolling rule came from."},{"t":"p","s":"This is not a brand new invention of the legislature. It goes back to a nineteen seventy six Michigan Supreme Court case, Tom Thomas Organization versus Reliance Insurance Company."},{"t":"p","s":"A film and tape company lost property. The policy gave 12 months to sue, and the insurer argued the clock ran from the date of loss."},{"t":"p","s":"The Supreme Court disagreed, holding that the appropriate resolution was to toll the running of the limitation from the time the insured gives notice until the insurer formally denies liability."},{"t":"p","s":"Walk the dates, because they show the rule doing real work. Notice went out around January of nineteen seventy two. The insurer denied liability in June of that year. Those 5 months did not count against the 12 month clock, so the lawsuit filed the following March came in on time."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is a piece of history that tells you how settled this is. The legislature did not write this section into the Insurance Code until nineteen ninety, 14 years after Tom Thomas. The courts built the tolling rule by hand first, and the legislature codified it later. The rule is older than the statute that now contains it."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Two traps inside Tom Thomas."},{"t":"p","s":"You need both of these, because both get misquoted."},{"t":"p","s":"First, Tom Thomas did not hold that a 1 year deadline is unreasonable or unconscionable. The Court said in so many words that it was unnecessary to reach that argument, and rested its holding on tolling instead. If you ever see that sentence quoted without the word not, the source has inverted the holding."},{"t":"p","s":"Second, Tom Thomas is partly overruled. In Rory versus Continental Insurance Company, a two thousand five Michigan Supreme Court case, the Court said that to the degree Tom Thomas and its progeny abrogate unambiguous contractual terms on the basis of reasonableness determinations, they are overruled."},{"t":"p","s":"Read the scope of that carefully. It reaches only the reasonableness talk. The tolling holding survives, and for fire policies it now lives in the statute anyway."},{"t":"p","s":"So the honest summary is: Tom Thomas is alive for tolling, dead for reasonableness."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Rory, and the limit of how far you can carry it."},{"t":"p","s":"Rory itself is the case on how hard Michigan courts enforce a clear deadline."},{"t":"p","s":"Two claimants sued their own auto insurer for uninsured motorist benefits about 16 months after the accident, past a 1 year policy deadline, and the lower courts refused to enforce it as unreasonable."},{"t":"p","s":"The Supreme Court reversed. A court must construe and apply unambiguous contract provisions as written, and a mere judicial assessment of reasonableness is an invalid basis to refuse to enforce a contract provision. Only a violation of law, or a traditional contract defense like fraud, duress, waiver, estoppel or unconscionability, can set a clear provision aside."},{"t":"p","s":"But do not carry Rory too far into a fire claim, and this is where people overreach."},{"t":"p","s":"Rory was uninsured motorist coverage, so subsection one q was not in play. In a fire policy the statute sets a floor. And a second statute, MCL 500.2860, makes any fire policy provision contrary to that chapter absolutely void."},{"t":"p","s":"Smitham used exactly that statute to strike a policy that tried to make tolling conditional on a formal denial. So a carrier cannot draft its way out of the tolling rule in a fire policy, and Rory does not let it."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. A fire policy says the 1 year suit clock is only tolled if the carrier issues a formal denial. Is that enforceable, and what kills it."},{"t":"a","s":"No. MCL 500.2860 makes a fire policy provision contrary to the chapter absolutely void, and Smitham struck exactly that kind of clause. Rory does not save it, because Rory was an uninsured motorist case with subsection one q not in play."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"Can your appraiser work on a percentage."},{"t":"p","s":"Yes, and this is where the independent versus impartial distinction from idea one pays off."},{"t":"p","s":"White versus State Farm Fire and Casualty Company, a published two thousand eleven case, held that a contingency fee does not disqualify a party appraiser from being independent. Because independent is not the same standard as impartial, and a party appraiser is allowed to advocate for the side that picked them, much like an attorney."},{"t":"p","s":"The one condition: the opposing party has to be told the contingency fee arrangement exists. Disclose it in the demand letter and you are fine."},{"t":"p","s":"So the umpire must be impartial and could not take a contingency. Your appraiser only has to be independent, and can."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"How hard is it to undo an award."},{"t":"p","s":"Very hard, and you should know that before you agree to the process."},{"t":"p","s":"Kwaiser set the standard and Dupree versus Auto Owners Insurance Company confirmed it. Judicial review of an appraisal award is limited to bad faith, fraud, misconduct, or manifest mistake. That is the same narrow standard courts use for arbitration awards."},{"t":"p","s":"Four grounds. Bad faith, fraud, misconduct, manifest mistake. Being unhappy with the number is not on the list."},{"t":"p","s":"Dupree teaches a second lesson on the same facts, and this one costs real money if you miss it. An award stating an actual cash value figure does not by itself entitle the insured to the replacement cost holdback, meaning the depreciation withheld until repairs are completed."},{"t":"p","s":"That insured had an award of over a 128,000 dollars in actual cash value and nearly forty thousand in calculated depreciation. But because she never proved she replaced the property, the Supreme Court held she was not owed the depreciation on top of the actual cash value number."},{"t":"p","s":"The practical lesson for anyone in restoration: an appraisal award is not the recovery of the holdback. Doing the work and proving it is what releases the holdback."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea twelve"},{"t":"p","s":"Let me run one whole claim through everything you just learned."},{"t":"p","s":"A hailstorm hits a Michigan roof. The contractor writes a full replacement estimate and the homeowner submits it with a signed proof of loss on day zero."},{"t":"p","s":"By day thirty the carrier agrees the storm is covered but pays only for a small patch on one slope, saying the rest of the roof needs more documentation."},{"t":"p","s":"Stop and classify that letter. It is not a formal denial under Smitham, because it asks for documents rather than unequivocally pointing her to court. So under Cantina this file sits on the appraisal track. Coverage admitted, only scope in dispute."},{"t":"p","s":"On day forty five the homeowner sends a written appraisal demand, names a competent, independent appraiser, and discloses any contingency fee right in the letter, following White."},{"t":"p","s":"Day sixty five, 20 days later, is the carrier's deadline to name its own appraiser."},{"t":"p","s":"Day eighty, 15 days after that, is the deadline for the two appraisers to agree an umpire or ask the circuit court to appoint one."},{"t":"p","s":"Once any two of the three sign, the amount is fixed."},{"t":"p","s":"Now change one fact and watch the whole thing move. Suppose the day thirty letter had said, explicitly, the north slope shows only pre existing wear, not hail impact, and that portion of the claim is denied."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the north slope must be carved out of the appraisal demand and given its own path, exactly the way the plumbing claim in Maksym had to be carved out."},{"t":"p","s":"One sentence in one letter changed which track half the claim rides on. That is why you read every letter line by line."},{"t":"p","s":"One honest gap before we recap, because I would rather give you the gap than a guess."},{"t":"p","s":"Nothing, not the statute and no case, tells you whether demanding appraisal by itself creates extra tolling of the suit clock beyond the notice to formal denial tolling the statute already gives you. In practice this rarely bites, because appraisal is only available once coverage is admitted, and an admission of coverage generally means no formal denial has happened yet. But if a carrier reverses course mid appraisal and issues a real denial, treat the interaction as an open question for counsel, not a settled rule."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the recap. Fast, as a self check."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection one m: either party demands in writing, 20 days to name appraisers, fifteen more to agree an umpire or ask the circuit court of the county of loss or property, item by item awards, any two of three bind, each side pays its own appraiser, umpire costs split fifty fifty. Kwaiser: coverage is for courts, amount of loss is for appraisers, and once coverage is admitted appraisal is mandatory. Cantina, twenty twenty four: continued processing without a formal denial equals an admission. Maksym, twenty twenty six: paying accepted items while expressly denying others is not a partial payment on the denied items. Smitham: a formal denial must be explicit and unequivocal, a partial payment does not restart the clock, and a policy cannot make tolling conditional on a denial. Subsection one q: 1 year after loss or the policy's longer period, tolled from notice until formal denial, a rule born in Tom Thomas in nineteen seventy six and written into the statute in nineteen ninety. Rory, two thousand five: courts enforce clear limitation periods as written, and Rory overruled Tom Thomas only on reasonableness. White, twenty eleven: contingency fee party appraisers are fine with disclosure. Dupree: awards are final except for bad faith, fraud, misconduct, or manifest mistake, and an actual cash value award does not automatically release the replacement cost holdback."},{"t":"p","s":"If any of the questions I asked gave you trouble, that is exactly where to drill."}],"words":3344,"questions":4},{"id":"06_adjuster-licensing-ch12_lecture","track":"law","title":"Adjuster and Public Adjuster Licensing, Chapter Twelve of the Michigan Insurance Code","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the domain that governs who may adjust an insurance claim in Michigan, who may charge a fee for helping a homeowner with a claim, and what happens to a contractor or public adjuster who steps over the line."},{"t":"p","s":"I want to tell you plainly why this chapter matters more to you than any other one in this track. It is the reason a restoration contractor cannot also be the person negotiating the claim. And it is the reason you cannot hold a Michigan adjuster license while you are connected to a restoration company. Not because of anything you did. Because of a sentence in a statute that closes the door before conduct is ever discussed."},{"t":"p","s":"So learn this one carefully. Fourteen ideas. After most of them, a question and silence. Answer out loud."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"The shape of Chapter Twelve."},{"t":"p","s":"Chapter Twelve of the Insurance Code of nineteen fifty six, Act two eighteen, runs from MCL 500.1200 through twelve forty seven."},{"t":"p","s":"It licenses four kinds of people in one chapter. Insurance producers, who you would call agents. Solicitors. Adjusters. And insurance counselors. All four touch an insurance transaction for pay, which is the common thread holding one chapter around four different jobs."},{"t":"p","s":"1200 through twelve forty seven. That is the range everything below lives inside."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"The first trap, and it is a strange one. The key words are never defined."},{"t":"p","s":"If you go looking for a definition of the word adjuster, or the phrase adjuster for the insured, which is Michigan's own term for what every other state calls a public adjuster, you will not find one."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.1201 is the definitions section. It defines agent, agent of the insured, agent of the insurer, insurance producer, negotiate, sell, and solicit."},{"t":"p","s":"It does not define adjuster. It does not define adjuster for the insured. It does not define independent adjuster."},{"t":"p","s":"Those are operative words used throughout the chapter without ever being pinned down."},{"t":"p","s":"And DIFS's own paperwork stumbles on the same gap. In one of the cases we will cover, DIFS pointed to two sections for the phrase fire repair contractor, and those sections actually define good moral character, not that phrase at all."},{"t":"p","s":"So when you meet these terms, understand what you are doing. You are reconstructing their meaning from how they are used, not reading a clean definition. That is an uncomfortable place to stand, and it is where the law actually leaves you."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Section 1222, subsection one. Three triggers, not one."},{"t":"p","s":"This is the whole ballgame section."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.1222, subsection one, says a person shall not adjust loss or damage under a policy of insurance, or advertise, solicit business, or hold himself or herself out to the public as an adjuster, unless he or she is licensed as an adjuster."},{"t":"p","s":"Notice there are three separate triggers packed into one sentence."},{"t":"p","s":"Actually adjusting a loss is one. Advertising as an adjuster is a second. And just holding yourself out as one, even without a single claim file in hand, is a third."},{"t":"p","s":"Hear what that means. You do not have to touch a claim to violate this section. Offering the service is enough. A website, a business card, a sentence in a sales conversation. That is the violation, standing alone."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. A contractor has never adjusted a claim in his life, but his website says he handles the insurance claim for you. Has he got a problem, and which trigger."},{"t":"a","s":"Yes. Advertising and holding himself out as an adjuster are separate triggers under 1222, subsection one. He does not need to have touched a claim."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"The five exemptions, and the sixth one that does not exist."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection two lists the exemptions, and there are exactly five, lettered A through E."},{"t":"p","s":"Memorize the count as hard as the content, because this is where exam writers and real world contractors both love to insert a sixth that does not exist."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter A, a person admitted to the practice of law in this state."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter B, a marine average adjuster, a narrow maritime specialty."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter C, an employee or manager of an authorized insurer adjusting a loss under a policy that same insurer issued. That is your everyday company adjuster, exempt because of the employment relationship, not because of a separate license."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter D, a licensed insurance producer to whom the insurer has granted claim authority."},{"t":"p","s":"Letter E, a narrow data entry carve out. Someone who collects or furnishes claim information and enters it into a claims system, but only while supervised by a licensed independent adjuster or an exempt insurer employee."},{"t":"p","s":"That is it. Five."},{"t":"p","s":"There is no contractor exemption anywhere in this list."},{"t":"p","s":"So say the consequence out loud, because it is the practical heart of the chapter. A restoration contractor who writes an estimate, submits it to the carrier on the homeowner's behalf, and negotiates the settlement for a fee is doing exactly what this section licenses. No relabeling changes that. The only way a contractor sits inside this list is if that contractor is also a licensed Michigan attorney."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Name the five exemptions, and tell me which one a restoration contractor fits into."},{"t":"a","s":"Attorney admitted in Michigan, marine average adjuster, the insurer's own employee or manager adjusting under that insurer's policy, a licensed producer with claim authority, and a supervised data entry person. A restoration contractor fits into none of them."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Getting the license, under 1224."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.1224 governs how you get it."},{"t":"p","s":"The commissioner decides a completed application within 60 days, may require a written exam, and can waive that exam for someone licensed as an adjuster within the preceding 12 months."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection three lists what the commissioner checks. Reasonable understanding of the insurance being adjusted. Reasonable understanding of Michigan insurance law. Good faith intent. Good business reputation. And good moral character."},{"t":"p","s":"Five checks in subsection three. Hold that number, because in a few minutes it is going to matter which subsection a real case was charged under."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection four. The sentence that closes your door."},{"t":"p","s":"This is the one this entire domain revolves around, so here it is close to verbatim."},{"t":"p","s":"The commissioner shall not issue a license to act as an adjuster to a person who is employed by, owns stock in, is an officer or director of, or in any other manner is connected with, a fire repair contractor."},{"t":"p","s":"Count the hooks in that sentence. There are four. Employment. Stock ownership. An officer or director title. And that broad catch all, in any other manner is connected with."},{"t":"p","s":"Now understand what kind of rule this is, because the category matters more than the words. This is a licensing bar, not a conduct standard. It stops the commissioner from ever issuing the license in the first place, before any misconduct is even alleged."},{"t":"p","s":"That is why you cannot hold a Michigan adjuster license today. It is not about how you would behave. Any role with a restoration contractor, ownership, an officer title, or that catch all connection, closes the door before conduct ever comes into question."},{"t":"p","s":"And notice how wide that fourth hook is. In any other manner is connected with does not have edges you can engineer around. That is the point of writing it that way."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. What are the four hooks in 1224, subsection four, and is it about what you did or about who you are connected to."},{"t":"a","s":"Employed by, owns stock in, is an officer or director of, or in any other manner is connected with a fire repair contractor. It is about the connection, not about conduct. It is a bar on issuing the license at all."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"1226, and the rules for the contract itself."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.1226 has four pieces."},{"t":"p","s":"First, an adjuster for the insured cannot pay someone to help solicit or adjust a loss unless that person is both regularly employed by the adjuster and separately licensed. Both, not either."},{"t":"p","s":"Second, when soliciting, the adjuster must orally identify as an adjuster for the insured and leave a card stating that title and their rates. And they cannot claim to be an insurer's adjuster, a fire investigator, or connected to a fire department."},{"t":"p","s":"Third, the number you must know cold. An adjuster for the insured shall not charge a rate exceeding 10 percent of the amount paid by the insurer in settlement of the loss."},{"t":"p","s":"DIFS's own guidance adds a wrinkle worth having. That 10 percent applies only to the coverages actually marked on the contract. So a public adjuster hired for building and contents cannot also take 10 percent of a loss of use payment the contract never covered."},{"t":"p","s":"Fourth, and this is the piece that generates the most litigation. An adjuster for an insured shall not provide services to a client until there is a written contract, on a form approved by the commissioner. And a contract signed within 48 hours of the loss is voidable at the insured's option for 10 days after signing. A copy must be given to the insured at signing."},{"t":"p","s":"Three separate requirements inside that fourth piece. The approved form. The 48 hour and 10 day voidability window. And the copy requirement. Skip any one of them and you are exposed. Remember that, because a case in a few minutes turns on exactly this."},{"t":"p","s":"10 percent, 48 hours, 10 days. Those are the three numbers."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Twelve twenty seven, the conduct companion."},{"t":"p","s":"A companion conduct section, twelve twenty seven, adds three rules."},{"t":"p","s":"No soliciting a loss while it is still happening or while fire department personnel are on scene."},{"t":"p","s":"No collecting a fee from a repair contractor for steering repair work to that contractor. That is the statutory wall against adjuster to contractor kickbacks, and it is worth knowing exactly because it describes an arrangement people propose casually."},{"t":"p","s":"And no advancing money to an insured while a claim is pending."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"A different license entirely. The insurance counselor."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.1232 requires a license for anyone who audits or abstracts policies, or gives advice, counsel, or opinion about a policy's benefits, coverage, terms, or value."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the trap, and it runs the opposite direction from what people fear."},{"t":"p","s":"The second sentence says a non licensee shall not use terms such as consultant or consulting services, or any other language, in a way which implies that he or she is a licensed insurance counselor."},{"t":"p","s":"Read that conditionally, because it is written conditionally. The word ban depends on the implication of licensure. It is not an outright ban on the word consultant showing up anywhere near an insurance sentence."},{"t":"p","s":"So people spend their worry on the wrong sentence. The much bigger exposure sits in the first sentence. Writing a claim estimate for every dollar owed and submitting it to a carrier is advice about the value of the policy, and that is licensed territory regardless of what you call the fee."},{"t":"p","s":"Attorneys are exempt from this section too, same as they are from the adjuster license requirement."},{"t":"p","s":"And a companion section, MCL 500.1236, requires a signed written fee agreement in advance of any counselor service, retained for at least 2 years."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"Discipline, and the emergency lever."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.1242 is the procedural engine."},{"t":"p","s":"The commissioner must give written notice with a hearing right before refusing a license, and may suspend or revoke after notice and a hearing for failing to maintain standards or violating the act."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection four is the emergency lever. Without any prior hearing, the commissioner may summarily suspend a license if protection of the public requires emergency action."},{"t":"p","s":"If a hearing is then requested, it must happen within 20 days, and a decision must be announced within 30 days after that hearing concludes."},{"t":"p","s":"20 days to the hearing, 30 days to the decision. A pair, in that order."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"The money, under 1244."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.1244 is where the dollar amounts live, and there are two tiers plus a separate tier for defiance."},{"t":"p","s":"After a hearing, the director can order a civil fine of not more than 1000 dollars per violation. That rises to not more than 5000 dollars per violation if the person knew or should have known they were violating the chapter. Capped at 50,000 dollars total per order."},{"t":"p","s":"The director can also order a refund of overcharges, restitution to the insured, and suspension or revocation of the license."},{"t":"p","s":"And if someone then knowingly violates a resulting cease and desist order, the stakes go up again. Up to 20,000 dollars per violation, capped at 100,000 dollars per order."},{"t":"p","s":"One thousand ordinary, five thousand knowing, fifty thousand cap. Then twenty thousand and one hundred thousand for defying the order. Two pairs."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Knowing violation, what is the per violation fine and the cap on the order, and what happens if he then ignores the cease and desist."},{"t":"a","s":"5000 dollars per violation, capped at fifty thousand per order. Knowingly violating the resulting cease and desist order runs up to twenty thousand per violation, capped at one hundred thousand per order."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea twelve"},{"t":"p","s":"Guldshtadt, where all of this actually happened to somebody."},{"t":"p","s":"The first real case is Aleksandr Guldshtadt, DIFS enforcement case number twenty five dash one eight three five zero."},{"t":"p","s":"He was a licensed non resident adjuster for the insured, affiliated with a firm called Nationwide Insurance Claims Advocates."},{"t":"p","s":"DIFS summarily suspended his license on November third, twenty twenty five, using that emergency authority in 1242, subsection four. His final decision came down April first, twenty twenty six. License revoked, and a 50,000 dollar civil fine, sitting right at the statutory cap."},{"t":"p","s":"What did DIFS find? He was listed as C E O of a California general contractor whose website advertised fire restoration, on that company's California corporate filings, and as its registered agent on the Michigan filings, even though he told a DIFS investigator he did not partner with contractors."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the connected with a fire repair contractor problem, in the flesh."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is a precision point worth carrying, because it shows the statute working in two different ways."},{"t":"p","s":"The categorical licensing bar is 1224, subsection four. But DIFS did not charge him under it. Because he was already licensed, DIFS ran the charge through 1224, subsection three, and 1242, subsection two. Failure to maintain the standards required for initial licensing."},{"t":"p","s":"Know both routes. The text bar for an applicant. The maintenance route for a sitting licensee. Subsection four keeps you from getting it. Subsection three plus 1242 takes it away once you have it."},{"t":"p","s":"He also entered more than twenty five public adjusting contracts before DIFS ever approved his contract form, many of them carrying a 35 percent fee. That is more than triple the 10 percent cap. And he used unlicensed individuals to present those contracts to homeowners."},{"t":"p","s":"One important detail about how much weight this decision carries. Guldshtadt never requested a hearing, so the whole decision rests on a default record. Unchallenged allegations accepted as true, not findings tested in a contested hearing. That does not make it wrong. It does mean you should describe it accurately."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea thirteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Tredenick, and an 8 percent fee that vanished."},{"t":"p","s":"The second case is Tredenick versus Nationwide Agribusiness, involving a public adjuster called Prime Adjusting Service. Michigan Court of Appeals number three four nine eight eight seven, decided April eighth, twenty twenty one."},{"t":"p","s":"Label it correctly before you use it. This one is unpublished and per curiam, which means under the court rules it is persuasive only, not binding precedent. Even though it is the clearest applied reading we have of that 48 hour and 10 day rule."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is what happened. After a farm fire, a licensed public adjuster signed an 8 percent contract with the Tredenicks."},{"t":"p","s":"The form Prime used was not a DIFS approved form. It omitted the 48 hour, 10 day voidability sentence. It also lacked the Home Solicitation Sales Act 3 day cancellation notice, though the Court of Appeals decided the case on the insurance code defect alone."},{"t":"p","s":"Now watch the timing, because it is the entire point of the case. That contract was actually signed about a month after the fire. So the missing 48 hour clause could not have helped these homeowners on these facts."},{"t":"p","s":"And the court held that made no difference at all."},{"t":"p","s":"The trial court let the Tredenicks void the contract, and denied the adjuster both its 8 percent fee and a fallback claim for quantum meruit, which is payment for the reasonable value of services actually performed even without an enforceable contract."},{"t":"p","s":"The Court of Appeals affirmed both denials. Its key line is worth memorizing: given that the adjuster failed to meet the statutory condition precedent to perform public adjuster services, there is no inequity in denying it payment for those services."},{"t":"p","s":"One small warning if you ever quote that line in writing. The opinion as published actually prints the word i n h e r i t, not inherent. So quote it exactly or paraphrase it, but never silently fix a court's text."},{"t":"p","s":"Read the holding straight through, because the result is brutal and instructive. A licensed adjuster, on a modest 8 percent contract, with a defective form, recovered nothing. Not the contract price. Not a fallback payment."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the baseline for measuring any percentage fee claim by an unlicensed party on a Michigan property loss. If a licensed adjuster with a paperwork defect gets zero, think about where an unlicensed contractor stands."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. The form defect could not have hurt these particular homeowners, because they signed a month after the fire. Why did the adjuster still lose everything."},{"t":"a","s":"Because compliance with the statute is a condition precedent to performing the services at all. It is not a harm based test. The court found no inequity in denying payment, and denied the 8 percent fee and quantum meruit both."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea fourteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Florida, strictly as contrast, and the exam logistics."},{"t":"p","s":"Florida's public adjuster statute defines a public adjuster as anyone who prepares, completes, or files a claim, or negotiates a settlement, for value, regardless of how that person describes or presents his or her services."},{"t":"p","s":"That last clause is Florida's anti relabeling rule, written right into the definition. Michigan has no equivalent sentence in 1222. Michigan reaches relabeling indirectly, through that conditional word ban we covered."},{"t":"p","s":"Florida also gives contractors an explicit safe harbor Michigan lacks. A licensed contractor may discuss or explain a repair bid with the owner or the insurer, as long as they are doing so for the usual and customary fees stated in their own construction contract."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan contractors get no comparable sentence. So the boundary here gets drawn case by case, through enforcement actions like Guldshtadt and decisions like Tredenick. That is a much less comfortable way to know where a line is."},{"t":"p","s":"One practical note before the recap. DIFS contracts its licensing exams to PSI Services LLC. The flat exam fee is 41 dollars, valid 1 year. The Public Adjuster exam is 2 hours, one hundred items, 71 percent to pass. The resident application fee is 15 dollars plus a 5 dollar transaction fee, and you have 6 months from filing to pass the exam or you reapply and pay again."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is an honest absence. Neither the statute nor DIFS's licensing page mentions continuing education for adjusters, unlike producers, who owe 24 hours every 2 years. Neither mentions a surety bond. If a source claims otherwise, it needs a citation, because none was found here."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the rapid fire recap. Fast, as a self check."},{"t":"p","s":"Chapter Twelve runs from 1200 through twelve forty seven. 1222 has exactly five exemptions, none for contractors. 1224, subsection four, bars anyone connected with a fire repair contractor from holding the license. 1226 caps the fee at 10 percent and requires the DIFS approved contract, with the 48 hour, 10 day voidability window. 1232 conditionally restricts the word consulting. 1242 gives summary suspension power. 1244 caps civil fines at 50,000 dollars per order, or one hundred thousand for violating a cease and desist order. Guldshtadt lost his license and paid fifty thousand. Tredenick's adjuster lost an 8 percent fee over a defective form."},{"t":"p","s":"Know these numbers and the five exemptions by heart. If any of the questions I asked gave you trouble, that is exactly where to drill."}],"words":3395,"questions":5},{"id":"07_claims-clocks-and-difs_lecture","track":"law","title":"Claims Clocks and DIFS","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This chapter turns Michigan insurance law into a calendar you can actually use on a real file."},{"t":"p","s":"If you remember nothing else from the next half hour, remember this one idea. Almost none of Michigan's claims clocks start by themselves. They start when a specific piece of paper reaches the insurance company. A notice of loss. A proof of loss. A written demand."},{"t":"p","s":"So here is the practical consequence, and it is the most valuable sentence in this chapter. If you wait for the carrier's own estimate before sending anything, you have handed it the calendar. Nothing is running. The carrier is under no pressure, because no paper started any clock."},{"t":"p","s":"That idea is the spine of everything below. Thirteen ideas. After most of them, a question and then silence. Answer out loud."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"Notice of loss, and a number that does not exist."},{"t":"p","s":"A loss happens. Under MCL 500.2833, subsection one, letter l, every Michigan fire policy must contain, quote, the notification requirements when a loss occurs, end quote."},{"t":"p","s":"Now notice what that does not say. It does not say how many days the insured has to report. The day count lives in the policy's conditions section, not the statute."},{"t":"p","s":"So if someone asks how many days a homeowner has to report a loss under Michigan statute, the honest answer is that the statute sets no number. Read the policy."},{"t":"p","s":"That is worth saying confidently, because people will insist there is a statutory number. There is not."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"The insurer's 30 day duty, and a shortcut that is wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"Once that notice of loss lands on the insurer's desk, the insurer has its own clock running."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2006, subsection three, says the insurer shall specify in writing the materials that constitute a satisfactory proof of loss not later than 30 days after receipt of a claim, unless the claim is settled within those 30 days."},{"t":"p","s":"Read the trigger carefully. 30 days from receipt of the claim itself, meaning the notice. Not from receipt of a proof of loss. So the moment a homeowner reports a loss, a 30 day clock starts on the insurer to tell her in writing exactly what documents it needs."},{"t":"p","s":"Now be careful, because there is a shortcut people repeat that is simply wrong. You will hear that if the insurer stays silent past day thirty, whatever proof of loss the homeowner later submits automatically counts as satisfactory, and that Griswold says so."},{"t":"p","s":"Griswold says the opposite. Whether a satisfactory proof of loss was received is a question of fact."},{"t":"p","s":"So do not build a strategy on that shortcut. But do not undervalue the duty either. It is a dated obligation the carrier either met or did not, and it locks in the document list the carrier said it needed. A carrier that later demands a pile of things it never listed has a problem."},{"t":"p","s":"That subsection has a second rule that is genuinely useful. If a proof of loss covers only part of the claim, that part is timely paid if paid within 60 days of receipt, and any later piece gets its own fresh 60 day clock."},{"t":"p","s":"That is why smart strategy splits a claim into the undisputed part and the fought over part. You get the clean money moving on its own clock instead of letting it sit hostage to the disputed part."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Homeowner reports a fire on June first. What does the carrier owe her by roughly July first, and what does silence past that date NOT give her."},{"t":"a","s":"By roughly July first the carrier owes a written specification of the materials that constitute a satisfactory proof of loss, under 2006, subsection three. Silence does not automatically make her later proof of loss satisfactory. Under Griswold that stays a question of fact."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"The mortgagee's 60 days, and what it is not."},{"t":"p","s":"A separate clock sits in the mortgagee clause."},{"t":"p","s":"Under MCL 500.2833, subsection one, letter j, if a loss is payable to a named mortgagee and the insured fails to render a proof of loss, the insurer can notify the mortgagee, which then has 60 days after that notice to render its own."},{"t":"p","s":"Two limits on it. It only matters if the insured never submits her own. If she does, the mortgagee clock never turns on at all."},{"t":"p","s":"And do not confuse it with an insured's own proof of loss deadline. The statute sets none. 60 days belongs to the mortgagee alone, as a backstop."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"The two money clocks, thirty and sixty, and the trap between them."},{"t":"p","s":"These are the two most confused clocks in the chapter. Two numbers, measuring different things."},{"t":"p","s":"The 30 day clock, in MCL 500.2833, subsection one, letter p, and echoed in MCL 500.2836, subsection two, says the loss is payable within 30 days after receipt of proof of the amount of loss. That is the payment deadline."},{"t":"p","s":"The 60 day clock is a different animal. It comes from MCL 500.2006, subsection four, and it governs when 12 percent simple interest starts piling up on unpaid benefits. From a date 60 days after satisfactory proof of loss was received, if the claimant is the insured or a person directly entitled to benefits."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the trap, and it is a good one. A claim paid on day forty five is late under the 30 day payment rule, but may owe zero interest, because interest does not start until day sixty one."},{"t":"p","s":"Lateness and interest are not the same thing. Say that back to yourself, because you will want to conflate them."},{"t":"p","s":"One more critical detail. The reasonably in dispute defense, which normally lets an insurer off the hook for an unfair trade practice, does not apply to this 12 percent when the claimant is a first party insured. It is written only into the sentence about third party tort claimants."},{"t":"p","s":"Two cases nail this down. Griswold Properties versus Lexington Insurance Company, two seventy six Michigan Appeals five fifty one, and Nickola versus MIC General Insurance Company, 500 Michigan one fifteen, at the Supreme Court. Both hold a first party insured gets the 12 percent regardless of dispute."},{"t":"p","s":"And one honest boundary. No published Michigan property case extends that right to a restoration contractor holding an assignment of benefits. It belongs to the insured."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Proof of the amount of loss lands March first, carrier pays April tenth. Late? Interest owed?"},{"t":"a","s":"Late, yes, because payment was due within 30 days under letter p. Interest, no, because the 12 percent does not begin until 60 days after satisfactory proof of loss, which would be roughly April thirtieth."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Appraisal, in one minute."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2833, subsection one, letter m, has two key day counts. Twenty and fifteen."},{"t":"p","s":"After a written demand, each side has 20 days to name a competent, independent appraiser and tell the other side who it picked. Those two then try to agree on a neutral umpire. If they cannot within 15 days, either party can ask a circuit judge, in the county where the loss occurred or the property sits, to pick one."},{"t":"p","s":"Whatever any two of those three sign off on in writing becomes the amount of the loss. Each side pays its own appraiser. The umpire's cost and other appraisal expenses split fifty fifty."},{"t":"p","s":"And remember what appraisal decides. Only the amount, never whether the loss is covered. That distinction comes from Auto Owners Insurance Company versus Kwaiser, one hundred ninety Michigan Appeals four hundred eighty two, where the court said flatly that the issue of coverage is for the court, not the appraisers."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Cancellation, where two statutes stack."},{"t":"p","s":"This is where people get confused about which rule controls, so let me separate the layers."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2833, subsection one, letter i, is the fire policy's own built in cancellation clause. The insurer can cancel at any time by mailing notice not less than 10 days before cancellation, postage prepaid, tendering or promising to refund the excess earned premium."},{"t":"p","s":"Understand what that 10 days is. It is the floor every fire policy must contain as a contract term. It is about what the policy must say."},{"t":"p","s":"For an actual homeowner's policy, that is not the end of it. The Essential Insurance Act, chapter twenty one, layers an effectiveness rule on top for what it calls home insurance."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2123 says a termination is not effective unless the insurer mails written notice at least 30 days before the termination date. Except a notice given inside the policy's first 55 days can be effective in as little as 20 days. And nonpayment notices follow whatever the policy itself says."},{"t":"p","s":"And termination is a defined word, at MCL 500.2104, subsection five. A refusal to continue to insure for reasons other than nonpayment of premium."},{"t":"p","s":"That definition is why nonpayment sits outside the 30 day rule. It was never inside it, because nonpayment is not a termination as the act defines it."},{"t":"p","s":"So the pair to hold: 10 days is what the fire policy must say, 30 days is when a home insurance termination becomes effective. Different statutes, different jobs. And 20 days inside the first fifty five."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"The closed list, and a bulletin about satellite photos."},{"t":"p","s":"DIFS spells this out in bulletin twenty twenty five dash twelve dash INS, on insurers using aerial imagery to assess roof condition."},{"t":"p","s":"It says that canceling or nonrenewing home insurance for reasons other than nonpayment triggers the 30 day rule, and that the reasons must come from the closed list in MCL 500.2117."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is that closed list. Not being an eligible person. Physical condition and occupancy. A changed adjacent hazard. Claim history. And underinsurance."},{"t":"p","s":"Five grounds. That is the whole list, and closed means closed."},{"t":"p","s":"Now notice what is not on it. Cosmetic roof discoloration seen in a satellite photo. The bulletin says almost exactly that: mere cosmetic roof issues, like discoloration or streaking, are not valid grounds for cancellation or nonrenewal."},{"t":"p","s":"That is a genuinely useful thing to know for a homeowner who just got a nonrenewal notice after a drone or satellite sweep."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"A stale number printed in a live statute."},{"t":"p","s":"On that claim history ground, here is a number that will catch you if you read only the statute."},{"t":"p","s":"The statute still prints three thousand and four thousand dollars as the 3 year paid claim minimums. Those are stale."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection five tells the director to raise them with inflation every sixth year and certify the new figures in a bulletin, and bulletin twenty twenty four dash zero two dash INS did exactly that."},{"t":"p","s":"Since January first, twenty twenty four, the real numbers are 5000 dollars excluding weather related claims and six thousand six hundred including them, with the next adjustment due in twenty thirty."},{"t":"p","s":"This is a classic superseded figure trap, and I want you to feel the shape of it, because it repeats all over insurance law. The statute is real. The number printed inside it is not current. If you quote three thousand and four thousand from the statute text, you are quoting something true on the page and false in the world."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Claim history grounds. Which dollar figures are live, which are printed in the statute, and what reconciles them."},{"t":"a","s":"Live are 5000 dollars excluding weather related claims and six thousand six hundred including them, since January first twenty twenty four. The statute still prints three thousand and four thousand. Subsection five directs the director to adjust them every sixth year by bulletin, and bulletin twenty twenty four dash zero two dash INS certified the current ones."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"The 1 year suit clock, frozen."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2833, subsection one, letter q, says an action under the policy must be commenced within 1 year after the loss, or within whatever longer period the policy itself specifies."},{"t":"p","s":"But that 1 year period is tolled, meaning the clock pauses, from the moment the insured notifies the insurer of the loss until the insurer formally denies liability."},{"t":"p","s":"Walk the dates. A loss January fifth, notice January sixth. If the insurer never formally denies, only that single day counts against the year. The clock stays frozen the whole time the claim sits open."},{"t":"p","s":"If a clear formal denial arrives 6 months later, the remaining days run again from that date."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the judgment call you will actually face. A partial payment with a reservation of rights is not, by itself, a formal denial. A letter saying the company considers the file closed reads much closer to one."},{"t":"p","s":"And the statute never defines formally denies. So here is the working rule: calendar every ambiguous letter as if it might be the one that restarts the clock. You cannot un-miss a deadline, and the cost of calendaring a letter that turned out to be harmless is nothing."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"DIFS, and the three things it cannot do."},{"t":"p","s":"The Department of Insurance and Financial Services describes its Office of Consumer Services as providing consumer information, investigating complaints, and assisting consumers in resolving them whenever possible, after first encouraging people to try the carrier directly."},{"t":"p","s":"You can file online or by mail, email, or fax. The call center runs Monday through Friday, eight to five."},{"t":"p","s":"But here is the honest limit you need cold, and it is the difference between helping a homeowner and misleading one."},{"t":"p","s":"What DIFS says it does is investigate and help resolve. What its own bulletin twenty twenty five dash twenty five dash INS says it can bring down on a carrier is fines, and suspension or revocation of the carrier's certificate of authority."},{"t":"p","s":"Now notice what is missing from all of that. Nothing DIFS publishes says it can decide whether your loss is covered, order a specific payment, or act as your attorney."},{"t":"p","s":"Do not promise a homeowner any of those three. That promise is how you lose someone's trust 2 months later."},{"t":"p","s":"And nowhere on that page does DIFS promise a number of days for its own response, or for forcing a carrier to answer. So if someone tells you DIFS makes carriers answer within 30 days, that is not supported by anything DIFS publishes."},{"t":"p","s":"One more piece of realism. The statute behind the enforcement, section 2026, reaches conduct other than isolated incidents showing a persistent tendency. So one badly handled file is not by itself the violation."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"Young, and the one line that divides the remedies."},{"t":"p","s":"Young versus Michigan Mutual Insurance Company holds an insured has no independent cause of action under section 2026, the fourteen unfair practices, because that section exists to give the regulator authority over continuing practices."},{"t":"p","s":"But the same case approves a private suit to recover the interest penalty under section 2006."},{"t":"p","s":"So memorize the split in one line. The 12 percent you can sue for. The fourteen practices you cannot."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea twelve"},{"t":"p","s":"The quiet statute that makes a complaint letter worth writing."},{"t":"p","s":"A quieter statute works in the background, and it is one almost nobody uses on purpose."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2026, subsection two. It is an unfair practice for an insurer to fail to keep a complete record of every insured complaint since its last examination, with the total count, classification by line, the nature of each, its disposition, and the time it took to process."},{"t":"p","s":"Think about what that means for you practically. A certified letter of complaint straight to the carrier, even before or instead of DIFS, creates a paper trail the company must log. It goes into a record a regulator will eventually read."},{"t":"p","s":"That is a low cost move with real downstream weight, and most people never make it."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea thirteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Two things outside the Insurance Code that decide when money actually arrives."},{"t":"p","s":"First, the mortgage servicer, which is often the real reason a homeowner is still waiting."},{"t":"p","s":"If a homeowner's mortgage is owned by Fannie Mae, the check usually goes to the servicer first, because of the mortgagee clause. Fannie Mae's Servicing Guide, section B dash five dash zero one, Insured Loss Events, tells servicers how to release it."},{"t":"p","s":"If the loan is current or less than 31 days behind, the servicer can release an initial payment up to the greater of 40,000 dollars, 33 percent of total proceeds, or whatever exceeds the loan's remaining balance plus interest and advances. Then the rest in draws tied to repair inspections."},{"t":"p","s":"If the loan is 31 days or more behind, the rules tighten. Small proceeds of 5000 dollars or less can go out in one payment. But anything larger gets an initial release of only 25 percent of the proceeds, capped at the greater of 10,000 dollars or the amount above the loan payoff, with the rest released in 25 percent increments after inspections and a required final inspection."},{"t":"p","s":"And critically for anyone in this business: servicers must prohibit paying a public adjuster or other third party fee out of proceeds unless Fannie Mae approves it in writing first."},{"t":"p","s":"Now label what that is. A private investor rule, not Michigan law. It binds the servicer, not the insurer. And it is often exactly why a homeowner wins the statutory 30 day payment fight and still waits weeks for cash. The carrier obeyed the statute. The servicer is following a different rulebook."},{"t":"p","s":"Second, understand what a DIFS bulletin is legally. Not a statute. It is the director's interpretive guidance on how DIFS reads and enforces the Insurance Code, replaceable by the next bulletin with no legislative vote."},{"t":"p","s":"That cuts both ways, and this is the subtle part. A bulletin can also be the only place a statutory number's live version lives, as with the five thousand and six thousand six hundred figures you just learned. Not law, and yet the only current source for a number the law relies on."},{"t":"p","s":"Three live bulletins to keep straight."},{"t":"p","s":"Bulletin twenty twenty four dash twenty six dash INS says that starting with homeowners and dwelling policies issued or renewed on or after July first, twenty twenty five, an insurer depreciating what it calls nontangible items, meaning labor, taxes, fees, and overhead and profit, in actual cash value must do it through a separate, optional, lower premium endorsement naming those items, rather than burying it in policy definitions. That conditions the practice. It does not ban it."},{"t":"p","s":"Bulletin twenty twenty five dash twenty five dash INS says dragging out claims administration or making unreasonable proof of loss demands to gain leverage violates the Code, citing section 2026. But it is written around auto claims, not property."},{"t":"p","s":"And bulletin 2026 dash sixteen dash INS sets the fire insurance maximum escrow under MCL 500.2227 at 25,090 dollars for July first, 2026 through June thirtieth, twenty twenty seven. Do not confuse it with the separate calendar year escrow under MCL 500.2845, which bulletin twenty twenty five dash twenty seven dash INS sets at 16,383 dollars for 2026."},{"t":"p","s":"Two provisions, two amounts, two periods. That is the whole distinction."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the recap. Fast, as a self check."},{"t":"p","s":"Notice starts the insurer's 30 day duty to specify proof of loss requirements, and starts tolling the 1 year suit clock. Proof of the amount of loss starts two clocks: 30 days to pay, 60 days before 12 percent interest begins. An amount dispute can go to appraisal, 20 days to name an appraiser, fifteen more to agree on an umpire. Fire policy cancellation needs at least 10 days notice, but a home insurance cancellation or nonrenewal for reasons other than nonpayment needs at least thirty, tied to the closed list in section 2117, whose claim history dollars are five thousand and six thousand six hundred, not the three thousand and four thousand printed in the statute. And the suit clock sits frozen from notice until a formal denial arrives."},{"t":"p","s":"Master that sequence and you have mastered this chapter. If any of the questions I asked gave you trouble, that is exactly where to drill."}],"words":3322,"questions":3},{"id":"08_contractor-lien-aob_lecture","track":"law","title":"Contractors, Liens and Assignment of Benefits in Claims","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"Three bodies of Michigan law collide inside a single restoration job."},{"t":"p","s":"The Construction Lien Act, which governs how a contractor gets paid by the homeowner. The Insurance Code, which governs the homeowner's claim against the carrier. And old fashioned assignment law, which governs whether the contractor can step into the homeowner's shoes and deal with the carrier directly."},{"t":"p","s":"Get any one of these wrong and the contractor can do excellent work and collect nothing for it. That is not a figure of speech. In the first case we are going to cover, a man did the work and ended up with nothing but exposure."},{"t":"p","s":"So this is the chapter where the law touches your actual business hardest. Fourteen ideas. After most of them, a question and then silence. Answer out loud."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"Epps, and the facts worth picturing."},{"t":"p","s":"The case that anchors this whole chapter is Epps versus four Quarters Restoration, four ninety eight Michigan five eighteen, decided in twenty fifteen."},{"t":"p","s":"A home in Detroit floods in 2006. The homeowners, Danny and Joyce Epps, are referred to a man named Troy Willis and his companies, four Quarters Restoration and Emergency Insurance Services."},{"t":"p","s":"Willis is not a licensed residential builder when he does the work. His builder's license had been revoked on January thirty first of 2006, and he did not tell the Epps that."},{"t":"p","s":"The Epps sign a repair agreement assigning the insurance proceeds to Willis's company. Willis went on to receive and endorse Auto Owners checks totaling one hundred twenty eight thousand and forty seven dollars, signing the Epps' names himself."},{"t":"p","s":"If that sounds like a modern assignment of benefits arrangement, that is because it basically is one. Which is exactly why this case sits at the front of the chapter."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"Void or voidable, and the word doing the work."},{"t":"p","s":"The Michigan Supreme Court had to answer a basic sounding question. Is a contract with an unlicensed builder void, meaning it never legally existed, or voidable, meaning it is valid unless and until someone chooses to void it?"},{"t":"p","s":"The difference is enormous. If void, neither side has any rights under it at all."},{"t":"p","s":"The Court held that contracts between an innocent homeowner and an unlicensed residential builder are voidable, not void, correcting sloppier language in older opinions."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the nuance that most summaries of this case get wrong, and it is worth slowing down for."},{"t":"p","s":"The word innocent in that holding is not decorative. The Michigan Supreme Court expressly limited its ruling to a homeowner who did not know the contractor was unlicensed. In the Epps' own case, there was no indication in the record that they knew Willis was unlicensed, so the Court said its analysis was limited to that fact pattern."},{"t":"p","s":"The Court did not decide what happens if a homeowner knowingly hires an unlicensed contractor."},{"t":"p","s":"So if you ever hear someone say Epps means the voidable rule applies no matter what the homeowner knew, that is an overstatement of the actual holding. Say the narrower version. It is the one that is true."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"The suit bar, which does not care what anybody knew."},{"t":"p","s":"There is a second piece of Epps that is completely separate from the void versus voidable question, and this one genuinely does not care what the homeowner knew."},{"t":"p","s":"The statute at issue, MCL 339.2412, subsection one, says an unlicensed contractor cannot bring or maintain a lawsuit to collect compensation without alleging and proving it was licensed during performance."},{"t":"p","s":"No knowledge exception anywhere in the text. None."},{"t":"p","s":"The Michigan Supreme Court described this rule using a phrase borrowed from an earlier Court of Appeals case, Parker versus McQuade Plumbing and Heating. The statute operates as a shield, not a sword."},{"t":"p","s":"Unpack that phrase, because it is precise. It removes the unlicensed contractor's power to sue. It does not remove the contractor's power to defend itself if the homeowner sues first. And it does not hand the homeowner an independent right to sue the unlicensed contractor to get money back. The Court specifically declined to read that private right of action into the statute."},{"t":"p","s":"So hold the two part structure of Epps in your head as two separate things. The suit bar under subsection one is absolute and knowledge independent. The void versus voidable classification of the contract itself was decided only for the innocent homeowner scenario."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Unlicensed contractor, homeowner refuses to pay. Can he sue? Can he defend if she sues him? Can she sue him just for being unlicensed?"},{"t":"a","s":"He cannot sue, because subsection one bars it with no knowledge exception. He can defend himself if she sues first, because it is a shield not a sword. And she has no independent private right of action just for the unlicensed status, because the Court declined to read one in."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"The rest of 2412."},{"t":"p","s":"That same statute, subsection three, also says an unlicensed contractor cannot impose a construction lien on the property."},{"t":"p","s":"And subsection four lets a prosecutor or the attorney general sue for a civil fine of not less than five thousand and not more than 25,000 dollars."},{"t":"p","s":"Put it together. No lawsuit, no lien, and a fine between five and twenty five thousand dollars. Licensing is not a technicality. It is the foundation everything else in this chapter sits on."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"The written contract rule that kills liens."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the Construction Lien Act rules for residential work, because a restoration job is almost always residential."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 570.1114 says a contractor has no right to a construction lien on a homeowner's property unless the improvement was done under a written contract, with any amendments also in writing, and that contract states, in type no smaller than the body of the contract, that the work requires a license and, if it does, that the contractor is licensed and the actual license number."},{"t":"p","s":"Three details there that people miss."},{"t":"p","s":"There is no dollar floor. This is not a rule that kicks in above some threshold. It applies to residential work, period."},{"t":"p","s":"The type size requirement is real. No smaller than the body of the contract."},{"t":"p","s":"And this is the one that costs money: miss the license number and the lien is gone, even if the contractor is genuinely licensed. A fully licensed contractor with a perfect job loses lien rights over a missing number on a page."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Licensed contractor, written contract, amendments in writing, but the license number was never typed in. Does he have a lien?"},{"t":"a","s":"No. Under 570 point 1114 the contract has to state the actual license number. Being genuinely licensed does not save the lien."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Three clocks, plus one for laborers."},{"t":"p","s":"You need these cold, so let me give them as a chain."},{"t":"p","s":"20 days. A subcontractor or supplier has to serve a Notice of Furnishing within 20 days after furnishing the first labor or material, under MCL 570.1109."},{"t":"p","s":"But here is a favorite trap. A contractor in direct contract with the homeowner does not have to serve this notice at all. The 20 day clock is for subs and suppliers, not the general contractor dealing straight with the owner."},{"t":"p","s":"90 days. Under MCL 570.1111, the right to a construction lien ceases to exist unless a claim of lien is recorded with the register of deeds within 90 days after the lien claimant's last furnishing of labor or material. That is a hard, absolute deadline."},{"t":"p","s":"1 year. Under MCL 570.1117, once you have recorded that claim of lien, you have to file suit to foreclose it within 1 year of the recording date, or the lien expires."},{"t":"p","s":"And one more to keep separate. A laborer, as opposed to a subcontractor or supplier, gets 30 days after wages were due but unpaid, under subsection two of 1109."},{"t":"p","s":"Twenty, ninety, 1 year. And thirty for a laborer. Notice that ninety runs from last furnishing, while the 1 year runs from recording. Two different starting guns."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Last day of work was August first. What is your recording deadline, and once you record on October twentieth, what is your next deadline?"},{"t":"a","s":"90 days from August first, so roughly October thirtieth, to record the claim of lien. Then 1 year from the October twentieth recording date to file suit to foreclose."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"The sworn statement."},{"t":"p","s":"Layered on top is MCL 570.1110, the sworn statement rule."},{"t":"p","s":"A contractor has to give the owner a sworn statement listing every sub, supplier, and unpaid laborer whenever payment is due, requested, or demanded."},{"t":"p","s":"Note all three triggers. Due, requested, or demanded. Any one of them."},{"t":"p","s":"And lie on it with intent to defraud, and that is a crime. Not a contract dispute. A crime."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"Assignment of benefits, and a case from eighteen eighty."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the assignment question, because this is where most of the confusion in a restoration practice actually lives."},{"t":"p","s":"Start with something surprising. Michigan has never passed a statute specifically regulating assignment of benefits for property insurance. There is no A O B statute here at all."},{"t":"p","s":"The entire doctrine is common law, and it goes all the way back to eighteen eighty."},{"t":"p","s":"The case is Roger Williams Insurance Company versus Carrington, forty three Michigan two fifty two. A livery stable's fire insurance policy covered property that burned down. After the fire, the insured assigned the policy to secure a debt, without asking the insurance company's permission. The company said the policy's anti assignment clause voided the whole thing."},{"t":"p","s":"The Michigan Supreme Court disagreed, and the language it used is still quoted by Michigan courts today. The assignment, having been made after the loss, did not require the company's consent, and a policy provision forfeiting coverage for an unconsented assignment is invalid as applied to the transfer of an already accrued claim."},{"t":"p","s":"The Court called it the absolute right of every person to assign such a claim, and said preventing that right would be against public policy."},{"t":"p","s":"Hear the logic, because it is clean. Before the loss, the carrier cares enormously who it is insuring. After the loss, the claim is just a debt, a fixed sum owed, and you can assign a debt. The identity of the person holding it does not change the carrier's risk at all."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Shah, the modern restatement, and its limits."},{"t":"p","s":"That rule got a modern restatement in twenty eighteen, in Jawad A. Shah, M.D., P C, versus State Farm, three twenty four Michigan Appeals one eighty two."},{"t":"p","s":"Label it right: this is a no fault case, not a property case. But healthcare providers had taken an assignment of a patient's no fault benefits after treating him, and the Court of Appeals leaned directly on Roger Williams to reach the same result."},{"t":"p","s":"State Farm's policy said no assignment of benefits was binding without its approval. The Court held that clause unenforceable, because the assignment here was of an accrued claim, made after the loss, for services already provided."},{"t":"p","s":"And the Court was careful about the reasoning, in a way you should copy. This is not about the clause being unreasonable. Michigan generally enforces clear contract language as written under Rory versus Continental. This is narrower: enforcing an anti assignment clause against a genuinely post loss transfer of an accrued claim violates the same public policy identified in eighteen eighty."},{"t":"p","s":"So for your restoration practice, here is the usable rule. If a homeowner signs an assignment of benefits for work already done, an anti assignment clause in her policy cannot block it."},{"t":"p","s":"But that protection is narrow, and the two limits matter more than the rule. It does not extend to a pre loss transfer of the policy itself. And it says nothing about whether the contractor can act as the adjuster once it has the assignment."},{"t":"p","s":"That is a separate question, and the answer to it is no."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"What an assignment does not give you."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is where a lot of restoration companies get themselves in real trouble, so let me be exact."},{"t":"p","s":"An assignment of benefits gives a contractor standing to sue the carrier as an assignee, stepping into the homeowner's contractual shoes for the assigned, accrued claim."},{"t":"p","s":"It does not create any new rights the homeowner did not already have. An assignee inherits the claim as it was, not a better version of it."},{"t":"p","s":"And it does not license the contractor to negotiate or adjust the claim."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.1222 says a person cannot adjust loss or damage under a policy, or hold themselves out as an adjuster, without a license, with exactly five exemptions. Michigan attorneys. Marine average adjusters. An insurer's own employees. A licensed producer with granted claim authority. And a narrow supervised data entry role."},{"t":"p","s":"A restoration contractor is not on that list. Assignment or not. Holding a piece of paper from the homeowner does not add a sixth exemption to a statute that has five."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. You hold a signed post loss assignment of benefits. What does it let you do, and what does it not let you do?"},{"t":"a","s":"It lets you stand in the homeowner's shoes to pursue the accrued claim, including suing the carrier as assignee, and an anti assignment clause cannot block it. It does not give you any new rights she did not have, and it does not let you adjust or negotiate the claim, because 1222 has five exemptions and contractor is not one."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"Guldshtadt, where that line got crossed."},{"t":"p","s":"This is exactly the mistake that got a real Michigan adjuster in real trouble."},{"t":"p","s":"DIFS case number twenty five, dash, one eight three five zero, involves an adjuster named Aleksandr Guldshtadt. He was summarily suspended in November of twenty twenty five, and the final decision issued April first of twenty twenty six revoked his license outright and ordered him to pay a 50,000 dollar civil fine."},{"t":"p","s":"Look at the pattern of conduct, because it reads like a checklist of this chapter. He held a financial interest in a fire repair contractor. He used adjusting contracts DIFS had never approved. He let unlicensed people sign consumers up. And he diverted claim money to a contracting business he had an interest in."},{"t":"p","s":"DIFS charged that under MCL 500.1226, and reached his license through twelve twenty four, subsection three, plus twelve forty two, subsection two."},{"t":"p","s":"Separately, subsection four of twelve twenty four is the flat rule that a person connected with a fire repair contractor cannot be issued an adjuster license at all."},{"t":"p","s":"Now draw the line to your own business, because this is the sentence to remember. A restoration company with an assignment of benefits that negotiates a disputed scope for a percentage fee is walking that exact same line."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea twelve"},{"t":"p","s":"The 12 percent, and an honest unknown."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 500.2006, subsection four, says an insurer owes 12 percent simple interest on a late paid claim, belonging to the insured or a person directly entitled to benefits."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the honest part. No published Michigan property insurance case has decided whether a contractor holding an assignment of benefits counts as a person directly entitled to benefits in its own right."},{"t":"p","s":"So do not promise that 12 percent as an automatic entitlement for the contractor. If you tell a homeowner or a partner that, you are stating as settled something no Michigan court has settled."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the honest framing that is still useful. The contractor pursues it derivatively, standing in the homeowner's shoes, because the homeowner's own right survives the assignment."},{"t":"p","s":"That is meaningfully different from claiming the contractor owns the 12 percent outright, and the difference is the kind of thing that gets noticed in a deposition."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea thirteen"},{"t":"p","s":"The Consumer Protection Act, and why it almost never works."},{"t":"p","s":"People reach for this constantly when frustrated with a carrier, and it almost never works."},{"t":"p","s":"The general exemption is MCL 445.904, subsection one, letter A. The act does not apply to conduct specifically authorized under laws administered by a regulatory board."},{"t":"p","s":"The case usually cited here is Smith versus Globe Life Insurance Company, four sixty Michigan four forty six, from nineteen ninety nine. But that case is usually oversimplified, and you should know why."},{"t":"p","s":"In nineteen ninety nine, the version of the statute then in force had a carve out permitting certain private actions for chapter twenty insurance code conduct, and that is specifically why the plaintiff's M CPA claim survived in Smith."},{"t":"p","s":"The legislature closed that carve out afterward. The current version, MCL 445.904, subsection three, says the act does not apply to, or create a cause of action for, unfair or deceptive practices made unlawful by chapter twenty of the insurance code, for conduct on or after March twenty eighth, two thousand one."},{"t":"p","s":"So today, current law bars an M CPA claim against an insurer's claims handling conduct."},{"t":"p","s":"And say the reason correctly. Smith is not wrong. It is describing a rule since closed. Those are different criticisms, and only one of them is accurate."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea fourteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Small claims, and two rescission windows."},{"t":"p","s":"If you end up in court over a small unpaid balance, know the venue rules."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 600.8401 sets Michigan's small claims jurisdictional limit at 7000 dollars, effective January first, two thousand twenty four, up from 5000 dollars back in twenty twelve."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 600.8408 says attorneys cannot participate except on their own behalf. So no lawyers on either side, unless a party removes the case to the general civil division before trial."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the last piece, and it is one you touch on nearly every emergency job. Rescission rights on work authorizations signed during a loss. Two different statutes, two different windows, easy to mix up."},{"t":"p","s":"The Home Improvement Finance Act, HIFA, applies only when the homeowner is financing the job on installment credit through the contractor, cash price over 300 dollars. Its rescission window, under MCL 445.1202, is short. Until five p m on the business day following the date of the contract. Call it roughly one business day."},{"t":"p","s":"The Home Solicitation Sales Act, HSSA, applies when the contractor solicited the sale at the homeowner's residence. Think a door knocking storm damage salesperson. Its window, under MCL 445.112, is three business days, running to midnight."},{"t":"p","s":"So the pair is: HIFA, financing, five p m the next business day. HSSA, solicited at the door, three business days to midnight. One business day versus three, and five p m versus midnight."},{"t":"p","s":"And one important limit on HSSA. A homeowner who calls a contractor herself after a pipe bursts has not been solicited, so HSSA does not automatically apply."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea fifteen"},{"t":"p","s":"The emergency exception, and the handwriting requirement."},{"t":"p","s":"HSSA has an emergency exception mitigation companies rely on constantly, and it has three conditions that must all be met."},{"t":"p","s":"One, the seller has to, in good faith, substantially begin performance before any cancellation notice."},{"t":"p","s":"Two, for goods, they have to be un returnable in substantially as good condition."},{"t":"p","s":"And three, critically, the homeowner has to give a separate, dated, signed personal statement, written in her own handwriting, describing the emergency and expressly waiving the 3 day right."},{"t":"p","s":"Read that third one again, because this is where companies fail it. A typed waiver clause at the bottom of the standard work order does not satisfy this. It has to be a separate page, written by hand, by the homeowner."},{"t":"p","s":"Skip that step and the homeowner keeps a live cancellation right for three business days, even while the drying equipment is already running in her basement. Think about what that means for your exposure on a job you have already staffed and started."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Storm damage, you knocked on the door, she signs your standard authorization with a typed emergency waiver clause. How long does she have to cancel?"},{"t":"a","s":"Three business days under HSSA, to midnight. The typed clause does not cut it off, because the exception requires a separate, dated, signed statement in her own handwriting describing the emergency and expressly waiving the right."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the rapid fire recap. Fast, as a self check."},{"t":"p","s":"90 days to record a construction lien after last furnishing labor or material. 1 year to foreclose it after recording. 20 days for a subcontractor's notice of furnishing, 0 days for a contractor in direct contract with the owner. Voidable, not void, for an unlicensed contractor's contract with an innocent homeowner, but innocent is doing real legal work, so do not extend it further than the Court did. Post loss assignment of an accrued claim survives an anti assignment clause, back to eighteen eighty, but that protection stops at pre loss transfers. 12 percent interest belongs to the insured, pursued by the contractor only derivatively. No Michigan Consumer Protection Act claim for chapter twenty insurance conduct after March of two thousand one. 7000 dollars is today's small claims ceiling, no lawyers allowed there. One business day to rescind under HIFA, three business days under HSSA, and a handwritten waiver, not a typed one, to cut that HSSA window short in a genuine emergency."},{"t":"p","s":"Lock these numbers in, and you will never confuse getting paid for the work with getting to adjust the claim. Two different licenses, two different bodies of law, and confusing them is exactly how a good contractor ends up in front of DIFS."}],"words":3539,"questions":5},{"id":"09_essential-insurance-act_lecture","track":"law","title":"Essential Insurance Act and Market","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the chapter that controls whether a Michigan insurer can turn you down for home insurance, whether it can drop you after a claim, and what it must tell you when it does either."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is why it earns your attention. It sits behind almost every nonrenewal letter a homeowner ever brings you. When somebody hands you a letter saying we are not renewing your policy, the rules that letter has to satisfy are in this chapter. And most of those letters are checkable against a closed list."},{"t":"p","s":"Chapter twenty one, MCL 500.2101 and following, carries three names on the statute text itself: Act two eighteen, Essential Insurance, and No Fault Insurance. It was added in nineteen seventy nine, and it covers both automobile and home insurance. We cover only the home half."},{"t":"p","s":"Fifteen ideas. After most of them, a question and then silence. Answer out loud."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"A trap in the very first section."},{"t":"p","s":"2101 is not the purpose section. People assume it is, because a chapter's first section usually announces what the chapter is for."},{"t":"p","s":"Open it and you find one sentence: the words and phrases defined in sections twenty one zero two to twenty one zero four have the meanings given in those sections. It is a pointer. That is all it is."},{"t":"p","s":"The definitions live across three sections, split alphabetically. Twenty one zero two covers A to D. Twenty one zero three covers E to I. Twenty one zero four covers P to U."},{"t":"p","s":"Learn that split, because the chapter's two biggest terms, eligible person and home insurance, both start with letters in the E to I range. Both sit in twenty one zero three."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"Who counts as an eligible person."},{"t":"p","s":"Under twenty one zero three, subsection two, an eligible person is the owner occupant or tenant of a house, condominium unit, cooperative unit, room, or apartment. Or the owner occupant of a building of no more than four residential units."},{"t":"p","s":"Notice the shape. It is personal and residential, and it tops out at four units."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"The carve outs, which are the menu a carrier orders from."},{"t":"p","s":"The statute then carves people out with a list worth knowing cold, because this is exactly what a carrier reaches for."},{"t":"p","s":"A conviction within the preceding 5 years for arson, conspiracy to commit arson, or one of the listed Michigan penal code burning and property destruction crimes knocks you out."},{"t":"p","s":"So does a successful insurer denial of a home claim based on arson or fraud in that same 5 year window. And insuring a dwelling used for an illegal or demonstrably hazardous purpose."},{"t":"p","s":"Refusing to buy enough insurance disqualifies, with three thresholds you should keep as a set. At least 80 percent of replacement cost under a replacement cost policy. 100 percent of market value under a repair cost policy. And 100 percent of actual cash value under a tenant or renter's policy."},{"t":"p","s":"Eighty, one hundred, one hundred. Only the replacement cost policy gets the eighty."},{"t":"p","s":"A nonpayment cancellation within the preceding 2 years knocks you out, unless the premium is paid in full before the policy issues or renews."},{"t":"p","s":"There are dollar floors on insured value. At least 15,000 dollars for a repair cost policy. At least 35,000 dollars, or another amount the director sets by rule, for a replacement policy."},{"t":"p","s":"Rounding out the list. Physical conditions presenting an extreme likelihood of a significant loss. Property taxes delinquent two or more years at renewal. And failing to keep a uniformly required club or organization membership."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Name the three insurance to value thresholds in the eligible person carve outs, and which policy type gets which."},{"t":"a","s":"80 percent of replacement cost under a replacement cost policy. 100 percent of market value under a repair cost policy. 100 percent of actual cash value under a tenant or renter's policy."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"What home insurance means, and what termination means."},{"t":"p","s":"Home insurance here means fire insurance for a dwelling of the type just described, plus other nonbusiness coverage bundled into or endorsed onto that fire policy, plus whatever the director adds by rule. It excludes anything meant to insure commercial, industrial, professional, or business property, obligations, or liabilities."},{"t":"p","s":"A personal lines chapter through and through."},{"t":"p","s":"Now one more definition, and it does double duty for the rest of the chapter. Twenty one zero four, subsection five, defines termination."},{"t":"p","s":"Termination means a refusal to continue to insure for reasons other than nonpayment. It also includes shifting you to an affiliated company at a worse rate or with less coverage, unless you asked for the move. And offering worse renewal terms, unless you asked or the old terms are gone everywhere in the state."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the key insight, and it is a structural one. This chapter has no two separately numbered systems for cancellation and nonrenewal. One word, termination, and one notice rule covering both."},{"t":"p","s":"That differs from the Standard Fire Policy chapter, which splits cancellation out into its own letter. Two chapters, two architectures. Do not import one into the other."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"The duty to insure, and the section number people get wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the heart of the domain."},{"t":"p","s":"It is twenty one one seven. Not twenty one one eight, which is the automobile parallel. And not twenty one one nine, which only governs how underwriting rules get written and filed."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection one says that, as a condition of keeping its certificate of authority, an insurer shall not refuse to insure, refuse to continue to insure, or limit coverage available to an eligible person for home insurance, except under underwriting rules built under this section and twenty one one nine."},{"t":"p","s":"Notice the framing carefully, because it is easy to overstate. This is not an absolute mandate to write every applicant. It is a condition on the company's license. And it protects only people who qualify as an eligible person."},{"t":"p","s":"So the two questions in order are: is this person an eligible person, and if so, is the carrier's reason on the closed list."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"The closed list of bases."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection two is a closed list of the only bases an insurer's underwriting rules can rest on. Three of them."},{"t":"p","s":"First, the same criteria as the eligible person exclusions you just learned."},{"t":"p","s":"Second, the physical condition of the property. But only if the rule is objective, tied to the perils insured against, and keyed to a real code or manufacturer specification, regardless of the building's age. And a government compliance certificate creates a rebuttable presumption the dwelling passes."},{"t":"p","s":"That phrase regardless of the building's age is doing real work. Old alone is not a reason."},{"t":"p","s":"Third, for renewal only, the insured's 3 year claim history. And only claims arising from the insured's own negligence, or a failure, after written notice, to fix a condition tied to a paid claim."},{"t":"p","s":"Read that third one twice. Renewal only. Not a new application. And not just any claims, only ones traceable to the insured's own negligence or an unfixed condition they were warned about in writing."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"The nonrenewal claim history numbers."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the number that shows up on almost every restoration claim, so get the pair exactly right."},{"t":"p","s":"For nonrenewal, an insurer can use paid claim history over the preceding 3 years, liability claims excluded, if there have been three or more paid claims totaling 3000 dollars or more excluding weather related claims. Or three or more totaling 4000 dollars or more including weather related claims."},{"t":"p","s":"Three thousand excluding weather. Four thousand including weather. 3 year window. At least three paid claims, either way."},{"t":"p","s":"Hear the logic of that pair. If you strip weather out, the dollar bar is lower, because non weather claims say more about the insured. Add weather back in and the bar rises."},{"t":"p","s":"One important caution. Subsection five indexes those floors to the consumer price index every sixth year from January first, two thousand six, by bulletin. So the statutory figures are the base, not necessarily today's certified numbers. Same superseded figure problem you met in the claims clocks chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Work the example. A Detroit homeowner with three water damage claims in 3 years totaling 5200 dollars. Three claims, and five thousand two hundred clears the 4000 dollar threshold. So a nonrenewal on that ground is lawful, if the carrier follows the notice rule."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Two paid claims totaling 9000 dollars in 3 years. Can the carrier nonrenew on claim history?"},{"t":"a","s":"No. The rule requires three or more paid claims, not just a dollar total. Two claims does not reach it no matter how large they are."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"The other nonrenewal grounds, and two traps inside them."},{"t":"p","s":"An insurer can nonrenew for a dwelling unoccupied more than 60 days, if there is evidence of intent to vacate or keep it vacant. Note both halves. 45 days with clear intent to return does not meet that bar."},{"t":"p","s":"It can nonrenew for an adjacent physical hazard, but only if the hazard changed since the policy issued. And here is the trap: residential property or traffic patterns near the dwelling can never be that hazard, however well documented. So a carrier pointing at a new apartment building or a busier road has picked something the statute rules out by name."},{"t":"p","s":"It can nonrenew when the insured failed to buy insurance in excess of 80 percent of replacement cost, but only under two conditions together. Buying more than 80 percent had to be a stated sale condition, and the insurer also has to sell a form requiring only 80 percent as its floor."},{"t":"p","s":"And it can decline or nonrenew someone who threatened, harassed, or physically assaulted an insurer employee or agent, if reported to law enforcement."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"How underwriting rules have to be made."},{"t":"p","s":"Those rules follow a process, in twenty one one nine, and the process itself gives you leverage."},{"t":"p","s":"Every rule must be in writing, and an insurer cannot transact home insurance inconsistently with them."},{"t":"p","s":"They must be applied uniformly and without exception statewide, so every conforming applicant is insured and every non conforming one refused, once the information is available."},{"t":"p","s":"They get filed with DIFS for informational purposes, open to public inspection. And if DIFS finds a rule inconsistent with the chapter, it can, after a hearing, order the insurer to stop using it."},{"t":"p","s":"Sit with uniformly and without exception for a second. That means a carrier cannot apply a rule to one homeowner and waive it for another. And because the rules are publicly inspectable, that is a checkable claim."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"Inspections."},{"t":"p","s":"Twenty one twenty one covers inspections."},{"t":"p","s":"An insurer inspecting dwellings to decide eligibility cannot pick which properties to inspect based on race, color, creed, marital status, sex, national origin, residence, age, disability, or lawful occupation. That applies whether it is a one time underwriting inspection or an ongoing sweep of existing business."},{"t":"p","s":"Inspectors get civil immunity, other than contract claims, for anything related solely to physical condition findings."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"Rating factors, and the trap that catches almost everybody."},{"t":"p","s":"Twenty one one one gives the closed set of factors a home rating classification can rest on."},{"t":"p","s":"Amount and type of coverage. Security and safety devices. Repairable structural defects tied to risk. Fire protection class. Construction, meaning size, materials, and number of units. The insured's own controllable loss experience. Use of smoking materials in the structure. Distance to a fire hydrant. And availability of law enforcement or crime prevention services."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection nine lets an insurer use additional factors beyond that list, if the plan stays consistent with the act's purposes and reflects a reasonably anticipated change in losses or expenses."},{"t":"p","s":"Either way the plan gets filed with DIFS under twenty one zero eight, and an insurer cannot issue or renew a policy except in accordance with its filings in effect."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the trap, and it is the biggest one in this chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Subsection four of twenty one one one bans insurers from rating on sex, marital status, home ownership, education, occupation, postal zone, or credit score. And people quote that list constantly for home insurance."},{"t":"p","s":"That list applies to automobile insurance only."},{"t":"p","s":"Chapter twenty one has no matching prohibited list for home insurance. In fact subsection eight expressly allows home risks to be grouped by territory, so geography by itself is not banned."},{"t":"p","s":"So what is banned? Redlining. In twenty one zero eight, subsection eight. Every filing must specify that the insurer will not refuse to insure, refuse to continue to insure, or limit coverage because of the location of the risk, and an insurer shall not engage in redlining."},{"t":"p","s":"Hold both halves, because they sound contradictory until you separate them. Territory may be used to rate. Location may not be used to refuse, discontinue, or limit coverage."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. A homeowner says her credit score cannot be used on her home policy because the Essential Insurance Act bans it. Is she right?"},{"t":"a","s":"No. That prohibited list in twenty one one one, subsection four, is automobile insurance only. Chapter twenty one has no matching prohibited list for home insurance, and territory grouping is expressly allowed. What is banned is redlining, refusing or limiting coverage because of the location of the risk."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea twelve"},{"t":"p","s":"The notice rule, which is one rule for both."},{"t":"p","s":"Section twenty one twenty three. And because termination covers both cancellation and nonrenewal, this one section does the whole job."},{"t":"p","s":"A termination is not effective unless the insurer delivers or mails written notice at least 30 days before the termination date, to the named insured's last known address, stating the effective date and each specific reason."},{"t":"p","s":"Both pieces. The date and the specific reason. A vague letter fails."},{"t":"p","s":"Shorter window for new policies. If the notice goes out within the first 55 days after the policy was first issued, 20 days is enough."},{"t":"p","s":"Nonpayment terminations are carved out entirely. They run on whatever schedule the policy states, because nonpayment is not a termination as this chapter defines the word."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is a subtle check worth having, because it is the one people never think to make. A termination is not effective at all unless the reason conforms to the insurer's own underwriting rules. An insurer cannot invent an off the books reason, even a true one, and have it hold up."},{"t":"p","s":"30 days normally. Twenty inside the first 55 days. Nonpayment on its own track."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea thirteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Declinations, and immunity."},{"t":"p","s":"Before any of that, twenty one twenty two covers people who never had a policy at all."},{"t":"p","s":"When an insurer or agent declines to write someone, it must give each specific reason. A written application gets a written explanation. An oral one can get an oral explanation, but the insurer must offer a written one if the applicant asks within 90 days."},{"t":"p","s":"And refusing to even hand someone an application form counts as a declination, triggering the same duty. That closes an obvious dodge."},{"t":"p","s":"Now what protects an insurer when it gives those blunt reasons? Twenty one twenty four."},{"t":"p","s":"Insurers, agents, and DIFS staff get civil immunity for statements about an insured or applicant in a cancellation, nonrenewal, or declination communication, unless made with gross negligence or in bad faith with malice in fact."},{"t":"p","s":"That is why a blunt, factual nonrenewal letter does not expose the carrier to a defamation suit. The statute deliberately protects candor in those letters."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea fourteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Where a homeowner goes to fight it."},{"t":"p","s":"Section twenty one thirteen, and get this one right, because it is easy to confuse with its neighbor."},{"t":"p","s":"It says a person who has reason to believe an insurer improperly denied them home insurance, or charged an incorrect premium, is entitled to a private informal managerial level conference with the insurer, and to review before the commissioner if that conference does not resolve the dispute."},{"t":"p","s":"If the insurer does not provide a conference and a proposed resolution within 30 days of the request, or the person disagrees with the proposal, the person gets a determination by the commissioner. And if either side disagrees, on request the commissioner must hear it as a contested case."},{"t":"p","s":"Its neighbor, twenty one fourteen, is limited to rate filings. Never merge the two."},{"t":"p","s":"One honest caution. Twenty one thirteen is written around an improper denial or an incorrect premium, and the text does not say whether a nonrenewal counts as a denial. Do not overclaim it."},{"t":"p","s":"Alongside the statute, the everyday route is DIFS's consumer complaint process. Try the insurer first, then file online or by paper form, or call DIFS at eight seven seven, nine nine nine, six four four two."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea fifteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Forms, the pool, and the three licenses."},{"t":"p","s":"Three shorter pieces to close."},{"t":"p","s":"First, policy forms. Chapter twenty two, section twenty two thirty six. An insurer cannot deliver a basic policy form in Michigan unless it is filed with DIFS and approved, or 30 days pass with no director action, at which point it is deemed approved."},{"t":"p","s":"Personal lines forms must hit a readability score of at least forty five under a formula written into the statute, plus topical captions, an identified exclusions list, and a table of contents for longer forms. Use an unapproved or withdrawn form and the penalty is 25 dollars per offense, capped at 500 dollars per form series."},{"t":"p","s":"Second, Chapter twenty nine, the Michigan Basic Property Insurance Association, which the statute calls the pool. This is Michigan's FAIR plan. You will hear it called the market of last resort, and that phrase describes how it works but is not statutory language, so do not quote it as if it were."},{"t":"p","s":"Membership is not optional. Every insurer authorized to write the covered property and home lines, other than those writing exclusively under Chapter sixty eight, must belong as a condition of keeping its authority to transact insurance in Michigan."},{"t":"p","s":"Its home insurance underwriting program must let every qualified applicant get home insurance for qualified property in a convenient manner, with insurance to value floors of at least 80 percent of replacement cost for a replacement cost policy, or at least 100 percent of market value, up to 120 percent, for a repair cost policy."},{"t":"p","s":"For basic property insurance, its premium equals the licensed rating organization's rate for identical insurance, plus a uniform, commissioner approved surcharge."},{"t":"p","s":"And since nineteen eighty one, before every residential renewal, the pool must notify the insured of their rights to get home insurance both in the voluntary market under Chapter twenty one and through the pool under Chapter twenty nine."},{"t":"p","s":"One trap here worth carrying. The pool's fraud denial disqualifier is stricter than the voluntary market's. It applies where the pool successfully denied a claim based on fraud or conspiracy to commit fraud, and it requires the denied claim to exceed 2000 dollars and exceed 15 percent of coverage in force for a repair cost policy, or 10 percent for a replacement cost policy."},{"t":"p","s":"Arson sits in a separate paragraph with no dollar test. And the voluntary market's arson and fraud exclusion has no dollar or percentage floor at all."},{"t":"p","s":"So the contrast is: pool fraud denial has a 2000 dollar plus percentage test, voluntary market has none."},{"t":"p","s":"Third and last, the licensed players, because this is where you personally live."},{"t":"p","s":"Section twelve zero one, in a different chapter, defines agent as an insurance producer, and insurance producer as a person required to be licensed to sell, solicit, or negotiate insurance. It separates an agent of the insured, an unappointed producer treated as representing the insured, from an agent of the insurer, an appointed producer representing the carrier."},{"t":"p","s":"And the trap you have now met twice: it never defines the word adjuster."},{"t":"p","s":"The adjuster license requirement is a separate section, twelve twenty two, subsection one. A person shall not adjust loss or damage under a policy of insurance unless licensed as an adjuster, with exactly five exemptions."},{"t":"p","s":"Insurance counselor is a third license, section twelve thirty two. Nobody can audit policies, give advice or opinion on coverage, or use words like consultant implying that license, without holding it, subject to narrow carve outs including licensed attorneys."},{"t":"p","s":"Producer, adjuster, counselor. Three licenses, three lanes."},{"t":"p","s":"And a restoration contractor giving coverage opinions and negotiating claim reasons for a fee sits in none of them. That is the sentence to carry out of this chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the rapid fire recap. Fast, as a self check."},{"t":"p","s":"Chapter twenty one, MCL 500.2101 and following, added nineteen seventy nine. 2101 is a pointer, not a purpose section, and definitions split across twenty one zero two, twenty one zero three, and twenty one zero four. Eligible person and home insurance both sit in twenty one zero three. Insurance to value, 80 percent replacement cost, 100 percent market value repair cost, 100 percent actual cash value for a tenant. Dollar floors, fifteen thousand repair cost, thirty five thousand replacement. Arson and fraud, 5 year window. Nonpayment cancellation, 2 year window. Taxes delinquent two or more years. Termination is one word covering cancellation and nonrenewal. Twenty one one seven is the duty to insure, twenty one one eight is automobile, twenty one one nine is how the rules get written and filed. Claim history for nonrenewal, three or more paid claims, 3000 dollars excluding weather, four thousand including, over 3 years, indexed by bulletin every sixth year. Unoccupied more than 60 days with intent. Adjacent hazard only if changed, and never residential property or traffic patterns. Rating factors are a closed set, and the prohibited list on sex, marital status, home ownership, education, occupation, postal zone, and credit score is automobile only. Redlining is banned under twenty one zero eight, subsection eight. Notice, 30 days, or twenty inside the first 55 days, with the effective date and each specific reason, and nonpayment carved out. Declination reasons under twenty one twenty two, written on request within 90 days. Immunity under twenty one twenty four unless gross negligence or bad faith with malice in fact. Managerial conference under twenty one thirteen, 30 days, then the commissioner. Forms under twenty two thirty six, readability at least forty five, deemed approved after 30 days, 25 dollars per offense capped at 500 per form series. The pool under Chapter twenty nine, mandatory membership, 80 percent replacement cost or one hundred to one hundred twenty percent market value, fraud denial needs over 2000 dollars and 15 percent or 10 percent. And three licenses, producer, adjuster, counselor."},{"t":"p","s":"If any of the questions I asked gave you trouble, that is exactly where to drill."}],"words":3760,"questions":3},{"id":"10_bar-exam-and-foundations_lecture","track":"law","title":"Bar Exam and Legal Foundations","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the last chapter, and it does something different from the other nine."},{"t":"p","s":"Everything else was Michigan insurance law as it gets litigated. This one steps back a level. First, the mechanics of becoming a Michigan lawyer, straight from the Board of Law Examiners' rules. Second, the first year doctrines sitting underneath everything you already know."},{"t":"p","s":"That second half is the part that will surprise you. You have spent nine chapters learning insurance rules, and most of them turn out to be ordinary contract law wearing an insurance costume. When you see the doctrine underneath, the insurance rule stops being something to memorize and starts being something that follows."},{"t":"p","s":"Fifteen ideas. After most of them, a question and then silence. Answer out loud."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea one"},{"t":"p","s":"Rule one, and a requirement that is not what everyone thinks."},{"t":"p","s":"Rule one sets three baseline requirements. Be eighteen or older. Possess good moral character. And, the one worth memorizing precisely, have completed, before entering law school, at least sixty semester hours or ninety quarter hours toward an undergraduate degree."},{"t":"p","s":"Now notice what it does not say. It does not require a finished bachelor's degree."},{"t":"p","s":"The 4 year degree almost every applicant has comes from the law school's own admission standard, not from this rule. So if a question claims Michigan itself requires a bachelor's degree before law school, that describes common practice, not the rule's text."},{"t":"p","s":"Sixty semester hours or ninety quarter hours. Those are the same amount of school counted two ways."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea two"},{"t":"p","s":"Rule two, subsection B, where the degree pressure actually comes from."},{"t":"p","s":"Before sitting the exam you need a J D from a reputable and qualified law school."},{"t":"p","s":"A school approved by the American Bar Association is automatically reputable and qualified, with no petition needed."},{"t":"p","s":"Any other school can ask the Board to approve it. That is a discretionary call weighing relevant legal education and experience. So hear both halves: it is not a flat ban on non ABA degrees, and it is not a guarantee either."},{"t":"p","s":"And rule five, which lets a licensed out of state attorney skip the exam, does not dodge this. It expressly requires qualification under rules one and two B. There is no back door around the school requirement."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea three"},{"t":"p","s":"Deadlines, character, and the MPRE."},{"t":"p","s":"Filing deadlines come in pairs, one per exam."},{"t":"p","s":"November first for the February exam, late until December fifteenth. March first for the July exam, late until May fifteenth."},{"t":"p","s":"Every applicant goes through a character and fitness investigation run by the State Bar's committee, and must disclose any criminal conviction carrying a possible penalty of incarceration that has not been reversed or vacated. Read that carefully. It is keyed to the possible penalty, not to whether anyone actually went to jail."},{"t":"p","s":"Every applicant must also pass the Multistate Professional Responsibility Exam, the MPRE, at the score the Board sets. That score is eighty five, and Michigan puts no time limit on when you took it."},{"t":"p","s":"Eighty five, and no expiration. Hold that, because in one minute you will meet a different number that does expire."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea four"},{"t":"p","s":"The exam itself, and the number to memorize."},{"t":"p","s":"Rule three defines it as the Uniform Bar Exam, with three pieces."},{"t":"p","s":"The Multistate Bar Exam, the multiple choice portion. The Multistate Essay Exam, six essays. And two Multistate Performance Test items."},{"t":"p","s":"Who grades what: the National Conference of Bar Examiners grades the multiple choice portion, and the Michigan Board grades the essays and the performance test."},{"t":"p","s":"The weighting to know cold: multiple choice 50 percent, essays 30 percent, performance test 20 percent, on a four hundred point scale."},{"t":"p","s":"Fifty, thirty, twenty. They add to one hundred, which is how you check yourself."},{"t":"p","s":"The essay portion is six questions, thirty minutes each."},{"t":"p","s":"And Michigan's passing score, the number to memorize, is two hundred sixty eight."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Michigan's passing score, the MPRE score, and the three exam weights."},{"t":"a","s":"Two hundred sixty eight to pass, on a four hundred point scale. Eighty five on the MPRE. And 50 percent multiple choice, 30 percent essays, 20 percent performance test."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea five"},{"t":"p","s":"Portability, and the four part transfer test."},{"t":"p","s":"Three more mechanics, and they interlock."},{"t":"p","s":"To keep your score portable to another U B E state, you have to sit every component in one administration. And a passing score is good for 3 years."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan also requires the Michigan Law Component, a course on state specific topics, before admission by any route. Any route means any route, including admission without examination."},{"t":"p","s":"Transferring a score in requires four things, all four."},{"t":"p","s":"It must meet or exceed two hundred sixty eight. It must have been earned within the 3 years immediately preceding the Michigan exam date you would otherwise have sat for. You must have taken the MPRE and hit Michigan's score. And you must complete the Michigan Law Component."},{"t":"p","s":"Miss one, even by a month on the 3 year clock, and the transfer fails. There is no partial credit and no rounding in your favor."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea six"},{"t":"p","s":"Rule five, admission without examination, and its teeth."},{"t":"p","s":"This one sounds like a shortcut, and it is a narrow one."},{"t":"p","s":"You must qualify independently under rules one and two B. Be licensed and in good standing somewhere in the United States. Intend to practice or instruct full time in Michigan. And, after being licensed and for three of the 5 years preceding the application, have actively practiced law as a principal occupation, taught full time at a law school, or served on active duty as a judge advocate."},{"t":"p","s":"Three of the 5 years. 2 years out of five does not clear that bar, though the Board can extend for good cause."},{"t":"p","s":"Fees. 400 dollars for the exam. Three hundred for reexamination. Eight hundred for admission without examination."},{"t":"p","s":"And a nice detail about the Board's own power. It can waive almost any requirement, but never the fees and never the administration of the exam. So the two things that can never be waived are money and actually taking the test."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. You have been licensed in Ohio for 5 years but only practiced two of them. Do you qualify for admission without examination?"},{"t":"a","s":"No. Rule five requires actively practicing as a principal occupation, teaching full time, or serving as a judge advocate for three of the 5 years preceding the application. Two does not clear it, unless the Board extends for good cause."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea seven"},{"t":"p","s":"NextGen, and a date to be careful about."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan moves to the NextGen Uniform Bar Exam in July twenty twenty eight, the forty sixth jurisdiction to commit."},{"t":"p","s":"The last legacy exam here is February twenty twenty eight. And note where that date comes from, because sourcing matters: it comes from the legacy exam page's test dates, not from the NextGen press release."},{"t":"p","s":"NextGen runs about a day and a half, 6 hours on day one and three on day two. Compare that to the current 12 hours over two full days. It is a substantially shorter exam."},{"t":"p","s":"And the structure changes. It tests nine areas of doctrine together with seven foundational lawyering skills, rather than in separately labeled components."},{"t":"p","s":"One honest gap. Michigan's NextGen passing score is still to be announced. Do not guess at it, and do not assume it will be two hundred sixty eight."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eight"},{"t":"p","s":"The essay subjects already shrank."},{"t":"p","s":"This one is not a future change. The July twenty twenty six change already happened."},{"t":"p","s":"The National Conference of Bar Examiners now lists eight areas. Business associations. Civil procedure. Constitutional law. Contracts, including Article Two of the Uniform Commercial Code. Criminal law and procedure. Evidence. Real property. And torts."},{"t":"p","s":"The four that dropped out are conflict of laws, family law, trusts and estates, and secured transactions. With family law and trusts and estates moving to the performance test through February twenty twenty eight."},{"t":"p","s":"So they did not vanish entirely. Two of the four moved rather than left."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea nine"},{"t":"p","s":"Where insurance actually lives on that list."},{"t":"p","s":"There is no subject called insurance law. But insurance concepts are baked into three tested subjects, and this is the bridge back to the last nine chapters."},{"t":"p","s":"Inside contracts: formation. Parol evidence, as its own named topic. Conditions, express and constructive. And remedies, including expectation interest and causation, certainty, and foreseeability."},{"t":"p","s":"Every one of those maps onto how a policy gets formed, read, and enforced. Because a policy is first a contract, and only second an insurance thing."},{"t":"p","s":"Inside torts, the negligence section names the duty question, the standard of care, proof of fault, causation, and defenses."},{"t":"p","s":"And inside evidence, a section called privileges and other policy exclusions names insurance coverage specifically."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan's own version is MRE 411. Evidence that a person was or was not insured against liability is not admissible to prove whether the person acted negligently or otherwise wrongfully."},{"t":"p","s":"Now read the second sentence, because that is where the trap lives. The court may still admit that evidence for another purpose, such as proving a witness's bias or prejudice, or, if disputed, proving agency, ownership, or control."},{"t":"p","s":"The rule blocks the fault inference. It does not block every use of insurance evidence. Those are very different rules, and people state the first as if it were absolute."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea ten"},{"t":"p","s":"Contract formation, where Michigan counts to five."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is a trap most study guides walk you straight into."},{"t":"p","s":"The national shorthand is four elements. Offer, acceptance, consideration, mutual assent."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan courts recite five."},{"t":"p","s":"Hess versus Cannon Township, quoting Thomas versus Leja, gives them. One, parties competent to contract. Two, a proper subject matter. Three, a legal consideration. Four, mutuality of agreement, which is the meeting of the minds idea. Five, mutuality of obligation, meaning both sides are actually bound."},{"t":"p","s":"If a question asks for Michigan's formulation, give the five. Four is the national answer, not the Michigan one."},{"t":"p","s":"And the Michigan Supreme Court, in Titan Insurance Company versus Hyten, confirmed an insurance policy is treated like any other contract for these purposes, subject to the same construction principles as any other species of contract, unless a statute says otherwise."},{"t":"p","s":"That sentence is the whole reason this chapter belongs in an insurance track."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea eleven"},{"t":"p","s":"Parol evidence, and who actually said the famous sentence."},{"t":"p","s":"The case is UAW dash G M Human Resource Center versus K S L Recreation Corporation, nineteen ninety eight."},{"t":"p","s":"The sentence everyone quotes is: parol evidence of contract negotiations, or of prior or contemporaneous agreements that contradict or vary the written contract, is not admissible to vary the terms of a contract which is clear and unambiguous."},{"t":"p","s":"But UAW dash G M did not write that sentence. It is quoting an earlier case, Schmude Oil Company versus Omar Operating Company. If you attribute it to UAW dash G M as its holding, you are one step off."},{"t":"p","s":"What UAW dash G M itself held is the merger clause half. When the parties include an integration clause, it is conclusive, and parol evidence cannot show the agreement is not integrated. Two exceptions only: fraud that invalidates the integration clause itself, or where the writing is obviously incomplete on its face. A merger clause, the court said, nullifies all antecedent agreements."},{"t":"p","s":"Picture how that lands in your world. An adjuster tells a homeowner over the phone that the whole roof is covered. But the signed appraisal agreement lists two slopes and carries a merger clause."},{"t":"p","s":"That phone call cannot override the writing, because the clause made reliance on anything outside the document unreasonable."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea twelve"},{"t":"p","s":"Three fraud doctrines, and two citations people swap."},{"t":"p","s":"The same case sorts out three fraud doctrines that get mixed up constantly."},{"t":"p","s":"Actionable fraud is the affirmative lie."},{"t":"p","s":"Silent fraud, also called fraudulent concealment, is the failure to divulge a fact the defendant had a duty to disclose. Note the duty. Silence is only fraud when you were obligated to speak."},{"t":"p","s":"Innocent misrepresentation drops the knowledge requirement but adds privity. A trade: you no longer have to show they knew, but you do have to be in a direct contractual relationship."},{"t":"p","s":"Now get the citations right, because they are easy to swap. UAW dash G M cites McMullen versus Joldersma for silent fraud, and U S Fidelity and Guaranty versus Black for innocent misrepresentation. Not the reverse."},{"t":"q","s":"Question. Name the three fraud doctrines, what distinguishes each, and which case goes with which of the last two."},{"t":"a","s":"Actionable fraud, the affirmative lie. Silent fraud, failure to disclose where there was a duty, McMullen versus Joldersma. Innocent misrepresentation, no knowledge requirement but privity required, U S Fidelity and Guaranty versus Black."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea thirteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Conditions precedent, and the proof of loss rule they explain."},{"t":"p","s":"This ties straight back to the deadlines you already learned."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan's definition is intent based, not the Restatement phrasing. Harbor Park Market versus Gronda, quoting Mikonczyk: a condition precedent is a fact or event that the parties intend must take place before there is a right to performance. If it is not satisfied, there is no cause of action for failure to perform."},{"t":"p","s":"And the flip side matters enormously when the insurer is the one holding things up. A promisor who prevents the condition waives it, because there is an implied agreement that the promisor will place no obstacle in the way of the happening of such event."},{"t":"p","s":"Michigan courts apply that hard to a fire policy's sworn proof of loss requirement. The rule, restated across published Court of Appeals cases: failure to timely provide a sworn statement in proof of loss within the policy's explicit time requirement is an absolute bar to the insured's recovery."},{"t":"p","s":"Now note what does not save the insured. Prejudice. Dellar says the failure bars recovery without regard to whether the insurer was prejudiced. It does not matter that the carrier was not hurt by the delay."},{"t":"p","s":"What does save the insured is waiver or estoppel. And the two cases together draw the line cleanly."},{"t":"p","s":"In Dellar the insurer never sent the policy despite two timely demands, and the insured said she never got a proof of loss form. That was enough to put waiver and estoppel to a factfinder."},{"t":"p","s":"Compare Willett, where the insurer did send a letter stating the requirement, did enclose the blank form, and did warn of the consequence. There the estoppel argument went nowhere."},{"t":"p","s":"So the difference between those two cases is entirely about what the carrier sent. That is a genuinely useful thing to notice in a real file."},{"t":"p","s":"The same logic runs through Dupree versus Auto dash Owners, four ninety seven Michigan one. A policy paying only actual cash value until repair or replacement is completed left a claimant who never proved completed repair with actual cash value only, even after an appraisal award. The Court called the missing proof of actual loss a condition precedent, and MCL 500.2826 expressly permits that kind of clause."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea fourteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Damages, and why a wrongly delayed claim rarely pays for the suffering."},{"t":"p","s":"Hadley versus Baxendale, as Michigan adopted it in Kewin versus Massachusetts Mutual Life: the damages recoverable for breach of contract are those that arise naturally from the breach, or those in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made."},{"t":"p","s":"Applied to an ordinary contract, that caps damages at the contract's value as if performed. So a policyholder generally cannot recover for the emotional toll of a wrongly delayed claim, however real that toll is."},{"t":"p","s":"Kewin recognizes one narrow exception, from Stewart versus Rudner. Contracts concerned with life and death rather than trade and commerce, matters of mental concern and solicitude. The facts there were a doctor's broken promise to deliver a child by Caesarean section."},{"t":"p","s":"And Kewin declined to extend that exception to an ordinary disability policy. So the exception exists, and insurance is on the wrong side of it."},{"t":"p","s":"Kewin is also the source of the bad faith rule you met in chapter four: a bad faith breach of an insurance contract, standing alone, does not create an independent tort claim, absent a duty independent of and apart from the contractual undertaking."},{"t":"h","s":"Idea fifteen"},{"t":"p","s":"Fraud's six elements, negligence's four, and the limitations numbers."},{"t":"p","s":"Fraud first, Titan versus Hyten again. The six element test for actionable fraud."},{"t":"p","s":"A material representation. That was false. Made knowingly or recklessly. With intent that the plaintiff act on it. Actual reliance. And resulting injury."},{"t":"p","s":"All six must be proven. Missing one is fatal."},{"t":"p","s":"The facts are a favorite trap. The insurer issued an auto policy after the applicant checked a box saying no driver in the household had a suspended license, when the license was in fact suspended, something the insurer could have checked against Secretary of State records."},{"t":"p","s":"The Court of Appeals said that because the fraud was easily discoverable, the insurer could not use it against an injured third party. The Supreme Court reversed, overruling the easily ascertainable rule."},{"t":"p","s":"Learn the broader sentence, because it is the real holding: none of Michigan's fraud doctrines requires the party asserting fraud to prove the fraud could not have been discovered through the exercise of reasonable diligence. There is no duty to investigate."},{"t":"p","s":"Negligence next, and it is the freshest doctrine here, rewritten by the Michigan Supreme Court in twenty twenty three."},{"t":"p","s":"The core elements, restated in Kandil-Elsayed versus F and E Oil, five twelve Michigan ninety five: every negligence action requires four essential elements. Duty, breach, causation, and harm."},{"t":"p","s":"What changed is where the open and obvious nature of a hazard fits. For over 20 years, under Lugo, an open and obvious danger defeated duty altogether, unless the plaintiff showed special aspects, meaning an effectively unavoidable hazard or a substantial risk of death or severe injury."},{"t":"p","s":"Kandil-Elsayed threw that out. Open and obvious is now folded into breach and Michigan's comparative fault statutes, decided by the jury."},{"t":"p","s":"The division that survives is worth saying cleanly. Whether a duty exists remains a question of law for the court. Whether it was breached is a question of fact for the jury, open and obvious included."},{"t":"p","s":"Now statutes of limitation, quickly."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 600.5805 governs torts. 3 years after death or injury for damage to a person or property is the default, with named exceptions on top."},{"t":"p","s":"2 years for assault, battery, or false imprisonment, but 5 years where the assault was by a spouse, former spouse, household member, or dating partner. 10 years for criminal sexual conduct. 2 years for malicious prosecution and for malpractice. 1 year for libel or slander. 3 years for products liability."},{"t":"p","s":"MCL 600.5807 governs contracts, and its residual rule, subsection nine, covering an ordinary breach not described elsewhere in the section, sets 6 years."},{"t":"p","s":"Now burn this trap in, because it is the one that ties this chapter to chapter three. That 6 year period does not control a Standard Fire Policy suit. The fire policy has its own 1 year suit limitation, tolled from notice to formal denial, and that specific insurance code deadline controls."},{"t":"p","s":"If a question describes a fire policy dispute and offers 6 years, that is the wrong statute."},{"t":"p","s":"Last, evidence, and keep two separate orders straight, because one is cosmetic and one is not."},{"t":"p","s":"First, administrative file twenty twenty one dash ten restyled the whole body of evidence rules effective January first, twenty twenty four, to stay as consistent as possible with the twenty eleven restyling of the federal rules. That was formatting, not substance, and the order says so."},{"t":"p","s":"Second, administrative file twenty twenty two dash thirty amended Rule seven oh two and Rule eight oh four effective May first, twenty twenty four. That one is substantive. The party offering an expert must now show it is more likely than not that the admission requirements are satisfied, and it is the expert's opinion that must reflect a reliable application of principles and methods to the facts."},{"t":"p","s":"Federal Rule of Evidence seven oh two, amended effective December first, twenty twenty three, now reads the same way. But the Michigan order never says it was copying it. So state it carefully: the texts match. That is the claim you can defend, and it is different from claiming Michigan copied the federal rule."},{"t":"p","s":"One last thing, and it is not testable, so keep it off the flashcards."},{"t":"p","s":"Jacob's own plan runs through an undergraduate degree satisfying the sixty credit floor, an online ABA approved law degree, and the Michigan bar around twenty thirty one. That is a personal plan, not a Board rule. Do not let it sit in your memory next to the actual requirements as though it had the same status."},{"t":"p","s":"Everything else here, the two hundred sixty eight score, the eighty five MPRE, the 3 year windows, the five element Michigan contract test, the six element fraud test, the four element negligence test, and the limitations numbers, those are the rules."},{"t":"p","s":"Time for the rapid fire recap. Fast, as a self check."},{"t":"p","s":"Rule one, eighteen or older, good moral character, sixty semester hours or ninety quarter hours before law school, no bachelor's degree required by the rule. Rule two B, a J D from a reputable and qualified school, ABA approved is automatic. Filing November first for February, March first for July, late until December fifteenth and May fifteenth. MPRE eighty five, no time limit. Uniform Bar Exam, multiple choice 50 percent, essays 30 percent, performance test 20 percent, four hundred point scale, six essays at thirty minutes each, passing score two hundred sixty eight, portable for 3 years. Michigan Law Component required by any route. Rule five, three of the five preceding years in practice, teaching, or as a judge advocate. Fees four hundred, three hundred, eight hundred, never waivable. NextGen July twenty twenty eight, last legacy exam February twenty twenty eight, passing score not yet announced. Eight essay subjects since July twenty twenty six. MRE 411 blocks the fault inference only. Michigan contract formation, five elements. Merger clause is conclusive under UAW dash G M. Silent fraud is McMullen versus Joldersma, innocent misrepresentation is U S Fidelity and Guaranty versus Black. Sworn proof of loss is an absolute bar regardless of prejudice, defeated only by waiver or estoppel. Hadley damages, arising naturally or in contemplation, no emotional distress for an ordinary policy. Fraud, six elements, and no duty to investigate after Titan versus Hyten. Negligence, four elements, and open and obvious now goes to breach after Kandil-Elsayed. Torts 3 years default under 600 point 5805, contracts 6 years under 5807, and neither one controls a fire policy suit."},{"t":"p","s":"Know them cold. And if any of the questions I asked gave you trouble, that is exactly where to drill."}],"words":3767,"questions":3}],"social":[{"id":"00_introduction","track":"social","title":"Introduction. What this is, and how to use it.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is a plain English guide to everything you have collected about people. How to walk up to a stranger. How to keep a conversation going, and how to end one without it getting weird. What to do when somebody puts you on the spot. When being funny helps you and when it quietly costs you. How power and status actually work between two people. How the operators you study really think. And how to say a thing so that it gets repeated."},{"t":"p","s":"Every claim in here comes from research that was checked, or it is labeled as something that was not. That second part matters more than it sounds. A lot of what gets repeated confidently about human behavior turns out to have nothing underneath it. You will hear me say, more than once, that a famous idea did not survive the check. Those moments are not filler. They are the most valuable thing in this book, because believing a wrong rule about people costs you real money and real relationships."},{"t":"p","s":"A few things about how to listen to this."},{"t":"p","s":"You do not need to take notes. Everything here is written down already, in your own files, and you can go read the detail whenever you want. What this is for is putting the ideas in your head in the right order, so that in the moment, standing at a counter or sitting across from an adjuster, the right one comes to you."},{"t":"p","s":"The order is deliberate. It starts with the thing that stops most people, which is not knowing what to say. It is not walking up at all. Then it moves to what to say, then to what to do when it goes badly, and only after all of that does it get to being clever. That order is not a style choice. Being clever too early actively hurts you, and I will show you exactly why when we get there."},{"t":"p","s":"And one promise. I am not going to tell you to fake anything. There is a version of all this that runs on tricks and small lies, and it does work in the short run. But you are not doing street interviews with strangers you will never see again. You are building relationships with plumbers, adjusters, contractors and property managers you will see next Tuesday, and the year after that. Everything in here is built to survive being seen through, because in your business it will be."},{"t":"p","s":"Let us start with the reason walking up feels harder than it is."}],"words":424,"questions":0},{"id":"01_your_gut_is_wrong","track":"social","title":"Chapter one. Your gut is wrong about people, and we can prove it.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"Here is the single most useful fact in this entire book. When you imagine how a conversation with a stranger will go, your prediction is wrong. Not randomly wrong. Wrong in one specific direction, every time, and researchers have measured exactly how far off it is."},{"t":"p","s":"Two strangers were put in a room and asked to talk for five minutes. Afterwards, each one rated how much they liked the other person, and separately, how much they thought the other person liked them. On a seven point scale, actual liking came out at five point eight two. What people guessed the other person felt about them came out at five point one seven."},{"t":"p","s":"Sit with that gap for a second. Every single person in that room walked away thinking the conversation went worse than it actually went. The other person liked them more than they believed. Researchers call this the liking gap, and it is not a one off finding. It showed up again in first year college students sharing a dorm suite, tracked across an entire academic year with five separate check ins. The gap only closed at the fifth one. That is most of a school year of two people each quietly assuming the other one liked them less than they did."},{"t":"p","s":"So when you walk away from a conversation with a plumber at a supply counter thinking, that was awkward, he did not really want to talk to me, understand what is happening. You are not reading the room. You are reading a bias. The measurement says he probably liked you more than you think."},{"t":"p","s":"There is more, and it all points the same way."},{"t":"p","s":"Take commuters. People riding trains and buses were asked to strike up a conversation with a stranger. Another group was told to sit alone and keep to themselves. Now, a completely separate group of people was asked to predict which group would have the better ride, and they said, obviously the ones sitting quietly alone. Talking to a stranger on a train sounds like a small nightmare. But the people who actually did it had the better experience. The prediction was backwards. Researchers traced the error to one thing. We badly underestimate how interested other people are in connecting with us."},{"t":"p","s":"Then there is the spotlight effect. People consistently overestimate how much others notice what they are doing, what they are wearing, and how they are behaving. You feel watched. You are not being watched anywhere near as closely as it feels. The room is full of people thinking about themselves."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is the strangest one. People expect a real conversation, one with actual substance in it, to feel more awkward than small talk. It does not. When people were pushed past the weather and into something real, they did not feel awkward. They felt more connected. Again, driven by underestimating how much a stranger actually cares about what you have to say."},{"t":"p","s":"Notice what all four of those have in common. The error always runs the same direction. You predict less interest, less liking, and more awkwardness than you get. That is a bias in your forecasting machine. It is not an accurate read of the room."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the part that matters, because knowing this does not fix it."},{"t":"p","s":"You cannot think your way out of a miscalibrated prediction. You fix it by collecting data that contradicts it. In one study, people simply practiced approaching and talking to strangers for a week. Their expectations became, and this is the researchers' own wording, more positive and more accurate by the day. Not just cheerier. More accurate. Reality corrected the forecast. And the effect held for at least a week afterwards."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the whole mechanism. Every conversation you have is a data point filed against a bad prediction. Which means a conversation that goes badly still does the job. That is worth repeating. A rep counts if you opened your mouth. It does not have to go well."},{"t":"p","s":"So what is the single best tool for actually doing it?"},{"t":"p","s":"It is not motivation, and it is not confidence. It is a thing called an if then plan, and it is boring in a way that should make you trust it. Instead of deciding to be more social, you decide in advance on one specific trigger and one specific action. If I am at the supply counter and the guy ahead of me is holding a parts list, then I ask what the job is. That is it. The situation and the response, both nailed down before you are standing there."},{"t":"p","s":"This has the strongest evidence of any behavior change tool in this whole collection. Across ninety four separate tests, if then planning produced a medium to large effect on whether people actually followed through. Now let me be honest about that number, because being honest about numbers is the point of this book. Those ninety four tests were pooled across every kind of goal. Diet, exercise, medical screening, schoolwork. Nobody has measured it specifically on walking up to strangers. So use the tool because it is the best supported one that exists, not because that exact number describes your situation."},{"t":"p","s":"Why does it work? Because it moves the hard part. The hard part is not talking. The hard part is deciding to talk, in the moment, while your miscalibrated forecast is screaming at you. An if then plan makes that decision at a calm kitchen table on a Tuesday morning, so that fear does not get a vote when the moment actually arrives."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, two things you may have heard that did not survive the check."},{"t":"p","s":"The first is rejection therapy. The idea of going out and deliberately collecting rejections for a hundred days to burn the fear out of yourself. It sounds tough and it sounds plausible. When we went looking for the evidence, we found exactly one academic source, an unpublished dissertation, which says in its own text that the practice has not been investigated or standardized. That is not a study finding it works. That is a researcher pointing out that nobody has ever actually tested it. It is not disproven. It has simply never been checked, and you should know that before you build a program around it."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is why the real version is better anyway. When researchers looked at what actually drives improvement, two things mattered independently. One is habituation, just getting used to the discomfort. The other is expectancy violation, which is the predicted disaster failing to happen. If you go out hunting only for rejections, you are optimizing for the weaker of those two and skipping the stronger one, because a genuine attempt might actually go well, and that is the data point that corrects the forecast fastest."},{"t":"p","s":"The second thing that did not survive. There is no number for how many attempts it takes before the fear goes away. People want that number badly. I went looking for it and it does not exist in the research. So I am not going to invent one for you, and you should be suspicious of anyone who does. Do not measure yourself against a finish line nobody has ever located. The fear shrinks on a trend, not on a schedule."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is what to carry out of this chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Your prediction is wrong in a known direction. People like you more than you think, they are more interested than you think, and they are not watching you as closely as it feels. You cannot argue yourself out of that bias. You overwrite it with reps. And you make the reps happen by deciding the trigger in advance, so the decision is already made before you are standing there with your heart going."},{"t":"p","s":"Next chapter, we deal with the actual moment of walking up."}],"words":1311,"questions":0},{"id":"02_walking_up","track":"social","title":"Chapter two. You do not get rejected. You get classified.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"Here is a reframe that changes what you are actually solving for."},{"t":"p","s":"When you walk up to a stranger, you are not being evaluated. Not at first. In about a second, before you have finished your opening sentence, the other person sorts you into a category. Salesman. Panhandler. Lost guy. Someone who wants something. And that category arrives with a script already loaded."},{"t":"p","s":"No thanks, I am good."},{"t":"p","s":"That sentence is not a response to your offer. He never got to your offer. He ran the routine that disposes of people in the bucket he put you in, and it fired before he processed a word you said."},{"t":"p","s":"This explains something that otherwise makes no sense. Why does having a better pitch not fix cold approach? Because you are polishing a message that gets thrown away unopened. The problem is not the quality of what you are saying. The problem is that you got sorted before you said it."},{"t":"p","s":"So the real job is not persuasion. It is avoiding classification."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, there is a creator who has solved this, and studying him is instructive precisely because his solution is one you must not copy."},{"t":"p","s":"Johnny Hamcheck approaches complete strangers and behaves as though he already knows them. We pulled the actual captions from one hundred sixteen of his videos, about seventy six thousand words, and counted what he says."},{"t":"p","s":"In one hundred sixteen videos, he never once introduces himself the way a stranger introduces himself. No name. No reason for being there. No asking permission. Instead, four shapes come up over and over."},{"t":"p","s":"There he is. Twenty six times. Plus there she is, another seven."},{"t":"p","s":"Hey, sorry I am late. Nineteen times."},{"t":"p","s":"It is me, Johnny, project manager. Project manager appears forty six times."},{"t":"p","s":"And, you do not even recognize me, do you."},{"t":"p","s":"Look at what those have in common. There he is, is a thing you say about a person you were expecting. It cannot be said to a stranger. So if you hear it, the logic runs, you must not be a stranger."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is the engine, which is cleverer than a lie. He almost never claims you have met. He acts on the assumption and leaves you to be the one who contradicts it. That distinction is everything. A claim is easy to deny. No, we have not met, is one short sentence. But sorry I am late, is grammatically an apology. The appointment just rides along underneath it as settled background. To refuse it, you would have to interrupt a stranger in the middle of an apology to dispute something he did not technically say."},{"t":"p","s":"That is socially expensive. And every second you spend not doing it, doing it gets more expensive, because now you would also be admitting you have been standing there playing along, confused."},{"t":"p","s":"The cost of objecting rises with time. That is the whole mechanism."},{"t":"p","s":"He also manufactures history cheaply. Ponytails comes up thirty nine times, always as, I had two ponytails last time you saw me. That is not a random detail. It explains why you do not recognize him, so your blank face stops being evidence that he is a stranger. And then there are the names. Balboni, sixty times. Mike, about one hundred seventy three times. A boss who sent him. A mutual friend who is not present."},{"t":"p","s":"That last device is the most useful thing in the entire study, so hold onto it. Claiming a shared third person is a far lower bar than claiming shared history. You know Mike, right, only requires the other person to not deny a name. Almost nobody says, I know every Mike I have ever met and yours is not among them. Whereas, we have met before, makes them search their memory and come back empty."},{"t":"p","s":"Now. Why can you not run any of this?"},{"t":"p","s":"Because he never sees these people again, and it is filmed. You see yours next Tuesday. Assert one specific false fact to a plumber and when it collapses, and it will, you have not lost a conversation, you have lost a referral source."},{"t":"p","s":"So the question becomes, what else breaks the classification, honestly?"},{"t":"p","s":"The answer is specificity. And it works for a reason that is almost mechanical."},{"t":"p","s":"A salesman working a hundred people cannot be specific about your job, your truck, your claim, your building. He does not know any of it. So a specific sentence proves, all by itself, that you are not running a script, because a script could not have contained that sentence. Specificity is self authenticating. It cannot be faked at scale, and that is exactly why it works."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the honest twin of, I had two ponytails last time you saw me. His detail is fake and does the job by being particular. Yours can be completely true and does the same job."},{"t":"p","s":"So here is the test, and it is worth saying the same way every time so it becomes automatic."},{"t":"p","s":"Could a salesman have said this exact sentence to a hundred people today?"},{"t":"p","s":"If yes, the script fires and you are done before you started. If no, you just bought yourself a real conversation."},{"t":"p","s":"Now let us put shape on the actual approach, because there is a second creator worth learning from, and this one you can borrow directly."},{"t":"p","s":"James Dumoulin does street interviews. We pulled his real captions too, ten videos. Excuse me, sir, appears sixty one times. Real quick, fifty eight times, and it is his single most load bearing phrase. The structure is three beats, almost every time. Excuse me. Question for you. Then a question about a visible object. Is this your Ferrari."},{"t":"p","s":"Notice, he never opens on the person. He opens on a thing. A question about an object is low threat, and it earns a trivial yes before the other person has decided whether they want to talk to a stranger at all."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is one honest warning before you copy him, because it is the biggest thing the transcripts revealed. His real authority move is not the opener. It is the credibility line he drops mid conversation. I grew a channel to eighteen million followers. That fires in almost every clip, including as the direct reply to no thank you. You do not have that line. The opener without it is a much colder approach, and if you copy the words without knowing that, you will conclude the method does not work when what actually happened is you ran it without the engine."},{"t":"p","s":"So here is your version, built for the places you actually stand."},{"t":"p","s":"At the supply counter, when the guy ahead of you is holding a parts list. Question for you, what is that going in? Then, do you do mostly residential or commercial? Then the real one. Who do you call when there is water on the floor?"},{"t":"p","s":"At the building department, when someone near you is holding a plan set. Question for you, what is that one? Then, do you pull a lot here? Then, who handles your buildback?"},{"t":"p","s":"On a job where another trade is working. Question for you, how did you get called in on this? Then, do you get a lot of these? Then, who does the reconstruction after you?"},{"t":"p","s":"Look at the shape. Two easy answers come before the one that matters. That sequence is why you never need to say, if you do not mind me asking. In sixty one openers across those transcripts, that phrase appears zero times. The softening is structural. It is built into the order of the questions, not bolted on with an apology."},{"t":"p","s":"Three more things, taken straight from the footage."},{"t":"p","s":"Never apologize for existing. Neither man says sorry to bother you. That phrase classifies you as an intrusion before you have said anything, and the other person accepts the frame you handed them."},{"t":"p","s":"Keep the questions short. In the transcripts, nearly every line is three to nine words. Short questions, not statements. It is the single most copyable habit in the whole sample."},{"t":"p","s":"And when the opener dies, drop it and just talk. This is the part almost everyone misses. In one Hamcheck transcript the man says flatly, we have never met. Instead of defending the bit, Johnny abandons it and starts talking about music, and they have a real conversation about playing bass and drums before he leaves warmly. The pretext was only ever a door. Once it is dead, stop defending it and talk to the person."},{"t":"p","s":"Next chapter, the thing that makes all of this work or fail before you open your mouth. What your body is already telling them."}],"words":1447,"questions":0},{"id":"03_arriving_warm","track":"social","title":"Chapter three. Arriving warm, without telling a single lie.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"In the last chapter I showed you a man who gets strangers to open up by acting like he already knows them, and I told you not to copy it. This chapter explains why the trick works, and here is the surprise. The lie is not the ingredient that does the work."},{"t":"p","s":"That is not a moral dodge. It is a research finding, and it is close to 40 years old."},{"t":"p","s":"In nineteen eighty six, researchers took people who were about to meet a stranger and told some of them, falsely, that the stranger already liked them. They told others the stranger did not. Then they put them in a room together, and the stranger had no idea any of this had happened."},{"t":"p","s":"The people who believed they were liked disclosed more about themselves. They did more of the small work of building a relationship. They expressed more warmth. And the stranger, who knew nothing about the manipulation, actually ended up liking them more, and rating the whole interaction more positively."},{"t":"p","s":"Read that again, because everything in this chapter comes from it. A belief about the relationship changed how the person behaved, and that behavior made the belief come true. Nobody claimed a false shared history. Nobody said we have met. The entire effect came from the stance."},{"t":"p","s":"So when Johnny Hamcheck says, hey, it is me, how have you been, the power is not in the false claim. The power is in behaving like a man who has no reason to be uncertain with you. You can have all of that without the lie."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, why does merely feeling familiar do so much?"},{"t":"p","s":"There is a second line of research on this. Faces that people see more often get rated as more likeable, as more familiar seeming, and as more similar to the viewer, even though actual similarity never changed at all. A large review of two hundred eight experiments on this effect found it holds up robustly, and here is the strange part, it does not require the person to consciously remember having seen you before."},{"t":"p","s":"So a false sense of, I feel like I know this guy, works about as well as an actual memory. That is why the bit lands on strangers. And it is also the reason you do not need the bit. Being genuinely around people repeatedly does the exact same thing, honestly. The supply house. The building department. The same counter every week. Showing up is not a wasted trip even when you sell nothing."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the part that should worry you more than the trick excites you, because this mechanism runs in both directions."},{"t":"p","s":"In one study, men were told, falsely, that the woman they were about to speak to on the phone was attractive, or that she was not. Judges then listened to only her side of the conversation, with no idea which condition it was. They rated the women who were believed to be attractive as behaving in a friendlier, more likeable, more sociable way. Her actual behavior changed to match a belief she did not even know existed."},{"t":"p","s":"And in another, interviewers who expected less of a candidate sat further away, made more speech errors, and ran interviews 25 percent shorter. Then researchers took new subjects and treated them the way those candidates had been treated. Independent judges rated those new people as performing worse and looking more nervous. They had done nothing wrong. They were simply on the receiving end of somebody's low expectation."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is what that means for you, and I want to be blunt about it."},{"t":"p","s":"Your expectation leaks out of your body and produces the result. If you walk up to a general contractor braced for dismissal, you will usually get dismissed, and it will feel like proof that you were right to expect it. It is not proof. You caused it."},{"t":"p","s":"So the instruction is not, pretend you know them. The instruction is, do not arrive braced."},{"t":"p","s":"What does that actually look like? It means opening as though the conversation is already going fine, rather than as though you are auditioning for permission to have it. No hedge. No apology for existing. Treating goodwill as the default rather than something you have to earn in the first ten seconds."},{"t":"p","s":"Now let us draw the line precisely, because this is exactly where people talk themselves into something dumb."},{"t":"p","s":"The benign version is a stance with no checkable false claim inside it. Acting glad to see someone. Assuming goodwill. Opening warm instead of cautious. Nothing there is a lie. If they turned around and asked, have we met, you would say no, and nothing would collapse."},{"t":"p","s":"It becomes deception the moment you assert a specific false fact whose job is to stop them from checking. We have met before. I know your boss. I am supposed to be on the list. Sorry I am late, implying an appointment that does not exist."},{"t":"p","s":"There is a name for that in the security literature. It is called pretexting, fabricating a plausible relationship or shared context to lower somebody's guard, and it is catalogued as one of the most effective social engineering techniques there is, precisely because it exploits everything I just described. Effective is not the same as fine."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the test. Say it to yourself before you open your mouth."},{"t":"p","s":"If they found out five minutes from now exactly what you knew walking in, would they feel warmly disarmed, or would they feel lied to?"},{"t":"p","s":"Warmly disarmed is a stance. Lied to is a claim. That is the whole line."},{"t":"p","s":"There is one more piece worth knowing, because it explains why people let this slide. There is a well established theory in linguistics about politeness, which establishes that admitting a personal shortcoming is threatening to how we present ourselves, and people work hard to avoid it. Saying, I do not think I know you, risks looking both rude and forgetful at the same time. So people do not say it. They play along."},{"t":"p","s":"Now I want to be careful here, because this is the kind of place where a plausible extension gets repeated until it sounds like a finding. No study was located that specifically measures how reluctant people are to admit non recognition to somebody acting familiar. The theory is solid and the extension is reasonable. But it has not been directly tested, so do not present it as measured, and do not build a plan that depends on it."},{"t":"p","s":"So what do you actually do with this chapter?"},{"t":"p","s":"Three things."},{"t":"p","s":"First, arrive warm. Not performed, not loud. Just unbraced. The research says the stance alone gets you the whole effect, and it makes itself true through how you behave."},{"t":"p","s":"Second, use real familiarity where you can build it. Be at the same counter, the same department, the same supplier, often enough that your face stops being new. That is the honest version of the same lever, and it costs you nothing but showing up."},{"t":"p","s":"Third, if you want the third party device, use a true one. You work with Dave over at the supply house, right. That is the same low bar that makes Mike work in the transcripts, except yours is real, and when it gets checked, and it will get checked, nothing falls apart."},{"t":"p","s":"Next chapter, what to do once they are actually talking to you."}],"words":1235,"questions":0},{"id":"04_keeping_it_going","track":"social","title":"Chapter four. Keeping it going, and getting out cleanly.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"Most advice about conversation is about what to say. This chapter is mostly about what to ask, and then about how to leave, which nobody teaches and everybody gets wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"Start with the single most underused move available to you."},{"t":"p","s":"Ask questions."},{"t":"p","s":"That sounds too simple to be worth a chapter, so here is the evidence. In two separate experiments where people were coached to ask more questions during a conversation, the strangers they were talking to rated them as more likeable. The effect sizes were zero point three eight in one study and zero point two seven in the other. In plain terms, those are small to moderate effects, the kind that show up reliably rather than dramatically. And in real speed dating, across one thousand nine hundred sixty one date observations, people who asked more questions got more second dates. That held for both men and women."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the part that makes it an actual edge rather than just good advice."},{"t":"p","s":"Neither side expects it to work. When researchers looked at the relationship between how many questions somebody asked and how much they predicted they would be liked, the correlation was essentially zero. Around minus zero point zero one in one direction and minus zero point zero seven in the other. Nobody, on either side of the conversation, believes this matters."},{"t":"p","s":"That is why it is available. It is a lever sitting in plain sight that almost nobody pulls, because everyone is busy trying to be interesting instead."},{"t":"p","s":"Let me be careful about one thing here, because it is exactly the sort of claim that gets inflated. You will hear people say that follow up questions specifically are what drives the effect, and that there is a number attached. The published summary of that study does say the effect is driven by follow up questions. But the version of the manuscript that could actually be opened and checked does not break out a separate number for follow ups. So ask follow up questions, absolutely, the logic is sound and a follow up is what makes somebody feel heard rather than interviewed. Just do not quote a figure for it, because that figure could not be verified."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, the reason questions work is worth understanding, because it fixes a bad instinct."},{"t":"p","s":"Letting the other person talk is not you being passive, and it is not you failing to make an impression. People devote something like thirty to forty percent of their speech to telling others about themselves, and in laboratory conditions people will actually give up money for the chance to do it. Talking about yourself is rewarding in a measurable way."},{"t":"p","s":"So when you ask a good question and then shut up, you are not withholding your personality. You are handing them something they value. That reframe alone changes how the silence feels."},{"t":"p","s":"One practical habit from the transcripts, which is easier than performing enthusiasm. Instead of reaching for wow, or that is crazy, repeat part of their answer back as a question. So you run both crews? That is what the street interviewer actually does between answers, over and over, and it does two things. It proves you were listening, and it keeps them talking without you having to generate anything."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, the ending. This is the part that will surprise you most."},{"t":"p","s":"Researchers studied nine hundred thirty two real conversations. Here is what they found. Conversations almost never end when both people want them to. Not rarely. Almost never. And they rarely end even when just one person wants them to."},{"t":"p","s":"The numbers are worth hearing. Between people who already knew each other, the average conversation ran about fourteen minutes, and the mismatch between how long people wanted it to last and how long it actually lasted was around 56 percent. People also badly misjudged their partner. On average they believed the other person wanted the conversation to run about 61 percent longer than that person actually did. Between strangers in the lab, conversations ran about twenty minutes, with a mismatch of about 46 percent."},{"t":"p","s":"So nobody in the room knows when to stop, and everybody thinks the other person wants to keep going."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the one number in that study that helps you personally. About two thirds of people, roughly 66 percent in one study and 68 percent in the other, could point to a specific private moment where they felt ready to wrap it up. And that moment was real signal. People who had one rated the conversation lower afterwards, about four point seven out of seven versus five point seven."},{"t":"p","s":"So your own internal, I am ready to be done here, is trustworthy data, even though you cannot read theirs and they cannot read yours."},{"t":"p","s":"What does that mean practically?"},{"t":"p","s":"Stop waiting for the natural lull that confirms it is mutually fine to leave. It usually will not come, because both of you are independently misreading each other. When you notice your own ready moment, end it on purpose."},{"t":"p","s":"I will let you get back to it. Good to meet you. And then something concrete. I will send you that thing we talked about. I will stop by Thursday."},{"t":"p","s":"That is coordination, not rudeness. Given what the data says, cutting a conversation clean is a kindness. The alternative is the two of you dragging out something you both privately wanted to end ten minutes ago, which is exactly the interaction people remember as awkward."},{"t":"p","s":"One more piece, from the street transcripts, for when it does not go well at all."},{"t":"p","s":"On a hard no, the pattern is the same in both creators studied. One extra beat, then leave warm. Push once with a reason, and if that is refused, all right man, have a good one, and move on. In one hundred sixteen transcripts of one of them, there is not a single instance of arguing past a firm no. Not one."},{"t":"p","s":"That discipline matters more for you than it does for them, because they never see these people again and you will. The conversations you exit cleanly are the ones you get to restart in 6 months. The ones you argue through are closed permanently."},{"t":"p","s":"So, the whole chapter in five lines."},{"t":"p","s":"Ask more questions than feels natural, because it works and nobody expects it to."},{"t":"p","s":"Repeat their answer back as a question instead of performing interest."},{"t":"p","s":"Letting them talk is a gift, not a failure."},{"t":"p","s":"Your own sense of being ready to go is real data. Theirs is invisible to you. So end it on purpose, warmly, with a concrete next step."},{"t":"p","s":"And after a no, one beat, then leave well."},{"t":"p","s":"Next chapter is the hard one. What to do when the conversation turns on you."}],"words":1128,"questions":0},{"id":"05_when_it_turns_on_you","track":"social","title":"Chapter five. When it turns on you.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"Somebody challenges you in front of your crew. An adjuster asks a question you were not ready for. A partner puts you on the spot in a meeting. This chapter is about that moment, and it opens by contradicting the most repeated piece of advice on the subject."},{"t":"p","s":"You have heard it a hundred times. Take a thoughtful pause before you answer. Do not rush. Let the silence work for you."},{"t":"p","s":"That advice is wrong by default, and there is a large body of evidence saying so."},{"t":"p","s":"Researchers ran fourteen separate experiments with a combined total of seven thousand five hundred sixty five people, ten of those experiments preregistered, meaning the researchers committed to their predictions before collecting a single data point. Across American, British and French samples, they found that people systematically judge slower responses as less sincere. That held everywhere from trivial small talk up to police interrogations."},{"t":"p","s":"The reason is uncomfortable. A delay reads, to the listener, as you suppressing a truthful answer that came automatically, and manufacturing a replacement."},{"t":"p","s":"Two things soften it. Neither removes it. The penalty shrinks when the answer you are giving is socially unwelcome, because then slowness reads as reluctance rather than deception. And it shrinks when the delay is clearly explained by real mental effort, like a genuinely complicated question. But here is the finding that should settle it for you. When researchers explicitly told listeners to ignore how long the response took, it reduced the effect and did not eliminate it. People cannot stop doing this even when instructed to."},{"t":"p","s":"The authors flag the courtroom version themselves. An innocent person hesitating can read as guilty."},{"t":"p","s":"So what do you do instead, when you genuinely need a second to think?"},{"t":"p","s":"Make the delay audible instead of silent. Restate the question. Name the complexity. There are two parts to that. Or, before I answer that, which building are we talking about. That converts an unexplained pause, which is the thing that costs you, into an attributable one, which is the condition under which the penalty shrinks. You get your thinking time and you do not pay for it."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, the second question. When you cannot or will not fully answer, what is the safer failure?"},{"t":"p","s":"There are two ways to not answer, and they carry very different risks."},{"t":"p","s":"The first is the dodge, where you answer a similar but not quite identical question. Across four studies, researchers found that listeners often fail to catch this. The reason is that listeners are mostly aimed at a different question. They are asking, do I like this person, not, did that actually answer what I asked. And when people failed to detect the dodge, they did not penalize the dodger either."},{"t":"p","s":"But look at the conditions where detection went up, because these are the ones you actually operate in. Detection rose when attention was pointed at relevance. It rose when the dodge was wildly off topic instead of close. And it rose when the original question stayed visible while the answer was given."},{"t":"p","s":"That last one is the practical warning. Never dodge in writing. Never dodge when the question is on a screen, in an email thread, or in front of somebody taking notes. That is precisely the condition under which people catch it, and when they catch it, they rate you worse."},{"t":"p","s":"The second way is vagueness, and this one is not safe at all. In nine experiments, eight of them preregistered, ambiguous answers were judged less likeable than specific ones. People read vagueness as either concealment or as social disinterest, and they were less inclined to want anything more to do with the person. Ambiguous responders were also rated as less warm and more guarded. There is one exception. When the question is genuinely sensitive and the vagueness reads as softening a blow, it does not cost you."},{"t":"p","s":"Put those together and you get a rule that is genuinely useful."},{"t":"p","s":"Be specific, even when you are incomplete."},{"t":"p","s":"A short, direct, partial answer beats a long hedge every time. The cost is in sounding vague, not in being incomplete. I do not have that number in front of me, I will send it tonight, is a specific answer. Well, there are a lot of moving pieces and it depends, is the thing that hurts you."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the third piece, and this is the one I most want you to keep."},{"t":"p","s":"When something rattles you in a meeting, the instinct is to lock it down. Flat face, level voice, give them nothing. That feels like professionalism."},{"t":"p","s":"Researchers tested it. They took pairs of people who did not know each other and had them discuss an upsetting topic. Some were randomly assigned to suppress their emotional expression. Others responded naturally or reframed the situation."},{"t":"p","s":"The people who suppressed disrupted the communication, and, this is the part that should land, they raised their conversation partner's blood pressure. Not their own. The other person's. In a second study, suppression also reduced rapport and actively inhibited the relationship from forming, and raised blood pressure in both people."},{"t":"p","s":"So clamping down is not neutral. It measurably degrades the interaction for the person across from you. They feel something is wrong even though you believe you have hidden it perfectly."},{"t":"p","s":"The alternative that does work is reappraisal, which means changing what the moment means to you rather than changing what your face does. In a separate set of five studies, people who habitually reframe situations experienced more positive emotion, less negative emotion, and had measurably better relationships. People who habitually suppress had less positive emotion, more negative emotion internally, and worse relationships."},{"t":"p","s":"Concretely, in the moment. Instead of he is attacking me in front of my guys, try, he is nervous about the number and he is doing it badly. Same facts. Different meaning. Your body follows the meaning, and the other person stops absorbing whatever you were trying to hold in."},{"t":"p","s":"Two more, quickly."},{"t":"p","s":"There is a real finding behind reframe your nerves as excitement. Across karaoke singing, public speaking and math tests, people who reappraised their anxiety as excitement performed better than people who tried to calm down. The mechanism is that anxiety and excitement are both high energy states, so relabelling is an easier switch than trying to power down to calm. And the intervention was tiny. Saying, I am excited, out loud, was enough."},{"t":"p","s":"One honest limit. Every task they tested was anticipatory. Nerves before the thing. Not the middle of a hostile exchange. So use it in the truck before you walk in. Do not claim it is proven for the middle of the argument, because that was not tested."},{"t":"p","s":"And afterward. Do not run it back. Both a general sample and a clinically anxious sample support the same direction, that replaying a socially bad moment afterward tends to maintain the bad feeling rather than resolve it. I will flag one thing carefully. The strongest of those studies used people diagnosed with social anxiety disorder in a structured protocol, so do not treat its size as a description of your ordinary evening brooding. But the direction holds. The rerun in your head does not process it. It preserves it."},{"t":"p","s":"Finally, the cheapest repair move there is."},{"t":"p","s":"In four studies, apologizing for something you are obviously not responsible for, the rain, the traffic, a delay that was not your doing, increased the other person's trust in you. It works because it signals that you care about their experience, not that you did anything wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"So, sorry, that came out sharper than I meant, buys real goodwill and concedes nothing. Use it."},{"t":"p","s":"Two things I will not tell you, because the research does not support them. There is no study testing whether turning a hostile question back on the asker works. People will tell you it does. It has not been tested, so I am not going to hand you a number for it. And self deprecating humor as a way to recover after a mistake is popular advice with nothing retrievable behind it either way."},{"t":"p","s":"The chapter in six lines."},{"t":"p","s":"Answer fast, or make your delay audible."},{"t":"p","s":"Be specific even when incomplete. Vagueness costs more than a partial answer."},{"t":"p","s":"Never dodge in writing."},{"t":"p","s":"Reframe what the moment means. Do not just freeze your face, because they feel it."},{"t":"p","s":"Say I am excited out loud, before the room, not during the fight."},{"t":"p","s":"And do not rerun it that night."},{"t":"p","s":"Next chapter, being funny, and the reason it is more dangerous than it looks."}],"words":1425,"questions":0},{"id":"06_being_funny","track":"social","title":"Chapter six. Being funny is a bet, not a free upside.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"There is a reason this chapter is sixth and not first."},{"t":"p","s":"Everyone wants to be the quick, sharp, funny one in the room. It is one of the most requested social skills there is. And the research on it is genuinely encouraging in one direction and genuinely alarming in the other, and almost nobody hears the second half."},{"t":"p","s":"Start with the alarming half, because it changes when you should even try."},{"t":"p","s":"Across eight experiments, researchers found that successful humor increases your status, both with people who already know you and with people who have just met you. Good. That is the part everybody expects. But unsuccessful or inappropriate humor decreases your status. Not neutral. Decreased."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the mechanism, and it is the most useful thing in this chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Humor signals two things at the same time. It signals confidence, because you were willing to take the swing. And it signals competence, because you read the room correctly and landed it."},{"t":"p","s":"When a joke fails, you still sent the confidence signal. You were obviously willing to swing. What you failed to send is the competence signal. And the specific combination of high confidence and low competence is exactly the combination that damages how people rate you."},{"t":"p","s":"So understand what you are actually risking. The risk of a failed joke is not that you look unfunny. The risk is that you look confident and wrong. That is a far worse read, and it is the read that costs you."},{"t":"p","s":"This is not a one study finding either. A separate team built and validated a measurement scale specifically for failed humor in leaders, tested on three hundred eighty five employees. Failed humor predicted worse relationships between leader and employee, and it did so over and above existing measures of humor style. In other words, failing at humor is its own distinct kind of damage, not just the absence of a benefit."},{"t":"p","s":"Which is why this chapter comes after the ones on walking up, arriving warm, and holding a conversation. Those build standing. Humor spends it. Swinging for a joke in a room where you have no standing yet is the highest variance thing you can do, and the downside is real."},{"t":"p","s":"So what is the version that pays?"},{"t":"p","s":"Across four studies, researchers looked at what they called humorous bragging. Not standalone jokes. Humor folded into self promotion. Talking about yourself, but with a light touch on it."},{"t":"p","s":"That version signalled warmth and competence together. It beat plain self promotion in resume screening. And it beat other humor styles for entrepreneurs trying to secure funding."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the finding to actually use, and it fits your situation exactly. The play is not to become the funny guy at the supply counter. The play is that when you have to say something about what you do, which you constantly have to, the humor rides along with it. Straight self promotion reads cold. The same content, delivered with a bit of lightness, reads warm and capable at once."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, is wit an ability you either have or do not?"},{"t":"p","s":"It is a real, measurable ability, and it has been studied properly."},{"t":"p","s":"In one study of two hundred seventy adults, using serious statistical modelling of how mental abilities cluster, general intelligence had a large effect on humor production ability. The standardized effect was zero point five one, with a confidence interval running from zero point three two to zero point seven zero. Meaning the researchers can be reasonably sure the true effect sits in that range, and even the low end is substantial."},{"t":"p","s":"But here is the detail worth knowing. Humor was predicted mainly by general intelligence and by something called broad retrieval ability, and not by fluid reasoning. Broad retrieval is roughly how quickly and richly you can pull relevant material out of memory. Fluid reasoning is your ability to work out a novel problem on the spot."},{"t":"p","s":"That distinction matters. It suggests wit is less about thinking faster in the moment, and more about having more material available to reach for."},{"t":"p","s":"I want to be careful here, because that is the exact point where a finding turns into advice that was never tested. The loading on retrieval is verified. What I am about to say is my inference from it, not a research result. It suggests that stocking specific, concrete material you actually have, your own stories, real numbers, particular details, is a better route to sounding quick than trying to compute clever lines in real time. That is a reasonable read. It is not a finding. Treat it accordingly."},{"t":"p","s":"Two more numbers, for completeness. In a study of four hundred people, both general and verbal intelligence predicted humor production ability, measured by how funny independent raters found the captions people wrote for cartoons. And in a meta analysis pulling together thirty six effect sizes from twenty eight studies, covering five thousand fifty seven people, men's humor output was rated funnier on average, with an effect size of zero point three two. That is a small to moderate difference, and it held up across different ways of slicing the data."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, what about improv training? People recommend it constantly as the cure for being slow on your feet."},{"t":"p","s":"The honest answer is, promising, not proven, and I will show you exactly why."},{"t":"p","s":"The best of the studies used two experiments, with seventy four and one hundred thirty one participants, and, importantly, a matched control group who did an ordinary social activity instead of improv. That study found twenty minutes of improv exercises improved divergent thinking, lifted mood, and increased tolerance for uncertainty compared to the control. That is real controlled evidence, and tolerance for uncertainty is a genuinely valuable thing to move."},{"t":"p","s":"The study people usually cite for social anxiety is weaker. Two hundred sixty eight adolescents, a ten week school program, and no control group at all. The teenagers who screened positive for social anxiety improved. But with no control group you cannot rule out simply getting older, or the general effect of being in any school program. The abstract says so itself."},{"t":"p","s":"A third study did have a comparison group, improv versus regular theatre training, and found improv specifically improved how coherently teenagers told stories. But that study had twenty seven people in it total."},{"t":"p","s":"And a review of improv research published in twenty twenty four states plainly that the field needs rigorous quantitative methods, larger samples, and randomized controlled trials. That is the field's own assessment of itself."},{"t":"p","s":"So if you want to do improv, do it. There is real evidence it helps you tolerate not knowing what comes next, which is genuinely the thing that makes people freeze. Just do not tell yourself it is a proven treatment, and do not be surprised if it does not transform you."},{"t":"p","s":"Finally, two things people will cite at you that are not what they appear."},{"t":"p","s":"The first is Keith Johnstone's status transactions, from his book on improv. It is influential, it is genuinely interesting, and it is a theatre teaching framework, not a scientific model. The only academic engagement found was a qualitative, exploratory study applying it to training attorneys for jury selection. No control group, no effect sizes, no hypothesis testing. There is real science on dominance and status, and we will get to it in the next chapter, but that is a separate research tradition. It is not a confirmation of Johnstone."},{"t":"p","s":"The second is that French phrase, esprit de l'escalier. Staircase wit. The perfect comeback that arrives twenty minutes too late, on the stairs on your way out. Everybody has felt it. When we went looking for research on it, there is none. Every result was etymological, tracing the phrase back to Diderot. It has never been studied as a measurable effect. So the feeling is real and universal, and there is no science about it, and anyone who quotes you a mechanism for it is making it up."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the chapter, short."},{"t":"p","s":"A joke that lands raises you. A joke that misses lowers you, because you still looked confident and no longer look competent."},{"t":"p","s":"So do not swing in a room where you have not built standing yet."},{"t":"p","s":"The version with evidence behind it is humor wrapped around something you were going to say about yourself anyway."},{"t":"p","s":"Stock material rather than trying to compute cleverness live."},{"t":"p","s":"And a failed joke is real damage, not a neutral event."},{"t":"p","s":"Next chapter, the two kinds of power, and why using the wrong one is the most expensive mistake in this whole book."}],"words":1433,"questions":0},{"id":"07_two_kinds_of_power","track":"social","title":"Chapter seven. Two kinds of power, and they work in opposite situations.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"If you take one idea out of this entire book and apply it tomorrow, make it this one."},{"t":"p","s":"The old model of influence says there is one thing called power, and you get it by being the strongest presence in the room. Be more certain. Take up more space. Do not back down."},{"t":"p","s":"The research says something different and far more useful. There are two separate routes to influence. Both of them work. And they work under opposite conditions."},{"t":"p","s":"The study that established this put real people into leaderless groups, one with ninety seven participants and another with one hundred twenty four, and tracked not just who people said was influential but who actually swayed the group's decisions, and even where people's eyes went."},{"t":"p","s":"They found two independent routes."},{"t":"p","s":"The first is dominance. Influence through force, assertion, and a degree of fear. The second is prestige. Influence through respected competence and genuine generosity, where people defer to you because they want to, not because they have to."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is the finding. Both independently predicted influence. Not one being the good version and the other the bad version. Two different roads that both arrive. A follow up study found both routes also hold onto rank over time, not just win it."},{"t":"p","s":"So the question is never which one is right. The question is which one fits the situation, and that has an answer."},{"t":"p","s":"Dominance wins under threat, crisis, uncertainty, and direct competition between groups. When people feel like they have lost control, they prefer the decisive, forceful figure. One study demonstrated this across individuals, across postal codes, and across countries, showing that economic uncertainty lowers people's sense of control and raises their preference for a dominant leader over a prestigious one. I will note honestly that the size of that effect was not available in what could be verified, so take the direction and not a magnitude."},{"t":"p","s":"Prestige wins in stable, low threat situations where people interact repeatedly. In most ordinary face to face groups, people gain standing by demonstrating competence, generosity, and commitment to the group. Not by pushing."},{"t":"p","s":"Now put that against your actual week, because this is where it becomes concrete."},{"t":"p","s":"A flooded basement at two in the morning is a crisis. The homeowner is frightened and has lost control of their own house. They will defer to whoever walks in calm and decisive and tells them what happens next. That reads as dominance, and it is the correct behavior."},{"t":"p","s":"A plumber you want referring you work every month for the next 5 years is the opposite situation. Stable, repeated, no emergency. The exact posture that won you the basement will cost you that relationship."},{"t":"p","s":"Same man. Same competence. Opposite correct behavior."},{"t":"p","s":"I would go so far as to say that almost every serious mistake in a negotiation or a relationship is running the right route under the wrong conditions. Being forceful with someone who was going to help you anyway. Being deferential with someone who needed a decision made."},{"t":"p","s":"So, name the game before you pick the move. One shot or repeated? Crisis or calm? That question answers which route."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, the second half of this chapter, and it is the defensive half that almost nobody uses."},{"t":"p","s":"Confidence gets read as competence, even when it is not earned."},{"t":"p","s":"In one study, people who were naturally dominant were rated as more competent by their group members, by outside observers, and by staff, and that held after statistically controlling for their actual ability. The dominant behavior itself was signalling competence, independent of whether the competence was there."},{"t":"p","s":"Across six further studies, overconfidence predicted higher attained status, in the lab and in real groups."},{"t":"p","s":"There is a boundary, and it is important. When the overconfidence is exposed as unwarranted, the status gain is at risk. So confidence is a loan against a future demonstration, not income. You still have to deliver."},{"t":"p","s":"Now flip it around, because this is the single most useful sentence in the chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"A confident adjuster is not evidence that the adjuster is right."},{"t":"p","s":"Their certainty is information about them, not about your claim. The same goes for a confident investor, a confident general contractor, a confident partner. Their tone is a signal they are broadcasting, and you have just learned it is only loosely connected to accuracy."},{"t":"p","s":"Ask for the document."},{"t":"p","s":"And this applies to you as well, which is uncomfortable. In five experiments, feeling powerful produced overconfident decisions that lost real money. That effect disappeared when the powerful person happened to feel incompetent in that specific area. So your own sense of being on top of a deal is not evidence either. It might just be the feeling of having power."},{"t":"p","s":"Next, the cheapest move in this entire book."},{"t":"p","s":"Status tracks well being, self esteem and health, across cultures, across ages, across genders. And here is the part that makes it useful. Getting status raises the desire for more of it. It does not fill up. It does not satiate."},{"t":"p","s":"Which means giving status away is free for you, and it never stops working."},{"t":"p","s":"Visible respect. Credit in front of other people. Deference on the things you genuinely do not care about. It costs you nothing you need, and the appetite on the other side never closes. If you want one habit out of this chapter, make it that. You will be surprised how far it carries with adjusters, with subs, and with partners."},{"t":"p","s":"Finally, one thing to throw away."},{"t":"p","s":"Power posing. Standing in a big open stance for two minutes before a meeting to raise your testosterone and lower your stress hormone. It was one of the most famous findings in psychology for a decade."},{"t":"p","s":"It did not replicate. The original study had forty two people. A later study with two hundred participants, properly blinded, and with more than enough statistical power to detect the effect, found no hormone effects and no behavioral effects. An analysis of thirty four studies found no evidential value. And the first author of the original paper wrote publicly that she does not believe power pose effects are real."},{"t":"p","s":"The only piece that survives is that standing openly may change how you feel. That is it. No hormones, no outcomes. So never tell anyone it works, and do not build a pre meeting ritual on it."},{"t":"p","s":"The chapter in five lines."},{"t":"p","s":"There are two routes to influence and they win in opposite conditions."},{"t":"p","s":"Dominance in a crisis. Prestige in anything repeated. Most of your work is repeated."},{"t":"p","s":"Confidence reads as competence, so never treat someone's certainty as evidence, including your own."},{"t":"p","s":"Give status away constantly. It is free and the appetite never closes."},{"t":"p","s":"And power posing is dead. Do not carry it in."},{"t":"p","s":"Next chapter, we take a real negotiation system, the one you already study, and put it inside this framework."}],"words":1144,"questions":0},{"id":"08_alp_and_when_not_to","track":"social","title":"Chapter eight. Marshall Wilkinson's system, and when not to use it.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"You already study this man, so this chapter does two things. It lays out his system in plain terms with the evidence behind each piece. And then it tells you the thing nobody has told you, which is when the system should not run at all."},{"t":"p","s":"His framework is three letters. A, L, P. Altitude, Logic, Pressure."},{"t":"p","s":"Altitude is being the expert in the room. His line for it is, experts do not justify. The more you justify, the more altitude you lose. When somebody challenges your number, the instinct is to explain yourself, and his claim is that explaining is what costs you."},{"t":"p","s":"Does that hold up? It does, and from two directions. Remember from the last chapter that confidence gets read as competence even after you control for actual ability. And remember from chapter five that fast answers are read as more sincere while slow ones read as manufactured. Both of those support Altitude directly."},{"t":"p","s":"But there is a boundary he actually gets right. Overconfidence that gets exposed costs you the status you gained. Which means Altitude has to be real preparation, not a manner you put on. That is exactly what he says, so on this one the practitioner and the research agree."},{"t":"p","s":"Logic is his second pillar. Make the answer self evident. Educate the other side until their position looks unreasonable to them, without you ever having to call it unreasonable. His line is that the most emotional thing you can do to somebody is tell them the truth."},{"t":"p","s":"The evidence here is good. Precise numbers that are justified beat round numbers when you are dealing with someone experienced. Repeating the same justification raises how true it feels, with a moderate and reliable effect. And stating a hard consequence coldly works better than delivering it angrily."},{"t":"p","s":"Pressure is the third, and this is where the chapter turns."},{"t":"p","s":"His definition is specific and better than most people's. Pressure is not aggression. Pressure is refusing to leave the deal. It is applied through documents, deadlines and standards. Not through volume. Not through anger. He says pressure done with logic is a great thing, and separately, that if you never apply any, all you will ever close are the easy ones."},{"t":"p","s":"The evidence backs his distinction precisely. A meta analysis of hardline concession patterns found they win money and lose the relationship. And research on strategic anger found it only moves people who have weak alternatives, and it backfires on people who have more power than you. So system pressure yes, emotional pressure no. That is his distinction, and the data agrees with him."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the thing that was missing, and it is the reason for this chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Look at those three pillars through the two routes from the last chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Altitude draws on prestige. It is demonstrated competence. Logic draws on prestige too. Nothing about it requires anyone to be afraid of you."},{"t":"p","s":"Pressure is the dominance lever."},{"t":"p","s":"So his system is two thirds prestige and one third dominance. And you now know that the dominant third only pays under specific conditions. Crisis. A stall. Someone with weak alternatives."},{"t":"p","s":"The sequence is taught as Altitude, then Logic, then Pressure, as if it always runs to the end. In a repeated relationship, it usually should not. With a referral partner, Altitude and Logic are the entire play. Reaching for Pressure there means spending a relationship to win a transaction."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the correction the evidence adds to his system, and it is not a criticism of him. It is the condition his framework does not state."},{"t":"p","s":"And here is something remarkable. He appears to have worked this out himself."},{"t":"p","s":"We finally read all of his raw material this week, about two hundred seventy six thousand characters of it. In one section, labelled as his modern method, he says, and this is word for word, the old school understanding of selling and hard closing, in my opinion those days are over. And separately, I have been in a position where I have hard closed people and they have been the worst customers."},{"t":"p","s":"That sits in the same body of work as his rapid fire objection flipping system, where he says reaction time must be immediate, in milliseconds. Elsewhere he says that high pressure objection handling creates fear, and that the discount the customer then demands becomes their insurance policy against the fear you created."},{"t":"p","s":"Those are two different eras of his own teaching, not one consistent doctrine. He moved from the dominance lever toward the prestige one over time. So when you use his material, know which version you are running. That distinction had never been recorded anywhere in your files until now."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, three warnings about the material itself, and these are the kind of thing that saves you from embarrassment."},{"t":"p","s":"The first is about where his quotes come from. The four files you have are not raw transcripts. They are an analyst's notes about transcripts, and they mix three different things. Lines actually marked as exact quotes. The analyst's own paraphrasing. And a large table that is the analyst's mapping of his ideas onto restoration work, which he never said at all. So before you attribute a line to him, check that it sits under an exact quotes heading. Otherwise you may be quoting someone summarizing him."},{"t":"p","s":"The second is about his numbers. He describes himself as a two and a half billion dollar man. Elsewhere, in the same breath as pitching a course, he refers to fifteen billion dollars of contracting between him and his father. Those two figures are not consistent with each other, neither has ever been independently verified, and both are self published marketing. If you ever reference them, say Wilkinson claims. Do not state them as fact."},{"t":"p","s":"The third is about the gaps. His material references numbered concepts, and only concepts twenty five through forty three are actually defined anywhere. One through twenty four exist only as numbers. Separately, he names nine strategies as teasers for his paid course, things like the Power Clip and the Man Down strategy, and only one of them gets any explanation at all. So if you ever see a confident description of what the Man Down strategy is, understand that somebody invented it. And there is a line in his transcripts reading ninety two out of ten people, which is an impossible ratio and almost certainly a transcription error for nine out of ten. Do not repeat the ninety two."},{"t":"p","s":"Now let me give you what does survive, because plenty does, and some of it is genuinely excellent."},{"t":"p","s":"On objections, he says there are not multiple objections, there is one real objection, and they are not going to tell you at the beginning. And, if you bite on that objection you will validate it. Accept the objection and move on. That is good, and it matches the research that argues you rarely win by engaging a stated reason directly."},{"t":"p","s":"On price, three lines that are worth memorizing. Price is a myth. Stop telling clients what things cost, start telling them what things are worth. And, you cannot negotiate price without reducing scope."},{"t":"p","s":"That last one is the most protective sentence in his whole system for a contractor. Price and scope move together. If you cut the number without cutting the work, you did not negotiate, you donated."},{"t":"p","s":"On leverage. Leverage is something the other side cannot walk away from. It is not being slick, it is knowing what they need. And, in life you get what you negotiate, you do not get what you deserve."},{"t":"p","s":"And the single line with the most money attached to it for you is not a persuasion trick at all. It is about contract administration. Talking about the notice provisions in a contract, he calls them the silent killer of your change orders, and says if you miss the notice, you lose the right to recover, period."},{"t":"p","s":"That is worth more than any closing technique in the collection. A change order you performed and cannot recover on is a pure loss, and the thing that causes it is a deadline in a contract that nobody read."},{"t":"p","s":"So, the chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Altitude and Logic are safe in any conversation. They are prestige moves."},{"t":"p","s":"Pressure is the dominance lever, so use it in a crisis, against a staller, or against someone with weak alternatives, and use it cold, through documents and deadlines, never volume."},{"t":"p","s":"Before you push, ask whether you need this person next quarter. If you do, the answer is usually Altitude and Logic and nothing more."},{"t":"p","s":"Never quote his dollar figures."},{"t":"p","s":"And go read the notice provisions in your own contracts tonight."},{"t":"p","s":"Next chapter, how the operators you study actually think, and which of their famous habits turned out not to be real."}],"words":1472,"questions":0},{"id":"08b_going_for_no","track":"social","title":"Chapter eight and a half. Going for no, and the fear behind the no.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"Start with a correction, because you are going to say this out loud to people and you want to say it right."},{"t":"p","s":"Going for no is not part of Marshall Wilkinson's ALP. ALP is three letters, Altitude, Logic, Pressure, and you just read the chapter on it. Going for no is a different idea from a different place. It comes out of Chris Voss, the hostage negotiator, and in your own notes it lives in the Voss tactics file, not in anything of Wilkinson's."},{"t":"p","s":"What confused the two is that Wilkinson does say the thing you remember. He just does not call it ALP. So let us separate the two halves, because one of them is his and one of them is not, and you will sound sharper if you know which is which."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is the half that is his. In the captures, under the headings marked as his exact words, he says this. I am not being rejected. I am dealing with fear. And that fear has been living inside them for a long time. He says, when I realized that my job is to manage fear and to kill it rather than to convince somebody to do something, then the sky was the limit for me. And in another session he says that every person we come in contact with is going to be deathly afraid of making a bad decision, deathly afraid of taking responsibility, and deathly afraid of spending money."},{"t":"p","s":"That is your idea, almost word for word. Rejection is not about you. It is fear that was in the room before you walked in."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the honest caveat, because it matters and it is small. Those files are an analyst's notes on his videos, not raw transcripts. The notes separate what they claim are his exact words from the analyst's own summaries, and all three of those lines sit in the exact words section. So they are his, as claimed by the person who captured them. Nobody has gone back to the original video and checked them frame by frame. That is a fine level of confidence for a thing you say in a conversation. It is not the level of confidence you would want before printing it on a wall."},{"t":"p","s":"One more thing in that same file is labeled by the analyst as a new concept rather than a quote. It is the line about becoming fear in a negotiation. The sentence itself is in the quotes section, but the theory built on top of it, the idea that you weaponize their fear by becoming the thing they are afraid of walking away from, is the analyst talking, not Wilkinson. Do not repeat that part as his."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the half that is not his. Going for no."},{"t":"p","s":"The reframe goes like this. The usual fear of hearing no is backwards. No is not rejection. No is the beginning of the real conversation. Yes is the word that makes people anxious, because yes carries commitment and they cannot take it back. No makes people feel safe, and protected, and in control. So you stop trying to extract a yes, and you start making it cheap and easy for them to tell you no, because the moment they are allowed to say it, they relax enough to tell you what is actually going on."},{"t":"p","s":"And when somebody does say no, they are almost never saying never, go away forever. They are usually saying one of a few much smaller things. I need more information. I do not feel safe enough yet. I want you to know I have other options. The terms are not right yet. Or, I am not actually the person who decides this."},{"t":"p","s":"Grade that honestly. That is doctrine. It is a negotiation teacher's framework, and a good one, and it sits in a file in your own corpus that carries a warning at the top saying its numbers are unverified unless individually labeled. There is no experiment behind the reframe. So use it as a way of thinking, which is what it is, and do not tell anyone there is a study behind it."},{"t":"p","s":"There is one place where something closer to evidence shows up, and it is worth knowing precisely because it changes what you do."},{"t":"p","s":"Two researchers, Dixon and McKenna, ran machine analysis over about two and a half million recorded sales calls and published the result as The JOLT Effect. Their claims. Somewhere between forty and sixty percent of lost deals do not end in a loss to a competitor at all, they end in no decision. Medium to high indecision shows up in about 87 percent of deals. And of the deals lost to no decision, roughly 56 percent came from indecision, the fear of messing it up, while the other roughly 44 percent came from simply preferring things the way they already are."},{"t":"p","s":"Now grade that too, because the grade is the whole point. That is the vendor's own platform data. It is their calls, their software, their classification, and their book. No independent group has replicated it. So the right sentence is, JOLT's own data claims 87 percent. The wrong sentence is the one that drops the owner of the number and presents it as a settled finding. Say it the wrong way to somebody who checks, and you lose the room, deservedly."},{"t":"p","s":"But look at what those two failure modes are. Fear of messing up, and preference for the status quo. Neither of them is a person who does not want the thing. Both of them are a person who is afraid. Which is the same picture Wilkinson describes from the other direction, and that convergence is why the idea is worth carrying even with the caveats stapled to it."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is why this changes behavior rather than just vocabulary."},{"t":"p","s":"If you believe a no means they do not want it, the natural move is to want it harder. More urgency. Deadline. Discount that expires. And on a frozen buyer, that is the worst available move, because you just added a new thing to be afraid of. JOLT's own prescription runs the other way. Judge how frozen they actually are. Then make a recommendation instead of offering a menu, because a menu is more decisions and more decisions is more fear. Then cap the exploration, meaning you stop feeding the research spiral and tell them what is enough to decide on. Then take risk off the table, with a guarantee, or a phased commitment, or a first step small enough that being wrong about it costs them almost nothing."},{"t":"p","s":"Wilkinson's version of the same instinct, in his words, is that there are not multiple objections, there is one real objection, and that objections are bait. Which is the same advice wearing different clothes. Do not chase the six things they said. Find the one thing they are afraid of, and take that specific fear off the table."},{"t":"p","s":"Put it in your world for a second. A homeowner with a large water loss says no, I want to think about it. Read that as fear and it decodes immediately. They are afraid of making a bad decision on the biggest repair bill of their life. They are afraid of being the one who is blamed for picking the wrong contractor. They are afraid of the deductible leaving their account. Those are the exact three Wilkinson names. So the move is not a discount and not a deadline. The move is one recommendation instead of three options, a clear statement of what happens first and what it costs, and a first step that is reversible. You have not persuaded them. You have removed the thing that was stopping them."},{"t":"p","s":"Keep three things out of this chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"The idea is right and the label was wrong. Going for no is Voss. The fear behind the no is Wilkinson, in his own words, from an analyst's capture."},{"t":"p","s":"Say the grade out loud when you use the number. JOLT's own data claims it. Nobody has checked their work."},{"t":"p","s":"And the practical inversion. When somebody freezes, the instinct is to push. The push is what freezes them further. Recommend, narrow, and de-risk instead."}],"words":1373,"questions":0},{"id":"09_how_they_actually_think","track":"social","title":"Chapter nine. How the operators actually think.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This chapter is the thinking tools, separated from the mythology. Every one of these is traced to something the person actually said or wrote. Where a famous line turned out not to be traceable, I will tell you, because in this area the fake quotes outnumber the real ones."},{"t":"p","s":"Start with the best evidenced item in the whole collection, and it is a deletion procedure."},{"t":"p","s":"Musk's five step algorithm, in this order, and the order is the point."},{"t":"p","s":"Step one. Make the requirements less dumb. His rule is that every requirement has to come with the name of a person attached, never a department. Because you cannot ask a department why it wanted something. And he adds that requirements from smart people are the most dangerous ones, because nobody questions them."},{"t":"p","s":"Step two. Delete the part or the process. And here is the test that makes this real rather than a slogan. If you are not adding back at least 10 percent of what you deleted, you did not delete enough. In other words, if nothing you cut ever has to come back, you were too timid."},{"t":"p","s":"Step three. Simplify and optimize. Third, deliberately. His line is that the most common error of a smart engineer is to optimize something that should not exist."},{"t":"p","s":"Step four. Accelerate the cycle time. Speed up only what survived the first three steps."},{"t":"p","s":"Step five. Automate. Dead last."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, why should you believe this rather than the hundred other frameworks? Because there is a documented failure case, and it is his own. Tesla ran the algorithm backwards in twenty seventeen and twenty eighteen, automating the Model 3 line before deleting and simplifying it. The robots jammed. The automation had to be torn back out. His own retrospective was, excessive automation at Tesla was a mistake, to be precise, my mistake, humans are underrated."},{"t":"p","s":"That is a rare thing, a framework with a public example of what happens when you invert it."},{"t":"p","s":"Apply that to your business. Do not buy software for a process you have not deleted down first. Order matters. Delete, simplify, speed up, then automate. Most people start at automate because it is the fun one."},{"t":"p","s":"Next, Bezos, and two tools."},{"t":"p","s":"The first is regret minimization. Project yourself forward to 80 years old and pick the option you will regret least. In his words, he knew that if he failed he would not regret it, but the one thing he might regret was never having tried."},{"t":"p","s":"One honest note. He described this as a one time tool for a single decision, leaving his job in nineteen ninety four. Later commentators turned it into a daily algorithm. It is not one. It is for the big irreversible ones."},{"t":"p","s":"Which brings us to the second tool, and it is the most useful decision idea in this book."},{"t":"p","s":"One way doors and two way doors."},{"t":"p","s":"A two way door is a decision you can reverse. You walk through, you do not like it, you walk back. A one way door is consequential and effectively irreversible."},{"t":"p","s":"His instruction is that one way doors get made slowly, carefully, with deliberation and consultation. Two way doors do not. And here is the failure mode he names, which is the one most people actually have. Applying the slow careful process to reversible decisions causes slowness, unthoughtful risk aversion, not enough experimenting, and less invention as a result."},{"t":"p","s":"Most people are not too reckless. They are too slow on things they could simply undo."},{"t":"p","s":"And the companion rule, from his twenty sixteen letter, in his own words. Most decisions should probably be made with somewhere around 70 percent of the information you wish you had. And, being wrong may be less costly than you think, whereas being slow is going to be expensive."},{"t":"p","s":"For you, concretely. A subcontractor you can replace, a supplier you can switch, a marketing test you can stop. Two way doors. Decide now at 70 percent. A lease, a partner, a hire you will not be able to undo, a lawsuit. One way doors. Slow down and pressure test those."},{"t":"p","s":"Two more from that same letter, both useful. Disagree and commit, which he frames as, I know we disagree on this but will you gamble with me on it. And, the process becomes the proxy for the result you want. That second one is what happens when a company starts serving its own procedure instead of the outcome the procedure was built for."},{"t":"p","s":"Munger gives you inversion. Solve the problem backwards. Instead of asking how do I succeed here, ask how would this fail, then avoid that. His version of it is a peasant's line. Tell me where I am going to die, so I will not go there."},{"t":"p","s":"He also warns against the single discipline thinker. The man with only a hammer, to whom every problem looks like a nail. Which for you means, do not run every business problem through the estimating lens, or every people problem through the contract lens."},{"t":"p","s":"Buffett gives you the circle of competence, and I can give you this one word for word from his nineteen ninety six letter. You only have to be able to evaluate companies within your circle of competence. The size of that circle is not very important. Knowing its boundaries, however, is vital."},{"t":"p","s":"Note what that does not say. It does not say make your circle huge. It says know the edge."},{"t":"p","s":"Thiel gives you a question. What important truth do very few people agree with you on. That is the whole tool. He also splits the world into definite and indefinite optimism, the difference being whether you think the future is something you can plan and shape or something essentially random that you just react to."},{"t":"p","s":"Dalio gives you believability weighting. When opinions conflict, weight them by the person's actual track record on that specific question, and by whether they can explain the cause and effect, not by seniority. His formula for how progress happens is pain plus reflection equals progress."},{"t":"p","s":"Jobs gives you subtraction, and I want to be precise about the quote because there are two versions. The one that is verified, from nineteen ninety seven, is, focusing is about saying no. The longer, more famous version about saying no to a thousand things could not be verified in the actual transcript, so use the short one."},{"t":"p","s":"Naval gives you specific knowledge and leverage. Specific knowledge is the kind you cannot be trained for, the thing that feels like play to you but looks like work to other people. And then you multiply it with leverage that does not require anyone's permission, meaning code and media, rather than labor and capital, which do."},{"t":"p","s":"Walton gives you permission to copy. He openly took the warehouse club concept and part of his own company's name from a competitor. His ninth rule, in his own words, is that you can make a lot of different mistakes and still recover if you run an efficient operation, or you can be brilliant and still go out of business if you are too inefficient."},{"t":"p","s":"O'Leary gives you the one framework in this collection that is downside first, and for your business it may be the most relevant of all of them. Return of capital before return on capital. Structure the deal so that you get your money back on a schedule whether or not the upside ever arrives. And his working assumption when he evaluates something is, assume the company stays ordinary."},{"t":"p","s":"That is a genuine counterweight to all the asymmetric bet thinking in the rest of the chapter. For a rehab, or a partner deal, or a piece of equipment, the base case is not the upside case. It is that things stay normal."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, two names you asked about, and I am going to be straight with you on both."},{"t":"p","s":"Trump's book gives maxims, think big, protect the downside, maximize options, use leverage, and a phrase called truthful hyperbole. Here is the thing you should know. The book was written by a ghostwriter, Tony Schwartz, who shadowed him for the project. Schwartz went on the record about that phrase and said, truthful hyperbole is a contradiction in terms, it is a way of saying it is a lie but who cares. He called the whole project a Faustian bargain and said he felt deep remorse about it. Your own files call this the clearest case of a constructed persona in the collection. The maxims may still be useful. Just know whose sentences they are."},{"t":"p","s":"And Andrew Tate. There is no sourced, coherent thinking framework for him comparable to any of the others. That is not a political judgment, it is a sourcing one. When the research went looking for a documented method, there was not one to find. So he is in your files as a fact check subject and nothing more, and I am not going to invent a framework to fill the gap."},{"t":"p","s":"Finally, four famous lines that did not survive."},{"t":"p","s":"Buffett's rule number one, never lose money. It is in no shareholder letter. It traces to a secondhand book and a recollection. Do not quote it as his written words."},{"t":"p","s":"The five twenty five rule, where you write twenty five goals, circle five, and avoid the other twenty. Attributed to Buffett everywhere. The citation chain contains no interview, no letter, no talk, and no direct quote from him. It is folklore."},{"t":"p","s":"Simplicity is the ultimate sophistication, usually credited to da Vinci. It appeared on an Apple brochure in nineteen seventy seven, unattributed. The da Vinci link is almost certainly false."},{"t":"p","s":"And good artists copy, great artists steal, attributed to Picasso. There is no evidence Picasso said it."},{"t":"p","s":"None of that makes the underlying ideas worthless. It makes the quotes unusable, and there is a difference. Next chapter, how they actually work, and what the physiology says about it."}],"words":1656,"questions":0},{"id":"10_how_they_actually_work","track":"social","title":"Chapter ten. How they actually work, and how to run a crew.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"The last chapter was about thinking. This one is about hours, sleep, practice and managing people, and it has a peculiar property worth stating up front."},{"t":"p","s":"Your own files found this and it is worth repeating. The peer reviewed physiology, the research on hours, sleep, practice and management, was fully verifiable from primary sources. Almost every famous routine anecdote, the empty calendar, the five minute time blocks, the 8 hours of sleep and the ten a m first meeting, could not be confirmed."},{"t":"p","s":"Think about what that means. The routine stories, which are the most repeated part of all billionaire advice, are the least verifiable part of it. The physiology, which nobody repeats, is nailed down."},{"t":"p","s":"So let us do the part that is nailed down."},{"t":"p","s":"Hours first."},{"t":"p","s":"The best study on this is unusual. It used British munitions workers during the First World War, where output was measured precisely and hours varied a lot, from twenty four to over seventy per week."},{"t":"p","s":"The finding. Below about 49 hours a week, output rises in proportion to hours. Work more, produce more, one for one. Above 49 hours, output still rises but at a decreasing rate, and it peaks at about 63 hours."},{"t":"p","s":"And then the sentence that should change how you think about a hard week. Output at 70 hours differs little from output at 56 hours."},{"t":"p","s":"Fourteen extra hours. Almost nothing to show for them."},{"t":"p","s":"The researchers also compared a 70 hour week, 7 days of 10 hours, against a 48 hour week, 6 days of eight. Output was slightly higher on the 48 hour week, and the difference was about 1.4 percent."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, the honest caveat, and your files insist on it. That is manual factory work a century ago, not estimating or running a business. Do not present it as a law of knowledge work. But the shape of the curve is worth taking seriously, because nobody has produced a better measurement."},{"t":"p","s":"Then there is the health side, and this one is not a productivity question at all."},{"t":"p","s":"A joint World Health Organization and International Labour Organization study compared working fifty five or more hours a week against thirty five to forty. The result. 35 percent higher risk of stroke. 17 percent higher risk of dying from heart disease. In 1 year they attributed roughly seven hundred forty five thousand deaths to long working hours."},{"t":"p","s":"So the honest summary is not that an 80 hour week is heroic, and it is not even that it is merely unproductive past hour sixty three. It is a classified occupational health exposure. That is a different category of claim."},{"t":"p","s":"Sleep next, and this study will bother you, in a useful way."},{"t":"p","s":"Forty eight healthy adults were randomly assigned to four, six or eight hours in bed for fourteen straight nights. There was also a group kept awake completely for three nights, for comparison."},{"t":"p","s":"Restricting sleep to 6 hours or less produced cognitive deficits that accumulated day after day. By the end, the 6 hour group's performance was equivalent to up to two nights of total sleep deprivation."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is the part that matters most."},{"t":"p","s":"Their own ratings of how sleepy they felt jumped at the start and then barely moved. People in the 4 hour and 6 hour conditions could not tell each other apart on how tired they felt. In the researchers' words, subjects were largely unaware of these increasing cognitive deficits."},{"t":"p","s":"So the feeling of being fine on 5 hours is not evidence that you are fine on 5 hours. That self report is exactly the instrument the study showed to be broken. If you run short sleep, you have no reliable internal signal for what it is costing you."},{"t":"p","s":"Practice, briefly, because the famous version of it is wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"The original research on expert performance found that people sustaining serious practice over months and years limited it to two to four hours a day. Not eight. Two to four. And the same study found the expert musicians slept about 57 hours a week, roughly 8 hours a night, and the elite performers napped more than the comparison group. Sleep was part of the method, not a competing priority."},{"t":"p","s":"The 10,000 hours rule came from a popular book, not from the researcher. The researcher himself said, there is nothing special or magical about 10,000 hours. The violinists at that mark were, in his description, very good promising students, not masters. And half of them had not actually reached 10,000 hours."},{"t":"p","s":"Then a meta analysis asked how much of the difference between people practice actually explains. Games, 26 percent. Music, 21 percent. Sports, 18 percent. Education, 4 percent. Professions, less than 1 percent."},{"t":"p","s":"Less than 1 percent for professional work. So in your world, reps alone do not compound the way the story says. What you do in those reps, and what else you bring, matters more than the count."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, managing people, and here is the most valuable single fact in this chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Management is a teachable technology, and there is a randomized controlled trial proving it. Large Indian textile firms were randomly assigned to receive free consulting on modern management practices, or not. The treated plants raised productivity by 11 percent, through better quality, better efficiency, and less inventory. They also decentralized decisions and used computers more."},{"t":"p","s":"Why does that matter more than everything the operators in the last chapter said? Because there was a control group. Every framework in this book was extracted from people who won, which means you cannot tell what caused the winning. A randomized trial does not have that problem. 11 percent is a causal number, not a story."},{"t":"p","s":"The researchers' explanation for why firms had not already adopted these practices was informational. Modern management is a technology that diffuses slowly between firms. Meaning most people simply have not been shown."},{"t":"p","s":"Next, feedback, and this is the one to be careful with."},{"t":"p","s":"A meta analysis of six hundred seven effect sizes across more than twenty three thousand observations found that feedback improves performance on average. Fine. But over one third of feedback interventions made performance worse."},{"t":"p","s":"One in three. Made it worse."},{"t":"p","s":"And the mechanism is specific and usable. Feedback effectiveness decreases as attention moves up a hierarchy, from the task, to motivation about the task, to the self. The damage concentrates when the feedback points at the person rather than at the work. And that is not explained away by whether the feedback was positive or negative."},{"t":"p","s":"So, your framing on that ceiling is off, here is the line, is task level. You have got a quality problem, is self level. Same information. The second one is where the research says the damage lives."},{"t":"p","s":"On leadership styles, one number set worth knowing. Transformational leadership, the inspiring visionary style, has a validity of point four four. Contingent reward, which is the unglamorous business of saying clearly what you want and reliably paying for it, comes in at point three nine, and beats transformational on several criteria. And laissez faire, meaning absent, is minus point three seven."},{"t":"p","s":"The caveat is important. Those first two constructs correlate with each other at point eight zero, so they are not cleanly separable. And transformational leadership failed to predict the leader's own job performance."},{"t":"p","s":"Plain reading. Being clear and reliable is nearly as good as being inspiring, and it is much easier to do consistently. Being absent is actively destructive, more so than either of the others is helpful."},{"t":"p","s":"On hiring, one correction. A twenty twenty two paper found that decades of prior estimates had substantially overcorrected their statistics, meaning the predictive power of most selection methods has been overestimated, by roughly point one to point two. The method that came out top ranked was the structured interview. Same questions, same order, scored the same way, for everyone."},{"t":"p","s":"And finally, the family business finding, which I want to state precisely because the coarse version of it is unfair and your own files corrected themselves on this."},{"t":"p","s":"The coarse version, from a review paper, lists family ownership of firms as one of four reasons bad management persists."},{"t":"p","s":"The precise version, from the actual measurement study of seven hundred thirty two mid sized firms across four countries, is narrower and more useful. Poor management practices are more prevalent when competition is weak, and when family owned firms pass management control down to the eldest son. Primogeniture. Succession by birth order."},{"t":"p","s":"Your files are explicit about the distinction. A family firm that picks a successor or a manager on measured competence is not the documented failure case. The damage is in handing control to the eldest son because he is the eldest son."},{"t":"p","s":"That is a much more actionable finding, and it comes with a simple test. Would this decision survive if the person were not related to you?"},{"t":"p","s":"The chapter, condensed."},{"t":"p","s":"About 50 hours a week is the honest ceiling and past sixty three you are working for free, with a measured health cost attached."},{"t":"p","s":"You cannot feel your own sleep debt. The self report is broken."},{"t":"p","s":"Two to four hours of real focused work a day is what the experts actually sustained."},{"t":"p","s":"In a professional field, practice hours explain less than 1 percent. Do not expect reps alone to carry you."},{"t":"p","s":"Management is teachable and it is worth 11 percent, causally."},{"t":"p","s":"One in three feedback conversations makes things worse, and the ones that do are the ones aimed at the person instead of the work."},{"t":"p","s":"Being clear and reliable nearly matches being inspiring. Being absent is worse than both."},{"t":"p","s":"And succession by competence, not by birth order."}],"words":1610,"questions":0},{"id":"11_saying_it_so_it_sticks","track":"social","title":"Chapter eleven. Saying it so that it gets repeated.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This chapter is about the sentence itself. The shape of it, the speed you say it at, and why some lines travel and most do not."},{"t":"p","s":"Start with the most concrete finding in the whole collection."},{"t":"p","s":"Researchers analyzed four hundred seventy six British political conference speeches and looked at what was happening in the moments right before an audience applauded. They found seven rhetorical formats that together accounted for nearly 70 percent of the applause. And this held broadly regardless of which party the speaker was from, how senior they were, or how popular the message was."},{"t":"p","s":"70 percent of the applause, coming from seven repeatable shapes."},{"t":"p","s":"The two workhorses are worth learning by name."},{"t":"p","s":"The first is the contrast. Not X, but Y. It is not the water that ruins the house, it is the week you wait. The structure does work that the content alone does not."},{"t":"p","s":"The second is the three part list. Three items, delivered in a row, with the third one landing. There is something about three that finishes a thought in a way two does not and four dilutes."},{"t":"p","s":"One caution on that study. The breakdown of how much each individual format contributed was not available, so you can quote the combined figure, seven formats, 70 percent, four hundred seventy six speeches, but not a share for any single device."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, how many claims should you make?"},{"t":"p","s":"Three. And the condition attached to that is more interesting than the number."},{"t":"p","s":"Across four experiments, researchers found that when people know the person talking is trying to persuade them, the best number of positive claims is three. At the fourth, something flips. The listener's awareness that they are being sold to kicks in, and they start viewing all of the claims, including the good ones you already made, with skepticism."},{"t":"p","s":"The condition is the key part. This only applies when the audience knows you have a motive. When there is no persuasion motive in the room, when you are genuinely just explaining something, more claims are fine."},{"t":"p","s":"So on an estimate walkthrough, where the homeowner knows perfectly well you would like the job, three reasons. Not seven. The seventh reason does not add. It retroactively weakens the first three."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, metaphors. This is where the honest version differs sharply from the popular version."},{"t":"p","s":"A meta analysis of the effect of metaphor on changing someone's attitude found an overall effect of zero point zero seven. That is close to nothing. So the blanket claim that metaphors are persuasive does not hold."},{"t":"p","s":"But under specific conditions the effect climbs to zero point four two, which is substantial. Those conditions are precise. A single metaphor, not an extended one and not stacked with others. Novel, meaning fresh rather than a cliché. Aimed at a target the listener already understands. And placed early in what you are saying."},{"t":"p","s":"That is a recipe, not a vibe. One fresh comparison, early, then drop it."},{"t":"p","s":"Here is how strong it can be when it fits. In one study, describing crime as a beast versus describing crime as a virus shifted what policy people chose, 74 percent versus 56 percent in the first study, and seventy one versus fifty four in a second where only a single word changed. And only between three and fifteen percent of participants noticed the metaphor was there at all."},{"t":"p","s":"One word. Nobody notices. Twenty points of movement."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, what actually gets shared?"},{"t":"p","s":"Researchers analyzed six thousand nine hundred fifty six New York Times articles over 3 months, hand coding more than two thousand five hundred of them for emotional content. They measured what a one standard deviation increase in each emotion did to the odds of making the most emailed list."},{"t":"p","s":"Anger, plus 34 percent. Awe, plus 30 percent. Anxiety, plus 21 percent. And sadness, minus 16 percent."},{"t":"p","s":"To put the anger number in perspective, the researchers noted it was roughly equivalent to spending an additional 2.9 hours as the lead story on the site."},{"t":"p","s":"Notice the pattern. The three that help are all high energy states, whether pleasant or not. Sadness is the one that hurts, and sadness is low energy. It is not about positive or negative. It is about activation. Something that makes people feel wound up gets passed on. Something that makes them feel deflated does not."},{"t":"p","s":"There is a matching finding on headlines. Across twenty two thousand seven hundred forty three randomized headline tests, involving something like one hundred five thousand variations and five point seven million clicks, each additional negative word in a headline raised click through by 2.3 percent. Positive words lowered it."},{"t":"p","s":"I want to flag the obvious risk in that finding. It is a real effect and it is also the mechanism behind a lot of content you find exhausting. Knowing it works is different from deciding to use it."},{"t":"p","s":"One more distinction that will change where you put your effort. Written and spoken word of mouth favor different things. Written sharing favors what is interesting, because you have time to compose and to think about how you will look. Spoken sharing favors whatever is top of mind. So an article can afford to be clever. A thing you want a plumber to mention to another plumber needs to be simple and easy to reach for."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, delivery."},{"t":"p","s":"Speaking faster helps. Two field experiments found that a faster rate functions as a general cue that increases credibility and enhances persuasion, and that was not explained by fast talk stopping people from arguing back. A separate set of three experiments compressed and expanded recordings by 30 percent and found slow talking speakers were judged less truthful, less fluent, less persuasive, and more passive."},{"t":"p","s":"Now here is where I have to stop you, because this is the single most commonly repeated fake number in this area."},{"t":"p","s":"There is no verified words per minute target. None. The figures you will hear, a hundred fifty for conversation, a hundred sixty for audiobooks, a hundred sixty for a good talk, are practitioner and vendor norms, not research findings. Two real studies on speech rate exist, but their numbers sit in the body of the papers, not in the parts that could be verified. So the direction is supported and the number is not. If you want a target, record yourself and use your own baseline, not somebody's slide."},{"t":"p","s":"Vocal fry, that creaky low register at the end of sentences, has a real penalty. Eight hundred listeners judged fourteen speakers saying the same phrase in a normal voice and in fry. The fry version was rated less competent, less educated, less trustworthy, less attractive and less hireable, all with strong statistical significance. The penalty was larger for female speakers, and female listeners judged it more harshly than male ones. Trustworthiness was the strongest predictor of who got hired."},{"t":"p","s":"Pitch has two separate findings that get merged constantly, and merging them is a mistake."},{"t":"p","s":"The first is about what listeners prefer. The same voice, digitally lowered, was chosen for leadership over the raised version, by men and women, for male and female speakers. And in a separate study, raising your pitch early in an interaction predicted ending up lower in the group's ranking, while deepening it predicted ending up higher."},{"t":"p","s":"The second is about what having power does to a voice. When people were assigned high rank, their pitch went up, not down, along with more variation in loudness."},{"t":"p","s":"So do not cite the second study to support the first. What power does to a voice is not the same as what a voice does to listeners. It is a genuinely easy mistake and it is in your files as a named trap."},{"t":"p","s":"Finally, speed of reply. Faster responses read as more connected, and the effect operates below a quarter of a second, which is well under conscious control. And from an earlier chapter, slower responses read as less sincere. So the general rule is answer promptly, and when you need time, make the reason for the pause audible rather than silent."},{"t":"p","s":"One last rule, and it is the one that outranks everything else in this chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Fact first, always. Every device here is a way of carrying something true. A well delivered line with nothing behind it is a slogan, and there is research showing that a rhetorical format used without real content does not even get the applause. The shape does not save an empty sentence."},{"t":"p","s":"Next chapter is short, practical, and about photographs."}],"words":1420,"questions":0},{"id":"12_photographs","track":"social","title":"Chapter twelve. Photographs.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"Short chapter, high return. Most of what people believe about looking good in photos is wrong, and the thing that actually matters is not about your face at all. It is about where the camera is standing."},{"t":"p","s":"Start with the single biggest lever."},{"t":"p","s":"Stand back and zoom in."},{"t":"p","s":"At 12 inches from your face, a camera makes your nose render about 30 percent larger than it actually is for men, and about 29 percent larger for women. Your eyes appear about 7 percent further apart. That is not a flattering or unflattering lens. It is geometry. Anything closer to the camera gets magnified relative to what is behind it, and on a face at arm's length, that is your nose."},{"t":"p","s":"At 5 feet, which is a normal portrait distance, that distortion is essentially gone."},{"t":"p","s":"So every selfie taken at arm's length is showing people a version of your face that has a 30 percent larger nose than the one you actually have. Not a mood, not lighting. A measurable geometric error you are introducing."},{"t":"p","s":"Now, does it actually change how people judge you? Yes, and the study that shows it is elegant."},{"t":"p","s":"Researchers photographed the same face twice, simultaneously, through a half silvered mirror, one camera at forty five centimetres and one at one hundred thirty five centimetres. Same instant, same expression, same light. Only distance differed."},{"t":"p","s":"The farther photograph was rated higher on attractiveness, on trustworthiness, and on competence. And in a trust game where people decided how much real money to hand over to the person in the picture, they gave more money to the far shot."},{"t":"p","s":"The effect sizes were small but consistent, running between about zero point two and zero point three. And this is the part I like. No participant noticed the distance had been manipulated. They were not deciding they preferred a lens. They just found that face more trustworthy and did not know why."},{"t":"p","s":"A separate study with forty five subjects and three hundred sixty nine raters found the same direction with lenses. Photographs shot at fifty millimetres were rated significantly less attractive and less dominant than the same faces at eighty five and one hundred five millimetres."},{"t":"p","s":"So the instruction is simple. Never take a portrait at arm's length. Back the camera up and zoom in, or have someone stand 5 feet away. If you must take it yourself, use a timer and put the phone down somewhere."},{"t":"p","s":"Second lever. Camera height."},{"t":"p","s":"Shoot from at or slightly above eye level. When researchers photographed the same faces from lower vantage points, people overestimated their body weight. From higher vantage points, they underestimated it. Low angles make you read heavier."},{"t":"p","s":"But do not run to the opposite extreme, because there is a trade there. In four experiments with two hundred seventy two participants, the classic selfie perspective, held up high, produced faces judged slimmer, and also judged least attractive of the options tested. Your files describe that precisely as a trade, not a win. And in another study, faces photographed from above were rated significantly less helpful."},{"t":"p","s":"There is no magic number of degrees. A fixed tilt rule is folklore. At or slightly above eye level, and stop there."},{"t":"p","s":"Third, the expression."},{"t":"p","s":"A genuine smile involves the muscle around the eye, the one that creates the crease at the corner. A meta analysis found that smiles with that eye involvement are rated more authentic, more genuine, more attractive and more trustworthy than smiles without it. The difference is bigger in video than in still photos, and bigger for naturally produced smiles than posed ones."},{"t":"p","s":"I have to stop short of giving you a number there. The full text is behind a paywall, so the size of that effect is unknown. Direction only. But the direction is solid and the practical instruction is easy. You cannot voluntarily produce the eye part on command. That is essentially the whole reason say cheese produces bad photos. So get caught mid laugh instead of posing. Have somebody say something funny right before the shot."},{"t":"p","s":"Fourth, and this one is genuinely reassuring."},{"t":"p","s":"Take many and pick one."},{"t":"p","s":"In a study of how much a single person's photographs vary, the variation within one person exceeded the variation between different people. Unfamiliar viewers looking at different photographs of the same person sometimes concluded they were looking at different individuals."},{"t":"p","s":"Read that again, because it disposes of a belief a lot of people carry around. One bad photo of you is not the truth about your face. It is a bad draw from a wide distribution. The gap between your worst and your best picture can be larger than the gap between you and a stranger."},{"t":"p","s":"So take ten. Pick one. That is not vanity, it is sampling."},{"t":"p","s":"And finally, the reason you dislike photos of yourself, which is not that you are unphotogenic."},{"t":"p","s":"In a pair of studies, people reliably preferred their own mirror image over their true image. Their friends and partners preferred the opposite, the true image, the one in the photograph."},{"t":"p","s":"The likely explanation is simple exposure. You have spent your entire life looking at your mirror image. It is the version of your face you know. Everyone else has only ever seen the other one, and that is the version they are fond of."},{"t":"p","s":"So when you look at a photo and think, that does not look like me, you are correct. It does not look like the version you know. It looks like the version everybody else knows and likes."},{"t":"p","s":"Two things to throw out."},{"t":"p","s":"The camera adds ten pounds. There is no study testing this claim. And the one relevant measurement in the literature pointed the other way, finding that faces photographed farther away were rated as heavier, which is the opposite of what the saying predicts, since far is the flattering distance on everything else. It was an incidental control on eighteen faces, so I would not build anything on it either. Just stop repeating the ten pounds."},{"t":"p","s":"And lighting direction rules. The forty five degree key light, and all its cousins. No study isolating the effect of lighting direction on attractiveness was located. That does not mean lighting does not matter. It means nobody has measured the rule you are being sold, so it is unsourced rather than disproven."},{"t":"p","s":"The chapter in five lines."},{"t":"p","s":"Back up and zoom in. This is the whole game."},{"t":"p","s":"Camera at or just above eye level, and no further."},{"t":"p","s":"Get caught mid laugh. You cannot fake the eyes."},{"t":"p","s":"Shoot many, pick one. Your worst and best photo differ more than you and a stranger."},{"t":"p","s":"And you dislike your photos because you know your mirror. Everyone else prefers the one you are complaining about."}],"words":1125,"questions":0},{"id":"13_what_not_to_believe","track":"social","title":"Chapter thirteen. What not to believe.","blocks":[{"t":"p","s":"This is the last chapter and I think it is the most valuable one, because everything in it is something you would otherwise have repeated in a meeting with complete confidence."},{"t":"p","s":"There is a pattern worth noticing before we start. Almost every item on this list is more quotable than the truth that replaced it. That is not a coincidence. Claims survive by being repeatable, not by being correct, and a clean number with a famous name attached spreads faster than an honest uncertainty. So the question to keep asking is not, does this sound right. It is, who benefits if I believe this."},{"t":"p","s":"Start with the social ones."},{"t":"p","s":"Rejection therapy. A hundred days of deliberately getting rejected to burn out the fear. When we went looking for evidence, we found exactly one academic source, an unpublished dissertation, which states in its own text that the practice has not been investigated or standardized. Nobody has tested it. It is not disproven. It has simply never been checked."},{"t":"p","s":"How many attempts until the fear goes away. There is no number. No dose response figure exists in the located literature. If someone gives you one, they made it up."},{"t":"p","s":"The thirty six questions that make someone fall in love with you. The romantic framing is journalism, not a finding. The actual study used a forty five minute escalating disclosure task, mostly between same sex strangers in a lab, to build closeness. The authors never tested romantic outcomes. And in their own follow up studies, they specifically tried priming people to expect they would like each other, and found no significant effect from the priming, despite having enough participants to detect one."},{"t":"p","s":"That French phrase for the perfect comeback that arrives too late, on the stairs. Every search returned etymology, tracing it to Diderot. There is no research on the phenomenon at all. The feeling is universal and the science does not exist."},{"t":"p","s":"Status transactions, from the famous improv book. Influential, genuinely interesting, and never tested. The only academic engagement located was a qualitative exploratory study on training attorneys. No control group, no effect sizes, no hypothesis testing."},{"t":"p","s":"And improv as a treatment for social anxiety. The study people cite had two hundred sixty eight teenagers, ten weeks, and no control group at all. With no control group you cannot separate the program from simply getting older. The abstract says so itself. There is better evidence that improv improves your tolerance for uncertainty, which is real and useful, but that is a narrower claim than the one being sold."},{"t":"p","s":"Now two that are worse, because they were in your own files as advice and turned out to be wrong."},{"t":"p","s":"Lead with warmth before competence. This is repeated constantly in business writing. A replication of the classic studies it rests on found no primacy of warmth effect, and concluded competence was at least as important in shaping impressions. Your files carried that rule, and it was withdrawn. What survives is a checklist rather than a sequence. A first meeting answers two questions, does he mean well and can he do it, and answering only one leaves the other one open."},{"t":"p","s":"And feed your weak ties. The famous idea that your loose acquaintances, not your close contacts, are where opportunity comes from. The large causal test of this found something more specific. Weak ties raised job mobility in more digital industries, while strong ties did so in less digital ones. Restoration, plumbing, adjusting, local real estate are about as low digital as work gets. So for your actual business the evidence points the other way, toward depth with the people who already know you. Keep the weak tie play for anything that lives online."},{"t":"p","s":"Power posing. Two minutes in a big open stance to change your hormones. The original study had forty two people. A properly blinded replication with two hundred participants, with more than enough power to find the effect, found no hormone effects and no behavioral effects. An analysis of thirty four studies found no evidential value. The first author of the original paper stated publicly that she does not believe power pose effects are real. The only surviving piece is that posture may change how you feel."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the speaking ones."},{"t":"p","s":"Body language is 55 percent of communication. The tone is thirty eight, the words are seven. This one is everywhere. The researcher whose work it comes from published a note saying that unless a communicator is talking about their feelings or attitudes, these equations are not applicable. His studies used single words about liking, with the tone deliberately contradicting the word. It was never a study of communication in general."},{"t":"p","s":"The ideal speaking rate. A hundred fifty words a minute, or a hundred sixty, depending who is selling. No words per minute figure in your entire collection is verified. Two real studies on speech rate exist but their numbers are in parts of the papers that could not be accessed. What is supported is direction only, that faster reads as more credible and slower reads as less truthful. If you want a target, record yourself and use your own baseline."},{"t":"p","s":"The Zeigarnik effect, used to justify open loops in videos. The idea that an unfinished task stays lodged in memory. A twenty twenty five replication with over a thousand people found the classic effect could not be reliably replicated. There was extra recall for unsolved problems only among people who scored high on hope of success, which is a narrow subgroup, not a general law. If you want a citation for curiosity gaps, there is a separate and better supported literature. Just do not use Zeigarnik."},{"t":"p","s":"Naming an idea makes it spread. No study was found testing a named versus unnamed idea with the same content. The one labeling theory with lab support is about category learning, not idea spread, and a twenty twenty six preprint failed to replicate its key result. The practitioner pattern is real. The mechanism is not evidenced."},{"t":"p","s":"The MrBeast memo rules. The first minute rule, the metric definitions, the re hooking rule. The document was never actually opened. Six verbatim excerpts are quotable. Everything else attributed to that memo is unsourced."},{"t":"p","s":"The three second hook rule for short video. No platform statement establishing a hook window was found. What does exist is a metric definition, the three second video view, which is very likely where the myth came from. Somebody turned a measurement threshold into a rule about human attention."},{"t":"p","s":"Fillers kill your credibility. Here it gets interesting. One study found that saying uh actually helps listeners recognize the words coming next. Um showed no such benefit. The claim that fillers cost you credibility is itself the lore."},{"t":"p","s":"And a specific misuse worth naming, because it is in your files as a trap. There is a study about pitch and power that gets cited to support lowering your voice to sound powerful. It found the opposite direction. Being given power raised people's pitch. That is what power does to a voice, not what listeners prefer in a leader. Those are two different studies and merging them produces a confident, wrong sentence."},{"t":"p","s":"Now the operator ones."},{"t":"p","s":"Buffett's rule number one, never lose money. It is in no shareholder letter. It traces to a secondhand book and a recollection."},{"t":"p","s":"The five twenty five rule. Write twenty five goals, circle five, avoid the other twenty. Attributed to Buffett everywhere. The citation chain contains no interview, no letter, no talk and no direct quote from him."},{"t":"p","s":"Simplicity is the ultimate sophistication, credited to da Vinci. It appeared unattributed on an Apple brochure in nineteen seventy seven."},{"t":"p","s":"Good artists copy, great artists steal, credited to Picasso. No evidence he said it."},{"t":"p","s":"Four in the morning wake ups as discipline. Chronotype is substantially biological. Whether five a m is early or brutal for you is not a moral quality."},{"t":"p","s":"The 40 percent productivity loss from multitasking. That figure does not appear in the paper it is attributed to. The real finding is about switching costs rising with rule complexity, with no 40 percent anywhere."},{"t":"p","s":"10,000 hours. The researcher whose work it came from said, there is nothing special or magical about 10,000 hours. The musicians at that mark were promising students, not masters, and half of them had not actually reached the number. And a meta analysis found practice explains 26 percent of the difference in games, twenty one in music, eighteen in sports, four in education, and less than 1 percent in professions."},{"t":"p","s":"Grit as a distinct trait. It correlates with plain conscientiousness at around point seven seven, with a genetic correlation of point eight six. It may not be a separate thing at all."},{"t":"p","s":"And a whole set of famous routine stories, the empty paper calendar, the five minute time blocks, the sleep and meeting schedule, that could not be confirmed to any primary source."},{"t":"p","s":"Which brings me to the finding underneath all of them."},{"t":"p","s":"Your files noticed that the peer reviewed physiology was fully verifiable, while almost every famous routine anecdote was not. The routines are the most repeated part of billionaire advice and the least verifiable. The physiology is the least repeated and the most solid."},{"t":"p","s":"That is worth sitting with. The part of the genre that is easy to check is boring and true. The part that is fun to repeat is the part nobody has checked."},{"t":"p","s":"Two more, close to home."},{"t":"p","s":"The figure that circulates about how much the Learning Company acquisition cost Mattel. Somebody went and pulled the actual annual filing. The commonly repeated number appears nowhere in it. The real figures are there and they are worse, but the number everybody quotes is not from the document."},{"t":"p","s":"And the negotiation dollar figures you have heard, the two and a half billion and the fifteen billion. Both are self published marketing, neither has been independently verified, and they contradict each other. Say the person claims it, or say nothing."},{"t":"p","s":"Finally, a rule that outlasts every specific item in this chapter."},{"t":"p","s":"Two things in this book were wrong not because someone lied but because a count got attached to the wrong sentence. In both cases the number was real, and it belonged to a shorter, more common phrase, not the polished quote it was printed next to. That is the most ordinary way a false fact gets made. Not fabrication. Attachment."},{"t":"p","s":"So when someone gives you a precise number stapled to a memorable quote, the useful question is not whether the number is real. It usually is. The question is whether it belongs to that sentence."},{"t":"p","s":"That is the end of the book. If you keep one thing, keep this. The most confident sentence in the room is not the most likely to be true, and now you know quite a few examples. Ask for the document. Including when the confident person is you."}],"words":1818,"questions":0}]};